::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|TELEMETRY AND COMPATIBILITY SCHEDULED TASKS
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.003|  What it is      : Seven scheduled tasks. Compatibility Appraiser
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.004|                    inventories your apps and drivers for upgrade-
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.005|                    readiness. Consolidator and UsbCeip are the Customer
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.006|                    Experience Improvement Program uploaders. DmClient and
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.007|                    DmClientOnScenarioDownload drive the feedback prompts.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.008|                    PcaPatchDbTask and StartupAppTask are local
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.009|                    housekeeping for the Program Compatibility Assistant
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.010|                    and for startup-app measurement. On Pro these tasks
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.011|                    are what actually moves data, because the
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.012|                    AllowTelemetry registry policy is clamped to Required
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.013|                    on non-Enterprise editions and writing 0 to it
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.014|                    achieves nothing.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.015|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.016|  Actual effect   : Runs schtasks /Change /Disable on each of the seven.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.017|                    They stay installed and can be switched back on from
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.018|                    Task Scheduler at any time.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.019|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.020|  Gain            : Partial, and worth being precise about. The only one
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.021|                    with a measurable machine cost is Compatibility
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.022|                    Appraiser: it can hold a core and the disk busy for
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.023|                    several minutes when it runs, which you notice on a
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.024|                    slow disk. The other six are cheap enough that turning
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.025|                    them off has no measurable gain in CPU, RAM or boot
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.026|                    time - the gain there is purely that less data leaves
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.027|                    the machine. Anyone who tells you this makes a PC
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.028|                    "telemetry-free" is wrong: DiagTrack itself is
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.029|                    untouched and the plain Compatibility Appraiser
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.030|                    variant may not even be matched by name.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.031|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.032|  Cost            : Windows Update reads appraiser signals to decide
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.033|                    whether your exact hardware and drivers are safe for
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.034|                    the next feature update. With the appraiser off those
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.035|                    signals go stale, so a feature update that a known
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.036|                    incompatibility would have held back can be offered to
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.037|                    you anyway. Nothing breaks day to day.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.038|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.039|  Windows default : All seven ship Enabled.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.040|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.041|  Possible values:
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.042|    enabled              : The shipped state. Each task fires on its own
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.043|                           triggers - mostly idle-time and logon.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.044|                           Compatibility Appraiser walks the installed
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.045|                           application and driver inventory and writes it
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.046|                           under %windir%\appcompat\Programs, where
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.047|                           DiagTrack picks it up; Consolidator and UsbCeip
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.048|                           upload CEIP samples; the two DmClient tasks
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.049|                           schedule feedback prompts and download feedback
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.050|                           scenarios.
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.051|    disabled             : schtasks /Change /Disable. The task definition
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.052|                           stays on disk with all its triggers and actions
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.053|                           intact - only the enabled flag flips. Nothing
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.054|                           else demand-starts these tasks, so a disabled
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.055|                           one simply never runs. One click in Task
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.056|                           Scheduler, or the restore branch of this
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.057|                           script, puts it back.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|  Why these profiles : Three profiles agree and one dissents, and the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|                       dissent is the interesting one. Gaming, server and
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.003|                       laptop all disable: none of them wants an idle-time
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.004|                       inventory sweep, and none of them relies on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.005|                       Microsoft to gate feature updates. Office is the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.006|                       profile that keeps them, because the office
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.007|                       archetype is exactly the machine you want Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.008|                       to be conservative with - a family PC or a work
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.009|                       machine that installs feature updates automatically
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.010|                       is the one case where the appraiser's signal earns
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.011|                       its keep, and where a support tool or an IT audit
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.012|                       may expect a stock task set. Laptop is not a
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.013|                       battery decision: most of these tasks already carry
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.014|                       a "start only if on AC power" condition, so on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.015|                       battery they were not running anyway - laptop
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.016|                       disables them for the same privacy reason as
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.017|                       gaming, not for runtime.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.018|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.019|  Known problems  : None confirmed. See uncertain for the Startup impact
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.020|                    claim, which I have deliberately not asserted.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.021|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.022|  Unverified      : Three things I cannot substantiate. (1) The task is
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.023|                    named "Microsoft Compatibility Appraiser Exp" only on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.024|                    some builds; on others it is plain "Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.025|                    Compatibility Appraiser", and schtasks then silently
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.026|                    does nothing - the script should check the exit code
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.027|                    and report which of the seven were actually found. (2)
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.028|                    The widely repeated claim that disabling
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.029|                    StartupAppTask blanks the Startup impact column in
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.030|                    Task Manager / Settings is plausible but I did not
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.031|                    verify it. (3) I have no evidence PcaPatchDbTask sends
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.032|                    anything anywhere; it looks like a local Program
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.033|                    Compatibility Assistant database patch task, so its
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.034|                    presence in a "telemetry" list may simply be folklore
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.035|                    inherited from other scripts.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.036|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.037|  Target          : schtasks /Change /Disable /TN on the seven tasks at
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.038|                    OPTY.bat lines 1556-1562:
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.039|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application Experience\Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.040|                    Compatibility Appraiser Exp",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.041|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.042|                    Experience\PcaPatchDbTask",
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.043|                    "\Microsoft\Windows\Application
