::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.001|SPONSORED STORE APP INSTALLS, RESTORE SIDE
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.002|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.003|  What it is      : DisableWindowsConsumerFeatures, the machine-wide
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.004|                    policy that blocks Windows from provisioning sponsored
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.005|                    apps and games. Same value as the setup question, from
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.006|                    the restore menu.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.007|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes the policy, so Windows may push promotional
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.009|                    apps into new profiles again.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.010|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.011|  Gain            : None for you personally. It matters only if a support
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.012|                    tool or an audit expects a strictly stock machine.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.013|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.014|  Cost            : Candy-Crush-class tiles can reappear on the Start menu
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.015|                    of profiles created afterwards, and each one is a
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.016|                    background download.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.017|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.018|  Windows default : Absent.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.019|
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.020|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.021|    1                    : Windows stops the cloud-driven "consumer
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.022|                           experiences": no sponsored app and game tiles
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.023|                           pushed into newly created profiles, no
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.024|                           promotional Start menu entries appearing on
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.025|                           their own. It blocks the delivery mechanism,
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.026|                           machine-wide; it does not uninstall anything
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.027|                           already present.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.028|    0                    : An explicit "consumer experiences allowed". A
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.029|                           third state, not the shipped condition.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.030|                           Deleting is the correct way back.
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.031|    DELETE               : Absent, the shipped state. Windows may again
::T|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.032|                           provision promotional apps into new profiles.
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles agree on 1 and there is no honest
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.002|                       distinction to draw between them - a clean Start
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.003|                       menu and no unrequested downloads are wanted by a
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.004|                       gaming rig, a headless box, a family PC and a
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.005|                       laptop equally, and the policy has no runtime cost
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.006|                       on any of them. Only the Windows answer deletes.
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.007|                       Note the overlap: SilentInstalledAppsEnabled on the
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.008|                       suggestions card is the per-user knob that does the
::X|EN|gr.consumerfeatures.del.009|                       same job, and unlike this one it is not edition-
