::T|EN|background.apps.off.001|BACKGROUND APPS (STORE / PACKAGED APPS)
::T|EN|background.apps.off.002|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.003|  What it is      : Packaged apps from the Microsoft Store can run tasks
::T|EN|background.apps.off.004|                    while they are not open - syncing mail, refreshing a
::T|EN|background.apps.off.005|                    widget, waiting to raise a notification. This step
::T|EN|background.apps.off.006|                    writes the per-user value GlobalUserDisabled=1, which
::T|EN|background.apps.off.007|                    on Windows 10 was the master switch forbidding all of
::T|EN|background.apps.off.008|                    them from doing so.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.009|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.010|  Actual effect   : Intended to block background execution for packaged
::T|EN|background.apps.off.011|                    apps, for this user account only. Classic desktop
::T|EN|background.apps.off.012|                    programs - Chrome, Steam, the desktop build of
::T|EN|background.apps.off.013|                    Spotify, anything installed from an .exe - are
::T|EN|background.apps.off.014|                    completely unaffected, which is the most common
::T|EN|background.apps.off.015|                    misunderstanding about this setting. On Windows 11 the
::T|EN|background.apps.off.016|                    reliable route is the per-app permission page, not
::T|EN|background.apps.off.017|                    this value.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.018|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.019|  Gain            : If the value is still honoured: a little less
::T|EN|background.apps.off.020|                    background CPU, memory and network. It does not touch
::T|EN|background.apps.off.021|                    the desktop applications that are almost certainly
::T|EN|background.apps.off.022|                    doing most of your background work, so no
::T|EN|background.apps.off.023|                    transformation is on offer here. If the value is no
::T|EN|background.apps.off.024|                    longer honoured on your build, the gain is zero.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.025|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.026|  Cost            : On any build that does honour it, you lose background
::T|EN|background.apps.off.027|                    sync and toast notifications from Store apps: a Store-
::T|EN|background.apps.off.028|                    packaged mail, calendar or messaging app stops
::T|EN|background.apps.off.029|                    notifying you until you open it. That is a real
::T|EN|background.apps.off.030|                    functional loss, not a cosmetic one, and it is silent
::T|EN|background.apps.off.031|                    - nothing tells you the notification did not arrive.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.032|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.033|  Windows default : Value absent - background apps are allowed. Note that
::T|EN|background.apps.off.034|                    OPTY deliberately does not use the machine-wide
::T|EN|background.apps.off.035|                    LetAppsRunInBackground policy, because that one greys
::T|EN|background.apps.off.036|                    out the Settings toggle with 'managed by your
::T|EN|background.apps.off.037|                    organization'.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.038|
::T|EN|background.apps.off.039|  Possible values:
::T|EN|background.apps.off.040|    1                    : Intended as the per-user master 'no background
::T|EN|background.apps.off.041|                           execution for packaged apps'. On Windows 10
::T|EN|background.apps.off.042|                           this was the global Background apps switch in
::T|EN|background.apps.off.043|                           Settings and it demonstrably worked. On Windows
::T|EN|background.apps.off.044|                           11 that global switch was removed from the UI
::T|EN|background.apps.off.045|                           and control moved to per-app 'Background apps
::T|EN|background.apps.off.046|                           permissions'; whether the broker still reads
::T|EN|background.apps.off.047|                           this value on 23H2/24H2/25H2 is not
::T|EN|background.apps.off.048|                           established, so on a current machine the write
::T|EN|background.apps.off.049|                           may simply do nothing.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.050|    0                    : Explicitly allows background execution.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.051|                           Behaviourally identical to the value being
::T|EN|background.apps.off.052|                           absent - 0 is not a third state here, it is the
::T|EN|background.apps.off.053|                           default written down.
::T|EN|background.apps.off.054|    DELETE               : Removes the value, leaving the registry as
::T|EN|background.apps.off.055|                           shipped. Packaged apps are governed by their
::T|EN|background.apps.off.056|                           individual permission under Settings > Apps >
::T|EN|background.apps.off.057|                           Installed apps > Advanced options, which is the
::T|EN|background.apps.off.058|                           control Windows 11 actually documents and the
::T|EN|background.apps.off.059|                           only one whose effect can be verified per app.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.001|  Why these profiles : Gaming, server and laptop write 1 on the same
::X|EN|background.apps.off.002|                       reasoning: none of them depends on a Store app to
::X|EN|background.apps.off.003|                       be told something, so if the value still works they
::X|EN|background.apps.off.004|                       gain a little and if it does not they lose nothing.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.005|                       Office deletes it, because that is the profile
::X|EN|background.apps.off.006|                       where someone is waiting on a message or a calendar
::X|EN|background.apps.off.007|                       reminder and a silently dropped notification is a
::X|EN|background.apps.off.008|                       genuine failure. Windows ships the value absent.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.009|                       Whichever you pick, the per-app page under Settings
::X|EN|background.apps.off.010|                       > Apps is the control that is documented to work on
::X|EN|background.apps.off.011|                       Windows 11.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.012|
::X|EN|background.apps.off.013|  Known problems  : No breakage confirmed on Windows 11 25H2, for the
::X|EN|background.apps.off.014|                    plain reason that the value may not be read at all any
::X|EN|background.apps.off.015|                    more. The Windows 10 behaviour - Store-packaged mail
::X|EN|background.apps.off.016|                    and messaging apps going quiet until opened - is the
::X|EN|background.apps.off.017|                    failure mode to watch for if your build does honour
::X|EN|background.apps.off.018|                    it.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.019|
::X|EN|background.apps.off.020|  Unverified      : Windows 11 removed the global Background apps toggle
::X|EN|background.apps.off.021|                    from Settings; per-app control now lives under Apps >
::X|EN|background.apps.off.022|                    Installed apps > Advanced options. Whether current
::X|EN|background.apps.off.023|                    builds still honour HKCU\...\BackgroundAccessApplicati
::X|EN|background.apps.off.024|                    ons\GlobalUserDisabled was not verified, so this write
::X|EN|background.apps.off.025|                    may do nothing at all on 23H2/24H2/25H2. Consequently
::X|EN|background.apps.off.026|                    the CPU/memory saving and the notification loss below
::X|EN|background.apps.off.027|                    are both stated as intent, not as measured outcome. On
::X|EN|background.apps.off.028|                    a machine using few Store apps the freed CPU and
::X|EN|background.apps.off.029|                    memory would be too small to measure even if the value
::X|EN|background.apps.off.030|                    is honoured.
::X|EN|background.apps.off.031|
::X|EN|background.apps.off.032|  Target          : HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Backgro
::X|EN|background.apps.off.033|                    undAccessApplications /v GlobalUserDisabled /t
