::X|FR|restore.point.028|
::X|FR|restore.point.029|  Cible           : :restore_point (OPTY.bat lines 3608-3632): Enable-
::X|FR|restore.point.030|                    ComputerRestore on %SystemDrive%, then
::X|FR|restore.point.031|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|restore.point.032|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|FR|restore.point.033|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency /t REG_DWORD /d 0,
::X|FR|restore.point.034|                    then Checkpoint-Computer -RestorePointType
::X|FR|restore.point.035|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS, then the frequency value written back
::X|FR|restore.point.036|                    as 1440
::T|EN|restore.point.001|CREATE A SYSTEM RESTORE POINT FIRST
::T|EN|restore.point.002|
::T|EN|restore.point.003|  What it is      : Before anything destructive, OPTY temporarily lifts
::T|EN|restore.point.004|                    Windows' one-point-per-24-hours throttle, enables
::T|EN|restore.point.005|                    System Protection on the system drive, creates a
::T|EN|restore.point.006|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS restore point, and puts the throttle
::T|EN|restore.point.007|                    back.
::T|EN|restore.point.008|
::T|EN|restore.point.009|  Actual effect   : You get a restore point dated now, even if one was
::T|EN|restore.point.010|                    already made today. If System Protection was OFF on
::T|EN|restore.point.011|                    C:, this turns it ON - a real change, because Windows
::T|EN|restore.point.012|                    then starts reserving shadow-copy space on the system
::T|EN|restore.point.013|                    drive and keeps doing so after OPTY exits.
::T|EN|restore.point.014|
::T|EN|restore.point.015|  Gain            : A one-click way back if a later step goes wrong.
::T|EN|restore.point.016|                    Several steps in this tool have no OPTY undo at all -
::T|EN|restore.point.017|                    netsh int ip reset and winsock reset among them - so
::T|EN|restore.point.018|                    this is what makes the rest safe to try. Note what a
::T|EN|restore.point.019|                    restore point does not cover: it does not restore your
::T|EN|restore.point.020|                    files, and it will not undo a deleted cache or a
::T|EN|restore.point.021|                    removed app.
::T|EN|restore.point.022|
::T|EN|restore.point.023|  Cost            : Disk space: shadow storage typically takes a few
::T|EN|restore.point.024|                    percent of the system drive. It is also a genuine
::T|EN|restore.point.025|                    behaviour change on a machine where System Protection
::T|EN|restore.point.026|                    was deliberately off, and OPTY does not turn it back
::T|EN|restore.point.027|                    off afterwards. Creating the point takes 30-60
::T|EN|restore.point.028|                    seconds.
::T|EN|restore.point.029|
::T|EN|restore.point.030|  Windows default : The frequency value is absent, meaning 1440 minutes.
::T|EN|restore.point.031|                    On many Windows 11 clean installs System Protection is
::T|EN|restore.point.032|                    OFF for C:, so on those machines a 'yes' here is
::T|EN|restore.point.033|                    switching a feature on, not just taking a snapshot.
::T|EN|restore.point.034|
::T|EN|restore.point.035|  Possible values:
::T|EN|restore.point.036|    RUN                  : Turns System Protection on for the system drive
::T|EN|restore.point.037|                           if it was off, lifts the one-point-per-24-hours
::T|EN|restore.point.038|                           throttle, creates a restore point dated now,
::T|EN|restore.point.039|                           then puts the throttle back. Guarded by RP_DONE
::T|EN|restore.point.040|                           so it happens at most once per session. Takes
::T|EN|restore.point.041|                           30-60 seconds.
::T|EN|restore.point.042|    SKIP                 : No restore point, no change to System
::T|EN|restore.point.043|                           Protection, and no write to the frequency
::T|EN|restore.point.044|                           value. Everything downstream then runs with no
::T|EN|restore.point.045|                           OPTY-provided way back. Reasonable only if you
::T|EN|restore.point.046|                           already take full disk images.
::T|EN|restore.point.047|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 0 :
::T|EN|restore.point.048|        The throttle lifted: Windows will create a restore point on every
::T|EN|restore.point.049|        request instead of refusing a second one within 24 hours. OPTY
::T|EN|restore.point.050|        sets this only for the few seconds around Checkpoint-Computer.
::T|EN|restore.point.051|        Left at 0 permanently - which older OPTY builds did - it means
::T|EN|restore.point.052|        every application install can create its own point and shadow
::T|EN|restore.point.053|        storage fills much faster.
::T|EN|restore.point.054|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 1440 :
::T|EN|restore.point.055|        What OPTY writes back afterwards: 1440 minutes, i.e. one point per
::T|EN|restore.point.056|        24 hours. Behaviourally identical to the shipped state, but the
::T|EN|restore.point.057|        shipped state is the value being ABSENT - so this leaves behind a
::T|EN|restore.point.058|        value Windows never wrote. A :killkey would be the literal
::T|EN|restore.point.059|        restore.
::X|EN|restore.point.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles run it, because the value of a way
::X|EN|restore.point.002|                       back does not depend on what the machine is for.
::X|EN|restore.point.003|                       Profile 5 is the only honest 'no': Windows does not
::X|EN|restore.point.004|                       create restore points on your behalf, and a machine
::X|EN|restore.point.005|                       that is already covered by full disk images gains
::X|EN|restore.point.006|                       nothing from a second, weaker safety net. If you
::X|EN|restore.point.007|                       say no, be aware that several later steps become
::X|EN|restore.point.008|                       one-way.
::X|EN|restore.point.009|
::X|EN|restore.point.010|  Known problems  : On a machine where System Protection is off and
::X|EN|restore.point.011|                    Enable-ComputerRestore fails - a Home edition with
::X|EN|restore.point.012|                    protection disabled by policy, or a system drive with
::X|EN|restore.point.013|                    no space for shadow storage - Checkpoint-Computer
::X|EN|restore.point.014|                    returns an error, OPTY prints 'Restore point not
::X|EN|restore.point.015|                    created' and continues. The rest of the run then has
::X|EN|restore.point.016|                    no restore point behind it.
::X|EN|restore.point.017|
::X|EN|restore.point.018|  Unverified      : OPTY writes 1440 back instead of deleting the value.
::X|EN|restore.point.019|                    The behaviour is identical to the absent default, but
::X|EN|restore.point.020|                    strictly this is not the shipped state and a later
::X|EN|restore.point.021|                    audit will read it as a deliberate setting. Also note
::X|EN|restore.point.022|                    this is the one place in OPTY that shells out to
::X|EN|restore.point.023|                    powershell.exe - the in-file comment justifies it as
::X|EN|restore.point.024|                    having no CMD equivalent since WMIC was removed; it is
::X|EN|restore.point.025|                    recorded here as a fact of the current implementation,
::X|EN|restore.point.026|                    not as a proposal.
::X|EN|restore.point.027|
::X|EN|restore.point.028|  Target          : :restore_point (OPTY.bat lines 3608-3632): Enable-
::X|EN|restore.point.029|                    ComputerRestore on %SystemDrive%, then
::X|EN|restore.point.030|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|restore.point.031|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|EN|restore.point.032|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency /t REG_DWORD /d 0,
::X|EN|restore.point.033|                    then Checkpoint-Computer -RestorePointType
::X|EN|restore.point.034|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS, then the frequency value written back
::X|EN|restore.point.035|                    as 1440
::
:: ---- helper.regset (diagnostic) -------------------------------------
::P|helper.regset|ALWAYS-WRITE|ALWAYS-WRITE|ALWAYS-WRITE|ALWAYS-WRITE|ALWAYS-WRITE|
::T|FR|helper.regset.001|COMMENT OPTY ECRIT UNE VALEUR DE REGISTRE
::T|FR|helper.regset.002|
::T|FR|helper.regset.003|  Ce que c est    : L'unique fonction d'ecriture generique derriere
::T|FR|helper.regset.004|                    environ 25 reglages du script : un reg add avec la
::T|FR|helper.regset.005|                    cle, le nom de valeur, le type et la donnee fournis
::T|FR|helper.regset.006|                    par l'appelant, entoure d'une lecture prealable qui
::T|FR|helper.regset.007|                    n'existe que pour que le journal puisse dire ce qui
::T|FR|helper.regset.008|                    s'est reellement passe.
::T|FR|helper.regset.009|
::T|FR|helper.regset.010|  Effet reel      : Elle lit d'abord la valeur actuelle, uniquement pour
::T|FR|helper.regset.011|                    que le journal puisse afficher SET (absente), written
::T|FR|helper.regset.012|                    (deja correcte) ou FIXED (c'etait X, c'est maintenant
::T|FR|helper.regset.013|                    Y) - puis elle execute le reg add a chaque fois, y
::T|FR|helper.regset.014|                    compris quand la valeur correspondait deja. Rien n'est
::T|FR|helper.regset.015|                    saute sous pretexte que « c'est deja bon ». La
::T|FR|helper.regset.016|                    comparaison numerique passe par set /a parce que reg
::T|FR|helper.regset.017|                    query affiche les DWORD en 0xNN : 0x2 et 2 sont donc
::T|FR|helper.regset.018|                    egaux au lieu de produire un FIXED fantome.
::T|FR|helper.regset.019|
::T|FR|helper.regset.020|  Gain            : La justesse sur des machines qui ne sont pas les
::T|FR|helper.regset.021|                    tiennes, et un rapport verifiable. Un script qui saute
::T|FR|helper.regset.022|                    les valeurs « deja correctes » fait confiance a une
::T|FR|helper.regset.023|                    lecture qui peut etre fausse ; et sa sortie ne
::T|FR|helper.regset.024|                    distingue pas une reparation d'un coup dans l'eau, ce
::T|FR|helper.regset.025|                    qui rend invérifiable tout ce qu'il affiche.
::T|FR|helper.regset.026|
::T|FR|helper.regset.027|  Cout            : Une ecriture de registre a lieu meme quand rien ne
::T|FR|helper.regset.028|                    change : quelques millisecondes et une transaction par
::T|FR|helper.regset.029|                    appel. Aucune annulation : l'ancienne valeur n'est
::T|FR|helper.regset.030|                    consignee que dans la ligne de journal, elle n'est
::T|FR|helper.regset.031|                    sauvegardee nulle part.
::T|FR|helper.regset.032|
::T|FR|helper.regset.033|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet : c'est une primitive generique. La bonne
::T|FR|helper.regset.034|                    valeur par defaut appartient a chaque reglage qui
::T|FR|helper.regset.035|                    l'utilise.
::T|FR|helper.regset.036|
::T|FR|helper.regset.037|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|helper.regset.038|    ALWAYS-WRITE         : Le comportement actuel, et le seul : lire
::T|FR|helper.regset.039|                           d'abord pour le journal, puis ecrire quoi
::T|FR|helper.regset.040|                           qu'ait dit la lecture. Sur une machine neuve,
::T|FR|helper.regset.041|                           constructeur ou inconnue, c'est l'ecriture qui
::T|FR|helper.regset.042|                           rend la valeur vraie, et une lecture annoncant
::T|FR|helper.regset.043|                           « deja correct » peut se tromper : elle peut
::T|FR|helper.regset.044|                           lire une autre ruche sous redirection, ou une
::T|FR|helper.regset.045|                           valeur masquee par une strategie.
::T|FR|helper.regset.046|    SKIP-IF-EQUAL (not implemented) :
::T|FR|helper.regset.047|        L'alternative que retiennent la plupart des scripts : comparer et
::T|FR|helper.regset.048|        sauter l'ecriture si la valeur correspond. On y gagne quelques
::T|FR|helper.regset.049|        millisecondes par appel sur 25 points d'appel - autant dire rien -
::T|FR|helper.regset.050|        et on fait confiance a une lecture qu'on ne peut pas verifier.
::T|FR|helper.regset.051|        Explicitement refusee par le mainteneur.
::X|FR|helper.regset.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Il n'y a aucune dimension « profil » ici, et en
::X|FR|helper.regset.002|                         inventer une serait malhonnete. C'est le
::X|FR|helper.regset.003|                         mecanisme par lequel chacune de tes reponses sur
::X|FR|helper.regset.004|                         le registre est appliquee, quel que soit le
::X|FR|helper.regset.005|                         chiffre tape. Bon a savoir pour une raison : «
::X|FR|helper.regset.006|                         written (was already correct) » dans le rapport
::X|FR|helper.regset.007|                         signifie que la valeur a ete lue comme correcte
::X|FR|helper.regset.008|                         ET ecrite quand meme, pas qu'OPTY a renonce a
::X|FR|helper.regset.009|                         agir.
::X|FR|helper.regset.010|
::X|FR|helper.regset.011|  Cible           : :regset (OPTY.bat lines 3273-3304): reads the current
::X|FR|helper.regset.012|                    value with reg query, then runs reg add "%~1" /v "%~2"
::X|FR|helper.regset.013|                    /t %~3 /d %~4 /f unconditionally, then logs SET (was
::X|FR|helper.regset.014|                    absent) / FIXED (was X, now Y) / written (was already
::X|FR|helper.regset.015|                    correct)
::T|EN|helper.regset.001|HOW OPTY WRITES A REGISTRY VALUE
::T|EN|helper.regset.002|
::T|EN|helper.regset.003|  What it is      : The single generic writer behind about 25 settings in
::T|EN|helper.regset.004|                    this script: reg add with the key, value name, type
::T|EN|helper.regset.005|                    and data the caller supplies, wrapped in a read-
::T|EN|helper.regset.006|                    before-write that exists only so the log can tell you
::T|EN|helper.regset.007|                    what actually happened.
::T|EN|helper.regset.008|
::T|EN|helper.regset.009|  Actual effect   : It reads the current value first, purely so the log
::T|EN|helper.regset.010|                    can say SET (was absent), written (was already
::T|EN|helper.regset.011|                    correct), or FIXED (was X, now Y) - and then it
::T|EN|helper.regset.012|                    performs the reg add every single time, including when
::T|EN|helper.regset.013|                    the value already matched. Nothing is skipped as
::T|EN|helper.regset.014|                    already correct. Numeric comparison goes through set
::T|EN|helper.regset.015|                    /a because reg query prints DWORDs as 0xNN, so 0x2 and
::T|EN|helper.regset.016|                    2 compare equal instead of showing a phantom FIXED.
::T|EN|helper.regset.017|
::T|EN|helper.regset.018|  Gain            : Correctness on machines that are not yours, and a
::T|EN|helper.regset.019|                    report you can check. A script that skips 'already
::T|EN|helper.regset.020|                    correct' values is trusting a read that can be wrong;
::T|EN|helper.regset.021|                    and its output cannot distinguish a repair from a no-
::T|EN|helper.regset.022|                    op, which makes every claim it prints unfalsifiable.
::T|EN|helper.regset.023|
::T|EN|helper.regset.024|  Cost            : A registry write happens even when nothing changes - a
::T|EN|helper.regset.025|                    few milliseconds and one registry transaction per
::T|EN|helper.regset.026|                    call. There is no undo: the previous value is recorded
::T|EN|helper.regset.027|                    in the log line only, not backed up anywhere.
::T|EN|helper.regset.028|
::T|EN|helper.regset.029|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is a generic primitive. The
::T|EN|helper.regset.030|                    correct default belongs to each individual setting
::T|EN|helper.regset.031|                    that uses it.
::T|EN|helper.regset.032|
::T|EN|helper.regset.033|  Possible values:
::T|EN|helper.regset.034|    ALWAYS-WRITE         : The current and only behaviour: read first for
::T|EN|helper.regset.035|                           the log, then write regardless of what the read
::T|EN|helper.regset.036|                           returned. On a fresh, OEM or foreign machine
::T|EN|helper.regset.037|                           the write is what makes the value true, and a
::T|EN|helper.regset.038|                           read that says 'already correct' can be wrong -
::T|EN|helper.regset.039|                           it can be reading a different hive under
::T|EN|helper.regset.040|                           redirection, or a value a policy overlay is
::T|EN|helper.regset.041|                           shadowing.
::T|EN|helper.regset.042|    SKIP-IF-EQUAL (not implemented) :
::T|EN|helper.regset.043|        The alternative most scripts use: compare, and skip the write when
::T|EN|helper.regset.044|        the value matches. It is faster by a few milliseconds per call
::T|EN|helper.regset.045|        across 25 call sites - which is nothing - and it makes the tool
::T|EN|helper.regset.046|        trust a read it has no way to verify. Explicitly rejected by the
::T|EN|helper.regset.047|        maintainer.
::X|EN|helper.regset.001|  Why these profiles : There is no profile dimension here and inventing
::X|EN|helper.regset.002|                       one would be dishonest. This is the mechanism by
::X|EN|helper.regset.003|                       which every registry answer you give in the script
::X|EN|helper.regset.004|                       gets applied, whichever digit you pressed. Worth
::X|EN|helper.regset.005|                       knowing for one reason: 'written (was already
::X|EN|helper.regset.006|                       correct)' in the report means the value read as
::X|EN|helper.regset.007|                       correct AND was written anyway - not that OPTY
::X|EN|helper.regset.008|                       declined to act.
::X|EN|helper.regset.009|
::X|EN|helper.regset.010|  Target          : :regset (OPTY.bat lines 3273-3304): reads the current
::X|EN|helper.regset.011|                    value with reg query, then runs reg add "%~1" /v "%~2"
::X|EN|helper.regset.012|                    /t %~3 /d %~4 /f unconditionally, then logs SET (was
::X|EN|helper.regset.013|                    absent) / FIXED (was X, now Y) / written (was already
::X|EN|helper.regset.014|                    correct)
::
:: ---- helper.killkey (repair) ------------------------------------
::P|helper.killkey|DELETE-IF-PRESENT|DELETE-IF-PRESENT|DELETE-IF-PRESENT|DELETE-IF-PRESENT|DELETE-IF-PRESENT|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.001|SUPPRIMER LES VALEURS DONT LE DEFAUT EST L'ABSENCE
::T|FR|helper.killkey.002|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : Pour toute une famille de reglages, le vrai defaut de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.004|                    Windows n'est pas 0 : c'est que la valeur n'existe pas
::T|FR|helper.killkey.005|                    du tout. Les valeurs de strategie sous
::T|FR|helper.killkey.006|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies, OverlayTestMode et les residus
::T|FR|helper.killkey.007|                    de strategie WindowsUpdate en sont les cas classiques.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.008|                    Cette fonction supprime la valeur si elle est la, et
::T|FR|helper.killkey.009|                    ne fait rien si elle n'y est pas.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.010|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.011|  Effet reel      : Elle verifie que la valeur existe, consigne la
::T|FR|helper.killkey.012|                    suppression avec son chemin complet, puis la supprime.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.013|                    Environ 40 points d'appel dans les chemins de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.014|                    reaffirmation des defauts, d'alimentation, de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.015|                    restauration gaming et de cartographie.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.016|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.017|  Gain            : C'est la seule facon de vraiment restaurer les
::T|FR|helper.killkey.018|                    reglages ou l'absence et 0 different : valeurs de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.019|                    strategie, OverlayTestMode, residus WindowsUpdate.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.020|                    Pour PowerThrottlingOff, 0 et absent se comportent
::T|FR|helper.killkey.021|                    pareil : la limitation est active et Windows classe
::T|FR|helper.killkey.022|                    chaque processus lui-meme. Il n'y a donc pas de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.023|                    troisieme etat, et l'affirmation precedente selon
::T|FR|helper.killkey.024|                    laquelle 0 « active la limitation pour tout le monde »
::T|FR|helper.killkey.025|                    etait fausse. La suppression reste le bon geste, parce
::T|FR|helper.killkey.026|                    qu'elle laisse le registre tel qu'il est livre au lieu
::T|FR|helper.killkey.027|                    d'y epingler une valeur que le systeme n'a jamais
::T|FR|helper.killkey.028|                    ecrite. Supprimer les residus de strategie
::T|FR|helper.killkey.029|                    WindowsUpdate est souvent ce qui debloque un PC
::T|FR|helper.killkey.030|                    personnel coince sur « Certains parametres sont geres
::T|FR|helper.killkey.031|                    par votre organisation ».
::T|FR|helper.killkey.032|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.033|  Cout            : La valeur supprimee et sa donnee ne sont consignees
::T|FR|helper.killkey.034|                    que dans la ligne de journal, sans sauvegarde. Si tu
::T|FR|helper.killkey.035|                    maintiens volontairement l'une d'elles - par exemple
::T|FR|helper.killkey.036|                    tu veux reellement la limitation de puissance
::T|FR|helper.killkey.037|                    desactivee sur un fixe - elle disparait et il faudra
::T|FR|helper.killkey.038|                    la remettre apres coup.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.039|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.040|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet en general, mais toute la raison d'etre de
::T|FR|helper.killkey.041|                    cette fonction est que pour ces valeurs precises, le
::T|FR|helper.killkey.042|                    defaut livre EST l'absence.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.043|
::T|FR|helper.killkey.044|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|helper.killkey.045|    DELETE-IF-PRESENT    : Le comportement : interroger, et si la valeur
::T|FR|helper.killkey.046|                           existe, la consigner et la supprimer. Si elle
::T|FR|helper.killkey.047|                           est absente, la fonction sort immediatement
::T|FR|helper.killkey.048|                           sans rien afficher : le rapport reste muet sur
::T|FR|helper.killkey.049|                           les machines jamais bidouillees.
::T|FR|helper.killkey.050|    WRITE-ZERO (not what this helper does) :
::T|FR|helper.killkey.051|        La mauvaise approche pour cette famille de valeurs, et la raison
::T|FR|helper.killkey.052|        d'etre de la fonction. Pour une valeur de strategie, 0 signifie
::T|FR|helper.killkey.053|        generalement « strategie configuree pour ne pas restreindre », ce
::T|FR|helper.killkey.054|        qui laisse le controle des Parametres grise sous la banniere «
::T|FR|helper.killkey.055|        organisation » : le symptome visible survit. Pour OverlayTestMode
::T|FR|helper.killkey.056|        et les residus WindowsUpdate, 0 et absent ne sont pas non plus la
::T|FR|helper.killkey.057|        meme chose.
::X|FR|helper.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Aucune dimension « profil » : les cinq entrees
::X|FR|helper.killkey.002|                         sont identiques parce que c'est un mecanisme, pas
::X|FR|helper.killkey.003|                         un reglage. Les valeurs qu'on lui donne a traiter
::X|FR|helper.killkey.004|                         sont decidees par chaque fiche ; le fait qu'elle
::X|FR|helper.killkey.005|                         supprime au lieu d'ecrire 0 n'est pas une
::X|FR|helper.killkey.006|                         preference, c'est la difference entre restaurer
::X|FR|helper.killkey.007|                         un defaut et inventer un etat nouveau.
::X|FR|helper.killkey.008|
::X|FR|helper.killkey.009|  Cible           : :killkey (OPTY.bat lines 3589-3597): reg query "%~1"
::X|FR|helper.killkey.010|                    /v "%~2" and return if absent, otherwise log the
::X|FR|helper.killkey.011|                    removal and run reg delete "%~1" /v "%~2" /f. Roughly
::X|FR|helper.killkey.012|                    40 call sites across :reassert_defaults,
::X|FR|helper.killkey.013|                    :gaming_restore, the power path and the mapping path
::T|EN|helper.killkey.001|DELETING VALUES WHOSE DEFAULT IS ABSENCE
::T|EN|helper.killkey.002|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.003|  What it is      : For a whole class of settings the true Windows default
::T|EN|helper.killkey.004|                    is not 0 - it is that the value does not exist at all.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.005|                    Policy values under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies,
::T|EN|helper.killkey.006|                    OverlayTestMode, and leftover WindowsUpdate policy
::T|EN|helper.killkey.007|                    values are the classic cases. This helper deletes the
::T|EN|helper.killkey.008|                    value if it is there and does nothing if it is not.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.009|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.010|  Actual effect   : Checks the value exists, logs the removal with its
::T|EN|helper.killkey.011|                    full path, then deletes it. Around 40 call sites
::T|EN|helper.killkey.012|                    across the re-assert-defaults, power, gaming-restore
::T|EN|helper.killkey.013|                    and mapping paths.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.014|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.015|  Gain            : It is the only way to genuinely restore the settings
::T|EN|helper.killkey.016|                    where absence and 0 differ - policy values,
::T|EN|helper.killkey.017|                    OverlayTestMode, WindowsUpdate leftovers. For
::T|EN|helper.killkey.018|                    PowerThrottlingOff, 0 and absent behave the same:
::T|EN|helper.killkey.019|                    throttling is enabled and Windows classifies each
::T|EN|helper.killkey.020|                    process on its own, so there is no third state and the
::T|EN|helper.killkey.021|                    earlier claim that 0 'turns throttling on for
::T|EN|helper.killkey.022|                    everything' was wrong. Deleting is still the right
::T|EN|helper.killkey.023|                    move there, because it leaves the registry as shipped
::T|EN|helper.killkey.024|                    instead of pinning a value the OS never wrote.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.025|                    Removing leftover WindowsUpdate policy values is often
::T|EN|helper.killkey.026|                    what unblocks a home PC stuck showing 'Some settings
::T|EN|helper.killkey.027|                    are managed by your organization'.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.028|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.029|  Cost            : The removed value and its data are recorded in the log
::T|EN|helper.killkey.030|                    line only, not backed up. If you deliberately maintain
::T|EN|helper.killkey.031|                    one of these - for instance you really do want power
::T|EN|helper.killkey.032|                    throttling off on a desktop - this takes it away and
::T|EN|helper.killkey.033|                    you would have to set it again after the run.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.034|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.035|  Windows default : Not applicable in general, but the entire reason this
::T|EN|helper.killkey.036|                    helper exists is that for these particular values the
::T|EN|helper.killkey.037|                    shipped default IS absence.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.038|
::T|EN|helper.killkey.039|  Possible values:
::T|EN|helper.killkey.040|    DELETE-IF-PRESENT    : The behaviour: query, and if the value exists,
::T|EN|helper.killkey.041|                           log it and delete it. If it is absent the
::T|EN|helper.killkey.042|                           helper returns immediately and prints nothing,
::T|EN|helper.killkey.043|                           so the report stays quiet on machines that were
::T|EN|helper.killkey.044|                           never tweaked.
::T|EN|helper.killkey.045|    WRITE-ZERO (not what this helper does) :
::T|EN|helper.killkey.046|        The wrong approach for this class of values, and the reason the
::T|EN|helper.killkey.047|        helper exists. For a policy value, 0 usually means 'policy
::T|EN|helper.killkey.048|        configured to not-restrict', which keeps the Settings control
::T|EN|helper.killkey.049|        greyed out under the organisation banner - the visible symptom
