::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.001|STORAGE SENSE: CLEAN TEMP FILES RULE
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.002|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.003|  What it is      : The single checkbox inside Storage Sense labelled
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.004|                    'Keep Windows running smoothly by automatically
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.005|                    cleaning up temporary system and app files'. Without
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.006|                    it, turning Storage Sense on does almost nothing
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.007|                    useful.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.008|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.009|  Actual effect   : Sets StoragePolicy value 04 to 1. Windows empties the
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.010|                    standard temp locations during its scheduled pass,
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.011|                    with its own age filters applied.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.012|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.013|  Gain            : This is the rule that actually reclaims space. On a
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.014|                    machine used daily it is typically a few hundred MB to
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.015|                    a couple of GB per month that would otherwise just
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.016|                    accumulate. It is space, not speed - no throughput or
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.017|                    latency changes.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.018|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.019|  Cost            : An application that parks working data in %TEMP% and
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.020|                    expects it to survive for weeks could lose it. That is
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.021|                    rare and badly-behaved software, and Storage Sense
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.022|                    applies age and in-use filters rather than emptying
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.023|                    the folder outright.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.024|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.025|  Windows default : 1 - the checkbox ships ticked on Windows 11, and 1 is
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.026|                    the same value the Settings UI itself writes, so this
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.027|                    is a re-assert of the shipped state and not a third
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.028|                    position.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.029|
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.031|    1                    : The temp-file rule is on. During its scheduled
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.032|                           pass Storage Sense empties the standard
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.033|                           temporary locations - %TEMP%, Windows\Temp, the
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.034|                           Delivery Optimization cache, thumbnail and
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.035|                           Windows Update cleanup targets - applying its
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.036|                           own age and in-use filters rather than deleting
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.037|                           blindly. This is the rule that actually
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.038|                           reclaims space; the master switch alone
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.039|                           reclaims almost nothing.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.040|    0                    : The temp-file rule is off. Storage Sense still
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.041|                           runs on its schedule but only handles the
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.042|                           Recycle Bin, Downloads and cloud-dehydration
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.043|                           rules, so on a machine with the Downloads rule
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.044|                           pinned off it does close to nothing. Turning
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.045|                           the master switch on with 04 at 0 is the
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.046|                           configuration that makes people say Storage
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.047|                           Sense is useless.
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.048|    DELETE               : Value absent. The Settings checkbox still reads
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.049|                           ticked and Windows behaves as if it were 1, so
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.050|                           removing it changes nothing observable. Writing
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.051|                           1 therefore reproduces the shipped behaviour
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.052|                           exactly, unlike the 0-versus-absent cases
::T|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.053|                           elsewhere in this script.
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers, which is the correct table
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.002|                       rather than a lazy one: this is the shipped Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.003|                       behaviour and the entire point of Storage Sense,
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.004|                       and there is no profile for which accumulating temp
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.005|                       files is desirable. A server benefits as much as a
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.006|                       gaming desktop, a laptop as much as an office PC,
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.007|                       and WINDOWS agrees because 1 is what Windows ships.
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.008|                       The only person who should say no is someone using
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.009|                       %TEMP% as a personal scratch folder.
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.010|
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.011|  Unverified      : Whether StoragePolicy value 04 physically exists on a
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.012|                    never-touched 25H2 install, or is only materialised
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.013|                    the first time the Settings page is opened, was not
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.014|                    verified. Behaviourally it makes no difference: absent
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.015|                    and 1 both mean the box is ticked.
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.016|
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.017|  Target          : HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Storage
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.018|                    Sense\Parameters\StoragePolicy /v "04" /t REG_DWORD -
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.019|                    OPTY.bat line 891, call :regset "%SSP%" "04" REG_DWORD
::X|EN|storagesense.tempfiles.020|                    1 "clean temp files"
