::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|RESTORE GPU PREEMPTION TO THE SHIPPED STATE
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|  What it is      : EnablePreemption under GraphicsDrivers\Scheduler, a
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                    value that appears on machines that have been through
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                    a GPU tuning script. Windows does not create this key.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value if it is there, leaving the GPU
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                    scheduler on the driver's own preemption behaviour.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|  Gain            : Returning the machine to the state a fresh install is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|                    in - nothing more, and it is worth being blunt about
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|                    that. This is not a Microsoft-documented value, and
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    there is no published measurement showing that setting
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    it to 0 gains frames or that deleting it costs any.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    The reason to delete is that absence is what Windows
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    ships, not that removal buys you something. If it did,
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    this card would say a number.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|  Cost            : None. Nothing on a normal machine reads this key
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                    deliberately.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|  Windows default : Absent.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|    DELETE               : The value is removed and the GPU scheduler is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|                           left on whatever preemption granularity the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|                           driver reports it can do - which is the only
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.028|                           state a fresh Windows install has ever been in,
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.029|                           because Windows does not create this key.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.030|    0                    : What tuning scripts write, with the claim that
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.031|                           it removes scheduling overhead. Preemption is
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.032|                           the mechanism that lets the GPU interrupt a
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.033|                           long draw or compute job mid-flight so
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.034|                           something else - the desktop, a higher-priority
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.035|                           queue, the watchdog's reset - can get in. If
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.036|                           the value does what those scripts claim, the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.037|                           trade is a stuttering desktop under GPU load
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.038|                           and a TDR reset with less to work with. I have
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.039|                           found no published measurement showing it gains
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.040|                           anything, so treat the claim as unsupported in
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.041|                           both directions and just delete it.
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.042|    1                    : Setting 1 is not the restore either, even
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.043|                           though it reads like 'preemption on'. Windows
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.044|                           ships this key absent, so 1 is a written
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.045|                           override that happens to agree with the
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.046|                           driver's own default - and it would keep
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.047|                           overriding it if a future driver wanted
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.048|                           something else. Deletion is the restore.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : All five delete, and there is genuinely no per-
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|                       profile argument to make: the value is script
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|                       residue on every kind of machine equally, and the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                       correct state is the one Windows ships. Inventing a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|                       reason for the server or the laptop to keep it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|                       would be inventing a difference out of nothing.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|                       Answer 5 does the same thing as answers 1 to 4
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                       here, which is exactly what you would expect from a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|                       card whose whole content is 'put it back how it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|                       came'.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|  Unverified      : The existing card asserted that without preemption a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    long shader cannot be interrupted, the desktop
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    stutters and TDR recovery has nothing to work with.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    That is the mechanism preemption provides in general,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    but I have not verified that EnablePreemption=0 on a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    current WDDM 3.x driver actually disables it - the key
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                    is undocumented and drivers may ignore it. I have
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|                    therefore stated it conditionally rather than as fact.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|                    The deletion itself is correct regardless, since
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|                    absence is the shipped state.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|  Target          : call :killkey HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Gr
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|                    aphicsDrivers\Scheduler EnablePreemption - OPTY.bat
