::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.001|DISABLE MULTI-PLANE OVERLAY (FLICKER WORKAROUND)
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.002|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.003|  What it is      : The last-resort switch for multi-monitor flicker and
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.004|                    black flashing: it forces every window through the
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.005|                    compositor instead of letting the display controller
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.006|                    scan a window out of its own hardware plane.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.007|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.008|  Actual effect   : Writes OverlayTestMode=5, OverlayMinFPS=0 and
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.009|                    DisableOverlays=1. Reboot required.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.010|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.011|  Gain            : Sometimes, on some driver versions, it stops black
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.012|                    flashes or flicker on a multi-monitor setup with
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.013|                    mismatched refresh rates. Often it does not, and there
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.014|                    is no way to tell from the registry whether MPO is
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.015|                    your cause - the only test is set it, reboot, watch.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.016|                    It is not a performance tweak in any sense: it removes
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.017|                    a hardware fast path, it does not add one. Anyone
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.018|                    presenting this as an FPS setting has it backwards.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.019|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.020|  Cost            : Every windowed frame goes back through a full DWM
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.021|                    composite pass, so higher GPU power draw and one more
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.022|                    stage of latency in windowed and borderless play. VRR
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.023|                    in borderless stops working, and the presentation-path
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.024|                    features layered on top - Optimized Windowed Mode,
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.025|                    Auto HDR for windowed games - are widely reported to
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.026|                    go with it.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.027|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.028|  Windows default : All three absent, MPO on.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.029|
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.031|    SKIP                 : Leaves the three values exactly as they are. On
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.032|                           a normal machine they are already absent and
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.033|                           nothing happens; on a machine where somebody
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.034|                           applied this workaround, SKIP keeps it applied
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.035|                           - which is the point, because the person who
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.036|                           applied it may still need it.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.037|    DELETE               : Removes all three, which is the actual Windows
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.038|                           default. Not 0 - absence. Writing zeros here
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.039|                           would leave a written override that reads as
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.040|                           zero, which is a third state neither Windows
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.041|                           nor the workaround ever produces.
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.042|    OverlayTestMode=5 (+ OverlayMinFPS=0, DisableOverlays=1) :
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.043|        The workaround itself. DWM stops offering hardware planes to the
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.044|        driver, so every windowed frame is blended into the desktop bitmap
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.045|        by the compositor before it reaches the panel. Of the three, only
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.046|        OverlayTestMode=5 is the value Microsoft and the GPU vendors
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.047|        actually published; the other two ride along from copy-pasted
::T|EN|dt.mpo.off.048|        scripts and their effect is undocumented. Reboot required.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.001|  Why these profiles : Four skips and one delete, which is the honest
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.002|                       shape for a symptom-driven workaround rather than a
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.003|                       preference. No profile picks this: there is no kind
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.004|                       of machine that is better off with the hardware
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.005|                       path switched off, only a specific machine with a
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.006|                       specific flicker on a specific driver. So 1 to 4
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.007|                       leave it alone, and 5 deletes the values, because
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.008|                       the Windows default here is absence and not zero.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.009|                       If you want it, choose it by hand from the display
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.010|                       menu after you have already tried a different
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.011|                       cable, DisplayPort instead of HDMI, a clean driver
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.012|                       install, and matching the refresh rates - those fix
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.013|                       this more often than the registry does.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.014|
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.015|  Known problems  : Applying it is what causes the breakage, and it is
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.016|                    concrete: borderless windowed games lose variable
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.017|                    refresh rate and sit at the desktop refresh rate.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.018|                    Users then blame the monitor or the cable. If VRR
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.019|                    stopped working in borderless on a machine that has
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.020|                    been optimised by someone else, this is the first
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.021|                    value to look for.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.022|
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.023|  Unverified      : The exact feature list lost when MPO is disabled
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.024|                    varies by driver, and the Auto HDR and Optimized
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.025|                    Windowed Mode dependencies are widely reported rather
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.026|                    than verified here. The VRR-in-borderless loss is the
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.027|                    one I would state without hedging. Also: only
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.028|                    OverlayTestMode=5 is a published value; OverlayMinFPS
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.029|                    and DisableOverlays are carried along by convention
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.030|                    and OPTY writes them because removal scripts expect
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.031|                    all three, not because their effect is known.
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.032|
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.033|  Target          : reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm /v
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.034|                    OverlayTestMode REG_DWORD 5 ; same key /v
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.035|                    OverlayMinFPS REG_DWORD 0 ; reg add
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.036|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.037|                    /v DisableOverlays REG_DWORD 1 - OPTY.bat label
::X|EN|dt.mpo.off.038|                    :dt_mpo_off, lines 2062-2064
