::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.001|TURN OFF XBOX GAME DVR CAPTURE
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.002|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.003|  What it is      : Two per-user values that together decide whether the
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.004|                    Game Bar capture stack arms itself when a game or
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.005|                    full-screen app starts: GameDVR_Enabled under
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.006|                    HKCU\System\GameConfigStore and AppCaptureEnabled
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.007|                    under HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\G
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.008|                    ameDVR.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.009|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.010|  Actual effect   : Writes 0 to both, for the account running OPTY only.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.011|                    Other accounts on the machine are untouched, and the
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.012|                    machine policy that would grey out the Settings page
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.013|                    is deliberately not written.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.014|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.015|  Gain            : Close to nothing, and the popular version of this
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.016|                    tweak is folklore. Continuous background recording -
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.017|                    'Record what happened' - is a third switch,
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.018|                    HistoricalCaptureEnabled in the same GameDVR key, and
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.019|                    it ships at 0. On a machine where nobody turned that
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.020|                    on, nothing was recording in the background, so
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.021|                    turning Game DVR off frees no CPU, no GPU and no disk.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.022|                    What it genuinely does is stop the capture stack
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.023|                    registering itself when a game starts: one fewer
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.024|                    component in the way at launch, with no number
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.025|                    attached. Any guide claiming double-digit FPS from
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.026|                    this is wrong.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.027|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.028|  Cost            : WIN+ALT+R stops recording, the clip-the-
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.029|                    last-30-seconds button does nothing, and the Captures
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.030|                    page in Settings goes inert. Game Bar, WIN+G, the FPS
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.031|                    overlay and the widgets all keep working.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.032|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.033|  Windows default : Both 1.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.034|
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.035|  Possible values:
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.036|    0                    : Both per-user values set to 0. The Game Bar
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.037|                           capture stack does not register itself when a
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.038|                           game or full-screen app starts, WIN+ALT+R does
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.039|                           nothing and the Captures page in Settings goes
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.040|                           inert. Game Bar itself, WIN+G, its FPS counter
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.041|                           and its widgets are a different component and
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.042|                           keep working.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.043|    1                    : Both back to 1: capture is available again.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.044|                           Available, not running - see the gain field.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.045|                           This is the shipped state.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.046|    DELETE               : Behaviourally the same as 1 - Windows treats
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.047|                           absence as enabled and recreates
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.048|                           GameDVR_Enabled at the next sign-in. OPTY
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.049|                           writes 1 explicitly instead, because on a
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.050|                           foreign machine the write is what makes the
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.051|                           state true.
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.052|    AllowGameDVR policy = 0 (NOT written) :
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.053|        The machine policy under
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.054|        HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\GameDVR, which older
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.055|        versions of this script used to write. It is deliberately avoided:
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.056|        Windows' own GPBlockingRegKeyPath names that exact key as the
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.057|        reason Settings > Gaming > Captures greys out behind a 'Some
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.058|        settings are managed by your organization' banner on a personal
::T|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.059|        PC. The two HKCU values do the job without it.
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.001|  Why these profiles : 0 on gaming and server, 1 on the other three - and
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.002|                       the reason is explicitly not performance, because
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.003|                       there is none to speak of. Gaming: people who care
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.004|                       about capture use OBS, ShadowPlay or ReLive anyway,
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.005|                       and fewer things registering against a fullscreen
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.006|                       swapchain at launch is a preference worth honouring
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.007|                       even without a benchmark behind it. Server: a
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.008|                       headless box has nobody at the keyboard to press
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.009|                       WIN+ALT+R, so the whole feature is dead weight.
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.010|                       Office is a family or work machine where features
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.011|                       should be on and somebody will eventually want a
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.012|                       clip. Laptop keeps 1 as well - there is no
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.013|                       measurable battery gain, and inventing one to fill
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.014|                       the cell would be dishonest. Answer 5 writes 1
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.015|                       back.
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.016|
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.017|  Unverified      : I am confident that background recording is a separate
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.018|                    switch that ships off, and reasonably confident the
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.019|                    value is named HistoricalCaptureEnabled under the same
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.020|                    HKCU GameDVR key - the name should be checked on the
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.021|                    live machine before the wording ships, because the
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.022|                    whole honesty argument of this card rests on it. Also
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.023|                    unverified: the folklore pairing of GameDVR_Enabled=0
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.024|                    with GameDVR_FSEBehaviorMode to influence fullscreen
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.025|                    optimisations. OPTY does not write FSEBehaviorMode and
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.026|                    this card makes no claim about it.
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.027|
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.028|  Target          : reg add HKEY_CURRENT_USER\System\GameConfigStore /v
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.029|                    GameDVR_Enabled /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f and reg add HKEY_
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.030|                    CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion
::X|EN|mo.gamedvr.off.031|                    \GameDVR /v AppCaptureEnabled /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f -
