:: ---- mouse.apply.live (diagnostic) ----------------------------------
::P|mouse.apply.live|RUN|SKIP|RUN|RUN|RUN|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.001|APPLIQUER LES VALEURS DE SOURIS À LA SESSION EN COURS
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.002|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.003|  Ce que c est    : Un appel à rundll32
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.004|                    user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters. Il demande à
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.005|                    la session courante de relire les paramètres système
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.006|                    de l'utilisateur depuis le registre. C'est une action
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.007|                    et non un rapport, mais elle n'écrit et ne stocke
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.008|                    rien.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.009|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.010|  Effet reel      : Sans lui, les quatre valeurs Mouse dorment dans HKCU
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.011|                    et ne changent rien jusqu'à la prochaine fermeture
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.012|                    puis réouverture de session. Avec lui, le pointeur
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.013|                    change sous votre main pendant que le script est
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.014|                    encore ouvert.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.015|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.016|  Gain            : Vous jugez le changement maintenant plutôt que demain
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.017|                    — la différence entre un réglage que vous pouvez
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.018|                    réellement évaluer et un réglage que vous oublierez
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.019|                    avoir fait. Il n'y a ici aucune dimension de
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.020|                    performance.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.021|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.022|  Cout            : Rien de persistant. Certains environnements
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.023|                    redessinent brièvement le bureau — le clignotement de
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.024|                    fond d'écran que les gens rapportent, c'est cet appel
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.025|                    — et c'est fini instantanément.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.026|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.027|  Defaut Windows  : Ce n'est pas un réglage. Il n'y a pas de valeur
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.028|                    d'usine puisque rien ne change sur le disque.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.029|
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.031|    RUN                  : Exécute l'appel rundll32. La session relit les
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.032|                           paramètres système de l'utilisateur depuis HKCU
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.033|                           : les valeurs de souris écrites à l'instant
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.034|                           prennent effet immédiatement. Rien n'est écrit
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.035|                           sur le disque et il n'y a aucun état à annuler.
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.036|    SKIP                 : Les valeurs du registre changent quand même :
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.037|                           elles ne prennent simplement effet qu'à la
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.038|                           prochaine fermeture puis réouverture de
::T|FR|mouse.apply.live.039|                           session. Rien n'est perdu, seulement différé.
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : À exécuter partout où des valeurs de souris ont
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.002|                         réellement été écrites, soit quatre profils sur
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.003|                         cinq — le tableau est presque uniforme et il n'y
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.004|                         a pas lieu de faire semblant du contraire. Le 2
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.005|                         serveur est SKIP pour une seule raison : il n'a
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.006|                         rien écrit, il n'y a donc rien à appliquer. Le 5
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.007|                         Windows l'exécute quand même, car « la valeur
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.008|                         d'usine » n'a aucun sens pour un appel d'API, et
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.009|                         les valeurs d'origine qu'il vient d'écrire
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.010|                         méritent elles aussi d'être actives tout de
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.011|                         suite.
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.012|
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.013|  Non verifie (en)  : This call is normally documented with arguments —
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.014|                      rundll32.exe
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.015|                      user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters 1, True.
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.016|                      The argument-less form used here is the one that
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.017|                      circulates widely, and the visible desktop redraw
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.018|                      shows it does something, but I did not verify that
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.019|                      it re-reads exactly the same set of parameters as
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.020|                      the documented form. If a mouse change ever fails to
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.021|                      appear live, this is the first thing to test.
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.022|
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.023|  Cible           : rundll32.exe user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.024|                    — the :mouseapply subroutine, OPTY.bat lines
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.025|                    2364-2367, called from :mouse_apply, :mouse_restore
::X|FR|mouse.apply.live.026|                    and the global restore block.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.001|APPLY THE MOUSE VALUES TO THE RUNNING SESSION
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.002|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.003|  What it is      : A call to rundll32
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.004|                    user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters. It asks the
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.005|                    current session to re-read the per-user system
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.006|                    parameters out of the registry. It is an action rather
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.007|                    than a report, but it writes and stores nothing.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.008|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.009|  Actual effect   : Without it, the four Mouse values sit in HKCU and
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.010|                    change nothing until you sign out and back in. With
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.011|                    it, the pointer changes under your hand while the
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.012|                    script is still open.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.013|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.014|  Gain            : You judge the change now instead of tomorrow — the
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.015|                    difference between a setting you can actually evaluate
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.016|                    and one you forget you made. There is no performance
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.017|                    dimension to this at all.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.018|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.019|  Cost            : Nothing that persists. Some shells briefly redraw the
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.020|                    desktop — the wallpaper flicker people report is this
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.021|                    call — and it is over in an instant.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.022|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.023|  Windows default : Not a setting. There is no shipped default because
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.024|                    nothing on disk changes.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.025|
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.027|    RUN                  : Executes the rundll32 call. The session re-
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.028|                           reads the per-user system parameters from HKCU,
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.029|                           so the mouse values written a moment earlier
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.030|                           take effect at once. Nothing is written to disk
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.031|                           and there is no state to undo.
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.032|    SKIP                 : The registry values still change; they simply
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.033|                           do not take effect until the next sign-out and
::T|EN|mouse.apply.live.034|                           sign-in. Nothing is lost, only delayed.
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.001|  Why these profiles : Run it wherever mouse values were actually written,
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.002|                       which is four profiles out of five — the table is
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.003|                       near-uniform and there is no reason to pretend
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.004|                       otherwise. 2 server is SKIP for exactly one reason:
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.005|                       it wrote nothing, so there is nothing to apply. 5
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.006|                       Windows still runs it, because 'the shipped
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.007|                       default' has no meaning for an API call and the
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.008|                       stock values it just wrote deserve to be live too.
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.009|
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.010|  Unverified      : This call is normally documented with arguments —
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.011|                    rundll32.exe user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.012|                    1, True. The argument-less form used here is the one
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.013|                    that circulates widely, and the visible desktop redraw
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.014|                    shows it does something, but I did not verify that it
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.015|                    re-reads exactly the same set of parameters as the
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.016|                    documented form. If a mouse change ever fails to
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.017|                    appear live, this is the first thing to test.
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.018|
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.019|  Target          : rundll32.exe user32.dll,UpdatePerUserSystemParameters
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.020|                    — the :mouseapply subroutine, OPTY.bat lines
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.021|                    2364-2367, called from :mouse_apply, :mouse_restore
::X|EN|mouse.apply.live.022|                    and the global restore block.
