:: ---- net.nti.setup.write10 (repair) -----------------------------
::P|net.nti.setup.write10|10|10|10|10|10|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.001|NETWORKTHROTTLINGINDEX = 10 DANS LA PASSE DE CONFIGURATION
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.002|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.003|  Ce que c est    : MMCSS limite le nombre de paquets reseau traites par
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.004|                    milliseconde en dehors d'un thread multimedia, pour
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.005|                    qu'un trafic intense n'affame pas la lecture audio et
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.006|                    video. La valeur d'origine est 10. Un tweak tres
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.007|                    repandu met 0xFFFFFFFF pour supprimer completement la
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.008|                    limite.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.009|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 10 sans condition, sans lecture prealable. Sur
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.011|                    une machine intacte, cela ne change rien : l'objectif
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.012|                    est d'annuler le tweak 0xFFFFFFFF si un outil
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.013|                    precedent l'a applique. Une mesure en direct sur cette
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.014|                    machine a justement trouve ce residu.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.015|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.016|  Gain            : Aucun sur une machine propre, et cette fiche ne
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.017|                    pretendra pas le contraire. L'interet est l'annulation
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.018|                    : sans plafond, le travail d'interruption et de DPC
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.019|                    reseau peut voler du temps au thread audio que MMCSS
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.020|                    existe pour proteger, ce qui s'entend en craquements
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.021|                    ou coupures pendant un trafic soutenu.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.022|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.023|  Cout            : Rien sur une machine d'origine. Si vous aviez leve le
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.024|                    plafond volontairement pour une ligne rapide, il
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.025|                    revient et vos telechargements retrouvent un plafond
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.026|                    souple d'environ 120 Mbit/s pendant la lecture audio.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.027|                    Demandez-le via le profil reseau, pas ici.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.028|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.029|  Defaut Windows  : 10 (0xa).
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.030|
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.032|    10 (0xa)             : Valeur d'origine. Tant qu'un flux multimedia
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.033|                           MMCSS est enregistre sur la machine, le
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.034|                           traitement reseau non multimedia est plafonne a
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.035|                           10 paquets par milliseconde. Sans lecture en
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.036|                           cours, le plafond ne fait rien du tout.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.037|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.038|        Le plafond est totalement supprime. Le travail reseau en DPC peut
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.039|        alors prendre autant de temps CPU qu'il veut pendant la lecture
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.040|        audio : c'est ce que fait le tweak repandu, et c'est pour cela
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.041|        qu'il provoque des craquements.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.042|    any other N          : Le plafond passe a N paquets par milliseconde.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.043|                           En dessous de 10 il se resserre, au-dessus il
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.044|                           se relache sans supprimer la protection.
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.045|    value absent         : Ce n'est pas l'etat livre : Windows livre la
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.046|                           valeur presente a 10. OPTY ecrit 10 au lieu de
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.047|                           supprimer, justement parce que le defaut ici
::T|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.048|                           est une valeur et non une absence.
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a 10. C'est la passe
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.002|                         d'entretien, et une passe d'entretien ne doit pas
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.003|                         lever une protection audio dans votre dos - meme
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.004|                         pas sur le profil serveur, la seule machine qui
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.005|                         puisse vraiment vouloir s'en passer. Ce choix
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.006|                         appartient au profil reseau Debit, qui s'execute
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.007|                         plus tard et ecrase cette valeur si vous le
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.008|                         demandez explicitement.
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.009|
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.010|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether MMCSS falls back to the same 10 packets per
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.011|                      millisecond when the value is deleted rather than
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.012|                      set was not tested. OPTY writes 10 instead of
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.013|                      deleting because 10 is the documented shipped value
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.014|                      and the value ships present.
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.015|
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.016|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.017|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.018|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex /t REG_DWORD /d 10 /f - the
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.019|                    unconditional write in the setup pass (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.020|                    ~1549), and the same value through :regset in
::X|FR|net.nti.setup.write10.021|                    :reassert_defaults (~1816)
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.001|NETWORKTHROTTLINGINDEX = 10 IN THE SETUP PASS
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.002|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.003|  What it is      : MMCSS limits how many network packets are processed
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.004|                    per millisecond outside a multimedia thread, so heavy
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.005|                    network traffic cannot starve audio and video
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.006|                    playback. The shipped value is 10. A very popular
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.007|                    tweak sets 0xFFFFFFFF to remove the limit entirely.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.008|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.009|  Actual effect   : Writes 10 unconditionally, without reading first. On
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.010|                    an untouched machine this changes nothing; the point
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.011|                    is to undo the 0xFFFFFFFF tweak if a previous tool
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.012|                    applied it. Live measurement on this machine found
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.013|                    exactly that leftover.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.014|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.015|  Gain            : None on a clean machine, and this card will not
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.016|                    pretend otherwise. What it buys is the undo: with the
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.017|                    cap removed, network interrupt and DPC work can steal
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.018|                    time from the audio thread MMCSS exists to protect,
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.019|                    heard as crackle or dropouts during heavy traffic.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.020|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.021|  Cost            : Nothing on a stock machine. If you had lifted the cap
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.022|                    on purpose for a fast link, this puts it back and your
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.023|                    downloads meet a soft ceiling of roughly 120 Mbit/s
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.024|                    again while audio plays. Ask for that through the
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.025|                    network profile instead of here.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.026|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.027|  Windows default : 10 (0xa).
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.028|
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.029|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.030|    10 (0xa)             : Shipped value. While an MMCSS multimedia stream
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.031|                           is registered on this machine, non-multimedia
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.032|                           network processing is capped at 10 packets per
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.033|                           millisecond. With nothing playing, the cap does
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.034|                           nothing at all.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.035|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.036|        The cap is removed entirely. Network DPC work can then take as
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.037|        much CPU time as it wants while audio plays, which is what the
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.038|        popular tweak does and why it causes crackle.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.039|    any other N          : The cap becomes N packets per millisecond.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.040|                           Below 10 it tightens, above 10 it loosens
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.041|                           without removing the protection.
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.042|    value absent         : Not the shipped state - Windows ships the value
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.043|                           present at 10. OPTY writes 10 rather than
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.044|                           deleting, precisely because the default here is
::T|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.045|                           a value, not an absence.
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns at 10. This is the
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.002|                       maintenance pass, and a maintenance pass must not
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.003|                       lift an audio protection behind your back - not
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.004|                       even on the server profile, which is the one
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.005|                       machine that may genuinely want the cap gone. That
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.006|                       choice belongs to the network throughput profile,
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.007|                       which runs later and overwrites this value when you
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.008|                       pick it deliberately.
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.009|
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.010|  Unverified      : Whether MMCSS falls back to the same 10 packets per
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.011|                    millisecond when the value is deleted rather than set
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.012|                    was not tested. OPTY writes 10 instead of deleting
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.013|                    because 10 is the documented shipped value and the
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.014|                    value ships present.
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.015|
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.016|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.017|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.018|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex /t REG_DWORD /d 10 /f - the
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.019|                    unconditional write in the setup pass (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.020|                    ~1549), and the same value through :regset in
::X|EN|net.nti.setup.write10.021|                    :reassert_defaults (~1816)
::
:: ---- net.nagle.setup.del (repair) -------------------------------
::P|net.nagle.setup.del|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.001|SUPPRIMER LE TWEAK DESACTIVER NAGLE
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.002|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.003|  Ce que c est    : TcpAckFrequency et TCPNoDelay sont des valeurs par
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.004|                    carte reseau que dix ans de guides gaming ont
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.005|                    conseille d'ajouter pour desactiver l'algorithme de
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.006|                    Nagle et baisser le ping. Windows est livre sans
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.007|                    aucune des deux.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.008|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.009|  Effet reel      : Les deux valeurs sont supprimees de chaque GUID
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.010|                    d'interface sous Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces, ce qui
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.011|                    retablit la configuration d'origine. Effet immediat,
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.012|                    sans redemarrage.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.013|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.014|  Gain            : Retablit le comportement TCP par defaut. Soyons clairs
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.015|                    sur ce que valait le tweak au depart : le trafic des
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.016|                    jeux competitifs est presque entierement en UDP, que
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.017|                    ni Nagle ni l'ACK retarde ne touchent - il ne pouvait
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.018|                    donc pas baisser votre ping. En revanche, il doublait
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.019|                    bel et bien le nombre d'ACK et reduisait le debit
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.020|                    maximal en telechargement.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.021|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.022|  Cout            : Rien de mesurable. Si vous avez une application TCP
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.023|                    reellement sensible a la latence et que vous les aviez
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.024|                    ajoutees sciemment, il faudra les remettre a la main,
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.025|                    GUID par GUID.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.026|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.027|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs absentes sur toutes les interfaces.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.028|
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.030|    TcpAckFrequency absent :
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.031|        Etat livre. Windows utilise l'ACK retarde : un accuse est envoye
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.032|        apres le deuxieme segment plein ou au bout de 200 ms au plus, le
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.033|        premier des deux.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.034|    TcpAckFrequency=1    : Un accuse est envoye pour chaque segment recu.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.035|                           Le volume d'ACK en emission double, ce qui sur
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.036|                           une ligne asymetrique grignote l'envoi dont vos
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.037|                           telechargements dependent.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.038|    TcpAckFrequency=2 to 13 :
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.039|        Un accuse tous les N segments. Au-dessus du defaut, cela retarde
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.040|        le retour vers l'emetteur et peut ralentir la montee de la fenetre
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.041|        de congestion.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.042|    TCPNoDelay absent    : Etat livre. L'algorithme de Nagle regroupe les
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.043|                           petites ecritures pour les sockets qui n'ont
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.044|                           pas demande TCP_NODELAY elles-memes.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.045|    TCPNoDelay=1         : Nagle est desactive pour cette interface, pour
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.046|                           toute la machine : chaque petite ecriture part
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.047|                           dans son propre segment, avec ses 40 octets
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.048|                           d'en-tete.
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.049|    TCPNoDelay=0         : Une activation explicite. C'est un troisieme
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.050|                           etat, pas l'etat livre : c'est pourquoi OPTY
::T|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.051|                           supprime au lieu d'ecrire 0.
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a DELETE, et DELETE
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.002|                         plutot que 0 dans chacune, parce que le defaut
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.003|                         Windows est l'absence de la valeur : ecrire 0
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.004|                         serait un troisieme etat, pas une restauration.
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.005|                         Aucun profil n'y gagne : une application qui a
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.006|                         vraiment besoin de couper Nagle pose TCP_NODELAY
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.007|                         sur sa propre socket, sans qu'aucune valeur de
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.008|                         registre soit necessaire.
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.009|
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.010|  Cible           : for each key under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Servi
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.011|                    ces\Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces\{GUID}: reg delete /v
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.012|                    TcpAckFrequency /f and reg delete /v TCPNoDelay /f
::X|FR|net.nagle.setup.del.013|                    (OPTY.bat ~1552-1555)
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.001|REMOVE THE DISABLE-NAGLE TWEAK
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.002|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.003|  What it is      : TcpAckFrequency and TCPNoDelay are per-adapter values
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.004|                    that a decade of gaming guides told people to add in
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.005|                    order to disable Nagle's algorithm and reduce ping.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.006|                    Windows ships with neither present.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.007|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.008|  Actual effect   : Both values are deleted from every interface GUID
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.009|                    under Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces, restoring the
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.010|                    shipped configuration. Immediate, no reboot.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.011|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.012|  Gain            : Restores default TCP behaviour. Be clear about what
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.013|                    the tweak was worth in the first place: competitive
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.014|                    game traffic is almost entirely UDP, which neither
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.015|                    Nagle nor delayed ACK touches at all, so it could not
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.016|                    have been lowering your ping. What it did do was
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.017|                    double your ACK count and reduce maximum download
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.018|                    throughput.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.019|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.020|  Cost            : Nothing measurable. If you have a genuinely latency-
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.021|                    sensitive TCP application and knowingly added these,
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.022|                    you would re-add them per adapter GUID by hand.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.023|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.024|  Windows default : Both values absent on every interface.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.025|
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.027|    TcpAckFrequency absent :
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.028|        Shipped state. Windows uses delayed ACK: an acknowledgement is
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.029|        sent after the second full-size segment or after up to 200 ms,
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.030|        whichever comes first.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.031|    TcpAckFrequency=1    : An acknowledgement is sent for every single
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.032|                           received segment. That doubles the ACK volume
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.033|                           going upstream, which on an asymmetric line
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.034|                           eats the upload your downloads depend on.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.035|    TcpAckFrequency=2 to 13 :
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.036|        An acknowledgement after N segments. Above the default it delays
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.037|        feedback to the sender and can slow the congestion window climb.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.038|    TCPNoDelay absent    : Shipped state. Nagle's algorithm coalesces
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.039|                           small writes for sockets that did not opt out
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.040|                           with TCP_NODELAY themselves.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.041|    TCPNoDelay=1         : Nagle is disabled for that interface, machine-
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.042|                           wide: every small write leaves as its own
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.043|                           segment, with its own 40 bytes of header.
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.044|    TCPNoDelay=0         : An explicit enable. This is a third state, not
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.045|                           the shipped one - which is why OPTY deletes
::T|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.046|                           rather than writing 0.
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, and DELETE rather
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.002|                       than 0 in every one, because the Windows default is
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.003|                       the value not existing - writing 0 would be a third
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.004|                       state, not a restoration. No usage profile
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.005|                       benefits: an application that really needs Nagle
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.006|                       off sets TCP_NODELAY on its own socket, which no
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.007|                       registry value is needed for.
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.008|
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.009|  Target          : for each key under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Servi
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.010|                    ces\Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces\{GUID}: reg delete /v
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.011|                    TcpAckFrequency /f and reg delete /v TCPNoDelay /f
::X|EN|net.nagle.setup.del.012|                    (OPTY.bat ~1552-1555)
::
:: ---- net.nti.restore.write10 (repair) ---------------------------
::P|net.nti.restore.write10|10|10|10|10|10|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.001|NETWORKTHROTTLINGINDEX REMIS A 10
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.002|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.003|  Ce que c est    : Le plafond reseau de MMCSS, sur le chemin de
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.004|                    restauration. Il limite le traitement reseau non
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.005|                    multimedia a 10 paquets par milliseconde pendant une
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.006|                    lecture, pour que les threads audio ne soient pas
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.007|                    etouffes par le travail d'interruption reseau.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.008|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 10. C'est un simple reg add, sans condition :
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.010|                    sur une machine neuve il confirme au lieu de changer
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.011|                    quoi que ce soit.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.012|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.013|  Gain            : Aucun si la valeur est deja a 10. Ce qu'il apporte,
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.014|                    c'est la disparition d'un risque de coupure audio
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.015|                    introduit par un tweak precedent qui avait mis
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.016|                    0xFFFFFFFF.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.017|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.018|  Cout            : Rien sur une ligne lente. Mais soyons precis sur ce
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.019|                    qu'est ce plafond : environ 10000 paquets par seconde,
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.020|                    soit a peu pres 120 Mbit/s avec des trames pleines, et
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.021|                    uniquement pendant qu'un flux multimedia joue. Sur une
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.022|                    fibre ou un cable, c'est un vrai plafond souple sur
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.023|                    les telechargements en cours de lecture - et c'est
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.024|                    exactement pour cela que le profil Debit propose de le
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.025|                    lever. En dessous d'environ 100 Mbit/s, vous ne
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.026|                    l'atteindrez jamais.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.027|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.028|  Defaut Windows  : 10 (0xa).
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.029|
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.031|    10 (0xa)             : Valeur d'origine. Plafonne le traitement reseau
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.032|                           non multimedia a 10 paquets par milliseconde,
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.033|                           soit environ 10000 paquets par seconde, tant
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.034|                           qu'un flux multimedia MMCSS est enregistre.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.035|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.036|        Plafond supprime. C'est ce qu'ecrivent les guides de tweak, et ce
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.037|        que le chemin de restauration est la pour annuler.
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.038|    any other N          : Plafond fixe a N paquets par milliseconde, ni
::T|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.039|                           la protection d'origine ni sa suppression.
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a 10. C'est le chemin de
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.002|                         restauration : son role entier est de remettre la
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.003|                         valeur Windows, donc tous les profils recoivent
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.004|                         10, y compris le serveur, qui est pourtant celui
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.005|                         qui pourrait vouloir lever le plafond. Une
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.006|                         machine dans ce cas le demande via le profil
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.007|                         reseau Debit, pas dans une etape d'annulation.
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.008|
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.009|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.010|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.011|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex /t REG_DWORD /d 10 /f in the
::X|FR|net.nti.restore.write10.012|                    restore path (OPTY.bat ~1628)
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.001|NETWORKTHROTTLINGINDEX BACK TO 10
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.002|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.003|  What it is      : The MMCSS network cap, on the restore path. It limits
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.004|                    non-multimedia network processing to 10 packets per
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.005|                    millisecond while media is playing, so audio threads
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.006|                    are not starved by network interrupt work.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.007|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.008|  Actual effect   : Writes 10. It is a plain reg add, unconditional, so on
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.009|                    a fresh machine it confirms rather than changes.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.010|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.011|  Gain            : None if the value is already 10. What it buys is the
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.012|                    removal of an audio dropout risk that a previous tweak
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.013|                    introduced by setting 0xFFFFFFFF.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.014|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.015|  Cost            : Nothing on a slow link. Be clear what the cap actually
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.016|                    is: about 10000 packets per second, which is roughly
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.017|                    120 Mbit/s with full-size frames, and only while a
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.018|                    multimedia stream is playing. On a fibre or cable
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.019|                    connection that is a real soft ceiling on downloads
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.020|                    during playback - which is exactly why the throughput
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.021|                    profile offers to lift it. Below about 100 Mbit/s you
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.022|                    never reach it.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.023|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.024|  Windows default : 10 (0xa).
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.025|
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.027|    10 (0xa)             : Shipped value. Caps non-multimedia network
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.028|                           processing at 10 packets per millisecond -
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.029|                           about 10000 packets per second - while an MMCSS
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.030|                           multimedia stream is registered.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.031|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.032|        Cap removed. This is what tweak guides write, and what the restore
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.033|        path is here to undo.
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.034|    any other N          : Cap set to N packets per millisecond, neither
::T|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.035|                           the shipped protection nor its removal.
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns at 10. This is the restore
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.002|                       path, and its whole job is to put the Windows value
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.003|                       back - so every profile gets 10, including the
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.004|                       server, which is the one that may want the cap
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.005|                       lifted. A machine that wants that asks for it in
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.006|                       the network throughput profile, not in an undo
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.007|                       step.
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.008|
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.009|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.010|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.011|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex /t REG_DWORD /d 10 /f in the
::X|EN|net.nti.restore.write10.012|                    restore path (OPTY.bat ~1628)
::
:: ---- net.nagle.restore.del (repair) -----------------------------
::P|net.nagle.restore.del|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.001|SUPPRIMER LES SURCHARGES NAGLE ET ACK RETARDE
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.002|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.003|  Ce que c est    : TcpAckFrequency et TCPNoDelay sont des valeurs par
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.004|                    carte reseau laissees par les scripts anti-lag. Ceci
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.005|                    est la copie de la suppression sur le chemin de
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.006|                    restauration.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.007|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.008|  Effet reel      : Efface les deux sur toutes les interfaces : TCP
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.009|                    redevient conforme a ce que livre Windows. Aucun
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.010|                    redemarrage necessaire.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.011|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.012|  Gain            : Retire des reglages dont le gain de latence n'a jamais
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.013|                    ete reproduit. La plupart des jeux passent en UDP, que
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.014|                    ces valeurs ne touchent pas du tout : on n'abandonne
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.015|                    donc aucune latence, on supprime seulement du surcout
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.016|                    d'ACK.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.017|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.018|  Cout            : Rien, c'est une simple remise en ordre. Une
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.019|                    application TCP sensible a la latence pour laquelle
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.020|                    vous les auriez ajoutees exigerait de les remettre,
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.021|                    GUID par GUID.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.022|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.023|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux absentes sur toutes les interfaces.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.024|
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.026|    TcpAckFrequency absent / TCPNoDelay absent :
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.027|        Etat livre sur toutes les interfaces. L'ACK retarde et Nagle se
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.028|        comportent comme Windows les a concus, et le TCP_NODELAY par
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.029|        socket fonctionne toujours pour les applications qui le demandent.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.030|    TcpAckFrequency=1    : Un ACK par segment recu. Double le volume d'ACK
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.031|                           en emission sans gain de latence mesurable sur
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.032|                           du trafic de jeu en UDP.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.033|    TCPNoDelay=1         : Nagle desactive sur toute la machine pour cette
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.034|                           interface. Chaque petite ecriture devient un
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.035|                           segment a part.
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.036|    either value = 0     : Une desactivation ecrite comme valeur, donc un
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.037|                           troisieme etat. Restaurer, c'est supprimer, pas
::T|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.038|                           ecrire 0.
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a DELETE. Le defaut
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.002|                         Windows est l'absence de la valeur : DELETE, et
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.003|                         non 0, est donc le bon litteral dans chaque
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.004|                         colonne, y compris celle de Windows - ecrire 0
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.005|                         laisserait la machine dans un etat qu'elle n'a
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.006|                         jamais eu d'origine.
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.007|
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.008|  Cible           : for each key under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Servi
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.009|                    ces\Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces\{GUID}: reg delete /v
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.010|                    TcpAckFrequency /f and reg delete /v TCPNoDelay /f, in
::X|FR|net.nagle.restore.del.011|                    the restore path (OPTY.bat ~1630-1632)
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.001|REMOVE NAGLE AND DELAYED-ACK OVERRIDES
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.002|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.003|  What it is      : TcpAckFrequency and TCPNoDelay are per-adapter values
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.004|                    left behind by ping tuning scripts. This is the
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.005|                    restore-path copy of the deletion.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.006|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes both on every network interface, so TCP
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.008|                    behaves as Windows ships it. No reboot needed.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.009|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.010|  Gain            : Removes settings whose latency benefit has never been
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.011|                    reproduced. Most games use UDP, which these values do
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.012|                    not touch at all - so there is no latency being given
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.013|                    up, only ACK overhead being removed.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.014|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.015|  Cost            : Nothing, this is a pure repair. A latency-sensitive
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.016|                    TCP application you added them for would need them re-
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.017|                    added per adapter GUID.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.018|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.019|  Windows default : Both absent on every interface.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.020|
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.021|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.022|    TcpAckFrequency absent / TCPNoDelay absent :
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.023|        Shipped state on every interface. Delayed ACK and Nagle behave as
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.024|        Windows designed them, and per-socket TCP_NODELAY still works for
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.025|        applications that ask.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.026|    TcpAckFrequency=1    : One ACK per received segment. Doubles upstream
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.027|                           ACK volume for no measurable latency gain on
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.028|                           UDP game traffic.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.029|    TCPNoDelay=1         : Nagle disabled machine-wide on that interface.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.030|                           Every small write becomes its own segment.
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.031|    either value = 0     : An explicit disable written as a value, which
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.032|                           is a third state. Restoring means deleting, not
::T|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.033|                           writing 0.
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns. The Windows default
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.002|                       is the absence of the value, so DELETE and not 0 is
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.003|                       the correct literal in every column, including the
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.004|                       Windows one - writing 0 would leave the machine in
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.005|                       a state it never shipped in.
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.006|
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.007|  Target          : for each key under HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Servi
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.008|                    ces\Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces\{GUID}: reg delete /v
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.009|                    TcpAckFrequency /f and reg delete /v TCPNoDelay /f, in
::X|EN|net.nagle.restore.del.010|                    the restore path (OPTY.bat ~1630-1632)
::
:: ---- net.firewall.allprofiles.on (repair) -----------------------
::P|net.firewall.allprofiles.on|on|on|on|on|on|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.001|REACTIVER LE PARE-FEU WINDOWS
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.002|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.003|  Ce que c est    : Le filtre de paquets integre, sur les trois profils
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.004|                    reseau : Domaine, Prive et Public.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.005|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.006|  Effet reel      : Passe l'etat a on sur les trois profils en une seule
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.007|                    commande. Effet immediat, sans redemarrage.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.008|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.009|  Gain            : Les connexions entrantes sont de nouveau filtrees. Sur
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.010|                    un portable qui se connecte a du Wi-Fi public, c'est
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.011|                    la ligne la plus utile de tout le script : c'est la
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.012|                    difference entre un service en ecoute qui ne regarde
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.013|                    que vous et un service que tout le monde peut
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.014|                    atteindre.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.015|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.016|  Cout            : Rien. Une application qui a besoin d'un port entrant
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.017|                    demande l'autorisation une fois, ou reclame une regle
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.018|                    que vous ajoutez vous-meme.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.019|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.020|  Defaut Windows  : on pour Domaine, Prive et Public.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.021|
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.022|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.023|    on                   : Le filtre de paquets est actif sur les profils
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.024|                           Domaine, Prive et Public. Les connexions
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.025|                           entrantes sans regle d'autorisation sont
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.026|                           bloquees, le sortant reste autorise par defaut.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.027|    off                  : Le filtre est desactive par administration sur
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.028|                           ce profil. Chaque port en ecoute de la machine
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.029|                           devient joignable depuis le segment reseau
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.030|                           auquel elle est rattachee, y compris un Wi-Fi
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.031|                           d'hotel ou de cafe.
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.032|    notconfigured        : Efface le reglage local pour laisser decider la
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.033|                           strategie de groupe, ou le defaut livre s'il
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.034|                           n'y a pas de strategie. N'a de sens que sur une
::T|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.035|                           machine geree.
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a on. Aucun profil
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.002|                         d'usage ne justifie un chemin entrant non filtre
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.003|                         : un serveur qui publie des ports le fait avec
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.004|                         des regles, pas en retirant le filtre, et un jeu
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.005|                         qui a besoin d'un port declenche une invite. La
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.006|                         colonne Windows est a on parce que on est ce que
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.007|                         Windows livre. Le seul non defendable : une
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.008|                         machine derriere un pare-feu materiel que vous
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.009|                         administrez vous-meme.
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.010|
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.011|  Cible           : netsh advfirewall set allprofiles state on - in
::X|FR|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.012|                    :reassert_defaults (OPTY.bat ~1792)
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.001|TURN THE WINDOWS FIREWALL BACK ON
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.002|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.003|  What it is      : The built-in packet filter, on all three network
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.004|                    profiles: Domain, Private and Public.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.005|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.006|  Actual effect   : Sets state on for all three profiles in one command.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.007|                    Immediate, no reboot.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.008|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.009|  Gain            : Inbound connections are filtered again. On a laptop
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.010|                    that joins public Wi-Fi this is the single most
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.011|                    valuable line in the whole script - it is the
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.012|                    difference between a listening service being your
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.013|                    business and being everyone's.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.014|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.015|  Cost            : Nothing. An application that needs an inbound port
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.016|                    prompts once, or needs a rule you add yourself.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.017|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.018|  Windows default : on for Domain, Private and Public.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.019|
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.020|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.021|    on                   : The packet filter is active on the Domain,
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.022|                           Private and Public profiles. Inbound
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.023|                           connections that have no allow rule are
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.024|                           blocked; outbound is allowed by default.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.025|    off                  : The filter is administratively off on that
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.026|                           profile. Every listening port on the machine is
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.027|                           reachable from the network segment it is
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.028|                           attached to, including a hotel or cafe Wi-Fi.
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.029|    notconfigured        : Clears the local setting so Group Policy, or
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.030|                           the shipped default when there is no policy,
::T|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.031|                           decides. Only meaningful on a managed machine.
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns at on. There is no usage
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.002|                       profile for which an unfiltered inbound path is the
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.003|                       right answer - a server that publishes ports
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.004|                       publishes them with rules, not by removing the
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.005|                       filter, and a game that needs a port gets a prompt.
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.006|                       The Windows column is on because on is what Windows
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.007|                       ships. The only defensible no is a machine sitting
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.008|                       behind a firewall appliance you administer
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.009|                       yourself.
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.010|
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.011|  Target          : netsh advfirewall set allprofiles state on - in
::X|EN|net.firewall.allprofiles.on.012|                    :reassert_defaults (OPTY.bat ~1792)
::
:: ---- net.svc.dnscache.auto (repair) -----------------------------
::P|net.svc.dnscache.auto|auto|auto|auto|auto|auto|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.001|CLIENT DNS (DNSCACHE) EN AUTOMATIQUE
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.002|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.003|  Ce que c est    : Dnscache met en cache les resolutions de noms pour
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.004|                    toute la machine et applique l'ordre de resolution, y
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.005|                    compris les regles par interface et le split-DNS des
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.006|                    VPN.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.007|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.008|  Effet reel      : sc config Dnscache start= auto. Les resolutions sont
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.009|                    de nouveau mises en cache, et ipconfig /flushdns a
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.010|                    enfin quelque chose a vider.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.011|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.012|  Gain            : Moins d'allers-retours DNS repetes, et la resolution
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.013|                    de noms en VPN ou split-DNS se comporte correctement.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.014|                    C'est une question d'exactitude plus que de vitesse :
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.015|                    vous ne le sentirez pas au chargement d'une page, vous
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.016|                    le sentirez quand un nom interne cessera de resoudre a
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.017|                    travers le VPN.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.018|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.019|  Cout            : Aucun, c'est la valeur d'usine. Si vous l'aviez coupe
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.020|                    pour la vie privee, sachez que cela ne cache rien a
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.021|                    votre resolveur : sans cache, PLUS de requetes sortent
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.022|                    de la machine, pas moins.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.023|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.024|  Defaut Windows  : 2 (Automatique).
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.025|
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.026|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.027|    auto (Start=2)       : Valeur d'origine. Le client DNS demarre au
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.028|                           boot, met en cache les reponses pour toute la
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.029|                           machine et applique la table de politique de
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.030|                           resolution de noms, ou vivent les regles par
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.031|                           interface et le split-DNS des VPN.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.032|    delayed-auto (Start=2 plus DelayedAutostart=1) :
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.033|        Demarre quelques minutes apres le boot, en priorite d'E/S basse.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.034|        Tant qu'il n'est pas la, les resolutions contournent le cache
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.035|        partage et la table de politique : mauvais compromis pour un
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.036|        service par lequel toute la machine resout.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.037|    demand (Start=3)     : Rien, en session normale, ne le lance. La
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.038|                           resolution de noms fonctionne encore, car
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.039|                           dnsapi.dll bascule sur une resolution interne a
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.040|                           chaque processus, mais vous perdez le cache
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.041|                           partage et les regles de politique qui rendent
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.042|                           correctes la resolution en split-DNS et en VPN.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.043|                           C'est le cas qui compte vraiment : ici, demand
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.044|                           se comporte comme disabled.
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.045|    disabled (Start=4)   : Meme effet pratique que demand, et en plus tout
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.046|                           composant qui tenterait de le lancer echoue
::T|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.047|                           franchement.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a auto. Tous les profils
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.002|                         resolvent des noms et aucun ne gagne quoi que ce
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.003|                         soit a resoudre sans cache ni table de politique.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.004|                         Si une colonne avait plus a perdre, ce serait le
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.005|                         portable, la machine la plus susceptible
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.006|                         d'utiliser un VPN en split-DNS - mais la reponse
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.007|                         est la meme pour les cinq, il n'y a donc pas de
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.008|                         distinction a inventer.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.009|
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.010|  Problemes connus : OPTY n'ecrit pas cette valeur aujourd'hui. Dnscache
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.011|                     n'apparait que dans le rapport de services, ligne
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.012|                     2208 : cette fiche decrit donc un reglage que le
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.013|                     script signale mais ne repare jamais.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.014|
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.015|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether any service trigger in Windows 11 25H2
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.016|                      demand-starts Dnscache was not verified on the live
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.017|                      machine. The description of what demand means
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.018|                      assumes nothing does, which is the case that
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.019|                      matters.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.020|
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.021|  Cible           : sc config Dnscache start= auto, i.e.
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.022|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\Dnscache
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.023|                    Start=2. Not currently written anywhere in OPTY:
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.024|                    Dnscache appears only in the :svcshow report list
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.025|                    (OPTY.bat ~2208). Implementing it means one call
::X|FR|net.svc.dnscache.auto.026|                    :svcset "Dnscache" auto 2 "Dnscache (DNS Client)".
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.001|DNS CLIENT (DNSCACHE) TO AUTOMATIC
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.002|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.003|  What it is      : Dnscache caches name lookups for the whole machine and
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.004|                    implements the resolution order, including per-
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.005|                    interface rules and VPN split-DNS.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.006|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.007|  Actual effect   : sc config Dnscache start= auto. Lookups are cached
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.008|                    again, and ipconfig /flushdns has something to flush.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.009|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.010|  Gain            : Fewer repeated DNS round trips, and split-DNS or VPN
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.011|                    name resolution behaves properly. This is a
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.012|                    correctness feature more than a speed one - you will
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.013|                    not feel it on a normal page load, you will feel it
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.014|                    when an internal hostname stops resolving over the
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.015|                    VPN.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.016|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.017|  Cost            : None; it is the shipped value. If you turned it off as
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.018|                    a privacy measure, note that it hides nothing from
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.019|                    your resolver - without the cache, MORE queries leave
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.020|                    the machine, not fewer.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.021|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.022|  Windows default : 2 (Automatic).
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.023|
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.025|    auto (Start=2)       : Shipped value. The DNS Client starts at boot,
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.026|                           caches answers for the whole machine, and
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.027|                           applies the name resolution policy table -
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.028|                           which is where per-interface rules and VPN
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.029|                           split-DNS live.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.030|    delayed-auto (Start=2 plus DelayedAutostart=1) :
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.031|        Starts a couple of minutes after boot, at low I/O priority. Until
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.032|        it comes up, lookups bypass the shared cache and the policy table
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.033|        - the wrong trade for a service everything on the machine resolves
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.034|        through.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.035|    demand (Start=3)     : Nothing in a normal session starts it. Name
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.036|                           resolution still works, because dnsapi.dll
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.037|                           falls back to resolving inside each process,
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.038|                           but you lose the shared cache and the policy
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.039|                           rules that make split-DNS and VPN name
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.040|                           resolution correct. This is the case that
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.041|                           actually matters: demand here behaves like
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.042|                           disabled.
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.043|    disabled (Start=4)   : Same practical effect as demand, and in
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.044|                           addition any component that does try to start
::T|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.045|                           it fails outright.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns at auto. Every profile
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.002|                       resolves names and none of them gains anything from
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.003|                       resolving without a cache or without the policy
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.004|                       table. If one column had more to lose it would be
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.005|                       the laptop, because that is the machine most likely
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.006|                       to use a VPN with split-DNS - but the answer is the
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.007|                       same for all five, so there is no distinction to
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.008|                       invent.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.009|
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.010|  Known problems  : OPTY does not write this today. Dnscache appears only
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.011|                    in the service report at line 2208, so this card
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.012|                    describes a setting the script reports and never
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.013|                    repairs.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.014|
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.015|  Unverified      : Whether any service trigger in Windows 11 25H2 demand-
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.016|                    starts Dnscache was not verified on the live machine.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.017|                    The description of what demand means assumes nothing
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.018|                    does, which is the case that matters.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.019|
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.020|  Target          : sc config Dnscache start= auto, i.e.
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.021|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\Dnscache
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.022|                    Start=2. Not currently written anywhere in OPTY:
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.023|                    Dnscache appears only in the :svcshow report list
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.024|                    (OPTY.bat ~2208). Implementing it means one call
::X|EN|net.svc.dnscache.auto.025|                    :svcset "Dnscache" auto 2 "Dnscache (DNS Client)".
::
:: ---- net.nic.itr.adaptive (preference) ------------------------------
::P|net.nic.itr.adaptive|1/65535|1/65535|1/65535|1/65535|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.001|MODERATION ADAPTATIVE DES INTERRUPTIONS RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.003|  Ce que c est    : La moderation d'interruptions permet a la carte reseau
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.004|                    de regrouper plusieurs paquets recus en une seule
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.005|                    interruption, au lieu de deranger le CPU pour chacun.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.006|                    ITR en fixe la cadence ; 65535 signifie adaptatif,
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.007|                    c'est-a-dire que le pilote decide selon le trafic.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.008|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit *InterruptModeration=1 et ITR=65535 en REG_SZ
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.010|                    dans la cle de classe de la carte, bornes au maximum
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.011|                    du pilote. La carte regroupe alors les interruptions
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.012|                    sous charge et cesse de le faire quand le trafic est
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.013|                    faible. Rien ne prend effet avant un redemarrage de la
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.014|                    carte ou de la machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.015|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.016|  Gain            : Une forte baisse du nombre d'interruptions et du temps
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.017|                    passe en DPC sous charge, visible dans la colonne CPU
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.018|                    pendant un gros telechargement. Au debit de paquets
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.019|                    d'un jeu, le mode adaptatif ne regroupe deja
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.020|                    pratiquement rien : cela ne se paie donc pas en
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.021|                    latence.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.022|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.023|  Cout            : Dans le pire cas theorique, une cadence fixe ajoute
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.024|                    quelques dizaines de microsecondes a la remise d'un
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.025|                    paquet. Le mode adaptatif existe precisement pour
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.026|                    eviter cela. Le conseil repandu de couper completement
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.027|                    la moderation echange une hausse de CPU mesurable
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.028|                    contre un gain de latence que personne n'a demontre
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.029|                    sur une ligne domestique.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.030|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.031|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote, pas par Windows. Les
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.032|                    cartes filaires Intel sont livrees avec
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.033|                    *InterruptModeration=1 et ITR=65535 (Adaptatif). Les
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.034|                    autres fabricants different, et la valeur qui fait foi
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.035|                    pour votre carte est Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default, que
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.036|                    cette etape ne consulte pas.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.037|
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.038|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.039|    *InterruptModeration=0 :
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.040|        La carte leve une interruption par paquet recu. Charge
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.041|        d'interruptions et de DPC maximale pour le CPU, et delai de remise
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.042|        theorique minimal pour un paquet isole.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.043|    *InterruptModeration=1 :
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.044|        La carte regroupe les paquets recus et leve moins d'interruptions.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.045|        C'est ensuite ITR qui fixe la cadence.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.046|    ITR=65535            : Adaptatif. Le pilote choisit la cadence selon
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.047|                           le trafic observe : il regroupe sous charge et
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.048|                           cesse pratiquement de regrouper quand le trafic
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.049|                           est faible, ce qui explique qu'il ne coute rien
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.050|                           en latence au debit de paquets d'un jeu.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.051|    ITR=0                : Aucune cadence de moderation, meme avec
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.052|                           *InterruptModeration=1 : equivaut en pratique a
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.053|                           couper la moderation.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.054|    ITR=any other enumerated number :
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.055|        Une cadence de moderation fixe. Les nombres et les noms que les
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.056|        fabricants leur donnent varient d'un pilote a l'autre ; la liste
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.057|        qui s'applique a votre carte est dans Ndi\Params\ITR\Enum, sur
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.058|        votre propre machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.059|    SKIP                 : Aucun des deux mots-cles n'est ecrit : la carte
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.060|                           garde ce que le pilote a livre, la valeur
::T|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.061|                           publiee dans Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default.
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, parce que la
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.002|                         moderation adaptative est le bon reglage quoi que
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.003|                         fasse la machine : elle regroupe quand il y a de
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.004|                         la charge et s'arrete quand il n'y en a pas, donc
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.005|                         rien n'est echange et il n'y a aucune distinction
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.006|                         a inventer. La colonne Windows est SKIP et non
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.007|                         1/65535 : ecrire les nombres d'Intel sur une
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.008|                         Realtek ou une Killer impose une valeur, cela
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.009|                         n'en restaure aucune. La seule facon honnete
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.010|                         d'obtenir la valeur livree est de ne pas toucher
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.011|                         aux mots-cles, ou de passer par l'etape de
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.012|                         restauration, qui les lit.
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.013|
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.014|  Non verifie (en)  : Realtek and Killer factory values for these two
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.015|                      keywords were not verified. The names attached to
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.016|                      the intermediate ITR numbers come from vendor
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.017|                      documentation, not from a reading on this machine -
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.018|                      the authoritative list for your card is
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.019|                      Ndi\Params\ITR\Enum.
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.020|
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.021|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*InterruptModeration" "1" and
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.022|                    call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "ITR" "65535" - REG_SZ writes
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.023|                    into HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Class\{4d36
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.024|                    e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\<NNNN> (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.nic.itr.adaptive.025|                    ~2845-2846)
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.001|ADAPTIVE INTERRUPT MODERATION ON THE NIC
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.003|  What it is      : Interrupt moderation lets the network card group
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.004|                    several received packets into one interrupt instead of
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.005|                    interrupting the CPU for every single one. ITR sets
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.006|                    the rate; 65535 means adaptive, so the driver decides
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.007|                    according to the traffic pattern.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.008|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.009|  Actual effect   : Writes *InterruptModeration=1 and ITR=65535 as REG_SZ
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.010|                    into the adapter's class key, clamped to the driver's
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.011|                    own maximum. The card then batches interrupts under
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.012|                    load and stops batching when traffic is sparse.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.013|                    Nothing happens until the adapter restarts or the
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.014|                    machine reboots.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.015|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.016|  Gain            : A large drop in interrupts and DPC time under load,
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.017|                    visible in the CPU column during a big download. At
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.018|                    the packet rate a game produces, adaptive mode is
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.019|                    already effectively not batching, so it is not paid
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.020|                    for in latency.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.021|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.022|  Cost            : In the theoretical worst case a fixed moderation rate
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.023|                    adds tens of microseconds to a single packet's
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.024|                    delivery. Adaptive exists precisely to avoid that. The
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.025|                    popular advice to disable moderation entirely trades a
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.026|                    measurable CPU increase for a latency win nobody has
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.027|                    demonstrated on a home link.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.028|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.029|  Windows default : Driver-supplied, not a Windows value. Intel wired
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.030|                    adapters ship *InterruptModeration=1 with ITR=65535
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.031|                    (Adaptive). Other vendors differ, and the
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.032|                    authoritative value for your card is
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.033|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default, which this step does not
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.034|                    consult.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.035|
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.036|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.037|    *InterruptModeration=0 :
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.038|        The card raises one interrupt per received packet. Maximum
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.039|        interrupt and DPC load on the CPU, and the theoretical minimum
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.040|        delivery delay for one packet.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.041|    *InterruptModeration=1 :
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.042|        The card groups received packets and raises fewer interrupts. ITR
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.043|        then decides at what rate.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.044|    ITR=65535            : Adaptive. The driver picks the moderation rate
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.045|                           from the observed traffic pattern - it batches
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.046|                           under load and effectively stops batching when
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.047|                           traffic is sparse, which is why it costs
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.048|                           nothing in latency at game packet rates.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.049|    ITR=0                : No moderation rate at all, even with
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.050|                           *InterruptModeration=1 - equivalent in practice
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.051|                           to turning moderation off.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.052|    ITR=any other enumerated number :
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.053|        A fixed moderation rate. The numbers and the names vendors give
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.054|        them differ between drivers; the list that applies to your card is
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.055|        at Ndi\Params\ITR\Enum on your own machine.
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.056|    SKIP                 : Neither keyword is written, so the adapter
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.057|                           keeps whatever the driver shipped - the value
::T|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.058|                           published at Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default.
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns because adaptive moderation
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.002|                       is right whatever the machine does - it batches
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.003|                       when there is load and stops when there is not, so
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.004|                       nothing is traded and there is no distinction to
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.005|                       invent. The Windows column is SKIP rather than
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.006|                       1/65535: writing Intel's numbers onto a Realtek or
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.007|                       Killer card imposes a value, it does not restore
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.008|                       one. The only honest way to get the shipped value
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.009|                       is to leave the keywords alone, or to use the
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.010|                       restore step, which reads them.
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.012|  Unverified      : Realtek and Killer factory values for these two
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.013|                    keywords were not verified. The names attached to the
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.014|                    intermediate ITR numbers come from vendor
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.015|                    documentation, not from a reading on this machine -
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.016|                    the authoritative list for your card is
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.017|                    Ndi\Params\ITR\Enum.
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.018|
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.019|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*InterruptModeration" "1" and
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.020|                    call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "ITR" "65535" - REG_SZ writes
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.021|                    into HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Class\{4d36
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.022|                    e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\<NNNN> (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.nic.itr.adaptive.023|                    ~2845-2846)
