:: ---- net.nic.offloads.apply (repair) ----------------------------
::P|net.nic.offloads.apply|1/3|1/3|1/3|1/3|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.001|DECHARGEMENTS MATERIELS ACTIFS (LSO ET SOMMES DE CONTROLE)
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.003|  Ce que c est    : Sept mots-cles du pilote qui decident si la puce
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.004|                    reseau prend en charge la segmentation TCP et les
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.005|                    sommes de controle IPv4/IPv6/TCP/UDP, ou si c'est
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.006|                    votre processeur qui s'en charge.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.008|  Effet reel      : Met les deux mots-cles LSO a 1 et tous ceux de somme
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.009|                    de controle a 3, c'est-a-dire les deux sens en
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.010|                    materiel. Ce sont deja les valeurs d'usine des pilotes
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.011|                    filaires Intel et Realtek ; l'habitude des guides de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.012|                    bidouille est de tout couper.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.013|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.014|  Gain            : Les gros transferts - telechargements, Steam, copies
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.015|                    de fichiers en reseau local - consomment nettement
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.016|                    moins de CPU. Sur un processeur lent, c'est parfois ce
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.017|                    qui fait la difference entre saturer un lien gigabit
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.018|                    ou non. Sur une machine ou ils n'ont jamais ete
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.019|                    coupes, le gain honnete est nul.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.020|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.021|  Cout            : Historiquement, quelques vieux pilotes bogues
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.022|                    corrompaient les sommes de controle avec le
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.023|                    dechargement actif ; ce n'est plus un souci sur les
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.024|                    pilotes actuels. Couper les dechargements reste un
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.025|                    test valable quand on traque une corruption de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.026|                    paquets, mais comme optimisation permanente, cela ne
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.027|                    fait que deplacer le travail vers le CPU.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.028|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.029|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote. Sur pratiquement tous
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.030|                    les pilotes filaires actuels, LSOv2 est livre actif et
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.031|                    les sommes de controle a 3 : on reaffirme donc en
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.032|                    general la valeur d'usine - mais OPTY ne lit pas
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.033|                    Ndi\Params pour le verifier sur votre carte.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.034|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.036|    *LsoV2IPv4 / *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 :
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.037|        Large Send Offload actif. Le pilote remet a la puce un gros
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.038|        tampon, que la puce decoupe elle-meme en segments a la taille du
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.039|        MTU : la pile est traversee une fois par tampon au lieu d'une fois
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.040|        par paquet. Moins de CPU sur les gros envois.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.041|    *LsoV2IPv4 / *LsoV2IPv6 = 0 :
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.042|        Le CPU decoupe chaque paquet en logiciel. Les memes octets partent
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.043|        sur le cable, mais il en coute plus de CPU pour les y mettre.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.044|    checksum keyword = 0 : Desactive. Le CPU calcule les sommes de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.045|                           controle a l'emission et les verifie a la
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.046|                           reception, pour chaque paquet.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.047|    checksum keyword = 1 : Tx Enabled. La puce calcule les sommes de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.048|                           controle a l'emission seulement ; la reception
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.049|                           reste verifiee par le CPU.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.050|    checksum keyword = 2 : Rx Enabled. La puce verifie a la reception
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.051|                           seulement ; l'emission reste calculee par le
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.052|                           CPU.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.053|    checksum keyword = 3 : Rx et Tx Enabled, les deux sens en materiel.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.054|                           C'est ce que livrent les pilotes filaires Intel
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.055|                           et Realtek actuels.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.056|    SKIP                 : Aucun des sept mots-cles n'est ecrit : chacun
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.057|                           garde la valeur propre du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, parce que le
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.002|                         dechargement materiel est le bon reglage pour
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.003|                         tous les usages. Le trafic d'un jeu, ce sont de
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.004|                         petits datagrammes UDP qui ne passent jamais par
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.005|                         le LSO : couper les dechargements coute du CPU en
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.006|                         telechargement et n'apporte rien a la latence, il
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.007|                         n'y a donc pas de distinction gaming contre
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.008|                         bureautique a faire. La colonne Windows est SKIP
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.009|                         parce que 1 et 3 sont des valeurs du pilote, pas
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.010|                         de Windows, et que seule l'etape de restauration
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.011|                         lit les vraies.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.013|  Cible           : call :nicset on the adapter class key for
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.014|                    *LsoV2IPv4=1, *LsoV2IPv6=1, *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.015|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv6=3, *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.016|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv6=3, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.apply.017|                    all REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2849-2856)
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.001|HARDWARE OFFLOADS ON (LSO AND CHECKSUMS)
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.003|  What it is      : Seven driver keywords deciding whether the NIC chip
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.004|                    does TCP segmentation and IPv4/IPv6/TCP/UDP checksum
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.005|                    work, or whether your CPU does it.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.006|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.007|  Actual effect   : Writes the two LSO keywords to 1 and every checksum
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.008|                    keyword to 3, meaning both directions in hardware.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.009|                    These are already the shipped values on Intel and
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.010|                    Realtek wired drivers; the tweak-guide habit is to
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.011|                    turn them all off.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.012|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.013|  Gain            : Bulk transfers - downloads, Steam, LAN file copies -
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.014|                    use noticeably less CPU. On a slow CPU that can be the
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.015|                    difference between saturating a gigabit link and not.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.016|                    On a machine that never had them disabled, the honest
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.017|                    gain is zero.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.018|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.019|  Cost            : Historically a few buggy old NIC drivers corrupted
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.020|                    checksums with offload on; that is not a live concern
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.021|                    on current drivers. Turning offloads off is a valid
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.022|                    diagnostic step when chasing packet corruption, but as
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.023|                    a permanent optimisation it only moves work to the
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.024|                    CPU.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.025|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.026|  Windows default : Driver-supplied. On practically every current wired
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.027|                    driver LSOv2 ships enabled and the checksum offloads
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.028|                    ship at 3, so this normally re-asserts the factory
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.029|                    value - but OPTY does not read Ndi\Params to confirm
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.030|                    it on your card.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.031|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.033|    *LsoV2IPv4 / *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 :
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.034|        Large Send Offload on. The driver hands the chip one large buffer
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.035|        and the chip cuts it into MTU-sized segments itself, so the stack
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.036|        is traversed once per buffer instead of once per packet. Lower CPU
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.037|        on bulk sends.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.038|    *LsoV2IPv4 / *LsoV2IPv6 = 0 :
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.039|        The CPU segments every packet in software. Same bytes on the wire,
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.040|        more CPU to put them there.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.041|    checksum keyword = 0 : Disabled. The CPU computes checksums on send
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.042|                           and verifies them on receive, for every packet.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.043|    checksum keyword = 1 : Tx Enabled. The chip computes checksums on send
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.044|                           only; receive is still verified by the CPU.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.045|    checksum keyword = 2 : Rx Enabled. The chip verifies on receive only;
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.046|                           send is still computed by the CPU.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.047|    checksum keyword = 3 : Rx and Tx Enabled, both directions in hardware.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.048|                           This is what current Intel and Realtek wired
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.049|                           drivers ship.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.050|    SKIP                 : None of the seven keywords is written, so each
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.051|                           keeps the driver's own value.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns because hardware offload is
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.002|                       right for every workload. Game traffic is small UDP
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.003|                       datagrams that never go through LSO at all, so
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.004|                       turning offloads off costs CPU on downloads and
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.005|                       buys nothing for latency - there is no gaming-
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.006|                       versus-office distinction to make. The Windows
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.007|                       column is SKIP because 1 and 3 are the driver's
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.008|                       values rather than Windows values, and only the
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.009|                       restore step reads the real ones.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.010|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.011|  Target          : call :nicset on the adapter class key for
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.012|                    *LsoV2IPv4=1, *LsoV2IPv6=1, *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.013|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv6=3, *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.014|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv6=3, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4=3,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.apply.015|                    all REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2849-2856)
::
:: ---- net.nic.rss.queues (preference) --------------------------------
::P|net.nic.rss.queues|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.001|RSS AVEC UN NOMBRE DE FILES REELLEMENT SUPPORTE
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.003|  Ce que c est    : Le RSS repartit le traitement des paquets entrants sur
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                    plusieurs coeurs au lieu de tout concentrer sur un
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.005|                    seul. *NumRssQueues indique combien de files de
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.006|                    reception la carte doit utiliser.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit *RSS=1, puis lit les valeurs que votre pilote
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                    enumere reellement et ecrit la plus elevee. La plupart
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.010|                    des scripts codent 4 ou 8 en dur ; si la carte
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.011|                    n'enumere que 1 et 2, le pilote ignore l'ecriture en
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.012|                    silence et le script annonce une reussite qui n'a
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.013|                    jamais eu lieu.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.014|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.015|  Gain            : Sur une carte a plusieurs files, un gros debit cesse
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.016|                    de saturer un seul coeur : cela se voit pendant les
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.017|                    gros telechargements ou les transferts LAN intensifs,
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.018|                    surtout sur un CPU ancien ou a peu de coeurs. Sur une
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.019|                    liaison 1 Gb/s avec un huit coeurs moderne, la
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.020|                    difference n'est generalement pas mesurable, et cette
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.021|                    fiche ne pretendra pas le contraire.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.022|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.023|  Cout            : Un peu plus de travail reparti entre les coeurs pour
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.024|                    le meme trafic, et une consommation marginalement plus
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.025|                    elevee. Aucune penalite de latence significative.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.026|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.027|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote. *RSS est livre actif sur
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.028|                    pratiquement toutes les cartes multi-files ; la valeur
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.029|                    d'origine de *NumRssQueues depend du chipset et vaut
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.030|                    souvent 2. La vraie valeur de votre carte se lit dans
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.031|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\default.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.032|
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.034|    *RSS=0               : Chaque paquet recu est traite sur l'unique CPU
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.035|                           auquel l'interruption de la carte est affectee.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.036|                           Sur une connexion rapide, ce coeur sature avant
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.037|                           le lien.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.038|    *RSS=1               : Le traitement en reception est reparti par
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.039|                           hachage sur plusieurs coeurs, une file par
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.040|                           coeur. Livre actif sur pratiquement toutes les
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.041|                           cartes multi-files.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.042|    *NumRssQueues=1      : Le RSS est actif mais il n'y a qu'une file : le
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.043|                           comportement est exactement celui du RSS
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.044|                           desactive.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.045|    *NumRssQueues=2, 4, 8, 16 :
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.046|        Autant de files de reception, si le pilote enumere la valeur. Un
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.047|        nombre qu'il n'enumere pas est ecrit dans le registre, accepte,
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.048|        puis ignore par le pilote - c'est pour cela que les scripts qui
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.049|        codent 8 en dur annoncent une reussite qui n'a jamais eu lieu.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.050|    ENUMMAX              : Le litteral d'OPTY : la valeur la plus elevee
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.051|                           reellement presente dans
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.052|                           Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum sur votre propre
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.053|                           carte. Sur une Intel I211, c'est 2 et non 8.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.054|    SKIP                 : Aucun des deux mots-cles n'est ecrit ; les deux
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.055|                           restent aux valeurs livrees par le pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, et la raison est que
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.002|                         la valeur ecrite n'est pas un nombre fixe : c'est
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.003|                         ce que votre propre pilote enumere. Une carte a
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                         file unique recoit un non-evenement, une carte
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.005|                         multi-files recoit son propre plafond - il ne
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.006|                         reste donc rien qu'un profil d'usage puisse faire
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.007|                         varier. La colonne Windows est SKIP parce qu'OPTY
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.008|                         ecrit la valeur la plus elevee ENUMEREE et non
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                         celle par defaut du pilote, ce qui depasse
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.010|                         l'usine sur certaines cartes.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.012|  Non verifie (en)  : The shipped *NumRssQueues for a given adapter cannot
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.013|                      be asserted here - it is per chipset. OPTY writes
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.014|                      the highest ENUMERATED value, not the driver's
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.015|                      default, so on some adapters this raises the queue
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.016|                      count above what the card shipped with.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.017|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.018|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*RSS" "1", then call
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.019|                    :nicenummax "%NICKEY%" "*NumRssQueues" which reads the
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.020|                    highest REG_SZ entry under
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.021|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum into NENUMMAX, then call
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.022|                    :nicset with that value (OPTY.bat ~2858-2860, helper
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.023|                    :nicenummax ~3483)
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.001|RECEIVE SIDE SCALING WITH A REAL QUEUE COUNT
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.003|  What it is      : RSS spreads incoming packet processing across several
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                    CPU cores instead of pinning it all to one.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.005|                    *NumRssQueues is how many receive queues the card is
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.006|                    told to use.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.008|  Actual effect   : Writes *RSS=1, then reads the values your driver
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                    actually enumerates and writes the highest one. Most
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.010|                    scripts hardcode 4 or 8; if the card only enumerates 1
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.011|                    and 2 the driver silently ignores that and the script
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.012|                    reports a success that never happened.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.013|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.014|  Gain            : On a card with more than one queue, high-throughput
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.015|                    traffic stops saturating a single core - visible
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.016|                    during large downloads or heavy LAN transfers,
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.017|                    particularly on older or low-core CPUs. On a 1 Gbit
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.018|                    link with a modern 8-core CPU the difference is
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.019|                    usually not measurable, and this card will not claim
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.020|                    otherwise.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.021|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.022|  Cost            : Slightly more cross-core work for the same traffic and
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.023|                    marginally higher power draw. No meaningful latency
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.024|                    penalty.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.025|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.026|  Windows default : Driver-supplied. *RSS ships enabled on essentially all
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.027|                    multi-queue adapters; the shipped *NumRssQueues varies
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.028|                    by chipset and is often 2. Your card's real default is
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.029|                    readable at Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\default.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.030|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.032|    *RSS=0               : Every received packet is processed on the
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.033|                           single CPU the adapter's interrupt is
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.034|                           affinitised to. That core saturates before the
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.035|                           link does on a fast connection.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.036|    *RSS=1               : Receive processing is hashed across several
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.037|                           cores, one queue per core. Shipped enabled on
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.038|                           essentially every multi-queue adapter.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.039|    *NumRssQueues=1      : RSS is on but there is a single queue, so it
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.040|                           behaves exactly like RSS off.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.041|    *NumRssQueues=2, 4, 8, 16 :
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.042|        That many receive queues, if the driver enumerates the value. A
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.043|        number the driver does not enumerate is written to the registry,
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.044|        accepted, and ignored by the driver - which is why scripts that
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.045|        hardcode 8 report a success that never happened.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.046|    ENUMMAX              : OPTY's literal: the highest value actually
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.047|                           present under Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum on
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.048|                           your own card. On an Intel I211 that is 2, not
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.049|                           8.
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.050|    SKIP                 : Neither keyword is written; both stay at the
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.051|                           driver's shipped values.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns, and the reason is that the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.002|                       value written is not a fixed number - it is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.003|                       whatever your own driver enumerates. A single-queue
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                       card gets a no-op, a multi-queue card gets its own
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.005|                       ceiling, so there is nothing left for a usage
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.006|                       profile to differ about. The Windows column is SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.007|                       because OPTY writes the highest ENUMERATED value
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.008|                       rather than the driver's default, and on some
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                       adapters that is above factory.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.010|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.011|  Unverified      : The shipped *NumRssQueues for a given adapter cannot
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.012|                    be asserted here - it is per chipset. OPTY writes the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.013|                    highest ENUMERATED value, not the driver's default, so
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.014|                    on some adapters this raises the queue count above
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.015|                    what the card shipped with.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.016|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.017|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*RSS" "1", then call
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.018|                    :nicenummax "%NICKEY%" "*NumRssQueues" which reads the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.019|                    highest REG_SZ entry under
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.020|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum into NENUMMAX, then call
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.021|                    :nicset with that value (OPTY.bat ~2858-2860, helper
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.022|                    :nicenummax ~3483)
::
:: ---- net.nic.jumbo.apply (repair) -------------------------------
::P|net.nic.jumbo.apply|1514|1514|1514|1514|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|TRAMES JUMBO DESACTIVEES (*JUMBOPACKET = 1514)
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|  Ce que c est    : Les trames jumbo transportent jusqu'a 9000 octets par
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|                    trame Ethernet au lieu des 1500 standard.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|                    *JumboPacket=1514, c'est la position desactive chez
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|                    Intel et Realtek.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 1514, ce qui garde la carte sur des trames de
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|                    taille standard. Sur un PC configure normalement, cela
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|                    reaffirme ce que le pilote livrait deja.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.012|  Gain            : Evite un probleme classique qu'on se cree soi-meme :
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.013|                    les trames jumbo ne marchent que si tous les
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.014|                    equipements du chemin sont d'accord. Sur une machine
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.015|                    deja a 1514, il n'y a aucun gain, et cette fiche le
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.016|                    dit.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.017|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.018|  Cout            : Si vous avez un NAS sur un VLAN de stockage dedie ou
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.019|                    tout est regle a 9000 octets, ceci l'annule et vous
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.020|                    perdez le debit et l'efficacite CPU pour lesquels
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.021|                    cette installation existe. C'est le seul cas reel.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.022|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.023|  Defaut Windows  : 1514 (jumbo desactive) sur les pilotes filaires Intel
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.024|                    et Realtek, et sur pratiquement tous les pilotes
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.025|                    reseau Windows actuels.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.026|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.028|    1514                 : Trame Ethernet standard : un MTU de 1500 octets
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.029|                           plus l'en-tete de 14 octets. C'est la position
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.030|                           desactive des trames jumbo chez Intel et
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.031|                           Realtek.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.032|    4088 or 4096         : Jumbo de taille intermediaire, valeur exacte
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.033|                           selon le fabricant. Utile seulement si chaque
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.034|                           equipement du chemin est configure pareil.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.035|    9014 or 9000         : Jumbo complet. Ne fonctionne que si chaque port
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.036|                           de switch, routeur et machine d'en face est
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.037|                           d'accord. Active sur un seul PC, le trafic vers
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.038|                           Internet, dont le chemin est a 1500 octets, se
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.039|                           fragmente et le debit s'effondre.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.040|    SKIP                 : Le mot-cle n'est pas ecrit ; la carte garde le
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.041|                           reglage de taille de trame du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 1514. Tout ce qui
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|                         sort de votre reseau local rencontre un chemin a
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|                         1500 octets et se fragmente : les trames jumbo ne
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|                         paient que sur un reseau de stockage ferme, et
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|                         aucun de ces quatre profils n'en decrit un. Ici,
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|                         le profil serveur signifie torrents, Plex et
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|                         serveurs de jeu sur Internet, pas une
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|                         infrastructure iSCSI 10G. Windows est SKIP parce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|                         que 1514 est la valeur du pilote et que ce chemin
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|                         ne lit pas la valeur par defaut du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.012|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514" - REG_SZ
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.013|                    in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat ~2863)
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|JUMBO FRAMES OFF (*JUMBOPACKET = 1514)
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|  What it is      : Jumbo frames send up to 9000 bytes per Ethernet frame
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|                    instead of the standard 1500. *JumboPacket=1514 is the
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|                    off setting on Intel and Realtek drivers.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|  Actual effect   : Writes 1514, keeping the adapter on standard-size
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|                    frames. On a normally configured PC this re-asserts
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|                    what the driver already shipped.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|  Gain            : Avoids a classic self-inflicted problem: jumbo frames
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.012|                    only work if every device and switch on the path
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.013|                    agrees. On a machine already at 1514 there is no gain
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.014|                    at all, and this card says so.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.015|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.016|  Cost            : If you run a NAS on a dedicated storage VLAN where
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.017|                    everything is set to 9000 bytes, this undoes it and
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.018|                    you lose the throughput and CPU efficiency that setup
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.019|                    exists for. That is the only real scenario.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.020|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.021|  Windows default : 1514 (jumbo disabled) on Intel and Realtek wired
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.022|                    drivers, and on effectively every current Windows NIC
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.023|                    driver.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.024|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.025|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.026|    1514                 : Standard Ethernet frame: a 1500-byte MTU plus
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.027|                           the 14-byte header. This is the off position
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.028|                           for jumbo frames on Intel and Realtek drivers.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.029|    4088 or 4096         : Mid-size jumbo, exact value vendor-dependent.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.030|                           Only useful if every hop is configured for it.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.031|    9014 or 9000         : Full jumbo. Works only if every switch port,
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.032|                           router and peer on the path agrees. Enable it
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.033|                           on one PC and traffic to the internet, whose
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.034|                           path is 1500 bytes, fragments and throughput
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.035|                           drops.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.036|    SKIP                 : The keyword is not written; the adapter keeps
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.037|                           the driver's own frame size setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 1514. Anything leaving
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|                       your LAN meets a 1500-byte path and fragments, so
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|                       jumbo frames only pay on a closed storage network -
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|                       and none of these four profiles describes one. The
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|                       server profile here means torrents, Plex and game
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|                       servers over the internet, not a 10G iSCSI fabric.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|                       Windows is SKIP because 1514 is the driver's value
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|                       and this path does not read the driver's default.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514" - REG_SZ
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|                    in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat ~2863)
::
:: ---- net.nic.buffers.raise (preference) -----------------------------
::P|net.nic.buffers.raise|1024|1024|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|AUGMENTER LES TAMPONS DE LA CARTE RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|  Ce que c est    : Les anneaux de descripteurs sont les files que la
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                    carte utilise pour passer les paquets au pilote et en
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|                    recuperer. Plus il y a d'entrees, plus il y a de marge
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|                    avant qu'une rafale doive etre jetee.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|  Effet reel      : Demande 1024 pour *ReceiveBuffers et *TransmitBuffers,
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                    puis borne au maximum du pilote et arrondit vers le
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                    bas selon son pas. La valeur reellement ecrite est
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|                    donc souvent tres inferieure a 1024, et le journal
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|                    indique laquelle.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|  Gain            : Moins de paquets perdus en rafale. Les cas ou cela
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|                    compte : une liaison saturee, un gros transfert LAN,
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|                    ou un client torrent a fond. Sur une machine au repos
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|                    ou peu chargee, vous ne mesurerez strictement rien.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|  Cout            : Un peu plus de memoire non paginee reservee en
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|                    permanence par le pilote, de quelques Ko a quelques
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|                    Mo. En theorie, des files plus profondes ajoutent un
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|                    peu de latence de mise en tampon en saturation
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|                    prolongee ; a ces tailles-la, cela ne se sent pas.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.024|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.025|  Defaut Windows  : Depend du pilote et du chipset. Une Intel I211, par
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.026|                    exemple, est livree a 256 en reception et 512 en
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.027|                    emission. La vraie valeur de votre carte est dans
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.028|                    Ndi\Params\*ReceiveBuffers\default.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.029|
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.031|    1024                 : La taille d'anneau demandee. La valeur
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.032|                           reellement ecrite est bornee a Ndi\Params\<mot-
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.033|                           cle>\max et arrondie vers le bas selon \step :
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.034|                           une carte dont le maximum est 512 recoit 512,
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.035|                           et le journal indique ce qui a ete ecrit et non
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.036|                           ce qui a ete demande.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.037|    256 or 512           : Tailles livrees courantes. Une Intel I211, par
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.038|                           exemple, est livree avec 256 descripteurs en
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.039|                           reception et 512 en emission.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.040|    80 or 128            : Petits anneaux. Moins de pool non pagine
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.041|                           reserve, moins de marge : une rafale qui arrive
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.042|                           plus vite que le pilote ne vide l'anneau est
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.043|                           jetee, et TCP retransmet.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.044|    SKIP                 : Aucun des deux mots-cles n'est ecrit ; les
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.045|                           tailles d'anneau du pilote restent celles
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.046|                           d'origine et aucun pool non pagine
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.047|                           supplementaire n'est reserve.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Gaming et serveur passent a 1024 parce que les
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|                         deux saturent reellement le lien : un
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|                         telechargement Steam et un client torrent a fond
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                         sont exactement la charge en rafale qu'un anneau
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|                         plus profond absorbe. Bureautique et portable
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|                         restent sur SKIP parce qu'ils n'atteignent jamais
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|                         le seuil de perte : la seule chose qu'ils
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|                         recolteraient serait une part de pool non pagine
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                         reservee en permanence - de la memoire qui ne
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                         sort jamais, ce qui pese davantage sur un
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|                         portable qu'ailleurs. Windows reste sur SKIP
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|                         parce que la taille livree depend du chipset et
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|                         que ce chemin ne la lit pas.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|  Non verifie (en)  : Per-adapter shipped buffer values vary too much
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|                      between chipsets to state one here. OPTY reads the
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|                      driver's max and step at runtime but does not read
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|                      the driver's default, so it cannot report how far
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|                      above factory the written value is.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*ReceiveBuffers" "1024" and
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|                    "*TransmitBuffers" "1024", clamped to
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max by :nicclamp and rounded down
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.024|                    to \step by :nicround (OPTY.bat ~2866-2867, helpers
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.025|                    ~3470-3480)
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|RAISE THE NIC RECEIVE AND TRANSMIT BUFFERS
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|  What it is      : Descriptor rings are the queues the card uses to hand
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                    packets to the driver and take packets from it. More
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|                    entries means more headroom before a burst of traffic
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|                    has to be dropped.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|  Actual effect   : Requests 1024 for both *ReceiveBuffers and
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                    *TransmitBuffers, then clamps to the driver's own
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                    maximum and rounds down to its step - so the value
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|                    actually written is frequently far below 1024, and the
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|                    log says which.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|  Gain            : Fewer dropped packets under burst load. The cases
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|                    where it matters are a saturated link, a busy LAN
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|                    transfer, or a torrent client at full tilt. On an idle
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|                    or lightly loaded machine you will not measure
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|                    anything at all.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|  Cost            : A little more non-paged pool permanently reserved by
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|                    the driver - kilobytes to low megabytes. In theory
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|                    deeper queues add a little buffering latency under
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|                    sustained saturation; at these sizes it is not
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.024|                    something you will feel.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.025|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.026|  Windows default : Driver- and chipset-dependent. An Intel I211 for
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.027|                    instance ships 256 receive and 512 transmit. Your
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.028|                    card's real default is at
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.029|                    Ndi\Params\*ReceiveBuffers\default.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.030|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.032|    1024                 : The requested ring size. The value actually
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.033|                           written is clamped to Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.034|                           and rounded down to \step, so a card whose
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.035|                           maximum is 512 gets 512 - and the log line says
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.036|                           what was written rather than what was asked
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.037|                           for.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.038|    256 or 512           : Common shipped sizes. An Intel I211 for
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.039|                           instance ships 256 receive and 512 transmit
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.040|                           descriptors.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.041|    80 or 128            : Small rings. Less non-paged pool reserved, less
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.042|                           headroom: a burst arriving faster than the
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.043|                           driver drains the ring is dropped, and TCP
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.044|                           retransmits.
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.045|    SKIP                 : Neither keyword is written; the driver's ring
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.046|                           sizes stay as shipped and no extra non-paged
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.047|                           pool is reserved.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|  Why these profiles : Gaming and server say 1024 because both actually
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|                       saturate the link: a Steam download and a torrent
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|                       client at full tilt are precisely the burst load a
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                       deeper ring absorbs. Office and laptop say SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|                       because they never reach the drop threshold, so the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|                       only thing they would collect is a permanently
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|                       reserved slice of non-paged pool - memory that
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|                       never pages out, which matters more on the laptop
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                       than anywhere else. Windows says SKIP because the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                       shipped size is per chipset and this path does not
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|                       read it.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|  Unverified      : Per-adapter shipped buffer values vary too much
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|                    between chipsets to state one here. OPTY reads the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|                    driver's max and step at runtime but does not read the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|                    driver's default, so it cannot report how far above
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|                    factory the written value is.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*ReceiveBuffers" "1024" and
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|                    "*TransmitBuffers" "1024", clamped to
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max by :nicclamp and rounded down
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|                    to \step by :nicround (OPTY.bat ~2866-2867, helpers
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|                    ~3470-3480)
::
:: ---- net.nic.eee.off (preference) -----------------------------------
::P|net.nic.eee.off|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.001|ETHERNET A ECONOMIE D'ENERGIE DESACTIVE
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.003|  Ce que c est    : L'Energy Efficient Ethernet (802.3az) laisse la puce
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.004|                    PHY dormir entre les trames. Chaque fabricant
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.005|                    l'appelle autrement, et OPTY ecrit 0 dans le mot-cle
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.006|                    que le pilote expose.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.008|  Effet reel      : Le port cesse d'annoncer et d'entrer en veille basse
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.009|                    consommation : le lien reste pleinement eveille, sans
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.010|                    transition de reveil entre les trames.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.011|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.012|  Gain            : C'est un reglage de stabilite du lien, pas un remede
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.013|                    au ping. Si votre lien renegocie, tombe une seconde ou
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.014|                    se cale a la mauvaise vitesse avec un commutateur
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.015|                    precis, l'EEE est un vrai suspect et le couper est une
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.016|                    vraie solution. Si votre lien est stable, le gain
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.017|                    honnete est nul : pendant une partie, le lien n'est
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.018|                    jamais inactif assez longtemps pour entrer en veille,
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.019|                    il n'y a donc aucune transition de reveil a retarder.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.020|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.021|  Cout            : Environ 0,5 W par port, en continu. Et cette etape
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.022|                    n'est volontairement PAS annulee par la restauration
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.023|                    reseau : le filtre de restauration exclut les mots-
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.024|                    cles EEE et Wake pour ne jamais reactiver en douce ce
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.025|                    que vous aviez coupe. L'annuler demande de passer par
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.026|                    le Gestionnaire de peripheriques, onglet Avance, a la
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.027|                    main.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.028|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.029|  Defaut Windows  : Active sur les pilotes qui l'exposent - l'EEE d'Intel
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.030|                    comme le Green Ethernet de Realtek sont actifs
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.031|                    d'origine.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.032|
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.034|    0                    : La puce PHY cesse d'annoncer 802.3az et n'entre
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.035|                           jamais en veille basse consommation. Le lien
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.036|                           reste pleinement eveille entre les trames :
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.037|                           plus de transition de reveil, ni de
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.038|                           renegociation declenchee par une telle
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.039|                           transition.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.040|    1                    : Ethernet a economie d'energie active, ce qui
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.041|                           est l'etat livre par tous les pilotes qui
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.042|                           l'exposent. La PHY passe en veille basse
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.043|                           consommation quand il n'y a pas de trafic et se
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.044|                           reveille a la trame suivante. Chaque fabricant
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.045|                           nomme le mot-cle autrement : *EEE,
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.046|                           EEELinkAdvertisement, AdvancedEEE,
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.047|                           EnableGreenEthernet.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.048|    SKIP                 : Aucun mot-cle n'est ecrit ; l'EEE reste tel que
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.049|                           le pilote l'a livre, et le chemin d'annulation
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.050|                           automatique reste intact pour le reste.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a SKIP, et c'est une
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.002|                         reponse assumee, pas une derobade. Cela se
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.003|                         diagnostique par symptome et non par usage : la
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.004|                         machine qui doit dire oui est celle dont le lien
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.005|                         tombe ou se cale a la mauvaise vitesse avec un
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.006|                         commutateur precis, et ce peut etre aussi bien un
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.007|                         PC de jeu qu'un poste bureautique ou un portable.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.008|                         Le portable a une raison de plus de ne pas y
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.009|                         toucher : c'est un cout de batterie continu sans
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.010|                         contrepartie. Dites oui uniquement si vous avez
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.011|                         constate la panne.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.013|  Non verifie (en)  : The 0.5 W per port figure is the commonly cited
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.014|                      802.3az saving for a gigabit copper PHY, not a
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.015|                      measurement taken on this machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.016|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.017|  Cible           : call :nicset for EEELinkAdvertisement=0, *EEE=0,
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.018|                    EnableGreenEthernet=0 and AdvancedEEE=0 - whichever
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.019|                    the driver exposes (OPTY.bat ~2869-2873). Deliberately
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.020|                    excluded from the :net_restore keyword filter, so the
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.021|                    network Restore never undoes it.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.001|ENERGY EFFICIENT ETHERNET OFF
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.003|  What it is      : 802.3az Energy Efficient Ethernet lets the PHY sleep
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.004|                    between frames. Vendors spell it differently, and OPTY
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.005|                    writes 0 to whichever keyword the driver exposes.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.006|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.007|  Actual effect   : The port stops advertising and entering Low Power
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.008|                    Idle, so the link stays fully awake and there is no
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.009|                    wake-from-idle transition between frames.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.010|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.011|  Gain            : This is a link-stability setting, not a ping fix. If
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.012|                    your link renegotiates, drops for a second, or trains
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.013|                    at the wrong speed with a particular switch, EEE is a
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.014|                    real suspect and turning it off is a real fix. If your
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.015|                    link is stable the honest gain is zero - during a game
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.016|                    the link is never idle long enough to enter Low Power
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.017|                    Idle in the first place, so there is nothing for the
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.018|                    wake transition to delay.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.019|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.020|  Cost            : About 0.5 W per port, continuously. And this step is
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.021|                    deliberately NOT undone by the network Restore: the
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.022|                    restore filter excludes the EEE and Wake keywords so
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.023|                    it never silently re-enables what you turned off.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.024|                    Undoing it means Device Manager, Advanced tab, by
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.025|                    hand.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.026|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.027|  Windows default : Enabled on drivers that expose it - Intel's EEE and
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.028|                    Realtek's Green Ethernet both ship on.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.029|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.031|    0                    : The PHY stops advertising 802.3az and never
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.032|                           enters Low Power Idle. The link stays fully
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.033|                           awake between frames, so there is no wake-from-
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.034|                           idle transition and no renegotiation triggered
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.035|                           by one.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.036|    1                    : Energy Efficient Ethernet enabled, which is how
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.037|                           every driver that exposes it ships. The PHY
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.038|                           drops to Low Power Idle when there is no
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.039|                           traffic and wakes on the next frame. Vendors
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.040|                           spell the keyword differently: *EEE,
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.041|                           EEELinkAdvertisement, AdvancedEEE,
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.042|                           EnableGreenEthernet.
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.043|    SKIP                 : No keyword is written; EEE stays as the driver
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.044|                           shipped it, and the automatic undo path stays
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.045|                           intact for everything else.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical SKIP columns, and that is the honest
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.002|                       answer rather than a shrug. This is diagnosed by
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.003|                       symptom, not by usage: the machine that should say
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.004|                       yes is the one whose link actually flaps or trains
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.005|                       at the wrong speed with a specific switch, and that
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.006|                       machine can equally be a gaming PC, an office PC or
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.007|                       a laptop. The laptop has one extra reason to leave
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.008|                       it alone - it is a continuous battery cost with no
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.009|                       counterpart. Say yes only if you have seen the
