:: ---- net.nic.flowcontrol.profile (preference) -----------------------
::P|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile|3|3|3|3|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|CONTROLE DE FLUX ETHERNET (PAUSE 802.3X)
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|  Ce que c est    : Le controle de flux permet a un switch d'envoyer une
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                    trame PAUSE demandant a votre carte d'arreter
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                    d'emettre un instant quand sa memoire tampon se
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|                    remplit. OPTY ecrit 3 pour les profils Equilibre et
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|                    Debit, et 0 pour le profil Faible latence.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|  Effet reel      : Active, une trame PAUSE peut bloquer votre envoi
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|                    jusqu'a environ 33,6 ms en gigabit. Desactive, le
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|                    switch jette ces paquets a la place et TCP les
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|                    retransmet.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|  Gain            : Seulement si quelque chose sur votre reseau local
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|                    envoie reellement des trames PAUSE, ce que la plupart
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                    des switchs et box grand public ne font jamais. Quand
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                    c'est le cas, le desactiver remplace un blocage
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                    d'envoi de quelques millisecondes par des paquets
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|                    perdus, ce qu'un jeu supporte mieux qu'un blocage. Si
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|                    rien n'envoie de PAUSE, l'effet mesure est exactement
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|                    nul, et le script le dit au lieu de s'attribuer un
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|                    gain.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.024|  Cout            : En saturation, vous echangez une breve pause contre de
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.025|                    vraies pertes de paquets, ce qui penalise les gros
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.026|                    envois et les sauvegardes. Et la restauration reseau
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.027|                    ne couvre pas *FlowControl : si vous ecrivez 0,
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.028|                    l'annulation se fait a la main dans le Gestionnaire de
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.029|                    peripheriques.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.030|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.031|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote, souvent 3 (Rx et Tx
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.032|                    actives) sur les cartes Intel. La vraie valeur de
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.033|                    votre carte est dans Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.034|
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.036|    0                    : Desactive. La carte n'envoie ni n'honore les
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.037|                           trames PAUSE 802.3x. Un switch dont la memoire
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.038|                           tampon se remplit jette vos paquets au lieu de
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.039|                           vous bloquer, et TCP les retransmet.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.040|    1                    : Tx Enabled. La carte envoie des trames PAUSE
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.041|                           quand son propre anneau de reception se
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.042|                           remplit, mais ignore celles qu'elle recoit.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.043|                           C'est l'option chirurgicale si ce que vous
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.044|                           voulez est que votre envoi ne soit jamais
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.045|                           bloque - OPTY ne la propose pas, mais c'est une
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.046|                           valeur valide du mot-cle.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.047|    2                    : Rx Enabled. La carte honore les trames PAUSE
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.048|                           recues mais n'en envoie jamais.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.049|    3                    : Rx et Tx Enabled, la valeur livree habituelle
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.050|                           sur les cartes Intel. Une trame PAUSE recue
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.051|                           peut bloquer votre emission jusqu'a environ
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.052|                           33,6 ms en gigabit.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.053|    SKIP                 : Le mot-cle n'est pas ecrit ; il reste a
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.054|                           Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default, et l'absence
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.055|                           d'annulation automatique ne devient jamais un
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.056|                           probleme.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 3, gaming compris,
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|                         parce que le choix Faible latence n'a aucun
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|                         mecanisme tant qu'un switch de votre reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                         n'envoie pas reellement des trames PAUSE - et si
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                         aucun ne le fait, ecrire 0 n'apporte rien et vous
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|                         coute l'annulation automatique. La colonne
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|                         serveur est la moins ambigue des quatre : elle
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|                         fait de gros envois, ou une breve pause coute
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|                         moins cher qu'une retransmission. Windows est
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|                         SKIP parce que 3 est une valeur du pilote et non
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|                         de Windows. Ne choisissez 0 que si vous
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|                         administrez un switch manageable et que vous le
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|                         voyez envoyer des trames de pause.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether your LAN sends PAUSE frames at all cannot be
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                      determined from a batch script. The only honest
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                      signal is the adapter's own Pause Frames Received
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                      counter, which the driver does not expose through
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|                      any scriptable interface - so OPTY deliberately
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|                      makes no claim about whether the change helped.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*FlowControl" - 3 in the
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|                    Balanced branch and in :net_prof_thr, 0 in
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.024|                    :net_prof_lat (OPTY.bat ~2898, ~2903, ~2916). Excluded
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.025|                    from the :net_restore keyword filter.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|ETHERNET FLOW CONTROL (802.3X PAUSE)
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|  What it is      : Flow control lets a switch send a PAUSE frame telling
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                    your card to stop transmitting for a moment when the
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                    switch's buffer fills. OPTY writes 3 for the Balanced
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|                    and Throughput profiles and 0 for the Low latency
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|                    profile.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|  Actual effect   : With it on, a PAUSE frame can stall your upload for up
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|                    to about 33.6 ms at gigabit. With it off, the switch
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|                    drops those packets instead and TCP retransmits them.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|  Gain            : Only if something on your LAN actually sends PAUSE
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|                    frames, and most consumer switches and routers never
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|                    do. When one does, turning it off replaces an
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                    occasional multi-millisecond upload stall with dropped
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                    packets, which a game tolerates better than a stall.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                    If nothing sends PAUSE frames the measured effect is
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|                    exactly zero, and the script says so instead of
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|                    claiming a win.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|  Cost            : Under saturation you trade a brief pause for real
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|                    packet loss, which hurts bulk uploads and backups. And
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.024|                    the network Restore does not cover *FlowControl, so if
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.025|                    you write 0 you undo it by hand in Device Manager.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.026|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.027|  Windows default : Driver-supplied, commonly 3 (Rx and Tx Enabled) on
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.028|                    Intel adapters. Your card's real default is at
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.029|                    Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.030|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.032|    0                    : Disabled. The card neither sends nor honours
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.033|                           802.3x PAUSE frames. A switch whose buffer
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.034|                           fills drops your packets instead of stalling
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.035|                           you, and TCP retransmits them.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.036|    1                    : Tx Enabled. The card sends PAUSE frames when
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.037|                           its own receive ring fills, but ignores
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.038|                           incoming ones. This is the surgical option if
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.039|                           what you want is never to have your upload
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.040|                           stalled - OPTY does not offer it, but it is a
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.041|                           valid keyword value.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.042|    2                    : Rx Enabled. The card honours incoming PAUSE
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.043|                           frames but never sends them.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.044|    3                    : Rx and Tx Enabled, the usual shipped value on
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.045|                           Intel adapters. A received PAUSE frame can
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.046|                           stall your transmit for up to about 33.6 ms at
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.047|                           gigabit.
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.048|    SKIP                 : The keyword is not written; it stays at
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.049|                           Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default, and the
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.050|                           missing automatic undo never becomes a problem.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 3, gaming included,
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|                       because the low-latency choice has no mechanism
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|                       unless a switch on your LAN really sends PAUSE
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                       frames - and if none does, writing 0 buys nothing
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                       and costs you the automatic undo. The server column
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|                       is the least ambiguous of the four: it does bulk
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|                       uploads, where a brief pause is cheaper than
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|                       retransmitting. Windows is SKIP because 3 is the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|                       driver's value rather than a Windows one. Choose 0
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|                       only if you administer a managed switch and can see
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|                       it sending pause frames.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|  Unverified      : Whether your LAN sends PAUSE frames at all cannot be
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|                    determined from a batch script. The only honest signal
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|                    is the adapter's own Pause Frames Received counter,
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                    which the driver does not expose through any
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                    scriptable interface - so OPTY deliberately makes no
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                    claim about whether the change helped.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*FlowControl" - 3 in the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|                    Balanced branch and in :net_prof_thr, 0 in
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|                    :net_prof_lat (OPTY.bat ~2898, ~2903, ~2916). Excluded
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|                    from the :net_restore keyword filter.
