::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.001|NETWORKTHROTTLINGINDEX BY PROFILE
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.002|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.003|  What it is      : An MMCSS setting that caps non-multimedia network
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.004|                    processing at roughly 10 packets per millisecond -
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.005|                    about 120 Mbit/s - WHILE multimedia is playing, so
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.006|                    audio threads are not starved by network interrupt
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.007|                    work. It does nothing when nothing is playing.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.008|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.009|  Actual effect   : The balanced and low-latency branches write 10, the
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.010|                    Windows default, rather than skipping the write. The
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.011|                    throughput branch writes 0xFFFFFFFF, lifting the cap
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.012|                    so a big download can use the whole link even while
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.013|                    audio plays.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.014|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.015|  Gain            : For 10: none over a stock machine, since it is the
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.016|                    default - the value exists to repair machines where a
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.017|                    guide wrote 0xFFFFFFFF. For 0xFFFFFFFF: full download
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.018|                    speed while music or video plays, genuinely visible on
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.019|                    a connection above about 120 Mbit/s and invisible
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.020|                    below it.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.021|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.022|  Cost            : For 0xFFFFFFFF: risk of audio crackle under heavy
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.023|                    network load, and you remove the protection MMCSS
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.024|                    exists for. That matters far more for a DAW or a
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.025|                    streamer than for background music.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.026|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.027|  Windows default : 10 (0x0000000a).
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.028|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.029|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.030|    10 (0x0000000a)      : Shipped value. While an MMCSS multimedia stream
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.031|                           is registered on this machine, non-multimedia
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.032|                           network processing is capped at 10 packets per
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.033|                           millisecond - about 10000 packets per second,
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.034|                           roughly 120 Mbit/s with full-size frames. With
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.035|                           no audio or video playing, the cap does
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.036|                           nothing.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.037|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.038|        The cap is lifted entirely, so a download can use the whole link
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.039|        even while audio plays. Network DPC work can then take time from
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.040|        the audio thread, which is the protection you are giving up.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.041|    any other N          : The cap becomes N packets per millisecond.
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.042|                           Below 10 it is tighter than shipped, above 10
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.043|                           it is looser without being removed.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.001|  Why these profiles : Only the server column differs, and for a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.002|                       mechanical reason rather than a taste. The cap
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.003|                       engages only while an MMCSS multimedia stream is
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.004|                       registered on THIS machine, and a box that hosts
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.005|                       torrents, Plex and game servers is serving media to
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.006|                       other machines rather than playing it locally - so
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.007|                       it rarely hits the cap at all, and on the occasions
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.008|                       it does, throughput is exactly what it exists for.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.009|                       Gaming, office and laptop keep 10 because all three
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.010|                       play audio constantly and crackle under load is
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.011|                       precisely what the cap prevents. Windows keeps 10
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.012|                       because 10 is what ships. Honest test either way: a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.013|                       large download with audio playing, before and
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.014|                       after.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.015|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.016|  Target          : call :regset "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.017|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile"
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.018|                    "NetworkThrottlingIndex" REG_DWORD <10 or 4294967295>
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.019|                    - 10 in the Balanced and Low latency branches,
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.020|                    4294967295 in :net_prof_thr (OPTY.bat ~2899, ~2911,
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.021|                    ~2917)
::
:: ---- net.tcp.globals.assert.profile (repair) --------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|REGLAGES TCP GLOBAUX REAFFIRMES APRES UN PROFIL RESEAU
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|  Ce que c est    : Les memes trois reglages TCP valables pour toute la
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                    machine, reecrits a la fin de l'application d'un
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                    profil reseau pour que le profil et la pile soient
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|                    d'accord : ajustement automatique de la fenetre de
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|                    reception, heuristiques de mise a l'echelle, et RSS au
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|                    niveau TCP.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh, valables pour toute la machine,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|                    effet immediat, sans redemarrage. Sur une machine
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|                    d'origine, les trois ne changent rien : la vraie
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|                    valeur de l'etape est d'annuler un tweak precedent, le
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|                    plus souvent autotuninglevel=disabled.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|  Gain            : Sur une machine massacree par un ancien script de
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|                    tweak, remettre l'autotuning sur normal est un gain de
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|                    debit important et facilement mesurable sur les
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|                    chemins a forte latence ou a haut debit. Sur une
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|                    machine intacte, les trois ecritures ne changent rien,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|                    et il faut le dire franchement : vous ne mesurerez
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|                    aucune difference.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.023|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.024|  Cout            : Quasiment aucun, puisque aucune des trois valeurs ne
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.025|                    s'ecarte de ce que Windows livre. Si un site precis ou
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.026|                    un VPN devient capricieux ensuite, netsh int tcp set
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.027|                    heuristics default est la seule ligne a remettre.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.028|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.029|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal, rss=enabled et les
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.030|                    heuristiques de mise a l'echelle desactivees sont
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.031|                    toutes des valeurs Windows d'origine - les
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.032|                    heuristiques sont livrees desactivees depuis Windows
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.033|                    8.1. netsh int tcp set heuristics default rend la
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.034|                    decision a Windows.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.035|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.036|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.037|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.038|        La fenetre de reception grandit jusqu'a ce que le chemin exige.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.039|        Valeur d'origine, et celle que les guides d'optimisation
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.040|        remplacent le plus souvent.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.041|    autotuninglevel=disabled, highlyrestricted, restricted :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.042|        Des plafonds de plus en plus bas sur la fenetre de reception. Sur
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.043|        un chemin long ou rapide, chacun est une coupe de debit, invisible
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.044|        en local et severe a distance.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.045|    autotuninglevel=experimental :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.046|        Un plafond extreme. Gaspille du pool non pagine sans aucun
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.047|        benefice sur un chemin domestique.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.048|    heuristics=disabled  : Windows cesse de remettre en cause votre niveau
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.049|                           d'autotuning a partir de son historique par
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.050|                           chemin.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.051|    heuristics=enabled   : Windows peut reduire en silence le facteur
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.052|                           d'echelle sur les chemins qu'il juge
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.053|                           problematiques.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.054|    heuristics=default   : Rend la decision a la politique interne de
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.055|                           Windows, qui est disabled : le comportement est
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.056|                           donc identique.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.057|    rss=enabled / disabled / default :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.058|        Enabled repartit le traitement TCP en reception sur plusieurs
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.059|        coeurs, disabled le fixe sur un seul, default laisse le choix a
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.060|        Windows, dont la politique est enabled.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques. Ce sont les valeurs
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|                         livrees : aucun profil d'usage n'a de raison d'en
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|                         vouloir d'autres, et la colonne Windows les veut
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                         aussi puisque les ecrire est precisement la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                         maniere de ramener une machine trafiquee a l'etat
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|                         d'origine. Des versions anterieures de cette
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|                         fiche affirmaient que heuristics=disabled etait
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|                         un ecart par rapport au standard et
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|                         construisaient tout un raisonnement dessus :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|                         c'etait faux, et plus rien ici n'en depend.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|  Non verifie (en)  : That Window Scaling Heuristics ships disabled rests
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|                      on documented Windows 8.1-and-later behaviour plus a
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|                      live Windows 11 25H2 reading, not on a Microsoft
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|                      statement for 25H2. netsh reports it separately from
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|                      the global parameters, which is why earlier versions
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|                      of this card guessed the opposite.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|  Cible           : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics disabled ; netsh int tcp
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|                    set global rss=enabled - run at :net_prof_done, after
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|                    a network profile has been applied (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.023|                    ~2923-2925)
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|TCP GLOBALS RE-ASSERTED AFTER A NETWORK PROFILE
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|  What it is      : The same three machine-wide TCP settings, written
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                    again at the end of applying a network profile so the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                    profile and the stack agree: receive-window
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|                    autotuning, Window Scaling Heuristics, and RSS at the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|                    TCP layer.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls, machine-wide and immediate, no
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|                    reboot. On a stock machine all three are no-ops - the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|                    step's real value is undoing a previous tweak, most
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|                    often autotuninglevel=disabled.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|  Gain            : On a machine a previous tweak script mangled, putting
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|                    autotuning back to normal is a large and easily
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|                    measurable throughput fix on high-latency or high-
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|                    bandwidth paths. On an untouched machine all three
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|                    writes change nothing, and the honest answer is that
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|                    you will measure no difference.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|  Cost            : Practically none, because none of the three departs
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|                    from what Windows ships. If a specific site or VPN
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.023|                    turns flaky afterwards, netsh int tcp set heuristics
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.024|                    default is the single line to put back.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.025|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.026|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal, rss=enabled and Window Scaling
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.027|                    Heuristics disabled are all Windows defaults - WSH has
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.028|                    shipped disabled since Windows 8.1. netsh int tcp set
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.029|                    heuristics default hands the decision back to Windows.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.030|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.032|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.033|        The receive window scales up to what the path needs. Shipped
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.034|        value, and the one optimizer guides most often replace.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.035|    autotuninglevel=disabled, highlyrestricted, restricted :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.036|        Progressively smaller ceilings on the receive window. On a long or
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.037|        fast path each one is a throughput cut, invisible locally and
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.038|        severe over distance.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.039|    autotuninglevel=experimental :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.040|        An extreme ceiling. Wastes non-paged pool with no benefit on any
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.041|        home path.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.042|    heuristics=disabled  : Windows stops second-guessing your autotuning
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.043|                           level from stored per-path history.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.044|    heuristics=enabled   : Windows may silently reduce the window scale
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.045|                           factor on paths it decides are problematic.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.046|    heuristics=default   : Hands the decision back to Windows built-in
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.047|                           policy - which is disabled, so the behaviour is
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.048|                           the same.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.049|    rss=enabled / disabled / default :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.050|        Enabled spreads TCP receive processing over several cores,
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.051|        disabled pins it to one, default hands the choice to Windows,
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.052|        whose policy is enabled.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns. These are the shipped
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|                       values, so no usage profile has a reason to want
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|                       anything else, and the Windows column wants them
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                       too because writing them is how a mangled machine
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                       gets back to shipped. Earlier drafts of this card
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|                       claimed heuristics=disabled was a departure from
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|                       stock and built a gain and cost argument on it -
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|                       that was wrong, and nothing here rests on it any
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|                       more.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|  Unverified      : That Window Scaling Heuristics ships disabled rests on
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|                    documented Windows 8.1-and-later behaviour plus a live
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|                    Windows 11 25H2 reading, not on a Microsoft statement
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|                    for 25H2. netsh reports it separately from the global
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|                    parameters, which is why earlier versions of this card
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|                    guessed the opposite.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|  Target          : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics disabled ; netsh int tcp
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|                    set global rss=enabled - run at :net_prof_done, after
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|                    a network profile has been applied (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|                    ~2923-2925)
::
:: ---- net.nic.restart.profile (preference) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restart.profile|RESTART|SKIP|RESTART|RESTART|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.001|REDEMARRER LA CARTE RESEAU MAINTENANT
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.003|  Ce que c est    : pnputil /restart-device sur la carte, pour que NDIS
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.004|                    relise tous les mots-cles qui viennent d'etre ecrits
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.005|                    au lieu d'attendre le prochain demarrage.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.006|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.007|  Effet reel      : La carte tombe et remonte en 2 a 4 secondes environ,
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.008|                    et les nouveaux reglages deviennent actifs tout de
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.009|                    suite.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.010|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.011|  Gain            : Pas besoin de redemarrer pour tester le profil. C'est
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.012|                    tout l'interet : les reglages sont identiques dans les
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.013|                    deux cas.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.014|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.015|  Cout            : Le lien tombe. Cela tue un telechargement en cours
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.016|                    sans reprise, coupe un VPN, met fin a une session RDP
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.017|                    ou SSH, et vous deconnecte d'un jeu en ligne ou d'un
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.018|                    appel. Une carte Wi-Fi doit en plus se reassocier.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.019|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.020|  Defaut Windows  : Ce n'est pas un reglage, c'est une action.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.021|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.022|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.023|    RESTART              : pnputil arrete et relance le peripherique
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.024|                           reseau. NDIS relit tous les mots-cles pendant
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.025|                           l'initialisation, les reglages deviennent donc
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.026|                           actifs. Le lien tombe environ 2 a 4 secondes.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.027|    SKIP                 : Rien ne se passe maintenant. Les memes mots-
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.028|                           cles sont lus au prochain demarrage, avec
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.029|                           exactement le meme resultat : les reglages sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.030|                           identiques dans les deux cas.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : La colonne serveur est a SKIP, et c'est elle qui
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.002|                         porte le raisonnement : cette machine est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.003|                         generalement administree a distance, elle partage
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.004|                         ou sert pendant que vous travaillez dessus, et un
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.005|                         trou de 2 a 4 secondes coute de vraies sessions -
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.006|                         elle peut attendre un redemarrage planifie.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.007|                         Gaming, bureautique et portable sont des machines
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.008|                         devant lesquelles vous etes assis : redemarrer la
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.009|                         carte est gratuit et vous evite un reboot. La
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.010|                         colonne Windows est a SKIP parce que Windows ne
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.011|                         redemarre jamais une carte de lui-meme.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.013|  Cible           : the prompt "Restart the adapter now? 1 (Yes) - 0 (No,
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.014|                    on next reboot)" at the end of :net_apply, which calls
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.015|                    :nicrestart -> pnputil /restart-device "%NPNP%"
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.016|                    (OPTY.bat ~2930-2935, helper :nicrestart ~3500-3510)
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.001|RESTART THE NETWORK ADAPTER NOW
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.003|  What it is      : pnputil /restart-device on the adapter, so NDIS re-
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.004|                    reads all the keywords just written instead of waiting
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.005|                    for the next boot.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.006|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.007|  Actual effect   : The adapter goes down and comes back up in roughly 2
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.008|                    to 4 seconds, and the new settings become active
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.009|                    immediately.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.010|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.011|  Gain            : You do not have to reboot to test the profile. That is
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.012|                    the entire benefit - the settings themselves are
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.013|                    identical either way.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.014|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.015|  Cost            : The link drops. That kills an in-flight download
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.016|                    without resume, drops a VPN, ends an RDP or SSH
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.017|                    session, and disconnects you from an online game or a
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.018|                    call. A Wi-Fi adapter also has to re-associate.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.019|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.020|  Windows default : Not a setting - it is an action.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.021|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.022|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.023|    RESTART              : pnputil stops and restarts the adapter device.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.024|                           NDIS re-reads every keyword during
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.025|                           initialisation, so the settings become live.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.026|                           The link is down for roughly 2 to 4 seconds.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.027|    SKIP                 : Nothing happens now. The same keywords are read
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.028|                           at the next boot, with exactly the same result
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.029|                           - the settings are identical either way.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.001|  Why these profiles : The server column is SKIP, and it is the one that
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.002|                       carries the argument: that machine is usually
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.003|                       administered over the network, is seeding or
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.004|                       serving while you work on it, and a 2 to 4 second
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.005|                       blackout costs real sessions - it can wait for a
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.006|                       scheduled reboot. Gaming, office and laptop are
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.007|                       machines you are sitting in front of, so restarting
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.008|                       now is free and saves you a reboot. The Windows
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.009|                       column is SKIP because Windows never restarts an
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.010|                       adapter on its own.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.012|  Target          : the prompt "Restart the adapter now? 1 (Yes) - 0 (No,
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.013|                    on next reboot)" at the end of :net_apply, which calls
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.014|                    :nicrestart -> pnputil /restart-device "%NPNP%"
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.015|                    (OPTY.bat ~2930-2935, helper :nicrestart ~3500-3510)
::
:: ---- net.nic.restore.profile (repair) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restore.profile|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.001|RETABLIR LES VALEURS D'USINE DU PILOTE RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.003|  Ce que c est    : L'annulation du profil reseau. Pour chacun des 14
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                    mots-cles qu'OPTY ecrit, il lit Ndi\Params\<mot-
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.005|                    cle>\default dans le pilote lui-meme et reecrit cette
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.006|                    valeur - jamais un chiffre en dur.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.008|  Effet reel      : Moderation d'interruptions, ITR, LSO, toutes les
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                    sommes de controle, RSS, nombre de files, jumbo et les
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.010|                    deux tailles de tampons reviennent exactement a ce que
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.011|                    livrait votre pilote. Les reglages TCP globaux sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.012|                    rendus a Windows et un redemarrage de la carte est
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.013|                    propose.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.014|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.015|  Gain            : Un retour propre au comportement d'usine de la carte
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.016|                    sans reinstaller le pilote - pratique quand vous
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.017|                    deboguez un souci reseau et voulez sortir la carte de
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.018|                    l'equation. C'est correct sur n'importe quel chipset
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.019|                    par construction, puisque les valeurs sont lues sur
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.020|                    votre propre machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.021|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.022|  Cout            : Vous perdez le profil applique. A noter, ce qu'il ne
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.023|                    touche volontairement PAS : Wake-on-LAN, l'Ethernet a
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.024|                    economie d'energie et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.025|                    exclus du filtre, parce qu'une version precedente les
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.026|                    retablissait aussi et rallumait en douce des choses
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.027|                    coupees expres. Effet de bord : si OPTY a desactive
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.028|                    l'EEE ou le controle de flux, ils restent desactives.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.029|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.030|  Defaut Windows  : Par construction, c'est la valeur d'usine propre a
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.031|                    chaque pilote.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.032|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.034|    APPLY                : Les 14 mots-cles reviennent a la valeur que
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.035|                           votre propre pilote publie comme defaut :
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.036|                           moderation d'interruptions et ITR, les deux
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.037|                           mots-cles LSO, les cinq sommes de controle, le
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.038|                           RSS et le nombre de files, les trames jumbo et
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.039|                           les deux tailles de tampons.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.040|    SKIP                 : Le profil que vous avez applique reste
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.041|                           exactement en place.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.042|    no default published : Il n'y a pas de troisieme choix a faire : si le
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.043|                           pilote ne publie aucun defaut pour un mot-cle,
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.044|                           la fonction laisse ce mot-cle tranquille au
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.045|                           lieu d'inventer un nombre.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les quatre colonnes d'usage sont a SKIP pour une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.002|                         raison evidente : lancer l'annulation pendant une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.003|                         passe de configuration ne fait qu'annuler ce que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                         vous etes venu faire. La colonne Windows est a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.005|                         APPLY, et c'est la seule etape de toute la
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.006|                         section reseau qui puisse honnetement pretendre
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.007|                         produire l'etat livre, puisqu'elle le lit au lieu
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.008|                         de le supposer. Dites oui en dehors d'une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                         configuration : en depannage, avant de ceder le
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.010|                         PC, ou si le profil n'a rien apporte.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.012|  Cible           : :net_restore - reg query "%NICKEY%\Ndi\Params"
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.013|                    filtered by findstr /i /e to the 14 keywords
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.014|                    :net_apply writes, then call :nicdefault for each,
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.015|                    which reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default and writes it
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.016|                    back as REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995)
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.001|RESTORE THE DRIVER'S OWN NIC DEFAULTS
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.003|  What it is      : The undo for the network profile. For each of the 14
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                    keywords OPTY writes, it reads
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.005|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default from the driver itself
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.006|                    and writes that back - never a hardcoded number.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.008|  Actual effect   : Interrupt moderation, ITR, LSO, all checksum offloads,
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                    RSS, queue count, jumbo and both buffer sizes go back
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.010|                    to exactly what your driver shipped with. The TCP
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.011|                    globals are handed back to Windows and an adapter
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.012|                    restart is offered.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.013|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.014|  Gain            : A clean way back to factory NIC behaviour without
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.015|                    reinstalling the driver - useful when you are
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.016|                    debugging a network problem and want the adapter out
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.017|                    of the equation. It is correct on any chipset by
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.018|                    construction, because it reads the values from your
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.019|                    own machine.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.020|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.021|  Cost            : You lose the profile you applied. Note what it
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.022|                    deliberately does NOT touch: Wake-on-LAN, Energy
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.023|                    Efficient Ethernet and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown are
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.024|                    excluded from the filter, because an earlier version
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.025|                    restored those too and silently switched back on
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.026|                    things people had turned off on purpose. The side
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.027|                    effect is that if OPTY turned EEE or Flow Control off,
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.028|                    this leaves them off.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.029|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.030|  Windows default : By construction, this IS the per-driver default.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.031|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.033|    APPLY                : All 14 keywords go back to the value your own
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.034|                           driver publishes as its default: interrupt
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.035|                           moderation and ITR, both LSO keywords, all five
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.036|                           checksum offloads, RSS and the queue count,
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.037|                           jumbo frames and both buffer sizes.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.038|    SKIP                 : The profile you applied stays exactly as it is.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.039|    no default published : There is no third choice to make: if the driver
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.040|                           publishes no default for a keyword, the helper
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.041|                           leaves that keyword alone rather than inventing
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.042|                           a number.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.001|  Why these profiles : The four usage columns are SKIP for an obvious
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.002|                       reason - running the undo during a setup pass just
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.003|                       cancels what you came to do. The Windows column is
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                       APPLY, and this is the only step in the whole
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.005|                       network section that can honestly claim to produce
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.006|                       the shipped state, because it reads that state
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.007|                       rather than assuming it. Say yes outside a setup
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.008|                       run: when troubleshooting, when handing the PC
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                       over, or when the profile did not help.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.010|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.011|  Target          : :net_restore - reg query "%NICKEY%\Ndi\Params"
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.012|                    filtered by findstr /i /e to the 14 keywords
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.013|                    :net_apply writes, then call :nicdefault for each,
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.014|                    which reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default and writes it
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.015|                    back as REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995)
::
:: ---- net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword (repair) -------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|RETABLIR LES TROIS REGLAGES TCP GLOBAUX
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|  Ce que c est    : L'annulation des reglages TCP, executee a la fin de la
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|                    restauration de la carte : autotuninglevel=normal,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|                    heuristics=default, rss=default.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh. Deux cessent completement
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|                    d'imposer une valeur et laissent Windows choisir, la
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|                    troisieme fixe l'autotuning sur le niveau que Windows
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|                    aurait choisi de toute facon. Effet immediat sur toute
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|                    la machine, sans redemarrage.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.013|  Gain            : Le bon geste si un site precis, un VPN ou un proxy
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.014|                    d'entreprise est devenu capricieux apres l'etape
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.015|                    d'application TCP. Soyons honnetes sur l'ampleur :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.016|                    puisque les heuristiques de mise a l'echelle sont
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.017|                    livrees desactivees, default et disabled aboutissent
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.018|                    au meme comportement - pour cette ligne, c'est une
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.019|                    formalite plutot qu'un correctif.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.020|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.021|  Cout            : Rien n'est perdu. Si vous aviez choisi volontairement
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.022|                    un autre niveau d'autotuning pour contourner un
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.023|                    routeur capricieux, il faudra le remettre a la main.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.024|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.025|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal est bien le niveau livre,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.026|                    l'ecriture litterale est donc equivalente. Pour les
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.027|                    heuristiques et le RSS, le mot-cle default est correct
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.028|                    par construction.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.029|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.031|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.032|        Ecrit comme valeur litterale plutot qu'avec le mot-cle default,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.033|        parce que normal EST le niveau livre : les deux sont equivalents
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.034|        ici.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.035|    heuristics=default   : Cesse d'imposer une valeur et rend la decision
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.036|                           a la politique interne de Windows, qui est
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.037|                           disabled : le comportement observable est
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.038|                           identique a heuristics=disabled. Pour cette
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.039|                           ligne, la restauration est une formalite et non
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.040|                           un retour en arriere.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.041|    rss=default          : Meme principe pour le RSS, et la politique
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.042|                           Windows est enabled : la aussi, on aboutit ou
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.043|                           l'etape d'application avait laisse les choses.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.044|    SKIP                 : Les trois reglages globaux restent la ou
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.045|                           l'etape d'application les a laisses, ce qui sur
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.046|                           une machine d'origine est le meme endroit.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes a SKIP parce que c'est la moitie
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|                         annulation de l'etape d'application : la lancer
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|                         pendant une passe de configuration ne fait que
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|                         l'annuler. La colonne Windows est a APPLY :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|                         rendre la decision avec le mot-cle default est la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006|                         seule forme de restauration qui reste juste meme
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007|                         si une future version de Windows change d'avis
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|                         sur ce qu'est le defaut.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|  Cible           : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012|                    set global rss=default - the three lines at the end of
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.013|                    :net_restore (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001)
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|RESTORE THE THREE TCP GLOBALS
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|  What it is      : The undo for the TCP settings, run at the end of the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|                    NIC restore: autotuninglevel=normal,
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|                    heuristics=default, rss=default.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls. Two of them stop asserting a value
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|                    at all and let Windows pick; the third pins autotuning
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|                    to the level Windows would have picked anyway.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|                    Machine-wide and immediate, no reboot.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012|  Gain            : The right move if a particular site, VPN or corporate
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.013|                    proxy turned flaky after the TCP assert step. Be
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.014|                    honest about the size of it: since Window Scaling
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.015|                    Heuristics ships disabled, default and disabled land
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.016|                    on the same behaviour, so for that line this is a
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.017|                    formality rather than a fix.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.018|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.019|  Cost            : Nothing is lost. If you had deliberately chosen a non-
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.020|                    default autotuning level to work around a broken
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.021|                    router, you would set it again by hand.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.022|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.023|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal is the shipped level, so the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.024|                    literal write is equivalent. For heuristics and rss
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.025|                    the keyword default is correct by construction.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.026|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.028|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.029|        Written as a literal rather than as the keyword default, because
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.030|        normal IS the shipped level - the two are equivalent here.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.031|    heuristics=default   : Stops asserting a value and hands the decision
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.032|                           to Windows built-in policy, which is disabled -
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.033|                           so the observable behaviour is identical to
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.034|                           heuristics=disabled. For this one line the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.035|                           restore is a formality, not a reversal.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.036|    rss=default          : Same idea for RSS, and the Windows policy is
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.037|                           enabled - so this too lands where the assert
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.038|                           step left it.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.039|    SKIP                 : The three globals stay wherever the assert step
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.040|                           left them, which on a stock machine is the same
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.041|                           place.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|  Why these profiles : Four SKIP columns because this is the undo half of
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|                       the assert step - running it during a setup pass
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|                       just cancels it. The Windows column is APPLY:
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|                       handing the decision back with the keyword default
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|                       is the only form of restore that stays correct even
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006|                       if a future Windows release changes its mind about
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007|                       what the default is.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|  Target          : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|                    set global rss=default - the three lines at the end of
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012|                    :net_restore (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001)
