:: ---- net.reset.ip.stack (risky) --------------------------------
::P|net.reset.ip.stack|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.001|NETSH INT IP RESET (REPARATION DESTRUCTIVE)
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.002|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.003|  Ce que c est    : Reecrit toute la configuration de la pile TCP/IP dans
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.004|                    le registre a l'etat d'usine. Menu Reparation
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.005|                    uniquement, derriere une confirmation a taper (RESET).
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.006|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.007|  Effet reel      : Toute la configuration IP de chaque interface est
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.008|                    effacee : adresses IP fixes, serveurs DNS fixes,
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.009|                    routes persistantes, metriques personnalisees. Toutes
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.010|                    les cartes repassent en DHCP. Un redemarrage est
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.011|                    obligatoire. OPTY reaffirme les trois reglages TCP
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.012|                    globaux juste apres, parce que la reinitialisation les
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.013|                    efface aussi.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.014|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.015|  Gain            : Repare une corruption au niveau de la pile que rien
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.016|                    d'autre n'atteint : cartes qui refusent de prendre une
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.017|                    adresse, interface toujours cassee apres
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.018|                    reinstallation du pilote, table de routage fausse sans
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.019|                    raison visible. Quand c'est bien cela la panne, c'est
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.020|                    ce qui la corrige.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.021|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.022|  Cout            : OPTY ne peut pas annuler cela. Sur une machine
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.023|                    simplement en DHCP, vous ne verrez rien. Si vous
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.024|                    pointez vers un Pi-hole ou NextDNS, si vous avez une
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.025|                    IP fixe, une route persistante vers un sous-reseau VPN
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.026|                    ou un NAS, ou un DNS impose par une configuration
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.027|                    d'entreprise, tout est efface et il faudra tout
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.028|                    ressaisir a la main. Sauvegardez la sortie d'ipconfig
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.029|                    /all et de route print avant de dire oui, et verifiez
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.030|                    qu'un point de restauration existe.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.031|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.032|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - par definition, cela retablit la
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.033|                    configuration de pile d'usine.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.034|
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.036|    RESET                : Toute la configuration TCP/IP par interface
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.037|                           dans le registre est reecrite a l'etat d'usine.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.038|                           Adresses IP fixes, serveurs DNS fixes, routes
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.039|                           persistantes et metriques d'interface
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.040|                           personnalisees disparaissent, chaque carte
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.041|                           repasse en DHCP, et un redemarrage est
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.042|                           obligatoire avant que la machine soit
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.043|                           utilisable comme configuree.
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.044|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est touche. C'est ce que produit toute
::T|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.045|                           saisie autre que RESET.
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes a SKIP, deliberement. Aucun profil
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.002|                         d'usage n'est une raison de reinitialiser une
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.003|                         pile TCP/IP ; une panne reproductible en est une,
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.004|                         et rien d'autre. Les colonnes serveur et portable
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.005|                         sont celles qui ont le plus a perdre, car ce sont
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.006|                         les machines les plus susceptibles de porter une
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.007|                         adresse fixe, un resolveur impose ou une route
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.008|                         persistante - mais meme la colonne Windows est a
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.009|                         SKIP, parce que Windows ne se fait pas cela a
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.010|                         lui-meme non plus.
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.011|
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.012|  Problemes connus : Le jeu de fiches contenait deux copies de cette
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.013|                     carte, avec des reponses par defaut et des textes de
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.014|                     cout differents. C'est la plus stricte qui est
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.015|                     conservee : il n'y a pas de reponse par defaut ici,
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.016|                     l'etape exige de taper RESET.
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.017|
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.018|  Cible           : netsh int ip reset, inside :netreset - reached only
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.019|                    from the Repair menu, behind a typed RESET
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.020|                    confirmation and a call :restore_point (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.reset.ip.stack.021|                    ~2723-2742)
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.001|NETSH INT IP RESET (DESTRUCTIVE REPAIR)
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.002|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.003|  What it is      : Rewrites the entire TCP/IP stack configuration in the
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.004|                    registry back to factory state. Repair menu only,
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.005|                    behind a typed RESET confirmation.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.006|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.007|  Actual effect   : Every per-interface IP configuration is wiped: static
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.008|                    IP addresses, static DNS servers, persistent routes,
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.009|                    custom interface metrics. All adapters go back to
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.010|                    DHCP. A reboot is required. OPTY re-asserts the three
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.011|                    TCP globals immediately afterwards, because the reset
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.012|                    reverts those too.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.013|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.014|  Gain            : Fixes stack-level corruption nothing else reaches -
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.015|                    adapters that will not take an address, an interface
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.016|                    still broken after a driver reinstall, a routing table
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.017|                    that is wrong for no visible reason. When that is the
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.018|                    fault, this is what fixes it.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.019|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.020|  Cost            : OPTY cannot undo this. On a plain DHCP machine you
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.021|                    will not notice. If you point at Pi-hole or NextDNS,
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.022|                    run a static IP, keep a persistent route to a VPN
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.023|                    subnet or a NAS, or your DNS was pushed by corporate
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.024|                    configuration, all of it is wiped and you re-enter it
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.025|                    by hand. Save the output of ipconfig /all and route
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.026|                    print before you say yes, and check that a restore
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.027|                    point exists.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.028|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.029|  Windows default : Not applicable - this restores the factory stack
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.030|                    configuration by definition.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.031|
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.033|    RESET                : The whole per-interface TCP/IP configuration in
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.034|                           the registry is rewritten to factory. Static IP
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.035|                           addresses, static DNS servers, persistent
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.036|                           routes and custom interface metrics are gone,
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.037|                           every adapter returns to DHCP, and a reboot is
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.038|                           required before the machine is usable as
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.039|                           configured.
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.040|    SKIP                 : Nothing is touched. This is what typing
::T|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.041|                           anything other than RESET does.
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.001|  Why these profiles : Five SKIP columns, deliberately. No usage profile
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.002|                       is a reason to reset a TCP/IP stack; a reproducible
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.003|                       fault is, and nothing else. The server and laptop
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.004|                       columns are the ones with most to lose, since those
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.005|                       are the machines most likely to carry a static
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.006|                       address, a pinned resolver or a persistent route -
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.007|                       but even the Windows column is SKIP, because
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.008|                       Windows does not do this to itself either.
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.009|
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.010|  Known problems  : The deck carried two copies of this card with
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.011|                    different default answers and different cost text. The
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.012|                    stricter one is kept: there is no default answer here
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.013|                    - the step requires typing RESET.
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.014|
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.015|  Target          : netsh int ip reset, inside :netreset - reached only
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.016|                    from the Repair menu, behind a typed RESET
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.017|                    confirmation and a call :restore_point (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.reset.ip.stack.018|                    ~2723-2742)
