:: ---- net.tcp.globals.handback (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.handback|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|RENDRE LES REGLAGES TCP GLOBAUX A WINDOWS
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.003|  Ce que c est    : Le pendant annulation de l'application TCP. Les
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.004|                    heuristiques et le RSS global passent sur le mot-cle
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|                    default, c'est-a-dire que Windows decide ;
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|                    l'autotuning, lui, est ecrit avec la valeur litterale
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|                    normal et non default.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.008|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.009|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh. Deux cessent completement
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|                    d'imposer une valeur et laissent Windows choisir, la
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|                    troisieme fixe l'autotuning sur le niveau que Windows
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|                    aurait choisi de toute facon. Effet immediat, sans
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.013|                    redemarrage.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|  Gain            : Remet la machine dans le comportement TCP d'origine
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|                    sans que vous ayez besoin de connaitre ces valeurs.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.017|                    Pratique avant de diagnostiquer quoi que ce soit de
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.018|                    reseau : vous deboguez Windows et non un tweak passe.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.019|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.020|  Cout            : Rien n'est perdu. Si vous aviez volontairement choisi
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.021|                    un autre niveau d'autotuning pour contourner un
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.022|                    routeur capricieux, il faudra le remettre.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.023|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.024|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal est bien le niveau livre,
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.025|                    l'ecriture litterale est donc equivalente ici. Pour
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.026|                    les heuristiques et le RSS, le mot-cle default est
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.027|                    correct par construction.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.028|
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.030|    the keyword default  : netsh cesse d'imposer une valeur pour ce
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.031|                           parametre et laisse decider la politique
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.032|                           interne de Windows. Cela reste juste meme si
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.033|                           une future version de Windows change son
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.034|                           defaut, puisque aucun nombre n'est fige.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.035|    a literal value such as normal or enabled :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.036|        netsh fixe le parametre exactement sur ce reglage. Correct
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.037|        aujourd'hui s'il correspond a ce que Windows livre, et
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.038|        silencieusement faux le jour ou ce n'est plus le cas.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.039|    autotuninglevel written as normal rather than default :
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.040|        Une asymetrie assumee : normal est le niveau livre, et l'ecrire
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.041|        litteralement rend le journal lisible, la ou default n'afficherait
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.042|        aucune valeur a verifier.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.043|    SKIP                 : Les trois reglages globaux gardent ce que
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.044|                           l'etape d'application a ecrit.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes a SKIP, parce que rendre les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|                         reglages globaux au milieu d'une passe de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.003|                         configuration ne fait qu'annuler l'etape
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.004|                         d'application lancee quelques instants plus tot.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|                         La colonne Windows est a APPLY, et il vaut la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|                         peine de comprendre pourquoi cette forme de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|                         restauration est la meilleure : deux des trois
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.008|                         lignes n'affirment plus rien du tout, elles ne
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.009|                         peuvent donc pas devenir fausses. Lancez-la avant
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|                         de signaler un probleme reseau a votre FAI ou a
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|                         un support : ils vous demanderont si la pile est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|                         d'origine.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.013|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|  Cible           : the same three lines as the NIC restore path: netsh
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|                    int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ; netsh int
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|                    tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp set global
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.017|                    rss=default (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001). This card explains
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.018|                    why one of the three is written as a literal and the
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.019|                    other two as the keyword default.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|HAND THE TCP GLOBALS BACK TO WINDOWS
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.003|  What it is      : The undo side of the TCP assert. Heuristics and global
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.004|                    RSS are set to the keyword default, meaning Windows
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|                    decides; autotuning is written as the literal value
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|                    normal rather than as default.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.008|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls. Two of them stop asserting a value
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.009|                    at all and let Windows pick, the third pins autotuning
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|                    to the level Windows would have picked anyway.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|                    Immediate, no reboot.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.013|  Gain            : Puts the machine back to shipped TCP behaviour without
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|                    you needing to know what the shipped values are.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|                    Useful before troubleshooting anything network-
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|                    related, so you are debugging Windows rather than
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.017|                    debugging a previous tweak.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.018|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.019|  Cost            : Nothing is lost. If you had deliberately chosen a non-
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.020|                    default autotuning level to work around a broken
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.021|                    router, you would set it again.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.022|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.023|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal is the shipped level, so the
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.024|                    literal write is equivalent here. For heuristics and
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.025|                    rss the keyword default is correct by construction.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.026|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.028|    the keyword default  : netsh stops asserting a value for that
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.029|                           parameter and lets the Windows built-in policy
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.030|                           decide. It stays correct even if a future
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.031|                           Windows release changes what the default is,
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.032|                           because no number is baked in.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.033|    a literal value such as normal or enabled :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.034|        netsh pins the parameter to that exact setting. Correct today if
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.035|        it matches what Windows ships, and silently wrong the day it does
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.036|        not.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.037|    autotuninglevel written as normal rather than default :
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.038|        A deliberate asymmetry: normal is the shipped level and writing it
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.039|        literally makes the log readable, since default would print no
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.040|        value for the reader to check.
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.041|    SKIP                 : The three globals keep whatever the assert step
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.042|                           wrote.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|  Why these profiles : Four SKIP columns, because handing the globals back
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|                       in the middle of a setup run just cancels the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.003|                       assert step you ran a moment earlier. The Windows
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.004|                       column is APPLY, and it is worth understanding why
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|                       this is the better shaped restore: two of the three
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|                       lines assert nothing at all, so they cannot become
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|                       wrong. Run it before reporting a network problem to
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.008|                       an ISP or a support desk - they will ask whether
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.009|                       the stack is stock.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|  Target          : the same three lines as the NIC restore path: netsh
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|                    int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ; netsh int
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.013|                    tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp set global
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|                    rss=default (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001). This card explains
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|                    why one of the three is written as a literal and the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|                    other two as the keyword default.
::
:: ---- net.nic.offloads.explain (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.nic.offloads.explain|1/3|1/3|1/3|1/3|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|REACTIVER LES DECHARGEMENTS MATERIELS DE LA CARTE
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|  Ce que c est    : Sept mots-cles du pilote traites comme une seule etape
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                    : Large Send Offload v2 en IPv4 et IPv6, plus les
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|                    sommes de controle TCP, UDP et IP. Ils deplacent le
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|                    decoupage des segments et le calcul des sommes de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|                    controle du CPU vers la puce reseau.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.008|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit LSOv2 = 1 et toutes les sommes de controle = 3.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|                    C'est volontairement l'inverse du conseil classique
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|                    des blogs d'optimisation, qui veut qu'on les
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|                    desactive.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.013|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|  Gain            : Sur une machine neuve, en general aucun : ils sont
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|                    livres actifs, l'ecriture confirme donc au lieu de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|                    changer quoi que ce soit. Sur une machine ou un tweak
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|                    precedent les avait coupes, vous recuperez le travail
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|                    que la carte etait censee faire, ce qui se voit a un
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.019|                    CPU plus bas pendant les gros transferts.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.020|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.021|  Cout            : Un pilote reellement bogue peut produire de mauvaises
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.022|                    sommes de controle avec le dechargement actif, ce qui
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.023|                    donne des connexions qui bloquent ou echouent. C'etait
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.024|                    courant il y a dix ans, c'est rare aujourd'hui, et la
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.025|                    restauration reseau les remet en arriere si vous
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.026|                    tombez sur un tel pilote.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.027|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.028|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote. Sur pratiquement tous
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.029|                    les pilotes actuels, LSOv2 est livre actif et les
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.030|                    sommes de controle a 3 : on reaffirme donc en general
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.031|                    la valeur d'usine.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.032|
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.034|    LSOv2 = 1            : La puce assure la segmentation TCP. Un gros
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.035|                           tampon traverse la pile au lieu de dizaines de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.036|                           paquets a la taille du MTU : le CPU par octet
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.037|                           transfere baisse sur les gros envois.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.038|    LSOv2 = 0            : Le CPU fait la segmentation. Les memes octets
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.039|                           sur le cable, plus de CPU pour les produire -
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.040|                           c'est ce que demandent les guides de bidouille.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.041|    checksum offload = 3 : Rx et Tx Enabled : la puce calcule les sommes
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.042|                           de controle a l'emission et les verifie a la
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.043|                           reception. Valeur livree sur pratiquement tous
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.044|                           les pilotes actuels.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.045|    checksum offload = 2 or 1 :
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.046|        Un seul sens est decharge, l'autre reste sur le CPU. Rarement un
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.047|        choix delibere : le plus souvent le residu d'un tweak applique a
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.048|        moitie.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.049|    checksum offload = 0 : Les deux sens sur le CPU. Etape de diagnostic
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.050|                           legitime quand on traque une corruption de
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.051|                           paquets due a un pilote suspect, mauvais
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.052|                           reglage permanent.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.053|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est ecrit ; les sept mots-cles gardent
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.054|                           les valeurs propres du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, et la raison merite
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|                         d'etre dite clairement : l'idee que les
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|                         dechargements nuisent au jeu ne resiste pas au
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                         fait que le trafic d'un jeu, ce sont de petits
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|                         datagrammes UDP qui ne passent jamais par le LSO.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|                         Aucune latence n'est echangee : gaming et
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|                         bureautique recoivent donc la meme reponse.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.008|                         Windows est a SKIP parce que 1 et 3 appartiennent
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.009|                         au pilote et non a Windows, et que seule l'etape
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|                         de restauration lit les vraies valeurs. Dites non
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|                         uniquement si vous avez un bug de pilote
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|                         documente.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.013|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|  Cible           : the same seven :nicset calls as the offloads step -
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|                    *LsoV2IPv4, *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 and
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6,
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4 =
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|                    3, REG_SZ in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.019|                    ~2849-2856). This card is the why, and reverses the
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.020|                    standard tweak-blog advice.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|RE-ENABLE THE NIC HARDWARE OFFLOADS
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|  What it is      : Seven driver keywords treated as one step: Large Send
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                    Offload v2 for IPv4 and IPv6, plus the TCP, UDP and IP
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|                    checksum offloads. They move segmentation and checksum
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|                    work off your CPU and into the network chip.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.008|  Actual effect   : Writes LSOv2 = 1 and every checksum offload = 3. This
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.009|                    deliberately reverses the standard optimizer-blog
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|                    advice to turn offloads off.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|  Gain            : On a fresh machine, usually none - these ship enabled,
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.013|                    so the write confirms rather than changes anything. On
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|                    a machine where a previous tweak disabled them, you
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|                    get back the work the card was supposed to be doing,
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|                    which shows up as lower CPU during large transfers.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|  Cost            : A genuinely buggy NIC driver can produce bad checksums
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.019|                    with offload on, which looks like connections that
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.020|                    stall or fail. That was common a decade ago and is
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.021|                    rare now, and the network Restore puts them back if
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.022|                    you turn out to have such a driver.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.023|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.024|  Windows default : Driver-supplied. On practically every current NIC
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.025|                    driver LSOv2 ships Enabled and the checksum offloads
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.026|                    ship at 3, so this normally re-asserts the factory
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.027|                    value.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.028|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.029|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.030|    LSOv2 = 1            : The chip performs TCP segmentation. One large
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.031|                           buffer crosses the stack instead of dozens of
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.032|                           MTU-sized packets, so CPU per transferred byte
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.033|                           drops on bulk sends.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.034|    LSOv2 = 0            : The CPU does the segmentation. Identical bytes
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.035|                           on the wire, more CPU to produce them - which
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.036|                           is what the tweak guides ask for.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.037|    checksum offload = 3 : Rx and Tx Enabled: the chip computes checksums
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.038|                           on send and verifies them on receive. The
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.039|                           shipped value on practically every current
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.040|                           driver.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.041|    checksum offload = 2 or 1 :
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.042|        Only one direction is offloaded; the other stays on the CPU.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.043|        Rarely a deliberate choice - usually the residue of a half-applied
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.044|        tweak.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.045|    checksum offload = 0 : Both directions on the CPU. A legitimate
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.046|                           diagnostic step when chasing packet corruption
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.047|                           from a suspect driver, and a poor permanent
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.048|                           setting.
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.049|    SKIP                 : Nothing is written; the seven keywords keep the
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.050|                           driver's own values.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns, and the reason is worth
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|                       stating plainly: the claim that offloads hurt
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|                       gaming does not survive the fact that game traffic
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                       is small UDP datagrams which never go through LSO
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|                       at all. There is no latency being traded, so gaming
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|                       and office get the same answer. Windows is SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|                       because 1 and 3 belong to the driver, not to
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.008|                       Windows, and only the restore step reads the real
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.009|                       values. Say no only if you have a documented driver
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|                       bug.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|  Target          : the same seven :nicset calls as the offloads step -
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.013|                    *LsoV2IPv4, *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 and
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4 =
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|                    3, REG_SZ in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|                    ~2849-2856). This card is the why, and reverses the
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|                    standard tweak-blog advice.
::
:: ---- net.nic.jumbo.explain (repair) -----------------------------
::P|net.nic.jumbo.explain|1514|1514|1514|1514|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|GARDER LES TRAMES STANDARD DE 1514 OCTETS
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003|  Ce que c est    : Les trames jumbo permettent a une carte reseau
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                    d'envoyer des trames Ethernet bien plus grandes que
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005|                    les 1514 octets standard. Elles n'apportent quelque
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|                    chose que si tous les equipements du chemin - switch,
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|                    routeur, machine d'en face - sont configures pareil.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|  Effet reel      : Ecrit *JumboPacket=1514, soit la taille de trame
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|                    standard, c'est-a-dire jumbo desactive. L'effet arrive
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|                    au prochain redemarrage de la carte ou de la machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.013|  Gain            : Sur une machine ou les trames jumbo avaient ete
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.014|                    activees sans switch ni NAS assortis, cela regle des
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.015|                    symptomes bien reels : gros transferts qui bloquent,
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.016|                    ou qui marchent en local mais pas vers Internet. Sur
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.017|                    une machine deja a 1514, rien ne change et,
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.018|                    honnetement, il n'y a aucun gain.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.019|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.020|  Cout            : Si vous avez monte volontairement un reseau en trames
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.021|                    jumbo - NAS 10G, iSCSI, virtualisation, avec chaque
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.022|                    port du switch regle sur un MTU de 9000 - ceci
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.023|                    l'annule et vous perdez le debit pour lequel cette
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.024|                    installation existe.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.025|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.026|  Defaut Windows  : 1514 (jumbo desactive) sur pratiquement tous les
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.027|                    pilotes reseau Windows : on reaffirme donc en general
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.028|                    la valeur d'usine.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.029|
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.030|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.031|    1514                 : Trame standard : 1500 octets de charge utile
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.032|                           plus un en-tete Ethernet de 14 octets. De toute
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.033|                           facon, tout ce qui sort de votre reseau local
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.034|                           utilise cette taille.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.035|    4088 or 4096         : Tailles jumbo intermediaires proposees par
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.036|                           certains pilotes. Meme exigence du tout ou rien
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.037|                           que le jumbo complet : chaque equipement du
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.038|                           chemin doit etre d'accord.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.039|    9014 or 9000         : Jumbo complet. Sur un chemin ou un equipement
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.040|                           reste a 1500, les gros transferts se
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.041|                           fragmentent ou bloquent : le symptome classique
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.042|                           d'un reglage jumbo applique a une seule
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.043|                           machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.044|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est ecrit ; la carte garde le reglage de
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.045|                           taille de trame du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 1514. La distinction
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|                         qui compterait ici n'est pas gaming contre
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003|                         bureautique, c'est de savoir si vous avez monte
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                         un reseau jumbo ferme - et aucun des quatre
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005|                         profils n'en decrit un. Windows est a SKIP parce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|                         que la valeur appartient au pilote et que ce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|                         chemin ne lit pas son defaut. Dites non
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008|                         uniquement si vous exploitez sciemment un reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|                         de stockage ou de virtualisation en jumbo.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|  Cible           : the same call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514"
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|                    as the jumbo step (OPTY.bat ~2863). This card explains
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.013|                    what the value means and when refusing it is correct.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|KEEP STANDARD 1514-BYTE FRAMES
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003|  What it is      : Jumbo frames let a NIC send Ethernet frames far larger
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                    than the standard 1514 bytes. They only help when
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005|                    every device along the path - switch, router, the
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|                    other machine - is configured for them too.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008|  Actual effect   : Writes *JumboPacket=1514, the standard frame size,
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|                    meaning jumbo frames off. It takes effect at the next
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|                    adapter restart or reboot.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|  Gain            : On a machine where jumbo frames were turned on without
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.013|                    a matching switch and NAS, this fixes real symptoms:
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.014|                    large transfers that stall, or that work on the LAN
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.015|                    but not to the internet. On a machine already at 1514,
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.016|                    nothing changes and there is honestly no gain.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.017|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.018|  Cost            : If you have a deliberately built jumbo-frame LAN - a
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.019|                    10G NAS, an iSCSI or virtualization setup where every
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.020|                    switch port is configured for a 9000-byte MTU - this
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.021|                    undoes it and you lose the throughput that setup
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.022|                    exists for.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.023|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.024|  Windows default : 1514 (jumbo disabled) on effectively every Windows NIC
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.025|                    driver, so this normally re-asserts the factory value.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.026|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.028|    1514                 : Standard frame: 1500 bytes of payload plus a
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.029|                           14-byte Ethernet header. Everything that leaves
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.030|                           your LAN uses this size anyway.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.031|    4088 or 4096         : Intermediate jumbo sizes offered by some
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.032|                           drivers. Same all-or-nothing requirement as
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.033|                           full jumbo - every hop must agree.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.034|    9014 or 9000         : Full jumbo. On a path where one hop is still at
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.035|                           1500, large transfers fragment or stall - the
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.036|                           classic symptom of a jumbo setting applied to
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.037|                           one machine only.
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.038|    SKIP                 : Nothing is written; the adapter keeps the
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.039|                           driver's frame size setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 1514. The distinction
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|                       that would matter here is not gaming versus office,
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003|                       it is whether you built a closed jumbo network -
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                       and none of the four profiles describes one.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005|                       Windows is SKIP because the value belongs to the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|                       driver and this path does not read the driver's
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|                       default. Say no only if you knowingly run a jumbo
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008|                       storage or virtualization network.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|  Target          : the same call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514"
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|                    as the jumbo step (OPTY.bat ~2863). This card explains
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|                    what the value means and when refusing it is correct.
::
:: ---- net.nic.restart.pnputil (risky) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restart.pnputil|RESTART|SKIP|RESTART|RESTART|SKIP|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|REDEMARRER LA CARTE POUR APPLIQUER LES REGLAGES
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|  Ce que c est    : NDIS ne relit les mots-cles de la carte qu'au moment
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004|                    ou le peripherique s'initialise : tous les reglages
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|                    ecrits plus haut ne font donc rien tant que la carte
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|                    n'a pas redemarre ou que la machine n'a pas reboote.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|  Effet reel      : pnputil /restart-device arrete et relance la carte
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|                    reseau. Le lien tombe environ 2 a 4 secondes puis
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|                    revient avec les nouveaux reglages actifs.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012|  Gain            : Les reglages prennent effet maintenant au lieu du
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|                    prochain redemarrage. C'est tout le benefice : les
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|                    reglages sont identiques dans les deux cas, et c'est
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015|                    pour cela que refuser ne coute rien.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.016|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.017|  Cout            : Pendant ces quelques secondes, la machine n'a plus de
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.018|                    reseau : les telechargements sans reprise meurent, les
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.019|                    tunnels VPN tombent, les parties en ligne se
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.020|                    deconnectent, les sessions de bureau a distance se
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.021|                    coupent. Si vous etes connecte a cette machine en RDP,
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.022|                    vous risquez de ne pas pouvoir revenir avant sa
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.023|                    reconnexion.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.024|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.025|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - c'est une operation, pas un reglage.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.026|
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.028|    RESTART              : Le peripherique est arrete puis relance. NDIS
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.029|                           relit tous les mots-cles pendant
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.030|                           l'initialisation, seul moyen de rendre actives
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.031|                           les valeurs ecrites plus haut sans redemarrer.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.032|                           Lien coupe environ 2 a 4 secondes, davantage en
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.033|                           Wi-Fi car la carte doit se reassocier.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.034|    SKIP                 : Les mots-cles restent dans le registre et sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.035|                           lus au prochain demarrage. Meme etat final,
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.036|                           aucune interruption maintenant.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : La colonne serveur est a SKIP et porte tout le
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|                         raisonnement : cette machine est generalement
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|                         jointe par le reseau, et elle partage, sert ou
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004|                         transcode pour quelqu'un d'autre pendant que vous
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|                         travaillez dessus - une coupure lui coute des
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|                         sessions qu'un redemarrage planifie ne couterait
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|                         pas. Les trois autres sont des machines devant
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|                         lesquelles vous etes assis : l'interruption n'est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|                         que la votre et dure quelques secondes. Windows
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|                         est a SKIP parce que redemarrer un peripherique
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011|                         est une action que Windows ne prend jamais de
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012|                         lui-meme.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|  Cible           : call :nicrestart "%NICKEY%" - reads NetCfgInstanceId
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015|                    from the class key, looks up PnPInstanceId under
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.016|                    Control\Network\{4d36e972-...}\<GUID>\Connection, then
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.017|                    runs pnputil /restart-device on it (OPTY.bat helper
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.018|                    ~3500-3510)
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|RESTART THE ADAPTER TO APPLY THE SETTINGS
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|  What it is      : NDIS only re-reads the adapter's keyword values when
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004|                    the device initialises, so every setting written above
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|                    does nothing until the adapter restarts or the machine
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|                    reboots.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|  Actual effect   : pnputil /restart-device stops and restarts the network
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|                    adapter. The link goes down for roughly 2 to 4 seconds
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|                    and comes back with the new settings live.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012|  Gain            : The settings take effect now rather than at your next
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|                    reboot. That is the entire benefit - the settings
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|                    themselves are identical either way, which is why
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015|                    declining costs you nothing.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.016|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.017|  Cost            : For those few seconds the machine has no network:
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.018|                    downloads without resume support die, VPN tunnels
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.019|                    drop, online game sessions disconnect, remote desktop
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.020|                    sessions break. If you are connected to this machine
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.021|                    over RDP you may not get back in until it reconnects.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.022|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.023|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is an operation, not a setting.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.024|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.025|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.026|    RESTART              : The device is stopped and started again. NDIS
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.027|                           re-reads every keyword during initialisation,
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.028|                           which is the only way the values written above
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.029|                           become active without a reboot. Link down
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.030|                           roughly 2 to 4 seconds, longer on Wi-Fi because
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.031|                           the adapter must re-associate.
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.032|    SKIP                 : The keywords sit in the registry and are read
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.033|                           at the next boot. Same end state, no
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.034|                           interruption now.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|  Why these profiles : The server column is SKIP and carries the whole
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|                       argument: that machine is usually reached over the
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|                       network, and it is seeding, serving or transcoding
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004|                       for someone else while you work on it - a blackout
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|                       costs sessions that a scheduled reboot would not.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|                       The other three are machines you are sitting in
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|                       front of, where the interruption is yours alone and
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|                       lasts seconds. Windows is SKIP because restarting a
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|                       device is an action Windows never takes on its own.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011|  Target          : call :nicrestart "%NICKEY%" - reads NetCfgInstanceId
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012|                    from the class key, looks up PnPInstanceId under
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|                    Control\Network\{4d36e972-...}\<GUID>\Connection, then
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|                    runs pnputil /restart-device on it (OPTY.bat helper
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015|                    ~3500-3510)
::
:: ---- net.nic.restore.driver14 (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.nic.restore.driver14|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|RESTAURER LA CARTE AUX VALEURS D'USINE DE SON PILOTE
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|  Ce que c est    : Remet les 14 mots-cles de la carte qu'OPTY ecrit, en
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                    lisant chaque valeur d'usine dans le Ndi\Params\<mot-
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|                    cle>\default du pilote lui-meme, au lieu de supposer
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.006|                    des valeurs pour votre chipset.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.008|  Effet reel      : Reecrit la moderation d'interruptions et ITR, les
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|                    dechargements LSO et checksum, le RSS et le nombre de
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|                    files, les trames jumbo et les tampons d'emission et
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|                    de reception avec ce que livre le pilote. Rend ensuite
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|                    les reglages TCP globaux a Windows et propose un
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|                    redemarrage de la carte.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.014|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.015|  Gain            : Un retour garanti au reglage du fabricant sur
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.016|                    n'importe quelle carte, sans que vous ayez besoin de
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.017|                    connaitre ces valeurs. C'est correct sur tous les
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.018|                    chipsets par construction, puisque les valeurs sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.019|                    lues sur votre propre machine.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.020|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.021|  Cout            : Deux choses qu'il ne restaure PAS, volontairement :
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.022|                    l'EEE 802.3az et *FlowControl. Le filtre exclut
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.023|                    sciemment Wake-on-LAN, EEE et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.024|                    pour que la restauration ne reactive pas des reglages
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.025|                    que vous aviez coupes en dehors d'OPTY. Effet de bord
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.026|                    : si OPTY a desactive l'EEE ou le controle de flux,
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.027|                    ils restent desactives - a annuler dans le
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.028|                    Gestionnaire de peripheriques, onglet Avance.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.029|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.030|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - il lit la vraie valeur par defaut de
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.031|                    votre carte dans le pilote au lieu d'en affirmer une.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.032|
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.034|    APPLY                : Chacun des 14 mots-cles est reecrit avec la
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.035|                           valeur que contient son propre Ndi\Params\<mot-
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.036|                           cle>\default sur cette machine - juste sur une
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.037|                           Intel, une Realtek, une Killer ou n'importe
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.038|                           quoi d'autre, et toujours juste apres une mise
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.039|                           a jour de pilote.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.040|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est reecrit et le profil applique reste
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.041|                           en place.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.042|    keyword excluded from the filter :
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.043|        Wake-on-LAN, les mots-cles EEE et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown ne sont
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.044|        jamais restaures, pour qu'un reglage coupe en dehors d'OPTY ne
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.045|        soit pas rallume dans votre dos. Le prix a payer : les ecritures
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.046|        EEE et controle de flux d'OPTY ne sont pas annulees non plus.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les quatre colonnes d'usage sont a SKIP parce que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|                         restaurer au milieu d'une configuration annule ce
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|                         pour quoi vous etes venu. La colonne Windows est
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                         a APPLY, et c'est cette etape qui donne un sens a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|                         cette colonne dans toute la section reseau :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.006|                         partout ailleurs, la reponse Windows doit etre
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|                         SKIP, parce qu'OPTY code des valeurs de fabricant
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.008|                         en dur au lieu de les lire. Ici il les lit, donc
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|                         APPLY produit reellement l'etat livre.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|  Cible           : the :net_restore loop over the 14 keywords the apply
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|                    path writes, each restored by call :nicdefault from
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default; the filter excludes
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.014|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown by design
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.015|                    (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995, helper :nicdefault ~3489-3499)
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|RESTORE THE NIC TO ITS OWN DRIVER DEFAULTS
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|  What it is      : Puts back the 14 adapter keywords OPTY writes, reading
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                    each factory value out of the driver's own
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default rather than assuming
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.006|                    values for your chipset.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.008|  Actual effect   : Rewrites interrupt moderation and ITR, the LSO and
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|                    checksum offloads, RSS and the queue count, jumbo
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|                    frames and the receive and transmit buffers to what
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|                    the driver ships. It then hands the TCP globals back
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|                    to Windows and offers an adapter restart.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.014|  Gain            : A guaranteed way back to the manufacturer's tuning on
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.015|                    any card, without you needing to know those values. It
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.016|                    is correct on any chipset by construction, because it
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.017|                    reads them from your own machine.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.018|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.019|  Cost            : Two things it does NOT restore, on purpose: 802.3az
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.020|                    EEE and *FlowControl. The filter deliberately excludes
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.021|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown so Restore
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.022|                    does not re-enable settings you turned off outside
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.023|                    OPTY. The side effect is that if OPTY turned EEE or
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.024|                    Flow Control off, this leaves them off - undo those
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.025|                    two in Device Manager, Advanced tab.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.026|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.027|  Windows default : Not applicable - it reads the real per-adapter default
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.028|                    out of the driver instead of asserting one.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.029|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.031|    APPLY                : Each of the 14 keywords is rewritten with the
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.032|                           value that keyword's own
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.033|                           Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default holds on this
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.034|                           machine - correct on an Intel, a Realtek, a
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.035|                           Killer or anything else, and still correct
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.036|                           after a driver update.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.037|    SKIP                 : Nothing is rewritten and the applied profile
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.038|                           stands.
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.039|    keyword excluded from the filter :
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.040|        Wake-on-LAN, the EEE keywords and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown are never
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.041|        restored, so a setting you turned off outside OPTY is not switched
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.042|        back on behind your back. The price is that OPTY's own EEE and
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.043|        Flow Control writes are not undone either.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|  Why these profiles : The four usage columns are SKIP because restoring
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|                       in the middle of a setup run undoes what you came
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|                       for. The Windows column is APPLY, and this is the
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                       step that gives that column meaning at all in the
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|                       NIC section: everywhere else the Windows answer has
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.006|                       to be SKIP, because OPTY hardcodes vendor values
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|                       rather than reading them. Here it reads them, so
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.008|                       APPLY genuinely produces the shipped state.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|  Target          : the :net_restore loop over the 14 keywords the apply
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|                    path writes, each restored by call :nicdefault from
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default; the filter excludes
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown by design
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.014|                    (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995, helper :nicdefault ~3489-3499)
::
:: ---- net.helper.nicset.regsz (preference) ---------------------------
::P|net.helper.nicset.regsz|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|COMMENT LES MOTS-CLES NDIS SONT VRAIMENT ECRITS
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|  Ce que c est    : La fonction d'ecriture derriere chaque reglage de
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|                    carte reseau : un reg add d'un mot-cle NDIS dans la
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|                    cle de classe de la carte, en REG_SZ.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|  Effet reel      : REG_SZ est le type que decrit le schema Ndi\Params et
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008|                    celui dans lequel sont stockees les proprietes
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|                    avancees de pratiquement tous les pilotes reseau
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010|                    Windows : c'est donc le choix sur. Un REG_DWORD ecrit
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                    sur le meme nom de valeur peut etre pris en compte ou
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|                    non selon le pilote - c'est une des raisons pour
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|                    lesquelles des scripts qui ecrivent des DWORD
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014|                    annoncent parfois une reussite sans que rien ne
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015|                    change. La fonction borne aussi la valeur demandee a
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|                    Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\max, l'arrondit vers le bas selon
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|                    \step, ignore les mots-cles que votre pilote n'expose
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|                    pas, et ecrit toujours au lieu de sauter une valeur
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.019|                    deja correcte.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.020|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.021|  Gain            : Les reglages que vous choisissez atteignent le pilote
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.022|                    sous la forme que decrit son propre schema de
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.023|                    parametres. Le journal distingue SET (la valeur etait
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.024|                    absente), written (elle etait deja correcte) et FIXED
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.025|                    : vous pouvez donc savoir si votre machine avait
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.026|                    vraiment besoin du changement.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.027|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.028|  Cout            : Aucun en soi. Le bornage fait que la valeur demandee
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.029|                    n'est souvent pas celle ecrite : 1024 tampons sur une
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.030|                    carte dont le maximum est 512 devient 512, et le
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.031|                    journal le dit au lieu de faire semblant.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.032|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.033|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - c'est une primitive generique. Chaque
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.034|                    vraie valeur par defaut se trouve dans
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.035|                    Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default sur votre propre machine.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.036|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.037|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.038|    REG_SZ               : Le type que decrit le schema Ndi\Params et
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.039|                           celui dans lequel sont stockees les proprietes
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.040|                           avancees de pratiquement tous les pilotes
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.041|                           reseau Windows. Toujours le choix sur, et c'est
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.042|                           pour cela que chaque ecriture de mot-cle par
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.043|                           OPTY l'utilise.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.044|    REG_DWORD            : Peut etre pris en compte ou non selon le
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.045|                           pilote. NDIS lit les mots-cles via
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.046|                           NdisReadConfiguration, qui renvoie un entier a
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.047|                           partir de l'un ou l'autre type, et certains
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.048|                           pilotes stockent effectivement des mots-cles en
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.049|                           DWORD. Ce n'est donc pas un echec silencieux
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.050|                           universel, mais c'est une raison pour laquelle
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.051|                           des scripts qui ecrivent des DWORD annoncent
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.052|                           parfois une reussite sans que rien ne change.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.053|    the clamped value    : Ce qui est reellement ecrit quand la demande
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.054|                           depasse Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\max ou n'est pas
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.055|                           un multiple de \step. 1024 tampons sur une
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.056|                           carte dont le maximum est 512 devient 512, et
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.057|                           le journal indique 512.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.058|    keyword absent from Ndi\Params :
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.059|        Rien n'est ecrit du tout et la fonction sort immediatement : un
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.060|        mot-cle que votre pilote n'expose pas est ignore plutot
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.061|        qu'invente.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien a decider ici, donc cinq colonnes
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|                         identiques. Cela vaut la lecture, car cela
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|                         explique pourquoi les chiffres d'OPTY different
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|                         parfois de ceux que vous avez demandes, et
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|                         pourquoi les reglages d'apparence identique
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|                         d'autres scripts peuvent n'avoir aucun effet sur
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|                         votre pilote precis.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether a given driver honours a REG_DWORD keyword
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010|                      is driver-specific and was not tested here. The
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                      claim made is only that REG_SZ is the type the
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|                      Ndi\Params schema describes and that it is always
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|                      accepted.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015|  Cible           : :nicset - reg add "<class key>" /v "<keyword>" /t
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|                    REG_SZ /d "<value>" /f, after checking the keyword
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|                    exists under Ndi\Params, clamping to \max via
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|                    :nicclamp and rounding down to \step via :nicround;
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.019|                    the old value is read only to label the log line SET,
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.020|                    written or FIXED (OPTY.bat ~3437-3465)
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|HOW NIC KEYWORDS ARE ACTUALLY WRITTEN
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|  What it is      : The writer behind every adapter setting: a reg add of
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|                    one NDIS keyword into the adapter's class key, as
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|                    REG_SZ.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|  Actual effect   : REG_SZ is the type the Ndi\Params schema describes and
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008|                    the type virtually every Windows NIC driver's advanced
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|                    properties are stored in, so it is the safe choice. A
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010|                    REG_DWORD written to the same value name may or may
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                    not be honoured depending on the driver - which is one
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|                    reason scripts that write DWORDs sometimes report
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|                    success while nothing changes. The helper also clamps
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014|                    the requested value to Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max,
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015|                    rounds it down to \step, skips keywords your driver
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|                    does not expose, and always writes rather than
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|                    skipping an already-correct value.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.019|  Gain            : The settings you choose reach the driver in the form
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.020|                    its own parameter schema describes. The log
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.021|                    distinguishes SET (the value was absent), written (it
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.022|                    was already correct) and FIXED, so you can tell
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.023|                    whether your machine needed the change at all.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.024|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.025|  Cost            : None on its own. The clamping means the value you
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.026|                    asked for is often not the value written - 1024
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.027|                    buffers on a card whose maximum is 512 becomes 512,
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.028|                    and the log says so rather than pretending.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.029|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.030|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is a generic primitive. Every
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.031|                    real default lives at Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default on
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.032|                    your own machine.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.033|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.034|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.035|    REG_SZ               : The type the Ndi\Params schema describes and
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.036|                           the type virtually every Windows NIC driver's
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.037|                           advanced properties are stored in. Always the
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.038|                           safe choice, which is why every OPTY keyword
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.039|                           write uses it.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.040|    REG_DWORD            : May or may not be honoured depending on the
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.041|                           driver. NDIS reads keywords through
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.042|                           NdisReadConfiguration, which returns an integer
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.043|                           from either type, and some drivers do store
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.044|                           keywords as DWORD - so this is not a universal
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.045|                           silent no-op, but it is one reason scripts that
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.046|                           write DWORDs sometimes report success while
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.047|                           nothing changes.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.048|    the clamped value    : What is actually written when the request
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.049|                           exceeds Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max or is not a
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.050|                           multiple of \step. 1024 buffers on a card whose
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.051|                           maximum is 512 becomes 512, and the log says
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.052|                           512.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.053|    keyword absent from Ndi\Params :
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.054|        Nothing is written at all and the helper returns immediately, so a
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.055|        keyword your driver does not expose is skipped rather than
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.056|        invented.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|  Why these profiles : Nothing to decide here, so five identical columns.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|                       It is worth reading because it explains why OPTY's
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|                       numbers sometimes differ from the ones you asked
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|                       for, and why other scripts' identical-looking
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|                       settings can have no effect on your particular
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|                       driver.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008|  Unverified      : Whether a given driver honours a REG_DWORD keyword is
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|                    driver-specific and was not tested here. The claim
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010|                    made is only that REG_SZ is the type the Ndi\Params
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                    schema describes and that it is always accepted.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|  Target          : :nicset - reg add "<class key>" /v "<keyword>" /t
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014|                    REG_SZ /d "<value>" /f, after checking the keyword
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015|                    exists under Ndi\Params, clamping to \max via
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|                    :nicclamp and rounding down to \step via :nicround;
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|                    the old value is read only to label the log line SET,
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|                    written or FIXED (OPTY.bat ~3437-3465)
::
:: ---- net.helper.nicdefault.read (repair) ------------------------
::P|net.helper.nicdefault.read|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|LIRE LA VALEUR D'USINE D'UN MOT-CLE
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003|  Ce que c est    : La primitive de restauration : pour un seul mot-cle
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                    NDIS, elle lit Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default - la
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|                    valeur d'usine publiee par le pilote lui-meme - et la
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006|                    reecrit en REG_SZ.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008|  Effet reel      : Rien n'est code en dur par chipset. La valeur vient du
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|                    pilote de votre propre machine : elle est donc juste
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|                    sur n'importe quelle carte et le reste apres une mise
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.011|                    a jour de pilote.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.012|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.013|  Gain            : Une restauration digne de confiance. Les scripts qui
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.014|                    codent un defaut en dur devinent pour votre carte ;
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.015|                    celui-ci demande au pilote, et c'est pourquoi la
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.016|                    restauration reseau peut etre lancee sans risque sur
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.017|                    du materiel que personne n'a teste.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.018|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.019|  Cout            : Aucun. Si le pilote ne publie aucune valeur par defaut
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.020|                    pour un mot-cle, la fonction laisse ce mot-cle
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.021|                    tranquille au lieu d'inventer une valeur.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.022|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.023|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - elle lit la vraie valeur par defaut au
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.024|                    lieu d'en affirmer une.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.025|
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.026|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.027|    the driver's published default, written as REG_SZ :
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.028|        La seule valeur que cette fonction ecrit jamais. Elle vient de
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.029|        Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default sur votre propre machine : elle est
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.030|        donc juste sur une Intel, une Realtek, une Killer ou n'importe
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.031|        quoi d'autre, et elle le reste apres une mise a jour de pilote.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.032|    no default published : La fonction sort sans rien ecrire. Il n'y a
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.033|                           aucun nombre de repli, parce qu'un defaut
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.034|                           devine est pire qu'un mot-cle laisse
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.035|                           tranquille.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.036|    a hardcoded number   : Ce que font d'autres scripts, et ce que cette
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.037|                           fonction ne fait jamais volontairement : le
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.038|                           defaut, c'est 512 est une supposition sur votre
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.039|                           carte, pas un fait la concernant.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien a decider, donc cinq colonnes identiques.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|                         C'est la brique sur laquelle repose la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003|                         restauration reseau, et ce qu'elle vous dit,
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                         c'est que cette restauration est fiable sur votre
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|                         carte precise et pas seulement sur celle que
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006|                         l'auteur possedait.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008|  Cible           : :nicdefault - strips the keyword name out of the full
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword> key path, reads its default value
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|                    with reg query, and writes it back to the class key as
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.011|                    REG_SZ; if no default is published the keyword is left
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.012|                    untouched (OPTY.bat ~3489-3499)
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|READING ONE KEYWORD'S FACTORY VALUE
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003|  What it is      : The restore primitive: for a single NDIS keyword it
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                    reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default - the factory value
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|                    the driver itself publishes - and writes that back as
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006|                    REG_SZ.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008|  Actual effect   : Nothing is hardcoded per chipset. The value comes from
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|                    your own machine's driver, so it is right on any
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|                    adapter and stays right after a driver update.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.011|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.012|  Gain            : A restore you can trust. Scripts that hardcode a
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.013|                    default are guessing for your card; this one asks the
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.014|                    driver, which is why the network Restore is safe to
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.015|                    run on hardware nobody tested it against.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.016|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.017|  Cost            : None. If the driver publishes no default for a
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.018|                    keyword, the helper leaves that keyword alone rather
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.019|                    than inventing a value.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.020|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.021|  Windows default : Not applicable - it reads the real default instead of
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.022|                    asserting one.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.023|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.025|    the driver's published default, written as REG_SZ :
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.026|        The only value this helper ever writes. It comes from
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.027|        Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default on your own machine, so it is right
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.028|        on an Intel, a Realtek, a Killer or anything else, and it stays
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.029|        right after a driver update.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.030|    no default published : The helper returns without writing anything.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.031|                           There is no fallback number, because a guessed
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.032|                           default is worse than an untouched keyword.
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.033|    a hardcoded number   : What other scripts do, and what this helper
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.034|                           deliberately never does - the default is 512 is
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.035|                           a guess about your card, not a fact about it.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|  Why these profiles : Nothing to decide, so five identical columns. This
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|                       is the brick the network Restore is built from, and
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003|                       what it tells you is that Restore is trustworthy on
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                       your specific adapter rather than on the adapter
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|                       the author happened to own.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|  Target          : :nicdefault - strips the keyword name out of the full
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword> key path, reads its default value
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|                    with reg query, and writes it back to the class key as
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|                    REG_SZ; if no default is published the keyword is left
