::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|NTFS LAST-ACCESS TIMESTAMP
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.003|  What it is      : NTFS can record a last-accessed time on every file you
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.004|                    read. It is one machine-wide switch covering every
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.005|                    NTFS volume, not a per-folder or per-user setting.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.006|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.007|  Actual effect   : Writes one of four states into
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.008|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate. Two axes are encoded in
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.009|                    the same number: who owns the decision (System Managed
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.010|                    or User Managed), and whether the write happens.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.011|                    OPTY's -fad writes 1, and +fad writes 2, the shipped
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.012|                    state.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.013|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.014|  Gain            : No measurable gain on this machine or any machine like
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.015|                    it. The mechanism is real but tiny: NTFS only
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.016|                    refreshes the timestamp when the stored one is more
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.017|                    than about an hour old, so a file read a thousand
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.018|                    times an hour costs one metadata write, not a
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.019|                    thousand. On an SSD that write is invisible next to
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.020|                    everything else the system does.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.021|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.022|  Cost            : Anything that reads last-access times stops being
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.023|                    trustworthy: some backup and archiving tools, File
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.024|                    Server Resource Manager, storage tiering rules, and
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.025|                    forensic timelines. That is a real loss traded for a
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.026|                    gain you cannot measure.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.027|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.028|  Windows default : 2 - System Managed with updates enabled. Verified
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.029|                    live: fsutil reports System managed, last access time
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.030|                    updates enabled.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.031|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.033|    0                    : User Managed, updates ENABLED. You have taken
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.034|                           the decision away from Windows and told NTFS to
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.035|                           keep stamping the last-access time. Same
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.036|                           observable behaviour as 2, but Windows will no
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.037|                           longer change it for you on a future upgrade.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.038|    1                    : User Managed, updates DISABLED. NTFS stops
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.039|                           writing the last-access field of
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.040|                           $STANDARD_INFORMATION entirely. This is the
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.041|                           only value that actually removes the write.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.042|    2                    : System Managed, updates ENABLED. Windows owns
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.043|                           the decision and currently keeps updates on.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.044|                           This is what a fresh Windows 11 install ships
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.045|                           with, verified live on this machine.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.046|    3                    : System Managed, updates DISABLED. Windows keeps
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.047|                           ownership but currently suppresses the write.
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.048|                           Windows itself moves between 2 and 3 on very
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.049|                           large volumes; you setting 3 by hand pins it
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.050|                           there.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|  Why these profiles : All five profiles get 2, and that is the honest
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002|                       answer rather than a shrug. The saving is bounded
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.003|                       at roughly one metadata write per file per hour,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.004|                       which no profile can feel - not the gaming desktop,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.005|                       not the torrent and Plex box, not the laptop on
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.006|                       battery. Even the server case, the one usually
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.007|                       cited, reads the same media files over and over,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.008|                       which is exactly the case the one-hour coalescing
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.009|                       already collapses to almost nothing. If you
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.010|                       administer a volume doing millions of distinct
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.011|                       small reads per hour and nothing there consumes
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.012|                       access times, -fad and value 1 are still there for
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.013|                       you - that is a measurement you take, not a profile
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.014|                       you pick.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.015|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.016|  Unverified      : An earlier audit note said the +fad option writes 0
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.017|                    rather than the shipped 2, so the script could not
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.018|                    undo its own change. Line 506 now reads: fsutil
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.019|                    behavior set disablelastaccess 2. That defect is
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.020|                    already fixed and must not be re-asserted. Value 3
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.021|                    exists and is documented, but I have not seen Windows
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.022|                    select it on this class of machine, so treat the claim
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.023|                    that Windows moves to 3 on very large volumes as
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.024|                    documented behaviour, not as something measured here.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.025|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.026|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disablelastaccess <N> - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.027|                    line 505 (-fad writes 1) and line 506 (+fad writes 2).
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.028|                    Backing value:
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.029|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.030|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate (REG_DWORD). Machine-wide,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.031|                    all NTFS volumes.
