::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.001|RECYCLE BIN ON EVERY FIXED DRIVE, EVERY USER
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.002|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.003|  What it is      : $Recycle.Bin at the root of each drive is where
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.004|                    deleted files wait until the bin is emptied. It holds
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.005|                    one subfolder per user account on the machine. This
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.006|                    step removes the whole store on every fixed drive with
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.007|                    rd /S /Q.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.008|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.009|  Actual effect   : Permanently destroys everything every account on this
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.010|                    machine had deleted but not yet purged. It bypasses
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.011|                    the shell API entirely, so this is not Empty Recycle
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.012|                    Bin for you - it is Empty Recycle Bin for everybody,
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.013|                    on C: and on every other fixed drive.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.014|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.015|  Gain            : Disk space, and sometimes a great deal of it: a bin
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.016|                    nobody has emptied for a year can hold tens of GB.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.017|                    There is no performance benefit of any kind - a full
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.018|                    Recycle Bin does not slow anything down.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.019|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.020|  Cost            : Every deleted file becomes unrecoverable, including
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.021|                    files another user of this machine deleted five
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.022|                    minutes ago and may still want back. Regeneration time
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.023|                    for this data is infinite: it never comes back, which
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.024|                    is exactly what makes it the odd one out among the
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.025|                    cleanup steps. System Restore does not cover it either
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.026|                    - a restore point snapshots the registry and system
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.027|                    files, not your deleted documents, so there is no
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.028|                    rollback of any kind. And because the sweep is part of
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.029|                    the auto path today, an unattended Auto full run
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.030|                    performs it without asking: empty and check your
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.031|                    Recycle Bin yourself before starting one.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.032|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.033|  Windows default : Not applicable - Windows keeps deleted items until the
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.034|                    bin is emptied by hand or the per-drive quota forces
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.035|                    the oldest out.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.036|
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.037|  Possible values:
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.038|    DELETE               : rd /S /Q removes the entire $Recycle.Bin folder
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.039|                           at the root of each fixed drive, including the
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.040|                           per-account subfolder named after each user's
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.041|                           SID. That takes both the recovered file data
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.042|                           and the $I metadata records that hold each
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.043|                           file's original path, so nothing remains to
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.044|                           restore from. Windows recreates the folder
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.045|                           silently the next time anyone deletes a file.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.046|    KEEP                 : The bins are left as they are. Windows keeps
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.047|                           deleted items until someone empties the bin or
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.048|                           until the per-drive size quota is reached, at
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.049|                           which point the oldest entries are purged
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.050|                           automatically.
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.051|    ASK                  : Prompt before the sweep, with no default
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.052|                           answer, and do not proceed on a timeout. This
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.053|                           is the only value that is safe inside an
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.054|                           unattended run, because it converts the run
::T|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.055|                           into an attended one at exactly this point.
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.001|  Why these profiles : The four use profiles all read DELETE, and that is
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.002|                       the owner's standing decision rather than a per-
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.003|                       profile finding: nothing about gaming, serving,
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.004|                       office work or battery life changes what a Recycle
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.005|                       Bin is. What does change by situation is who else
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.006|                       uses the machine, and that is a question the
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.007|                       profile scale cannot ask - on a family or shared PC
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.008|                       this destroys other people's files with no notice
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.009|                       to them. The Windows column is SKIP, correctly:
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.010|                       leaving the bin alone until a human empties it is
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.011|                       precisely what Windows does.
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.012|
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.013|  Unverified      : rd /S /Q removes the folder rather than emptying it.
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.014|                    Windows recreates $Recycle.Bin and the per-SID
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.015|                    subfolder on the next delete, but the desktop Recycle
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.016|                    Bin icon can keep showing a full bin until Explorer
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.017|                    refreshes or the session restarts. That is a cosmetic
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.018|                    artefact I expect from deleting the folder underneath
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.019|                    the shell, not something measured on this machine.
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.020|
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.021|  Target          : for %%D in (%FIXEDLIST%) do if exist
::X|EN|recycle.bin.all.drives.022|                    "%%D:\$Recycle.Bin" rd /S /Q "%%D:\$Recycle.Bin" -
