::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.001|OPTIMISE ALL VOLUMES (DEFRAG / RETRIM)
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.002|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.003|  What it is      : Windows' own volume optimiser, run over every volume
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.004|                    and adapted to the media. On an SSD that means TRIM
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.005|                    and slab consolidation, not defragmentation. On a
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.006|                    mechanical HDD it means real defragmentation.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.007|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.008|  Actual effect   : Runs the optimisation immediately, at normal priority.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.009|                    No setting is changed anywhere - Windows already runs
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.010|                    the same work weekly through a scheduled task, just
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.011|                    throttled and only when the machine is idle.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.012|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.013|  Gain            : On a rotational disk that has not actually been
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.014|                    optimised in a long time, a genuine and measurable
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.015|                    improvement in file access times. On an SSD, none
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.016|                    worth the words: the weekly task keeps TRIM current,
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.017|                    and defragmenting an SSD is not a thing that helps. Be
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.018|                    honest with yourself about which disk you have before
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.019|                    answering.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.020|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.021|  Cost            : Time and I/O, and /H means it takes that I/O from you
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.022|                    rather than waiting for you to be idle. On a large,
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.023|                    full mechanical disk it can run for a long time. On an
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.024|                    SSD it is quick and harmless but writes for no
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.025|                    benefit.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.026|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.027|  Windows default : Windows already does the equivalent weekly, at low
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.028|                    priority, via
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.029|                    \Microsoft\Windows\Defrag\ScheduledDefrag, and no
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.030|                    manual run is part of the shipped behaviour.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.031|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.033|    RUN                  : defrag /C /O /U /V /H. /C is every volume; /O
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.034|                           optimises by media type, which means a real
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.035|                           defragmentation pass on a rotational disk and a
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.036|                           TRIM plus slab consolidation pass on an SSD; /U
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.037|                           and /V print progress and a verbose report; /H
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.038|                           drops the low-priority throttle so the job
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.039|                           competes with your work instead of yielding to
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.040|                           it. The whole point of /H is that it finishes
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.041|                           rather than being deferred.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.042|    RUN WITHOUT /H       : The same pass at the default low priority. It
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.043|                           yields to your work, so on a busy machine it
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.044|                           can take far longer or be interrupted, but it
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.045|                           will not make the system feel sluggish while it
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.046|                           runs. This is what the weekly scheduled task
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.047|                           does.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.048|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs now.
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.049|                           \Microsoft\Windows\Defrag\ScheduledDefrag still
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.050|                           runs weekly at low priority through Automatic
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.051|                           Maintenance, which is the state Windows ships
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.052|                           in.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.001|  Why these profiles : Only the server column says RUN, and it earns it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.002|                       through a mechanism rather than a vibe.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.003|                       ScheduledDefrag runs inside Automatic Maintenance,
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.004|                       which needs the machine to be idle; a box seeding
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.005|                       torrents and serving Plex around the clock is
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.006|                       rarely idle, so on that machine the weekly job is
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.007|                       repeatedly deferred and, on a large bulk HDD, may
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.008|                       never finish a pass. /H is precisely the switch
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.009|                       that ignores that. The other three columns are SKIP
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.010|                       for the same reason in reverse: gaming, office and
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.011|                       laptop machines here are SSD-backed and go idle
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.012|                       regularly, so the weekly task has already done
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.013|                       everything a manual run would do, and on the laptop
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.014|                       it would also be minutes of full-rate disk activity
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.015|                       on battery. The Windows column is SKIP because a
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.016|                       manual, unthrottled run is not something Windows
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.017|                       ever does to itself.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.018|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.019|  Unverified      : Whether a manual run finds anything to do depends
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.020|                    entirely on whether the weekly task has actually been
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.021|                    completing. OPTY does not query the task's last run
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.022|                    result or the volumes' fragmentation state, so it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.023|                    cannot tell you in advance whether this will be a no-
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.024|                    op. The server reasoning above rests on that deferral
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.025|                    mechanism being the real cause on any given box - it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.026|                    is the documented behaviour of Automatic Maintenance,
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.027|                    not something measured here.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.028|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.029|  Target          : defrag /C /O /U /V /H - OPTY.bat line 1221, inside the
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.030|                    :defrag label. Step id clean.defrag, declared _F in
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.031|                    the step table at OPTY.bat line 19135 (auto-full
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.032|                    only). The equivalent Windows job is the scheduled
