:: ---- chkdsk.online.scan (diagnostic) --------------------------------
::P|chkdsk.online.scan|RUN|RUN|RUN|RUN|SKIP|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.001|ANALYSE D'INTEGRITE NTFS EN LIGNE
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.002|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.003|  Ce que c est    : chkdsk C: /scan verifie les structures du systeme de
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.004|                    fichiers NTFS pendant que Windows tourne. Il ne
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.005|                    verrouille pas le volume, n'exige aucun redemarrage et
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.006|                    ne repare rien sur le moment : il met les corruptions
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.007|                    trouvees en file pour la prochaine correction
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.008|                    ponctuelle.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.009|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.010|  Effet reel      : Lit et verifie. S'il trouve des problemes, il les
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.011|                    inscrit dans le journal de corruption du volume ; s'il
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.012|                    ne trouve rien, vous obtenez un rapport propre et rien
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.013|                    n'est modifie.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.014|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.015|  Gain            : Vous apprenez si votre systeme de fichiers est sain,
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.016|                    et c'est tout : c'est un rapport, pas une reparation.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.017|                    Un resultat propre est ici le resultat attendu,
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.018|                    puisque Windows execute deja une auto-reparation
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.019|                    equivalente en continu via le service Spot Verifier et
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.020|                    la tache de maintenance ProactiveScan.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.021|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.022|  Cout            : Quelques minutes de temps et d'acces disque. Rien
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.023|                    d'autre, quel que soit le type de disque. Totalement
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.024|                    sans danger sur SSD : aucune analyse de surface,
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.025|                    aucune ecriture.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.026|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.027|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet en tant que reglage - Windows assure de
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.028|                    lui-meme une auto-reparation en ligne permanente, et
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.029|                    cette etape ne configure rien.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.030|
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.032|    RUN                  : chkdsk C: /scan. Parcourt les metadonnees NTFS
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.033|                           en ligne, sans verrouiller le volume ni
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.034|                           redemarrer. Il ne repare rien sur le moment :
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.035|                           ce qu'il trouve est inscrit dans le journal de
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.036|                           corruption du volume et mis en file pour la
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.037|                           prochaine correction ponctuelle, qui dure
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.038|                           quelques secondes et non des heures.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.039|    SPOTFIX              : chkdsk C: /spotfix. L'operation complementaire
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.040|                           : elle applique ce qu'un /scan precedent a mis
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.041|                           en file. Elle a besoin du volume un court
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.042|                           instant, donc sur le disque systeme elle passe
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.043|                           par un redemarrage, mais de quelques secondes
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.044|                           et non de plusieurs heures. OPTY ne propose pas
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.045|                           cette variante.
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.046|    SKIP                 : Rien ne s'execute. Windows poursuit sa propre
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.047|                           auto-reparation en ligne permanente via le
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.048|                           service Spot Verifier et la tache de
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.049|                           maintenance ProactiveScan, ce qui est l'etat
::T|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.050|                           livre.
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes RUN identiques, parce qu'un
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.002|                         controle d'integrite en lecture seule n'a aucun
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.003|                         inconvenient qui varie selon l'usage. Le portable
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.004|                         reste a RUN lui aussi : quelques minutes de
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.005|                         lecture de metadonnees ne sont pas l'evenement
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.006|                         pour la batterie que serait une analyse de
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.007|                         surface /r. La colonne Windows est a SKIP parce
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.008|                         que Windows ne lance vraiment pas chkdsk /scan a
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.009|                         la demande - son equivalent est le travail
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.010|                         continu de ProactiveScan, qui a lieu de toute
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.011|                         facon quelle que soit votre reponse.
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.012|
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.013|  Cible           : chkdsk %SystemDrive% /scan - OPTY.bat line 1256,
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.014|                    inside the :chkdsk label, reached from the :mchkdsk
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.015|                    menu option 1. Step id clean.chkdsk, declared __ in
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.016|                    the step table at OPTY.bat line 19136, meaning it runs
::X|FR|chkdsk.online.scan.017|                    in neither auto mode.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.001|ONLINE NTFS INTEGRITY SCAN
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.002|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.003|  What it is      : chkdsk C: /scan checks the NTFS filesystem structures
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.004|                    while Windows is running. It does not lock the volume,
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.005|                    does not require a reboot, and does not repair
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.006|                    anything on the spot - it queues any corruption it
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.007|                    finds for the next spot-fix.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.008|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.009|  Actual effect   : Reads and verifies. If it finds problems it records
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.010|                    them in the volume's corruption record; if it finds
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.011|                    nothing you get a clean report and nothing changes.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.012|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.013|  Gain            : You learn whether your filesystem is healthy, and that
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.014|                    is the whole of it - this is a report, not a repair. A
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.015|                    clean result here is the expected result, because
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.016|                    Windows already runs an equivalent self-heal
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.017|                    continuously through the Spot Verifier service and the
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.018|                    ProactiveScan maintenance task.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.019|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.020|  Cost            : A few minutes of time and disk I/O. Nothing else, on
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.021|                    any drive type. It is entirely safe on SSDs - there is
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.022|                    no surface scan and no write.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.023|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.024|  Windows default : Not applicable as a setting - Windows performs
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.025|                    continuous online self-healing on its own, and this
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.026|                    step configures nothing.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.027|
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.029|    RUN                  : chkdsk C: /scan. Walks the NTFS metadata
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.030|                           online, without locking the volume and without
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.031|                           a reboot. It repairs nothing on the spot:
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.032|                           anything it finds is written into the volume's
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.033|                           corruption record and queued for the next spot-
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.034|                           fix, which is a seconds-long operation rather
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.035|                           than an hours-long one.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.036|    SPOTFIX              : chkdsk C: /spotfix. The companion operation: it
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.037|                           applies what a previous /scan queued. It does
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.038|                           need the volume briefly, so on the system drive
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.039|                           it takes a reboot, but seconds of it rather
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.040|                           than hours. OPTY does not offer this variant.
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.041|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs. Windows keeps doing its own
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.042|                           continuous online self-healing through the Spot
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.043|                           Verifier service and the ProactiveScan
::T|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.044|                           maintenance task, which is the shipped state.
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.001|  Why these profiles : Four identical RUN columns, because a read-only
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.002|                       integrity check has no downside that varies by use
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.003|                       case. The laptop stays RUN too: a few minutes of
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.004|                       metadata reads is not the battery event that a /r
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.005|                       surface scan would be. The Windows column is SKIP
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.006|                       because Windows genuinely does not run chkdsk /scan
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.007|                       on demand - its equivalent is the continuous
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.008|                       ProactiveScan work, which is already happening
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.009|                       whatever you answer here.
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.010|
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.011|  Target          : chkdsk %SystemDrive% /scan - OPTY.bat line 1256,
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.012|                    inside the :chkdsk label, reached from the :mchkdsk
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.013|                    menu option 1. Step id clean.chkdsk, declared __ in
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.014|                    the step table at OPTY.bat line 19136, meaning it runs
::X|EN|chkdsk.online.scan.015|                    in neither auto mode.
::
:: ---- chkdsk.full.repair (risky) --------------------------------
::P|chkdsk.full.repair|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.001|CHKDSK /F /R COMPLET
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.002|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.003|  Ce que c est    : CHKDSK /f corrige les erreurs du systeme de fichiers ;
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.004|                    /r lit en plus chaque secteur du disque pour reperer
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.005|                    les secteurs defectueux et deplacer les donnees encore
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.006|                    lisibles qui s'y trouvent.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.007|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.008|  Effet reel      : Depend du volume vise. Si le volume peut etre
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.009|                    verrouille, il s'execute sur-le-champ et le garde
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.010|                    pendant toute l'analyse. S'il ne le peut pas - le
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.011|                    disque systeme, pendant que Windows tourne - il
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.012|                    propose de planifier la verification au prochain
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.013|                    redemarrage, et celle-ci s'execute alors avant le
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.014|                    chargement de Windows, sans possibilite d'utiliser la
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.015|                    machine entre-temps.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.016|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.017|  Gain            : Sur un disque mecanique reellement en erreur, c'est
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.018|                    l'outil qui repare le systeme de fichiers et met de
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.019|                    cote les secteurs defaillants. Si votre probleme est
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.020|                    une vraie corruption disque, rien d'autre dans ce menu
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.021|                    ne le corrigera. Sur une machine saine, ou sur un SSD,
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.022|                    /r ne trouve rien et n'apporte rien.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.023|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.024|  Cout            : Sur un disque gros ou lent, cela peut durer des
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.025|                    heures, et on ne peut plus l'annuler une fois lance.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.026|                    L'analyse de surface malmene un disque deja mourant et
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.027|                    peut l'achever : sur un disque en fin de vie, il faut
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.028|                    copier vos donnees, pas les scanner. /r est concu pour
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.029|                    les disques a plateaux et n'a pas sa place sur un SSD,
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.030|                    ou le remappage effectue par le controleur reste de
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.031|                    toute facon invisible pour chkdsk. On annule une
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.032|                    verification planifiee au demarrage avec chkntfs /x C:
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.033|                    avant de redemarrer.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.034|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.035|  Defaut Windows  : Non planifie par defaut. Windows ne lance autochk que
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.036|                    sur un volume qu'il a marque comme sale.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.037|
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.038|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.039|    RUN                  : chkdsk /f /r sur le volume vise. /f corrige les
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.040|                           erreurs du systeme de fichiers et exige un
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.041|                           verrou exclusif. /r ajoute une analyse de
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.042|                           surface complete : chaque secteur est lu, et
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.043|                           toute donnee encore lisible dans un secteur
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.044|                           defaillant est recopiee ailleurs avant que le
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.045|                           secteur soit marque comme mauvais. Sur un
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.046|                           volume que Windows peut verrouiller, il demarre
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.047|                           immediatement et garde le volume pendant toute
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.048|                           la duree. Sur un volume qu'il ne peut pas
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.049|                           verrouiller, comme le disque systeme, il
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.050|                           propose de planifier la verification au
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.051|                           prochain redemarrage, et autochk l'execute
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.052|                           avant le chargement de Windows.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.053|    RUN /F ONLY          : chkdsk /f sans /r. Repare les structures du
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.054|                           systeme de fichiers mais saute l'analyse
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.055|                           secteur par secteur. C'est la forme appropriee
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.056|                           sur un SSD, ou lire chaque secteur n'apprend
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.057|                           rien d'utile sur la memoire flash sous-jacente
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.058|                           et ou le controleur a deja remappe ce qu'il
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.059|                           devait.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.060|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est planifie et rien ne s'execute.
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.061|                           Windows n'invoque autochk que sur un volume
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.062|                           qu'il a lui-meme marque comme sale, un etat
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.063|                           qu'il pose apres une defaillance precise et non
::T|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.064|                           en routine.
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq colonnes repondent SKIP, et c'est une
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.002|                         decision, pas une esquive. Le declencheur de cet
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.003|                         outil n'est pas un usage mais un symptome sur
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.004|                         support a plateaux : fichiers corrompus, erreurs
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.005|                         CRC dans le journal d'evenements, gels en
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.006|                         lecture. Aucun des quatre profils d'usage
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.007|                         n'implique l'un ou l'autre - un PC de jeu, un
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.008|                         serveur Plex, un poste bureautique et un portable
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.009|                         ont exactement autant de chances d'aller bien, et
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.010|                         les machines de ce projet sont sur SSD, ou /r ne
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.011|                         peut rien apporter. Celui qui a le symptome le
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.012|                         lance deliberement, apres sauvegarde, et n'a pas
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.013|                         besoin d'un profil pour le lui dire.
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.014|
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.015|  Problemes connus : La ligne 1268 est CHKDSK /f /r sans argument de
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.016|                     volume, alors que l'analyse en ligne de la ligne 1256
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.017|                     passe correctement %SystemDrive%. Sans argument,
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.018|                     chkdsk agit sur le disque du repertoire courant,
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.019|                     c'est-a-dire le volume depuis lequel OPTY.bat a ete
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.020|                     lance - sur cette machine, E: et non C:. Deux
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.021|                     consequences. Toute la premisse de la fiche, a savoir
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.022|                     qu'il doit se planifier au prochain redemarrage parce
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.023|                     que le disque systeme ne peut pas etre verrouille, ne
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.024|                     vaut que pour C: ; sur un volume de donnees, chkdsk
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.025|                     le verrouille et demarre immediatement une analyse de
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.026|                     surface de plusieurs heures, sans redemarrage et sans
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.027|                     seconde chance de se raviser. Et l'utilisateur qui a
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.028|                     choisi cette entree de menu pour verifier son disque
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.029|                     systeme n'a pas verifie son disque systeme. La ligne
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.030|                     1268 devrait etre : CHKDSK %SystemDrive% /f /r.
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.031|
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.032|  Non verifie (en)  : The bug above is read directly from the source line,
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.033|                      not observed at runtime, because running it to find
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.034|                      out is precisely the thing this card advises
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.035|                      against.
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.036|
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.037|  Cible           : CHKDSK /f /r - OPTY.bat line 1268, inside the
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.038|                    :chkdsk_full label, reached from the :mchkdsk menu
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.039|                    option 2. Note there is NO volume argument on that
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.040|                    line, unlike the online scan at line 1256 which passes
::X|FR|chkdsk.full.repair.041|                    %SystemDrive%.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.001|FULL CHKDSK /F /R
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.002|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.003|  What it is      : CHKDSK /f fixes filesystem errors; /r additionally
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.004|                    reads every sector on the disk looking for bad ones
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.005|                    and relocating whatever data is still readable out of
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.006|                    them.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.007|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.008|  Actual effect   : Depends on which volume it lands on. If the volume can
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.009|                    be locked it runs there and then, holding it for the
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.010|                    whole scan. If it cannot - the system drive, while
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.011|                    Windows is running - it offers to schedule the check
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.012|                    for the next restart, and the check then runs before
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.013|                    Windows loads with no way to use the machine
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.014|                    meanwhile.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.015|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.016|  Gain            : On a mechanical drive with real errors, this is the
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.017|                    tool that repairs the filesystem and maps out failing
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.018|                    sectors. If your problem is genuine disk corruption,
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.019|                    nothing else in this menu will fix it. On a healthy
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.020|                    machine, or on an SSD, /r produces no finding and no
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.021|                    benefit.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.022|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.023|  Cost            : On a large or slow disk it can run for many hours, and
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.024|                    it cannot be cancelled once it has started. The
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.025|                    surface scan hammers a drive that is already dying and
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.026|                    can be the push that finishes it - on a failing disk
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.027|                    you should be imaging your data, not scanning it. /r
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.028|                    is designed for spinning rust and has no place on an
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.029|                    SSD, where the controller's own remapping is invisible
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.030|                    to chkdsk anyway. You can cancel a scheduled boot-time
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.031|                    check with chkntfs /x C: before rebooting.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.032|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.033|  Windows default : Not scheduled by default. Windows only runs autochk on
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.034|                    a volume it has flagged dirty.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.035|
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.036|  Possible values:
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.037|    RUN                  : chkdsk /f /r on the target volume. /f fixes
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.038|                           filesystem errors and needs an exclusive lock.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.039|                           /r adds a full surface scan: every sector is
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.040|                           read, and any data still readable in a failing
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.041|                           sector is copied elsewhere and the sector
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.042|                           marked bad. On a volume Windows can lock, it
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.043|                           starts immediately and holds the volume for the
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.044|                           duration. On a volume it cannot lock, such as
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.045|                           the system drive, it offers to schedule the
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.046|                           check for the next restart, and autochk runs it
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.047|                           before Windows loads.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.048|    RUN /F ONLY          : chkdsk /f without /r. Repairs the filesystem
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.049|                           structures but skips the sector-by-sector
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.050|                           surface scan. This is the appropriate form on
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.051|                           an SSD, where reading every sector tells you
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.052|                           nothing useful about the flash underneath and
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.053|                           the controller has already remapped what it
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.054|                           needed to.
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.055|    SKIP                 : Nothing is scheduled and nothing runs. Windows
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.056|                           only invokes autochk on a volume it has itself
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.057|                           flagged dirty, which is a state it sets after a
::T|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.058|                           specific failure, not as routine.
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.001|  Why these profiles : All five columns say SKIP, and that is a decision
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.002|                       rather than an evasion. The trigger for this tool
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.003|                       is not a use case, it is a symptom on rotational
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.004|                       media: file corruption, CRC errors in the event
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.005|                       log, freezes on read. None of the four use profiles
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.006|                       implies either of those - a gaming desktop, a Plex
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.007|                       server, an office PC and a laptop are all equally
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.008|                       likely to be fine, and this project's machines are
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.009|                       SSD-backed, where /r cannot help at all. Anyone
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.010|                       with the symptom takes it deliberately, after a
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.011|                       backup, and does not need a profile to tell them.
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.012|
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.013|  Known problems  : Line 1268 is CHKDSK /f /r with no volume argument,
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.014|                    while the online scan on line 1256 correctly passes
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.015|                    %SystemDrive%. With no argument chkdsk operates on the
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.016|                    current directory's drive, which is whatever volume
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.017|                    OPTY.bat was launched from - on this machine that is
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.018|                    E:, not C:. Two consequences follow. The card's whole
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.019|                    premise, that it must schedule itself for the next
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.020|                    reboot because the system drive cannot be locked, only
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.021|                    holds for C:; on a data volume chkdsk locks it and
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.022|                    starts a multi-hour surface scan immediately, with no
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.023|                    reboot and no second chance to back out. And the user
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.024|                    who chose this menu item to check their system drive
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.025|                    did not check their system drive. Line 1268 should
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.026|                    read: CHKDSK %SystemDrive% /f /r.
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.027|
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.028|  Unverified      : The bug above is read directly from the source line,
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.029|                    not observed at runtime, because running it to find
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.030|                    out is precisely the thing this card advises against.
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.031|
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.032|  Target          : CHKDSK /f /r - OPTY.bat line 1268, inside the
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.033|                    :chkdsk_full label, reached from the :mchkdsk menu
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.034|                    option 2. Note there is NO volume argument on that
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.035|                    line, unlike the online scan at line 1256 which passes
::X|EN|chkdsk.full.repair.036|                    %SystemDrive%.
::
:: ---- ssd.trim.on (repair) ---------------------------------------
::P|ssd.trim.on|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.001|TRIM SUR SSD
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.002|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.003|  Ce que c est    : Le TRIM est la facon dont Windows indique a un SSD
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.004|                    quels blocs ne contiennent plus de donnees utiles,
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.005|                    pour que le disque puisse les effacer a l'avance au
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.006|                    lieu de le faire pendant votre prochaine ecriture.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.007|                    Sans lui, un SSD ecrit de plus en plus lentement a
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.008|                    mesure qu'il se remplit et vieillit.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.009|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 0 dans le comportement de notification de
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.011|                    suppression, donc les notifications TRIM sont emises.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.012|                    C'est la valeur par defaut ; l'ecriture compte sur une
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.013|                    machine ou quelqu'un a applique le vieux tweak disable
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.014|                    delete notify, et c'est une operation sans effet
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.015|                    partout ailleurs.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.016|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.017|  Gain            : Sur une machine ou le TRIM avait ete coupe, cela
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.018|                    arrete une degradation lente et cumulative des
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.019|                    performances en ecriture et laisse le disque se
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.020|                    retablir a mesure que les blocs sont reecrits. Sur une
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.021|                    machine ou il etait deja actif - c'est-a-dire presque
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.022|                    toutes - il n'y a aucun changement, et aucun gain a
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.023|                    revendiquer.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.024|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.025|  Cout            : Aucun. Il n'existe aucune raison credible de
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.026|                    desactiver le TRIM sur un SSD moderne. Le seul effet
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.027|                    de bord a nommer est que les donnees supprimees
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.028|                    deviennent vraiment irrecuperables plus tot, ce qui
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.029|                    est une propriete de securite et non un cout - mais
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.030|                    cela signifie qu'aucun outil de recuperation ne vous
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.031|                    aidera.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.032|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.033|  Defaut Windows  : 0 - le TRIM est active par defaut sur toutes les
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.034|                    versions de Windows 11 prises en charge.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.035|
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.036|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.037|    0                    : Notification de suppression ACTIVEE - le TRIM
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.038|                           est envoye. A la suppression d'un fichier, le
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.039|                           systeme de fichiers emet un TRIM (ou un UNMAP
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.040|                           sur NVMe) indiquant au disque quels blocs
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.041|                           logiques ne contiennent plus de donnees. Le
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.042|                           controleur peut alors effacer ces pages de
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.043|                           flash pendant ses temps morts, si bien que
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.044|                           votre prochaine ecriture arrive dans une page
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.045|                           deja effacee au lieu de declencher un cycle
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.046|                           lecture-modification-effacement-ecriture. C'est
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.047|                           la valeur livree.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.048|    1                    : Notification de suppression DESACTIVEE - aucun
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.049|                           TRIM n'est envoye. Le disque continue de croire
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.050|                           que chaque bloc qu'il a ecrit contient encore
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.051|                           des donnees vivantes, si bien que sa reserve de
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.052|                           pages pre-effacees se reduit a presque rien.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.053|                           Les performances en ecriture se degradent alors
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.054|                           progressivement et durablement, jusqu'a un
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.055|                           effacement securise du disque ou jusqu'a ce que
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.056|                           son ramasse-miettes interne devine juste, ce
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.057|                           qu'il ne peut largement pas faire sans TRIM.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.058|                           C'est l'etat que laisse le vieux tweak disable
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.059|                           delete notify.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques, et il n'y a pas a s'en
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.002|                         excuser : le TRIM releve de la facon dont la
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.003|                         flash s'efface, pas de ce que vous faites avec
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.004|                         l'ordinateur. Le jeu, l'hebergement, la
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.005|                         bureautique et l'autonomie veulent tous la meme
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.006|                         chose, et la colonne Windows aussi, puisque 0 est
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.007|                         exactement ce qui est livre. L'ecrire sans
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.008|                         condition est justement l'objectif : sur une
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.009|                         machine reglee par quelqu'un d'autre, c'est cette
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.010|                         ecriture qui rend le defaut a nouveau vrai.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.011|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.012|  Cible           : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.013|                    line 1558, in the setup path (the re-assert good
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.014|                    defaults block). Backing values:
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.015|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.016|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v RefsDisableDeleteNotify
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.017|                    (REG_DWORD). With no filesystem argument, current
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.018|                    builds set both.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.001|SSD TRIM
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.002|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.003|  What it is      : TRIM is how Windows tells an SSD which blocks no
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.004|                    longer hold useful data, so the drive can erase them
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.005|                    in advance instead of during your next write. Without
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.006|                    it an SSD gets progressively slower at writing as it
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.007|                    fills and ages.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.008|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.009|  Actual effect   : Writes 0 to the delete-notify behaviour, which means
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.010|                    TRIM notifications are sent. This is the default; the
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.011|                    write matters on a machine where someone applied the
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.012|                    old disable delete notify tweak, and is a confirmed
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.013|                    no-op everywhere else.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.014|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.015|  Gain            : On a machine where TRIM had been turned off, this
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.016|                    stops a slow, cumulative destruction of write
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.017|                    performance and lets the drive recover as blocks are
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.018|                    rewritten. On a machine where it was already on -
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.019|                    which is nearly all of them - there is no change at
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.020|                    all, and no gain to claim.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.021|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.022|  Cost            : None. There is no credible reason to have TRIM off on
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.023|                    a modern SSD. The only side effect worth naming is
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.024|                    that deleted data becomes genuinely unrecoverable
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.025|                    sooner, which is a security property, not a cost - but
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.026|                    it does mean undelete tools will not help you.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.027|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.028|  Windows default : 0 - TRIM is enabled by default on every supported
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.029|                    Windows 11 build.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.030|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.032|    0                    : Delete notification ENABLED - TRIM is sent.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.033|                           When a file is deleted, the filesystem issues a
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.034|                           TRIM (or UNMAP on NVMe) telling the drive which
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.035|                           logical blocks no longer hold data. The
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.036|                           controller can then erase those flash pages
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.037|                           during idle time, so your next write lands in
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.038|                           an already-erased page instead of triggering a
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.039|                           read-modify-erase-write cycle. This is the
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.040|                           shipped value.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.041|    1                    : Delete notification DISABLED - no TRIM is sent.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.042|                           The drive keeps believing every block it ever
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.043|                           wrote still holds live data, so its pool of
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.044|                           pre-erased pages shrinks toward nothing. Write
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.045|                           performance then degrades progressively and
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.046|                           permanently until the drive is secure-erased or
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.047|                           its own background garbage collection guesses
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.048|                           right, which without TRIM it largely cannot.
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.049|                           This is the state the old disable delete notify
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.050|                           tweak leaves behind.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeroes, and there is nothing to
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.002|                       apologise for: TRIM is a property of how flash
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.003|                       erases, not of what you do with the computer.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.004|                       Gaming, serving, office work and battery life all
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.005|                       want the same thing, and the Windows column wants
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.006|                       it too because 0 is exactly what ships. Writing it
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.007|                       unconditionally is the point - on a machine someone
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.008|                       else tuned, this is the write that makes the
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.009|                       default true again.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.010|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.011|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.012|                    line 1558, in the setup path (the re-assert good
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.013|                    defaults block). Backing values:
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.014|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.015|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v RefsDisableDeleteNotify
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.016|                    (REG_DWORD). With no filesystem argument, current
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.017|                    builds set both.
::
:: ---- disable8dot3.system.managed (repair) -----------------------
::P|disable8dot3.system.managed|2|2|2|2|2|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|STRATEGIE DES NOMS COURTS 8.3
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.003|  Ce que c est    : NTFS peut generer un nom court a l'ancienne (PROGRA~1)
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.004|                    en parallele de chaque nom long, pour la compatibilite
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.005|                    avec les logiciels ecrits avant les noms longs.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.006|                    Windows livre ce choix comme une decision par volume,
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.007|                    valeur 2. Un tweak courant force 1, ce qui desactive
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.008|                    les noms courts partout.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.009|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 2, la strategie par volume d'origine. Important
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.011|                    : cela n'affecte que les noms crees a partir de
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.012|                    maintenant. Les noms courts existants ne changent dans
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.013|                    aucun sens, donc cela ne repare pas les degats d'une
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.014|                    periode passee a la valeur 1 - un programme dont le
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.015|                    chemin court n'a jamais ete cree ne le trouvera
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.016|                    toujours pas.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.017|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.018|  Gain            : Cela evite une categorie de pannes plutot que
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.019|                    d'apporter de la vitesse. Les vieux desinstallateurs
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.020|                    MSI et les applications ayant memorise des chemins du
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.021|                    type PROGRA~1 cessent d'echouer, pour les fichiers
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.022|                    crees desormais. L'argument de performance en faveur
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.023|                    de la desactivation n'est reel que sur des volumes
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.024|                    comptant des centaines de milliers de fichiers dans un
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.025|                    seul dossier, ou chaque creation doit verifier
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.026|                    l'absence de collision de nom - pas sur un PC normal.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.027|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.028|  Cout            : Aucun sur un poste fixe ou portable. Sur un vrai gros
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.029|                    serveur de fichiers, vous pourriez mesurer une
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.030|                    difference et choisir 1 ou 3 en connaissance de cause
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.031|                    ; ici la difference n'est pas mesurable.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.032|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.033|  Defaut Windows  : 2 - par volume, ce qui sur une installation par defaut
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.034|                    signifie noms 8.3 actifs sur le volume systeme et
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.035|                    inactifs ailleurs.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.036|
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.037|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.038|    0                    : Creation des noms 8.3 ACTIVEE sur tous les
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.039|                           volumes. Chaque nouveau nom long recoit aussi
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.040|                           un nom court facon PROGRA~1 inscrit dans
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.041|                           l'entree de repertoire. Compatibilite maximale,
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.042|                           metadonnees maximales.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.043|    1                    : Creation des noms 8.3 DESACTIVEE sur tous les
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.044|                           volumes, disque systeme compris. C'est ce que
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.045|                           force le tweak populaire. C'est le reglage qui
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.046|                           casse les vieux desinstallateurs MSI et les
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.047|                           applications anciennes ayant memorise un chemin
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.048|                           court a l'installation et incapables de le
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.049|                           resoudre ensuite.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.050|    2                    : Par volume - chaque volume porte son propre
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.051|                           indicateur et Windows decide. Sur une
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.052|                           installation par defaut, cela signifie des noms
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.053|                           courts crees sur le volume systeme et pas sur
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.054|                           les autres. C'est la strategie livree.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.055|    3                    : Desactivee sur tous les volumes SAUF le volume
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.056|                           systeme. Le reglage de compromis : le volume
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.057|                           systeme garde la compatibilite dont dependent
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.058|                           les installeurs, les volumes de donnees aux
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.059|                           dossiers enormes s'epargnent le cout. Utile
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.060|                           seulement si vous avez reellement mesure ce
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.061|                           cout.
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq valeurs 2 identiques. Le seul argument en
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|                         faveur d'une autre valeur tient a la taille des
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.003|                         repertoires, et il exige des centaines de
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.004|                         milliers d'entrees dans un meme dossier avant que
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.005|                         la verification de collision coute quoi que ce
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.006|                         soit - un seuil qu'aucun des quatre profils
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.007|                         d'usage n'atteint, serveur de medias compris,
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.008|                         dont les fichiers sont gros et non nombreux. La
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.009|                         colonne Windows vaut 2 pour la raison la plus
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.010|                         simple qui soit : 2 est ce qu'a une installation
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.011|                         propre, donc cette fiche et le defaut livre
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.012|                         disent la meme chose.
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.013|
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.014|  Cible           : fsutil behavior set disable8dot3 2 - OPTY.bat line
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.015|                    1563, in the setup path. Backing value:
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.016|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.017|                    NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation (REG_DWORD).
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|8.3 SHORT FILENAMES POLICY
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.003|  What it is      : NTFS can generate an old-style 8.3 short name
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.004|                    (PROGRA~1) alongside every long filename, for
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.005|                    compatibility with software written before long names
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.006|                    existed. Windows ships this as a per-volume decision,
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.007|                    value 2. A common tweak forces 1, disabling short
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.008|                    names everywhere.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.009|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.010|  Actual effect   : Writes 2, the shipped per-volume policy. Important:
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.011|                    this only affects names created from now on. Existing
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.012|                    short names are unchanged either way, so it cannot
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.013|                    repair damage that a period at value 1 has already
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.014|                    done - a program whose short path was never created
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.015|                    still will not find it.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.016|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.017|  Gain            : It prevents a class of breakage rather than adding
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.018|                    speed. Legacy MSI uninstallers and older applications
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.019|                    that stored PROGRA~1 style paths stop failing on files
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.020|                    created from here on. The performance argument for
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.021|                    disabling 8.3 names is real only on volumes with
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.022|                    hundreds of thousands of files in a single directory,
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.023|                    where each creation has to scan for a name collision -
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.024|                    not on any normal PC.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.025|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.026|  Cost            : None on a desktop or laptop. On a genuine large file
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.027|                    server you might measure a difference and choose 1 or
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.028|                    3 deliberately; here the difference is not measurable.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.029|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.030|  Windows default : 2 - per-volume, which on a default install means 8.3
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.031|                    names on the system volume and off elsewhere.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.032|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.033|  Possible values:
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.034|    0                    : 8.3 name creation ENABLED on every volume.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.035|                           Every new long filename also gets a PROGRA~1
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.036|                           style short name written into the directory
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.037|                           entry. Maximum compatibility, maximum metadata.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.038|    1                    : 8.3 name creation DISABLED on every volume,
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.039|                           system volume included. This is what the
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.040|                           popular tweak forces. It is the setting that
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.041|                           breaks legacy MSI uninstallers and older
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.042|                           applications which stored a short path at
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.043|                           install time and cannot resolve it afterwards.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.044|    2                    : Per-volume - each volume carries its own flag
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.045|                           and Windows decides. On a default install that
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.046|                           means short names are created on the system
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.047|                           volume and not on the others. This is the
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.048|                           shipped policy.
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.049|    3                    : Disabled on every volume EXCEPT the system
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.050|                           volume. The compromise setting: the system
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.051|                           volume keeps the compatibility that installers
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.052|                           depend on, data volumes with huge directories
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.053|                           skip the cost. Meaningful only if you have
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.054|                           actually measured that cost.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical twos. The only argument for a
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|                       different value is directory-size driven, and it
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.003|                       needs hundreds of thousands of entries in one
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.004|                       folder before the name-collision scan costs
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.005|                       anything - a threshold none of the four use
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.006|                       profiles reaches, including the media server, whose
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.007|                       files are large rather than numerous. The Windows
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.008|                       column is 2 for the plainest reason available: 2 is
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.009|                       what a clean install has, so this card and the
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.010|                       shipped default are the same answer.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.011|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.012|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disable8dot3 2 - OPTY.bat line
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.013|                    1563, in the setup path. Backing value:
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.014|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.015|                    NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation (REG_DWORD).
