:: ---- rad.driverpolicy.killkeys (repair) -------------------------
::P|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|RETIRER LES TROIS STRATEGIES QUI BLOQUENT LES PILOTES
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|  Ce que c est    : Trois valeurs de strategie qui bloquent chacune une
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                    partie differente de la distribution des pilotes, des
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                    firmwares et des metadonnees de peripheriques :
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate et
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|  Effet reel      : Supprime les trois si elles existent, pour que
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                    pilotes, firmwares et metadonnees repassent
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                    normalement. Suppression plutot que mise a zero, parce
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|                    que le defaut des trois est que la valeur n'existe
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|                    pas. Chaque retrait est journalise ; une valeur deja
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|                    absente est laissee tranquille, en silence.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|  Gain            : Corrige le cas du peripherique fraichement branche qui
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                    n'obtient ni pilote ni mise a jour de firmware et
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                    s'affiche sous un nom de modele generique. C'est une
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|                    reparation avec un symptome visible a la cle, pas une
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|                    amelioration hypothetique : le symptome, c'est un
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|                    peripherique en erreur dans le Gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|                    peripheriques sans la moindre explication du fait que
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|                    Windows n'a jamais cherche.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|  Cout            : Rien sur une machine personnelle. Sur un parc
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|                    d'entreprise gere, l'informatique peut les avoir
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|                    posees volontairement : les retirer vous met alors
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|                    hors strategie, et la prochaine actualisation de
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|                    strategie de groupe les reecrira aussitot.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois absentes.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|    DELETE               : Les trois noms de valeur sont supprimes. Absent
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                           signifie non configure : le client Windows
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                           Update reinclut les pilotes dans les mises a
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|                           jour qualite, l'installation de peripherique
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|                           interroge Windows Update quand le magasin local
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                           n'a pas de pilote, et le service de metadonnees
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|                           peut recuperer sur le reseau le nom convivial,
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                           l'icone et la vignette Peripheriques et
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|                           imprimantes. L'absence est l'etat livre pour
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.044|                           les trois.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.045|    1                    : Strategie appliquee - chaque valeur bloque son
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.046|                           propre canal. ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.047|                           retire les pilotes des mises a jour qualite.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.048|                           DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 empeche totalement
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.049|                           l'installation de peripherique d'interroger
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.050|                           Windows Update : un peripherique sans pilote
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.051|                           integre reste dans le Gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.052|                           peripheriques avec une erreur et aucune
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.053|                           explication. PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.054|                           laisse les peripheriques affiches sous un nom
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.055|                           de modele generique, sans icone.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.056|    0                    : Strategie presente et explicitement desactivee.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.057|                           Le comportement rejoint celui de l'absence,
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.058|                           mais la valeur reste sous la ruche Policies, ou
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.059|                           les outils de strategie de groupe la signalent
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.060|                           comme configuree et ou une actualisation
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.061|                           ulterieure peut la rebasculer. C'est un
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.062|                           troisieme etat, pas le defaut - d'ou la
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.063|                           suppression plutot que la mise a zero.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes DELETE identiques, et la colonne
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|                         Windows est la plus solide des cinq plutot que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|                         l'intruse : l'absence est litteralement ce qui
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                         est livre, donc supprimer ces valeurs retablit le
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                         defaut au lieu de le contredire. Aucun des quatre
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|                         profils d'usage ne veut d'un peripherique
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|                         incapable d'obtenir un pilote - la machine de jeu
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|                         a besoin des pilotes de carte graphique et de
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|                         manette, le serveur des firmwares de stockage et
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|                         de carte reseau, le poste bureautique des
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                         imprimantes, le portable des stations d'accueil
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                         et du Wi-Fi. La seule machine qui devrait les
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|                         garder est celle dont l'informatique detient la
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|                         strategie, et ce n'est pas un profil de cette
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|                         echelle.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|  Problemes connus : OPTY se contredit sur
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                     ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate. La ligne 1482, dans
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                     :enable_windows_update, l'ecrit a 0 avec REG ADD -
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|                     creant la valeur sur une machine qui ne l'avait
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|                     jamais eue, soit le troisieme etat decrit plus haut
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|                     et non le defaut. La ligne 1845 la supprime,
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|                     correctement. La regle posee par ce projet est que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|                     DELETE signifie que le vrai defaut est l'absence : la
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|                     ligne 1482 devrait donc etre elle aussi un appel a
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|                     :killkey, pas un REG ADD de 0.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|  Non verifie (en)  : DontSearchWindowsUpdate lives under
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|                      Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching, while
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|                      the user-visible Device installation settings dialog
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|                      writes SearchOrderConfig under
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|                      CurrentVersion\DriverSearching. They are different
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|                      keys with overlapping effects, so a machine can show
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|                      driver downloads as allowed in the GUI while the
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|                      policy value still blocks them. Which one wins in
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                      every combination is not something I verified on
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                      this machine.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|  Cible           : Three :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1845 to 1847 -
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|                    /v ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching /v
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate, and
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.044|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.045|                    Metadata /v PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork. :killkey
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.046|                    (OPTY.bat line 3987) queries first and deletes only if
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.047|                    the value exists, logging the removal.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|REMOVE THE THREE DRIVER-BLOCKING POLICIES
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|  What it is      : Three policy values that each block a different part
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                    of driver, firmware and device-metadata delivery:
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate and
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes all three if present, so drivers, firmware and
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|                    device metadata can arrive normally again. Deletion
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                    rather than zeroing, because the default for all three
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                    is that the value does not exist. Each removal is
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|                    logged; a value that was already absent is left
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|                    silently alone.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|  Gain            : Fixes the case where a newly attached device gets no
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|                    driver and no firmware update and shows up under a
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                    generic model string. This is a repair with a visible
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                    symptom attached, not a speculative improvement - the
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|                    symptom is a device in Device Manager with an error
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|                    code and no explanation of why Windows never looked.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|  Cost            : Nothing on a personal machine. On a managed corporate
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|                    fleet these three may be set deliberately by IT, in
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|                    which case removing them puts you out of policy and
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|                    the next Group Policy refresh will write them straight
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|                    back.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|  Windows default : All three absent.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|    DELETE               : All three value names are removed. Absent means
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|                           not configured: the Windows Update client
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|                           includes drivers in quality updates again,
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|                           device installation queries Windows Update for
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                           a driver when the local store has none, and the
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                           device metadata service can fetch a device's
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|                           friendly name, icon and Devices and Printers
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|                           tile from the network. Absent is the shipped
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                           state for all three.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|    1                    : Policy enforced - each value blocks its own
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                           channel. ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|                           strips drivers out of quality updates.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.044|                           DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 stops device
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.045|                           installation from querying Windows Update at
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.046|                           all, so a device with no in-box driver sits in
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.047|                           Device Manager with an error and no
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.048|                           explanation. PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.049|                           leaves devices showing a generic model string
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.050|                           with no icon.
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.051|    0                    : Policy present and explicitly disabled. The
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.052|                           behaviour matches absent, but the value still
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.053|                           sits under the Policies hive, where Group
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.054|                           Policy tooling reports it as configured and
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.055|                           where a future refresh can flip it back. That
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.056|                           is a third state, not the default - which is
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.057|                           why this step deletes rather than zeroes.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, and the Windows
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|                       column is the strongest of the five rather than the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|                       odd one out: absent is literally what ships, so
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                       deleting these restores the default instead of
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                       overriding it. None of the four use profiles wants
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|                       a device that cannot get a driver - the gaming
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|                       machine needs GPU and controller drivers, the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|                       server needs storage and NIC firmware, the office
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|                       PC needs printers, the laptop needs docks and Wi-
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|                       Fi. The only machine that should keep these is one
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                       where IT owns the policy, and that is not a profile
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                       on this scale.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|  Known problems  : OPTY contradicts itself on
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate. Line 1482, in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|                    :enable_windows_update, writes it to 0 with REG ADD -
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|                    creating the value on a machine that never had it,
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                    which is the third state described above rather than
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                    the default. Line 1845 deletes it, correctly. The rule
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|                    this project set is that DELETE means the true default
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|                    is absence, so line 1482 should be a :killkey call
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|                    too, not a REG ADD of 0.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|  Unverified      : DontSearchWindowsUpdate lives under
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|                    Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching, while the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|                    user-visible Device installation settings dialog
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|                    writes SearchOrderConfig under
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|                    CurrentVersion\DriverSearching. They are different
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|                    keys with overlapping effects, so a machine can show
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|                    driver downloads as allowed in the GUI while the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|                    policy value still blocks them. Which one wins in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|                    every combination is not something I verified on this
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|                    machine.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|  Target          : Three :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1845 to 1847 -
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                    /v ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching /v
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate, and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|                    Metadata /v PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork. :killkey
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                    (OPTY.bat line 3987) queries first and deletes only if
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|                    the value exists, logging the removal.
::
:: ---- rad.trim.on (repair) ---------------------------------------
::P|rad.trim.on|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.001|REACTIVER LE TRIM POUR LES SSD
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.002|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.003|  Ce que c est    : Le TRIM indique au SSD quels blocs ne servent plus,
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.004|                    pour qu'il les efface a l'avance. Voici la version du
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.005|                    menu de restauration de la meme reparation.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.006|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.007|  Effet reel      : Met le comportement de notification de suppression a 0
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.008|                    : les notifications repartent, sur les volumes NTFS
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.009|                    comme ReFS.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.010|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.011|  Gain            : Reel et cumulatif sur SSD, mais uniquement la ou le
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.012|                    TRIM avait effectivement ete coupe. Sans TRIM, les
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.013|                    ecritures ralentissent au fil des mois faute de blocs
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.014|                    pre-effaces ; le retablir laisse le disque
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.015|                    reconstituer cette reserve a mesure que les blocs sont
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.016|                    reecrits. Sur la machine bien plus courante ou le TRIM
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.017|                    etait deja actif, cette ecriture ne change rien et ne
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.018|                    revendique rien.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.019|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.020|  Cout            : Rien. Une consequence merite d'etre dite franchement
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.021|                    plutot que tue : les donnees supprimees deviennent
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.022|                    vraiment irrecuperables bien plus tot. Si vous etes en
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.023|                    pleine tentative de recuperation d'un fichier,
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.024|                    terminez-la d'abord.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.025|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.026|  Defaut Windows  : 0, TRIM actif.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.027|
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.028|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.029|    0                    : Notifications de suppression emises. Supprimer
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.030|                           un fichier declenche un TRIM ou un UNMAP sur
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.031|                           ses blocs : le controleur efface alors ces
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.032|                           pages de flash pendant ses temps morts et votre
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.033|                           prochaine ecriture entre dans une page prete.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.034|                           Valeur livree.
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.035|    1                    : Notifications de suppression supprimees. Le
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.036|                           disque n'apprend jamais que des blocs sont
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.037|                           libres, sa reserve de pages pre-effacees
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.038|                           s'epuise, et chaque ecriture coute de plus en
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.039|                           plus souvent un cycle lecture-modification-
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.040|                           effacement-ecriture. Les degats s'accumulent
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.041|                           sur des mois et ne se resorbent qu'en
::T|FR|rad.trim.on.042|                           reecrivant ou en effacant securement le disque.
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques. Le comportement
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.002|                         d'effacement de la flash se moque de savoir si la
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.003|                         machine joue, heberge ou dort sur un bureau, et 0
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.004|                         est la valeur livree : la colonne Windows rejoint
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.005|                         donc les quatre autres au lieu de s'en
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.006|                         distinguer. Cette fiche et celle du chemin de
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.007|                         configuration emettent la meme commande depuis
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.008|                         deux menus differents - enchainer les deux est
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.009|                         sans danger et signalera simplement que la valeur
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.010|                         etait deja correcte.
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.011|
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.012|  Cible           : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.013|                    line 1870, in the RESTORE path (System perf defaults:
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.014|                    TRIM on, SysMain/Prefetch on, system-managed
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.015|                    pagefile). Same write and same backing values as the
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.016|                    setup-path TRIM card:
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.017|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.018|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v
::X|FR|rad.trim.on.019|                    RefsDisableDeleteNotify.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.001|RE-ENABLE TRIM FOR SSDS
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.002|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.003|  What it is      : TRIM tells an SSD which blocks are no longer in use so
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.004|                    it can erase them ahead of time. This is the restore-
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.005|                    menu version of the same repair.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.006|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.007|  Actual effect   : Sets the delete-notify behaviour to 0, so delete
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.008|                    notifications are sent again on both NTFS and ReFS
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.009|                    volumes.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.010|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.011|  Gain            : Real and cumulative on an SSD, but only where TRIM had
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.012|                    actually been turned off. Without TRIM, write
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.013|                    performance degrades over months as the drive runs out
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.014|                    of pre-erased blocks; restoring it lets the drive
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.015|                    rebuild that pool as blocks get rewritten. On the
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.016|                    overwhelmingly more common machine, where TRIM was
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.017|                    already on, this write changes nothing and claims
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.018|                    nothing.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.019|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.020|  Cost            : Nothing. One consequence worth stating plainly rather
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.021|                    than hiding: deleted data becomes genuinely
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.022|                    unrecoverable much sooner, so if you are in the middle
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.023|                    of trying to recover a deleted file, finish that
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.024|                    first.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.025|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.026|  Windows default : 0 - TRIM on.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.027|
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.029|    0                    : Delete notifications sent. Deleting a file
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.030|                           issues TRIM or UNMAP for its blocks, so the
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.031|                           controller erases those flash pages during idle
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.032|                           time and your next write goes into a ready
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.033|                           page. Shipped value.
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.034|    1                    : Delete notifications suppressed. The drive
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.035|                           never learns that blocks are free, its stock of
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.036|                           pre-erased pages drains, and each write
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.037|                           increasingly costs a read-modify-erase-write
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.038|                           cycle. The damage accumulates over months and
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.039|                           is only cleared by rewriting or secure-erasing
::T|EN|rad.trim.on.040|                           the drive.
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeroes. Flash erase behaviour does
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.002|                       not care whether the machine games, serves or sits
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.003|                       on a desk, and 0 is the shipped value, so the
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.004|                       Windows column agrees with the other four rather
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.005|                       than dissenting. This card and the setup-path TRIM
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.006|                       card issue the identical command from two different
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.007|                       menus - running one after the other is harmless and
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.008|                       will simply report that the value was already
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.009|                       correct.
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.010|
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.011|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.012|                    line 1870, in the RESTORE path (System perf defaults:
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.013|                    TRIM on, SysMain/Prefetch on, system-managed
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.014|                    pagefile). Same write and same backing values as the
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.015|                    setup-path TRIM card:
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.016|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.017|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v
::X|EN|rad.trim.on.018|                    RefsDisableDeleteNotify.
::
:: ---- rad.vuln.driver.blocklist (repair) -------------------------
::P|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist|1|1|1|1|1|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.001|REACTIVER LA LISTE DE BLOCAGE DES PILOTES VULNERABLES
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.002|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.003|  Ce que c est    : Une liste tenue par Microsoft de pilotes noyau signes
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.004|                    mais exploitables, que Windows refuse de charger. Elle
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.005|                    est mise a jour par Windows Update en meme temps que
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.006|                    le reste de l'integrite du code.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.007|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable=1 sous
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.009|                    CI\Config. Prend effet au demarrage, puis s'applique a
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.010|                    chaque chargement de pilote.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.011|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.012|  Gain            : Bloque la technique du pilote vulnerable embarque, par
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.013|                    laquelle une grande part des rancongiciels desactive
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.014|                    l'antivirus avant toute autre chose. Le cout est ici
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.015|                    veritablement nul : le controle est une consultation
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.016|                    effectuee une fois par chargement de pilote, jamais
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.017|                    dans un chemin critique. Contrairement a la plupart
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.018|                    des fonctions de securite, il n'y a donc aucun
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.019|                    argument de performance dans l'autre plateau de la
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.020|                    balance.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.021|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.022|  Cout            : Un tres vieil utilitaire ou outil d'overclocking
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.023|                    livrant un pilote blackliste cesse de fonctionner.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.024|                    C'est tout le cout, et la bonne reponse est une
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.025|                    version a jour de l'outil, pas un noyau sans
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.026|                    protection.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.027|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.028|  Defaut Windows  : 1, liste active. Sur les versions actuelles de Windows
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.029|                    11, elle est appliquee par defaut.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.030|
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.032|    1                    : Liste de blocage appliquee. L'integrite du code
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.033|                           consulte la liste Microsoft des pilotes noyau
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.034|                           signes mais exploitables et refuse de charger
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.035|                           tout pilote qui s'y trouve, meme avec une
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.036|                           signature valide. Le controle a lieu au
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.037|                           chargement du pilote, coute une consultation de
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.038|                           liste, et n'a aucun cout a l'execution ensuite.
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.039|    0                    : Liste de blocage non appliquee. Un pilote
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.040|                           figurant sur la liste se charge normalement des
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.041|                           lors que sa signature est valide. C'est l'etat
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.042|                           que laissent certains scripts de debloat, et la
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.043|                           condition prealable de la technique du pilote
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.044|                           vulnerable embarque : un logiciel malveillant
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.045|                           livre un pilote legitimement signe mais fautif,
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.046|                           le charge, et exploite sa faille pour obtenir
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.047|                           les privileges noyau et desactiver l'antivirus
::T|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.048|                           par en dessous.
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq valeurs 1 identiques, et la colonne jeu
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.002|                         merite qu'on s'y arrete, car c'est la qu'on la
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.003|                         desactive : frictions avec les anti-triche et
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.004|                         vieux utilitaires d'overclocking ou de controle
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.005|                         des ventilateurs sont les raisons habituelles, et
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.006|                         aucune ne vaut un acces noyau offert a ce qui se
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.007|                         chargera ensuite. Le cout est nul dans tous les
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.008|                         profils, donc il n'y a aucun arbitrage de
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.009|                         performance a peser - et c'est exactement pour
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.010|                         cela qu'aucun profil ne doit repondre 0.
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.011|
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.012|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether the shipped state is the value present as 1
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.013|                      or the value absent with enforcement on anyway
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.014|                      varies across builds and with whether Memory
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.015|                      Integrity or Smart App Control is enabled. Writing 1
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.016|                      asserts the protected state either way, so the card
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.017|                      does not need to resolve that - but the log line
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.018|                      will say was absent on a machine where enforcement
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.019|                      was in fact already active, and that wording should
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.020|                      not be read as a repair that was needed.
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.021|
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.022|  Cible           : call :regset
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.023|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Config"
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.024|                    "VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable" REG_DWORD 1 -
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.025|                    OPTY.bat line 1924. :regset (OPTY.bat line 3349) reads
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.026|                    the current value before writing so the log can
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.027|                    distinguish a real repair from a value that was
::X|FR|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.028|                    already correct.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.001|RE-ENABLE THE VULNERABLE DRIVER BLOCKLIST
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.002|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.003|  What it is      : A Microsoft-maintained list of signed but exploitable
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.004|                    kernel drivers that Windows refuses to load. It is
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.005|                    updated through Windows Update along with the rest of
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.006|                    Code Integrity.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.007|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.008|  Actual effect   : Writes VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable=1 under
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.009|                    CI\Config. Takes effect at boot, and applies at each
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.010|                    driver load thereafter.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.011|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.012|  Gain            : Blocks the bring-your-own-vulnerable-driver technique,
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.013|                    which is how a great deal of ransomware disables
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.014|                    antivirus before doing anything else. The cost side of
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.015|                    this is genuinely zero: the check is a lookup
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.016|                    performed once per driver load, not something in any
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.017|                    hot path, so unlike most security features there is no
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.018|                    performance argument on the other side of the scale.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.019|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.020|  Cost            : A very old utility or overclocking tool that ships a
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.021|                    blocklisted driver stops working. That is the entire
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.022|                    cost, and the correct response is an updated version
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.023|                    of the tool rather than an unprotected kernel.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.024|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.025|  Windows default : 1 - the blocklist is on. On current Windows 11 builds
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.026|                    it is enforced by default.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.027|
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.029|    1                    : Blocklist enforced. Code Integrity consults
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.030|                           Microsoft's list of signed but exploitable
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.031|                           kernel drivers and refuses to load any driver
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.032|                           on it, regardless of a valid signature. The
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.033|                           check happens at driver load, costs one list
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.034|                           lookup, and has no ongoing runtime cost
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.035|                           afterwards.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.036|    0                    : Blocklist not enforced. A driver on the list
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.037|                           loads normally provided its signature is valid.
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.038|                           This is the state some debloat scripts leave
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.039|                           behind, and it is the precondition for the
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.040|                           bring-your-own-vulnerable-driver technique:
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.041|                           malware ships a legitimately signed but flawed
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.042|                           driver, loads it, and uses its flaw to get
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.043|                           kernel privileges and switch off the antivirus
::T|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.044|                           from underneath.
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical ones, and the gaming column is the
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.002|                       one worth dwelling on, because it is where this
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.003|                       gets turned off: anti-cheat friction and old
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.004|                       overclocking or fan-control utilities are the usual
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.005|                       reasons someone disables the blocklist, and neither
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.006|                       is worth kernel access for whatever loads next. The
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.007|                       cost is zero in every profile, so there is no
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.008|                       performance trade to weigh - which is exactly why
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.009|                       there is no profile that should say 0.
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.010|
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.011|  Unverified      : Whether the shipped state is the value present as 1 or
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.012|                    the value absent with enforcement on anyway varies
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.013|                    across builds and with whether Memory Integrity or
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.014|                    Smart App Control is enabled. Writing 1 asserts the
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.015|                    protected state either way, so the card does not need
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.016|                    to resolve that - but the log line will say was absent
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.017|                    on a machine where enforcement was in fact already
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.018|                    active, and that wording should not be read as a
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.019|                    repair that was needed.
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.020|
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.021|  Target          : call :regset
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.022|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Config"
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.023|                    "VulnerableDriverBlocklistEnable" REG_DWORD 1 -
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.024|                    OPTY.bat line 1924. :regset (OPTY.bat line 3349) reads
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.025|                    the current value before writing so the log can
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.026|                    distinguish a real repair from a value that was
::X|EN|rad.vuln.driver.blocklist.027|                    already correct.
::
:: ---- rad.driververifier.killkey (repair) ------------------------
::P|rad.driververifier.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|ARRETER UNE SESSION DRIVER VERIFIER OUBLIEE
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : VerifyDrivers et VerifyDriverLevel mettent le noyau
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.004|                    dans un mode lourd de test de pilotes, concu pour les
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.005|                    developpeurs qui deboguent un plantage. Rien dans un
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.006|                    usage normal ne l'active : il faut verifier.exe, ou un
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.007|                    script qui pose ces valeurs a la main.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.008|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.009|  Effet reel      : Supprime les deux valeurs si elles existent, de sorte
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.010|                    que Verifier s'arrete au prochain demarrage. Le noyau
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.011|                    ne les lit qu'au demarrage : rien ne change dans la
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.012|                    session en cours.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.013|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.014|  Gain            : Important la ou cela s'applique, et nul ailleurs.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.015|                    Toute la conception de Verifier echange de la
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.016|                    performance contre de la detection - le pool special
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.017|                    et la verification des E/S ajoutent du travail a
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.018|                    chaque allocation noyau et a chaque requete d'entree-
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.019|                    sortie - et la documentation de Microsoft previent
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.020|                    elle-meme qu'un systeme verifie tourne sensiblement
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.021|                    plus lentement. Ce qui justifie cette fiche n'est pas
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.022|                    la taille du chiffre mais le fait qu'aucun element de
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.023|                    l'interface de Windows n'indique que Verifier tourne :
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.024|                    une machine peut rester des mois dans cet etat pendant
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.025|                    que son proprietaire accuse le materiel.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.026|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.027|  Cout            : Rien, sauf si vous deboguez volontairement un plantage
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.028|                    de pilote avec Verifier - auquel cas vous le savez
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.029|                    deja, et vous le relancerez vous-meme avec
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.030|                    verifier.exe.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.031|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.032|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs absentes, Verifier inactif.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.033|
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.035|    DELETE               : Les deux valeurs sont supprimees. Le noyau ne
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.036|                           les lit qu'au demarrage : Driver Verifier
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.037|                           s'arrete donc au prochain redemarrage, pas
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.038|                           avant. L'absence est l'etat livre : une machine
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.039|                           n'ayant jamais lance verifier.exe n'a ni l'une
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.040|                           ni l'autre.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.041|    PRESENT              : VerifyDrivers indique quels pilotes verifier -
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.042|                           une liste, ou une asterisque signifiant tous
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.043|                           les pilotes, le cas pathologique.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.044|                           VerifyDriverLevel est un masque de bits
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.045|                           selectionnant les controles actifs. Les plus
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.046|                           couteux sont le pool special, qui place chaque
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.047|                           allocation sur sa propre page gardee, et la
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.048|                           verification des E/S, qui encapsule et valide
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.049|                           chaque IRP. La detection d'interblocages et le
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.050|                           suivi du pool ajoutent leur propre surcout. Le
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.051|                           ralentissement est donc reel, mais son ampleur
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.052|                           depend entierement des bits poses et du nombre
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.053|                           de pilotes concernes.
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes DELETE identiques, Windows compris,
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|                         parce que l'absence est l'etat livre et qu'aucun
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.003|                         usage n'a de raison de faire tourner un mode de
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.004|                         debogage noyau en production. La bonne facon de
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.005|                         lire une distinction ici n'est pas le profil mais
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.006|                         l'intention : si vous n'avez pas lance Verifier,
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.007|                         il ne devrait pas tourner ; et si vous l'avez
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.008|                         lance, vous n'avez pas besoin de cette fiche.
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.009|
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.010|  Non verifie (en)  : The previous version of this card claimed Verifier
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.011|                      can roughly halve I/O throughput. I could not
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.012|                      substantiate that figure, and it cannot be right as
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.013|                      a constant: the cost is a function of the
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.014|                      VerifyDriverLevel bitmask and of how many drivers
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.015|                      VerifyDrivers covers, so it ranges from modest to
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.016|                      severe. The card now states the mechanism and drops
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.017|                      the number. The claim that survives is the one that
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.018|                      matters and is checkable: nothing in the Windows
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.019|                      interface tells you Verifier is on.
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.020|
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.021|  Cible           : Two :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1927 and 1928
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.022|                    against %MMK%, which is set at OPTY.bat line 1899 to
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.023|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.024|                    Manager\Memory Management - value names VerifyDrivers
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.025|                    (REG_SZ) and VerifyDriverLevel (REG_DWORD).
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|TURN OFF A LEFTOVER DRIVER VERIFIER SESSION
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.003|  What it is      : VerifyDrivers and VerifyDriverLevel put the kernel
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.004|                    into a heavy driver-testing mode meant for developers
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.005|                    debugging a driver crash. Nothing in normal use
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.006|                    switches it on - it takes verifier.exe or a script
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.007|                    writing these values by hand.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.008|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.009|  Actual effect   : Deletes both values if present, so Verifier stops at
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.010|                    the next boot. The kernel only reads them during
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.011|                    startup, so nothing changes in the current session.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.012|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.013|  Gain            : Substantial where it applies, and invisible otherwise.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.014|                    Verifier's whole design trades performance for
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.015|                    detection - special pool and I/O verification in
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.016|                    particular add work to every kernel allocation and
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.017|                    every I/O request - and Microsoft's own documentation
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.018|                    warns that a verified system runs measurably slower.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.019|                    What makes this worth a card is not the size of the
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.020|                    number but the fact that there is no indication
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.021|                    anywhere in the Windows interface that Verifier is
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.022|                    running: a machine can sit in this mode for months
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.023|                    while its owner blames the hardware.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.024|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.025|  Cost            : Nothing, unless you are actively debugging a driver
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.026|                    crash with Verifier on purpose - in which case you
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.027|                    already know, and you will restart it yourself with
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.028|                    verifier.exe.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.029|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.030|  Windows default : Both values absent, Verifier off.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.031|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.033|    DELETE               : Both values removed. The kernel reads them at
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.034|                           boot only, so Driver Verifier stops at the next
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.035|                           restart and not before. Absent is the shipped
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.036|                           state: a machine that has never run
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.037|                           verifier.exe has neither value.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.038|    PRESENT              : VerifyDrivers names which drivers to verify - a
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.039|                           list, or an asterisk meaning every driver,
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.040|                           which is the pathological case.
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.041|                           VerifyDriverLevel is a bitmask selecting which
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.042|                           checks run. The expensive ones are special
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.043|                           pool, which puts every allocation on its own
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.044|                           guarded page, and I/O verification, which wraps
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.045|                           and validates every IRP. Deadlock detection and
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.046|                           pool tracking add their own overhead. So the
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.047|                           slowdown is real but its size depends entirely
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.048|                           on which bits are set and on how many drivers
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.049|                           are in scope.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, Windows included,
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|                       because absent is the shipped state and no use case
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.003|                       has a reason to run a kernel debugging mode in
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.004|                       production. The right way to read a distinction
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.005|                       here is not by profile but by intent: if you did
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.006|                       not start Verifier, it should not be running, and
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.007|                       if you did, you do not need this card.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.008|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.009|  Unverified      : The previous version of this card claimed Verifier can
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.010|                    roughly halve I/O throughput. I could not substantiate
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.011|                    that figure, and it cannot be right as a constant: the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.012|                    cost is a function of the VerifyDriverLevel bitmask
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.013|                    and of how many drivers VerifyDrivers covers, so it
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.014|                    ranges from modest to severe. The card now states the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.015|                    mechanism and drops the number. The claim that
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.016|                    survives is the one that matters and is checkable:
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.017|                    nothing in the Windows interface tells you Verifier is
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.018|                    on.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.019|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.020|  Target          : Two :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1927 and 1928
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.021|                    against %MMK%, which is set at OPTY.bat line 1899 to
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.022|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.023|                    Manager\Memory Management - value names VerifyDrivers
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.024|                    (REG_SZ) and VerifyDriverLevel (REG_DWORD).
::
:: ---- svc.stop.update.stack (preference) -----------------------------
::P|svc.stop.update.stack|STOP|STOP|STOP|STOP|SKIP|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.001|ARRÊT DES SERVICES DE WINDOWS UPDATE
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.002|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.003|  Ce que c est    : Avant de toucher aux caches de Windows Update, OPTY
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.004|                    arrête cinq services : bits (transferts en arrière-
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.005|                    plan), wuauserv (Windows Update), msiserver
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.006|                    (installeur MSI), cryptsvc (services de chiffrement)
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.007|                    et appidsvc (identité d'application).
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.008|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.009|  Effet reel      : net stop agit uniquement sur l'état en cours
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.010|                    d'exécution. Les types de démarrage ne sont jamais
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.011|                    modifiés, donc rien n'est désactivé durablement. Les
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.012|                    fichiers sous SoftwareDistribution deviennent
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.013|                    supprimables parce que plus rien ne les tient ouverts.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.014|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.015|  Gain            : Le nettoyage du cache de mise à jour qui suit réussit
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.016|                    vraiment, au lieu de se heurter à des violations de
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.017|                    partage. En soi, cette étape ne libère rien.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.018|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.019|  Cout            : Entre cette étape et la fin de l'exécution, Windows
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.020|                    Update, les installations MSI et certaines
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.021|                    vérifications de signature ne fonctionnent pas. Deux
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.022|                    des cinq services sont des composants de sécurité.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.023|                    CryptSvc arrêté, la validation des chaînes de
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.024|                    certificats et la mise à jour des signatures de
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.025|                    Defender sont dégradées ; AppIDSvc arrêté, AppLocker
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.026|                    et Smart App Control cessent d'appliquer leurs règles,
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.027|                    donc du code normalement filtré ne l'est plus. En mode
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.028|                    Auto complet, cette fenêtre dure toute l'exécution -
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.029|                    éventuellement le temps d'une réparation DISM - alors
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.030|                    n'installez et ne lancez rien d'inconnu pendant ce
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.031|                    temps. OPTY relance les cinq à :endready, et un
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.032|                    redémarrage referme aussi la fenêtre.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.033|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.034|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet : c'est un état d'exécution, pas une
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.035|                    configuration. Ces services tournent ou démarrent à la
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.036|                    demande selon les besoins, et cette étape ne change
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.037|                    pas leur type de démarrage.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.038|
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.039|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.040|    STOP                 : Exécute net stop sur les cinq services. bits
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.041|                           arrête les transferts en arrière-plan, wuauserv
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.042|                           arrête l'agent Windows Update, msiserver arrête
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.043|                           le moteur d'installation MSI, cryptsvc arrête
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.044|                           la validation des certificats et des
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.045|                           catalogues, appidsvc arrête le service Identité
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.046|                           d'application dont dépendent AppLocker et Smart
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.047|                           App Control. Rien n'est écrit dans le registre,
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.048|                           donc tous les types de démarrage restent
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.049|                           intacts. L'objectif : les fichiers de
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.050|                           C:\Windows\SoftwareDistribution cessent d'être
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.051|                           ouverts, ce qui permet au nettoyage du cache de
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.052|                           les supprimer au lieu de se heurter à une
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.053|                           violation de partage.
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.054|    SKIP                 : Laisse les cinq services tourner. Le nettoyage
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.055|                           du cache de mise à jour qui suit échouera alors
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.056|                           sur les fichiers encore verrouillés, ne
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.057|                           supprimera qu'une partie de ce qu'il visait, et
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.058|                           signalera quand même une réussite parce que del
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.059|                           n'échoue pas bruyamment. Rien n'est abîmé : le
::T|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.060|                           nettoyage est simplement peu efficace.
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre profils sont d'accord parce que ce n'est
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.002|                         pas un réglage d'usage, c'est le prérequis de
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.003|                         l'étape suivante : si vous lancez le nettoyage du
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.004|                         cache de mise à jour, vous arrêtez les services,
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.005|                         quelle que soit la machine. WINDOWS est la seule
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.006|                         colonne différente, et uniquement parce que
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.007|                         l'état d'origine d'une machine est que ces
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.008|                         services sont libres de tourner : le profil par
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.009|                         défaut n'arrête rien.
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.010|
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.011|  Problemes connus : Les cinq arrêts ont lieu à :startready mais les
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.012|                     redémarrages correspondants seulement à :endready.
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.013|                     Tout ce qui se passe entre les deux tourne sans
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.014|                     CryptSvc ni AppIDSvc, et sur un parcours DISM ou
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.015|                     CHKDSK, cela fait longtemps.
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.016|
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.017|  Non verifie (en)  : Leaving cryptsvc and appidsvc stopped for the rest
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.018|                      of the run is a side effect of the ordering, not a
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.019|                      decision anyone made. Whether it produces a visible
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.020|                      failure depends entirely on what else runs inside
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.021|                      that window.
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.022|
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.023|  Cible           : OPTY.bat :startready, lines 535-544 - net stop bits /
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.024|                    net stop wuauserv / net stop msiserver / net stop
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.025|                    cryptsvc / net stop appidsvc. Running state only; no
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.026|                    Start value under
::X|FR|svc.stop.update.stack.027|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services is touched.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.001|STOP THE WINDOWS UPDATE SERVICE STACK
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.002|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.003|  What it is      : Before touching the Windows Update caches, OPTY stops
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.004|                    five services: bits (background transfers), wuauserv
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.005|                    (Windows Update), msiserver (MSI installer), cryptsvc
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.006|                    (cryptographic services) and appidsvc (application
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.007|                    identity).
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.008|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.009|  Actual effect   : net stop affects the running state only. Startup types
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.010|                    are never changed, so nothing is permanently disabled.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.011|                    Files under SoftwareDistribution become deletable
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.012|                    because nothing holds them open any more.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.013|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.014|  Gain            : The update-cache cleanup that follows actually
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.015|                    succeeds instead of hitting sharing violations. On its
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.016|                    own, this step frees nothing.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.017|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.018|  Cost            : Between this step and the end of the run, Windows
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.019|                    Update, MSI installs and some signature verification
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.020|                    will not work. Two of the five are security
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.021|                    components. With CryptSvc stopped, certificate chain
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.022|                    validation and Defender signature updates are
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.023|                    impaired; with AppIDSvc stopped, AppLocker and Smart
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.024|                    App Control stop enforcing their rules, so code they
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.025|                    would normally gate is no longer gated. In Auto full
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.026|                    that window lasts for the whole run - potentially the
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.027|                    length of a DISM repair - so do not install or launch
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.028|                    untrusted software during it. OPTY restarts all five
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.029|                    at :endready, and a reboot closes the window too.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.030|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.031|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is runtime state, not
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.032|                    configuration. These services normally run or demand-
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.033|                    start as needed, and their start types are unchanged
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.034|                    by this step.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.035|
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.036|  Possible values:
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.037|    STOP                 : Issues net stop against the five services. bits
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.038|                           stops background transfers, wuauserv stops the
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.039|                           Windows Update agent, msiserver stops the MSI
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.040|                           installer engine, cryptsvc stops certificate
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.041|                           and catalog validation, appidsvc stops the
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.042|                           Application Identity service that AppLocker and
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.043|                           Smart App Control depend on. Nothing is written
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.044|                           to the registry, so every start type survives
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.045|                           untouched. The point is that the files under
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.046|                           C:\Windows\SoftwareDistribution stop being held
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.047|                           open, which is what lets the cache cleanup
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.048|                           delete them instead of hitting a sharing
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.049|                           violation.
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.050|    SKIP                 : Leaves the five services running. The update-
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.051|                           cache cleanup that follows will then fail on
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.052|                           the files that are still locked, delete a
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.053|                           fraction of what it aimed at, and report
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.054|                           success anyway because del does not fail
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.055|                           loudly. Nothing is damaged; the cleanup is just
::T|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.056|                           mostly ineffective.
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles agree because this is not a use-case
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.002|                       setting, it is the prerequisite of the step that
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.003|                       comes next: if you are running the update-cache
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.004|                       cleanup, you stop the services, whatever the
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.005|                       machine is for. WINDOWS is the only column that
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.006|                       differs, and only because the shipped state of a
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.007|                       machine is that these services are free to run -
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.008|                       the default profile does not stop anything.
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.009|
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.010|  Known problems  : The five stops happen at :startready but the matching
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.011|                    restarts only at :endready. Everything between them
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.012|                    runs with CryptSvc and AppIDSvc down, and on a long
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.013|                    DISM or CHKDSK path that is a long time.
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.014|
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.015|  Unverified      : Leaving cryptsvc and appidsvc stopped for the rest of
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.016|                    the run is a side effect of the ordering, not a
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.017|                    decision anyone made. Whether it produces a visible
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.018|                    failure depends entirely on what else runs inside that
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.019|                    window.
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.020|
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.021|  Target          : OPTY.bat :startready, lines 535-544 - net stop bits /
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.022|                    net stop wuauserv / net stop msiserver / net stop
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.023|                    cryptsvc / net stop appidsvc. Running state only; no
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.024|                    Start value under
::X|EN|svc.stop.update.stack.025|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services is touched.
::
:: ---- endready.restart.services (repair) -------------------------
::P|endready.restart.services|START|START|START|START|START|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.001|REDÉMARRER LES SERVICES ARRÊTÉS PAR LE NETTOYAGE
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.002|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.003|  Ce que c est    : Le mode Auto complet arrête bits, wuauserv, msiserver,
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.004|                    cryptsvc et appidsvc pour pouvoir nettoyer leurs
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.005|                    fichiers. Cette étape les relance en fin d'exécution
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.006|                    afin de ne pas laisser Windows Update inerte et deux
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.007|                    services de sécurité à l'arrêt.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.008|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.009|  Effet reel      : Uniquement de l'exécution : un net start est émis.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.010|                    Aucun type de démarrage n'est modifié, et un
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.011|                    redémarrage aurait fait la même chose.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.012|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.013|  Gain            : Windows Update, le moteur d'installation et la
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.014|                    validation des certificats refonctionnent
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.015|                    immédiatement au lieu d'attendre le prochain
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.016|                    redémarrage, et AppLocker / Smart App Control
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.017|                    réappliquent leurs règles. C'est cette étape qui
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.018|                    referme la fenêtre ouverte par l'arrêt de la pile.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.019|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.020|  Cout            : Rien. Seule nuance : quatre de ces services sont
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.021|                    livrés en Manuel ou Manuel (déclenché), donc les
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.022|                    forcer à tourner s'écarte légèrement de l'état
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.023|                    d'origine au repos - sans danger, et remis à zéro au
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.024|                    prochain redémarrage.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.025|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.026|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet : aucun type de démarrage n'est modifié.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.027|                    D'origine, wuauserv, bits, msiserver et appidsvc sont
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.028|                    en Manuel ou Manuel (déclenché), et cryptsvc en
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.029|                    Automatique.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.030|
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.032|    START                : Exécute net start sur les cinq mêmes services
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.033|                           que :startready avait arrêtés. L'état
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.034|                           d'exécution revient immédiatement ; aucune
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.035|                           valeur Start n'est écrite dans le registre,
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.036|                           donc un service livré en Manuel se retrouve
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.037|                           simplement en train de tourner au lieu d'être
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.038|                           au repos. wuauserv peut de nouveau chercher des
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.039|                           mises à jour, msiserver servir un installeur,
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.040|                           cryptsvc reprend la validation des catalogues
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.041|                           et des certificats, et appidsvc réapplique les
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.042|                           règles d'AppLocker et de Smart App Control.
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.043|    SKIP                 : Les laisse arrêtés jusqu'à ce que quelque chose
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.044|                           les redémarre à la demande ou que vous
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.045|                           redémarriez. Windows Update reste inerte - une
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.046|                           machine nettoyée le matin peut ne pas chercher
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.047|                           de mise à jour de la journée - et la fenêtre de
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.048|                           sécurité ouverte à :startready (pas de
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.049|                           validation de certificats, pas d'application
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.050|                           d'AppLocker) reste ouverte jusqu'au prochain
::T|FR|endready.restart.services.051|                           redémarrage.
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils sont identiques et il n'y a
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.002|                         aucune façon honnête de les faire diverger.
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.003|                         Refuser laisse Windows Update à l'arrêt et deux
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.004|                         services de sécurité coupés jusqu'au redémarrage
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.005|                         : c'est strictement pire que l'état d'avant OPTY,
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.006|                         pour tous les usages sans exception.
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.007|
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.008|  Cible           : OPTY.bat :endready, lines 1280-1289 - net start bits /
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.009|                    net start wuauserv / net start msiserver / net start
::X|FR|endready.restart.services.010|                    cryptsvc / net start appidsvc.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.001|RESTART THE SERVICES THE CLEANUP STOPPED
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.002|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.003|  What it is      : Auto Full stops bits, wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.004|                    and appidsvc so their files can be cleaned. This step
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.005|                    starts them again at the end of the run so the machine
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.006|                    is not left with Windows Update inert and two security
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.007|                    services down.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.008|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.009|  Actual effect   : Runtime only - it issues net start. No service start
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.010|                    type is changed, and a reboot would have done the same
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.011|                    thing anyway.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.012|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.013|  Gain            : Windows Update, the installer engine and certificate
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.014|                    validation work again immediately instead of only
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.015|                    after your next restart, and AppLocker / Smart App
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.016|                    Control start enforcing again. This is what closes the
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.017|                    window that stopping the stack opened.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.018|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.019|  Cost            : Nothing. The only nuance is that four of these ship as
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.020|                    Manual or trigger-start services, so forcing them
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.021|                    running is slightly different from the shipped idle
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.022|                    state - harmless, and undone by the next reboot.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.023|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.024|  Windows default : Not applicable - no start type is changed. As shipped,
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.025|                    wuauserv, bits, msiserver and appidsvc are Manual or
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.026|                    Manual (Trigger Start), and cryptsvc is Automatic.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.027|
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.028|  Possible values:
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.029|    START                : Issues net start against the same five services
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.030|                           :startready stopped. The running state comes
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.031|                           back immediately; no Start value in the
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.032|                           registry is written, so a service that ships
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.033|                           Manual is simply running now rather than idle.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.034|                           wuauserv can poll for updates again, msiserver
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.035|                           can serve an installer, cryptsvc resumes
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.036|                           catalog and certificate validation, and
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.037|                           appidsvc resumes enforcing AppLocker and Smart
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.038|                           App Control.
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.039|    SKIP                 : Leaves them stopped until something demand-
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.040|                           starts them or you reboot. Windows Update stays
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.041|                           inert - a machine cleaned in the morning may
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.042|                           not check for updates all day - and the
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.043|                           security window opened at :startready (no
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.044|                           certificate validation, no AppLocker
::T|EN|endready.restart.services.045|                           enforcement) stays open until the next restart.
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.001|  Why these profiles : All five profiles are identical and there is no
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.002|                       honest way to make them differ. Saying no leaves
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.003|                       Windows Update stopped and two security services
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.004|                       down until you reboot - strictly worse than the
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.005|                       state the machine was in before OPTY ran, for every
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.006|                       use case there is.
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.007|
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.008|  Target          : OPTY.bat :endready, lines 1280-1289 - net start bits /
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.009|                    net start wuauserv / net start msiserver / net start
::X|EN|endready.restart.services.010|                    cryptsvc / net start appidsvc.
