:: ---- stop.docker.and.wsl (risky) -------------------------------
::P|stop.docker.and.wsl|APPLY|SKIP|APPLY|APPLY|SKIP|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.001|ARRET FORCE DE DOCKER DESKTOP ET DE WSL
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.002|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.003|  Ce que c est    : Un disque virtuel ne peut pas etre compacte tant qu'il
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.004|                    est monte : il faut d'abord le liberer. OPTY demande a
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.005|                    Docker Desktop de quitter, attend jusqu'a 120 secondes
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.006|                    en surveillant la distribution docker-desktop et le
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.007|                    processus backend, force l'arret de l'arborescence
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.008|                    Docker si elle est toujours la, puis lance wsl
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.009|                    --shutdown pour arreter toutes les distributions.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.010|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.011|  Effet reel      : Tout ce qui tourne dans WSL ou dans un conteneur est
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.012|                    termine. L'arret courtois dispose de 120 secondes ;
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.013|                    passe ce delai, c'est taskkill /f /t, qui n'a rien de
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.014|                    propre. Relancer Docker Desktop ensuite est a votre
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.015|                    charge.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.016|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.017|  Gain            : Aucun en soi. Rien n'est libere a ce stade : cette
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.018|                    etape ne fait que deverrouiller les .vhdx pour que le
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.019|                    compactage diskpart qui suit puisse s'executer.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.020|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.021|  Cout            : Les conteneurs en cours sont tues. Tout ce qui n'est
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.022|                    pas enregistre dans un shell WSL - un tampon
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.023|                    d'editeur, une compilation en cours, une modification
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.024|                    non validee faite depuis la distribution - est perdu.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.025|                    Une base de donnees en conteneur n'a pas d'extinction
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.026|                    propre si les 120 secondes expirent. Si vous
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.027|                    n'utilisez ni Docker ni WSL, l'etape ne trouve rien et
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.028|                    ne fait rien.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.029|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.030|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet. C'est de l'etat de processus et de machine
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.031|                    virtuelle, pas de la configuration.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.032|
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.034|    APPLY                : Docker Desktop est invite a quitter, dispose de
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.035|                           120 secondes, puis est tue avec toute son
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.036|                           arborescence de processus, et toutes les
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.037|                           distributions WSL sont arretees. Les conteneurs
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.038|                           et les shells WSL meurent avec. Les fichiers
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.039|                           .vhdx sont alors liberes pour le compactage.
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.040|    SKIP                 : Docker et WSL continuent de tourner. Les
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.041|                           disques virtuels restent verrouilles, l'etape
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.042|                           de compactage qui suit est donc sautee elle
::T|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.043|                           aussi et rien n'est recupere dessus.
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Seul le profil SERVEUR differe vraiment, pour une
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.002|                         raison concrete : cette machine existe pour faire
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.003|                         tourner des choses en continu, et un client
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.004|                         torrent, un Plex ou un serveur de jeu en
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.005|                         conteneur est precisement ce que cette etape tue.
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.006|                         JEU, BUREAU et PORTABLE sont a APPLY parce que
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.007|                         Docker y est le plus souvent inactif ou absent,
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.008|                         et un Docker inactif est justement le cas ou
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.009|                         compacter a du sens. Le profil WINDOWS passe son
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.010|                         tour parce que Windows ne tue jamais vos machines
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.011|                         virtuelles a votre place. Quel que soit le
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.012|                         profil, si vous avez du travail ouvert dans un
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.013|                         conteneur maintenant, repondez non pour cette
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.014|                         execution.
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.015|
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.016|  Cible           : :clean_wsl, OPTY.bat 806-848 - "%DOCKER_EXE%" -quit,
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.017|                    then a 120 s poll on wsl --list --running for docker-
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.018|                    desktop and on tasklist for com.docker.backend.exe,
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.019|                    then taskkill /f /t on Docker Desktop.exe,
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.020|                    com.docker.backend.exe, com.docker.build.exe,
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.021|                    com.docker.cli.exe and vpnkit.exe, then wsl --shutdown
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.022|                    and a 5 s wait. Prerequisite for the diskpart compact
::X|FR|stop.docker.and.wsl.023|                    pass on the .vhdx files.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.001|FORCE DOCKER AND WSL TO SHUT DOWN
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.002|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.003|  What it is      : A virtual disk cannot be compacted while it is
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.004|                    mounted, so it has to be released first. OPTY asks
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.005|                    Docker Desktop to quit, waits up to 120 seconds while
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.006|                    polling for the docker-desktop distro and the backend
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.007|                    process, force-kills the Docker process tree if it is
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.008|                    still there, then runs wsl --shutdown to stop every
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.009|                    distribution.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.010|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.011|  Actual effect   : Everything running inside WSL or inside a container is
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.012|                    terminated. The polite quit gets 120 seconds; after
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.013|                    that it is taskkill /f /t, which is not a graceful
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.014|                    stop. Restarting Docker Desktop afterwards is up to
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.015|                    you.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.016|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.017|  Gain            : None by itself. Nothing is freed at this stage - this
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.018|                    step only unlocks the .vhdx files so the diskpart
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.019|                    compact pass that follows can run.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.020|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.021|  Cost            : Running containers are killed. Anything unsaved inside
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.022|                    a WSL shell - an editor buffer, a build in progress,
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.023|                    an uncommitted change made from the distro - is lost.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.024|                    A database in a container gets no clean shutdown if
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.025|                    the 120-second window expires. If you use neither
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.026|                    Docker nor WSL, the step finds nothing and does
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.027|                    nothing.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.028|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.029|  Windows default : Not applicable. This is process and virtual-machine
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.030|                    state, not configuration.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.031|
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.033|    APPLY                : Docker Desktop is asked to quit, given 120
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.034|                           seconds, then killed with its whole process
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.035|                           tree, and every WSL distribution is shut down.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.036|                           Containers and WSL shells die with it. The
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.037|                           .vhdx files are then free to be compacted.
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.038|    SKIP                 : Docker and WSL keep running. The virtual disks
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.039|                           stay locked, so the compaction step that
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.040|                           follows is skipped too and no space is
::T|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.041|                           reclaimed from them.
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.001|  Why these profiles : Only the SERVER profile really differs, and for a
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.002|                       concrete reason: that machine exists to keep things
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.003|                       running, and a torrent client, a Plex instance or a
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.004|                       game server inside a container is exactly what this
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.005|                       step kills. GAMING, OFFICE and LAPTOP get APPLY
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.006|                       because on those machines Docker is usually idle or
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.007|                       absent, and an idle Docker is the whole point of
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.008|                       compacting. The WINDOWS profile skips because
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.009|                       Windows never force-quits your VMs for you.
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.010|                       Whatever the profile, if you have work open in a
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.011|                       container right now, answer no for this run.
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.012|
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.013|  Target          : :clean_wsl, OPTY.bat 806-848 - "%DOCKER_EXE%" -quit,
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.014|                    then a 120 s poll on wsl --list --running for docker-
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.015|                    desktop and on tasklist for com.docker.backend.exe,
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.016|                    then taskkill /f /t on Docker Desktop.exe,
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.017|                    com.docker.backend.exe, com.docker.build.exe,
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.018|                    com.docker.cli.exe and vpnkit.exe, then wsl --shutdown
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.019|                    and a 5 s wait. Prerequisite for the diskpart compact
::X|EN|stop.docker.and.wsl.020|                    pass on the .vhdx files.
::
:: ---- crash.dumps (risky) ---------------------------------------
::P|crash.dumps|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|KEEP|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.001|MINIDUMPS D'ECRAN BLEU ET MEMORY.DMP
::T|FR|crash.dumps.002|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.003|  Ce que c est    : A chaque ecran bleu, Windows ecrit un petit dump dans
::T|FR|crash.dumps.004|                    C:\Windows\Minidump et, selon votre reglage de dump,
::T|FR|crash.dumps.005|                    un C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP. Ce sont les seules traces de
::T|FR|crash.dumps.006|                    ce qui a reellement plante.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.007|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.008|  Effet reel      : Supprime les deux. La facon dont Windows ecrira les
::T|FR|crash.dumps.009|                    prochains dumps n'est pas modifiee, et MEMORY.DMP
::T|FR|crash.dumps.010|                    exige toujours un fichier d'echange sur le disque
::T|FR|crash.dumps.011|                    systeme pour pouvoir etre ecrit.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.012|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.013|  Gain            : De la place, uniquement. Les minidumps font quelques
::T|FR|crash.dumps.014|                    centaines de Ko chacun. MEMORY.DMP est ecrit en mode
::T|FR|crash.dumps.015|                    dump memoire automatique sur un Windows 11 d'origine,
::T|FR|crash.dumps.016|                    c'est-a-dire la memoire noyau seulement : en general
::T|FR|crash.dumps.017|                    de quelques centaines de Mo a 2 Go environ, PAS la
::T|FR|crash.dumps.018|                    taille de votre RAM. Il n'approche la taille de la RAM
::T|FR|crash.dumps.019|                    installee que si vous avez choisi vous-meme le dump
::T|FR|crash.dumps.020|                    memoire complet dans les parametres systeme avances.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.021|                    Regardez la taille du fichier avant de decider.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.022|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.023|  Cout            : Vous detruisez definitivement les preuves de tous vos
::T|FR|crash.dumps.024|                    ecrans bleus et plantages de pilote graphique. Si
::T|FR|crash.dumps.025|                    vous, ou quelqu'un qui vous aide, cherchez plus tard
::T|FR|crash.dumps.026|                    quel pilote tue la machine, la reponse a disparu. Et
::T|FR|crash.dumps.027|                    MEMORY.DMP n'existe que si vous avez deja plante,
::T|FR|crash.dumps.028|                    c'est-a-dire exactement quand il merite d'etre garde.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.029|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.030|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet. Windows les ecrit a chaque bugcheck et les
::T|FR|crash.dumps.031|                    conserve jusqu'a ce que quelque chose les supprime.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.032|
::T|FR|crash.dumps.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|crash.dumps.034|    DELETE               : C:\Windows\Minidump\* et C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP
::T|FR|crash.dumps.035|                           sont supprimes. La facon dont Windows ecrira le
::T|FR|crash.dumps.036|                           PROCHAIN dump n'est pas modifiee : le reglage
::T|FR|crash.dumps.037|                           des dumps dans les parametres systeme avances
::T|FR|crash.dumps.038|                           n'est pas touche, le prochain bugcheck ecrira
::T|FR|crash.dumps.039|                           donc un nouveau fichier.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.040|    KEEP                 : Les fichiers restent. Windows ne fait jamais le
::T|FR|crash.dumps.041|                           menage lui-meme : une machine qui a plante
::T|FR|crash.dumps.042|                           plusieurs fois conserve tous ses minidumps
::T|FR|crash.dumps.043|                           indefiniment, plus un MEMORY.DMP ecrase a
::T|FR|crash.dumps.044|                           chaque bugcheck.
::T|FR|crash.dumps.045|    ASK                  : Regardez d'abord la taille reelle de
::T|FR|crash.dumps.046|                           C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP, puis decidez. Ce seul
::T|FR|crash.dumps.047|                           chiffre resume tout le debat : un fichier de
::T|FR|crash.dumps.048|                           400 Mo ne vaut pas la perte d'un diagnostic.
::X|FR|crash.dumps.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les quatre premieres colonnes sont identiques a
::X|FR|crash.dumps.002|                         dessein : garder un dump depend de la presence
::X|FR|crash.dumps.003|                         d'un plantage inexplique a analyser, pas de
::X|FR|crash.dumps.004|                         l'usage de la machine, et inventer une
::X|FR|crash.dumps.005|                         distinction ici serait malhonnete. Elles disent
::X|FR|crash.dumps.006|                         DELETE parce que le proprietaire de ce script a
::X|FR|crash.dumps.007|                         tranche : les minidumps degagent. La colonne
::X|FR|crash.dumps.008|                         WINDOWS dit KEEP parce que Windows ne les
::X|FR|crash.dumps.009|                         supprime jamais de lui-meme. Passez outre le
::X|FR|crash.dumps.010|                         profil pour une execution si vous avez eu un
::X|FR|crash.dumps.011|                         ecran bleu recemment.
::X|FR|crash.dumps.012|
::X|FR|crash.dumps.013|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 992-994 - del /F /S /Q
::X|FR|crash.dumps.014|                    "%SystemRoot%\Minidump\*" and del /F /S /Q
::X|FR|crash.dumps.015|                    "%SystemRoot%\MEMORY.DMP". The cleanmgr pass at
::X|FR|crash.dumps.016|                    OPTY.bat 759-760 also sets StateFlags0064=2 on the
::X|FR|crash.dumps.017|                    "System error memory dump files" and "System error
::X|FR|crash.dumps.018|                    minidump files" handlers.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.001|BLUE-SCREEN MINIDUMPS AND MEMORY.DMP
::T|EN|crash.dumps.002|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.003|  What it is      : Every time Windows blue-screens it writes a small dump
::T|EN|crash.dumps.004|                    into C:\Windows\Minidump and, depending on your crash-
::T|EN|crash.dumps.005|                    dump setting, a C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP. These are the
::T|EN|crash.dumps.006|                    only record of what actually crashed.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.007|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes both. Nothing about how Windows writes future
::T|EN|crash.dumps.009|                    dumps is changed, and MEMORY.DMP still needs a
::T|EN|crash.dumps.010|                    pagefile on the system drive to be written at all.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.011|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.012|  Gain            : Space only. Minidumps are a few hundred KB each.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.013|                    MEMORY.DMP is written as an Automatic memory dump on a
::T|EN|crash.dumps.014|                    stock Windows 11, which means kernel memory only -
::T|EN|crash.dumps.015|                    usually a few hundred MB to about 2 GB, NOT the size
::T|EN|crash.dumps.016|                    of your RAM. It only approaches installed RAM if you
::T|EN|crash.dumps.017|                    deliberately picked 'Complete memory dump' in Advanced
::T|EN|crash.dumps.018|                    system settings. Check the file size before deciding
::T|EN|crash.dumps.019|                    this is worth it.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.020|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.021|  Cost            : You permanently destroy the evidence of every past
::T|EN|crash.dumps.022|                    blue screen and GPU driver crash. If you or anyone
::T|EN|crash.dumps.023|                    helping you later wants to know which driver is
::T|EN|crash.dumps.024|                    killing the machine, that answer is gone. And
::T|EN|crash.dumps.025|                    MEMORY.DMP only exists if you have already crashed,
::T|EN|crash.dumps.026|                    which is exactly when it is worth keeping.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.027|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.028|  Windows default : Not applicable. Windows writes these on every bugcheck
::T|EN|crash.dumps.029|                    and keeps them until something deletes them.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.030|
::T|EN|crash.dumps.031|  Possible values:
::T|EN|crash.dumps.032|    DELETE               : Both C:\Windows\Minidump\* and
::T|EN|crash.dumps.033|                           C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP are removed. Nothing
::T|EN|crash.dumps.034|                           about how Windows writes the NEXT dump changes
::T|EN|crash.dumps.035|                           - the crash-dump setting in Advanced system
::T|EN|crash.dumps.036|                           settings is untouched, so the next bugcheck
::T|EN|crash.dumps.037|                           writes a new file.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.038|    KEEP                 : The files stay. Windows never prunes them
::T|EN|crash.dumps.039|                           itself, so a machine that has crashed a few
::T|EN|crash.dumps.040|                           times keeps every minidump forever and one
::T|EN|crash.dumps.041|                           MEMORY.DMP, overwritten at each bugcheck.
::T|EN|crash.dumps.042|    ASK                  : Look at the actual size of
::T|EN|crash.dumps.043|                           C:\Windows\MEMORY.DMP first, then decide. That
::T|EN|crash.dumps.044|                           single number is the whole argument - a 400 MB
::T|EN|crash.dumps.045|                           file is not worth losing a diagnosis over.
::X|EN|crash.dumps.001|  Why these profiles : The first four columns are identical on purpose:
::X|EN|crash.dumps.002|                       whether a dump is worth keeping depends on whether
::X|EN|crash.dumps.003|                       you have an unexplained crash to investigate, not
::X|EN|crash.dumps.004|                       on what the machine is for, and inventing a split
::X|EN|crash.dumps.005|                       here would be dishonest. They read DELETE because
::X|EN|crash.dumps.006|                       the owner of this script decided minidumps go. The
::X|EN|crash.dumps.007|                       WINDOWS column is KEEP because Windows itself never
::X|EN|crash.dumps.008|                       removes them. Override the profile for one run if
::X|EN|crash.dumps.009|                       you have blue-screened recently.
::X|EN|crash.dumps.010|
::X|EN|crash.dumps.011|  Target          : OPTY.bat 992-994 - del /F /S /Q
::X|EN|crash.dumps.012|                    "%SystemRoot%\Minidump\*" and del /F /S /Q
::X|EN|crash.dumps.013|                    "%SystemRoot%\MEMORY.DMP". The cleanmgr pass at
::X|EN|crash.dumps.014|                    OPTY.bat 759-760 also sets StateFlags0064=2 on the
::X|EN|crash.dumps.015|                    "System error memory dump files" and "System error
::X|EN|crash.dumps.016|                    minidump files" handlers.
::
:: ---- shutdown.machine (risky) ----------------------------------
::P|shutdown.machine|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.001|ETEINDRE A LA FIN DE L'EXECUTION
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.002|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.003|  Ce que c est    : Enchaine une extinction a la fin de l'execution, via
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.004|                    shutdown /s /f /t 15 : extinction dans 15 secondes, en
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.005|                    fermant de force toutes les applications.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.006|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.007|  Effet reel      : Planifie l'extinction et patiente. L'option /f tue les
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.008|                    applications au lieu de le leur demander poliment :
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.009|                    aucune invite d'enregistrement n'apparait.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.010|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.011|  Gain            : Du confort : on lance un long nettoyage, on part, on
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.012|                    retrouve une machine eteinte. Aucun benefice
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.013|                    technique.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.014|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.015|  Cout            : Tout document non enregistre, editeur ouvert ou rendu
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.016|                    en cours est perdu sans avertissement. Vous avez 15
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.017|                    secondes pour annuler avec shutdown /a dans une invite
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.018|                    de commandes. Et si vous avez planifie un CHKDSK
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.019|                    complet plus tot dans cette execution, il ne tourne
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.020|                    PAS maintenant : il demarrera au prochain allumage et
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.021|                    peut monopoliser la machine des heures avant que
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.022|                    Windows ne charge. Annulez une verification planifiee
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.023|                    avec chkntfs /x C: avant d'eteindre si ce n'est pas ce
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.024|                    que vous voulez.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.025|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.026|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.027|
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.028|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.029|    APPLY                : shutdown /s /f /t 15 est lance : extinction
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.030|                           dans 15 secondes, /f signifiant que les
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.031|                           applications sont tuees et non invitees a se
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.032|                           fermer, donc rien ne peut vous proposer
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.033|                           d'enregistrer.
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.034|    SKIP                 : OPTY revient au menu et la machine reste
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.035|                           allumee. Vous l'eteignez vous-meme, quand vos
::T|FR|shutdown.machine.036|                           applications ont reellement enregistre.
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques, et c'est la reponse
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.002|                         honnete : ce n'est pas un reglage mais une
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.003|                         demande, et aucun profil ne peut savoir si vous
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.004|                         etes devant l'ecran avec un document ouvert. Le
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.005|                         defaut est SKIP partout parce que le cout est
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.006|                         irrattrapable et que le gain se resume a ne pas
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.007|                         cliquer sur Demarrer vous-meme. Repondez oui
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.008|                         seulement pour l'execution precise ou vous avez
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.009|                         tout ferme et ou vous partez.
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.010|
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.011|  Cible           : :shutdown label, OPTY.bat 1349-1356 - shutdown /s /f
::X|FR|shutdown.machine.012|                    /t 15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.001|SHUT DOWN WHEN THE RUN FINISHES
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.002|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.003|  What it is      : Chains a power-off onto the end of the run, with
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.004|                    shutdown /s /f /t 15 - power off in 15 seconds,
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.005|                    forcing every application closed.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.006|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.007|  Actual effect   : Schedules the shutdown and waits. The /f flag kills
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.008|                    applications rather than asking them politely, so no
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.009|                    save prompt appears.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.010|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.011|  Gain            : Convenience: start a long cleanup, walk away, come
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.012|                    back to a powered-off machine. No technical benefit
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.013|                    whatsoever.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.014|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.015|  Cost            : Any unsaved document, open editor or in-progress
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.016|                    render is lost without warning. You have a 15-second
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.017|                    window to abort with shutdown /a in a command prompt.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.018|                    And if you scheduled a full CHKDSK earlier in this
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.019|                    run, it does NOT run now - it runs the next time you
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.020|                    power the machine on, and can hold it for hours before
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.021|                    Windows loads. Cancel a scheduled check with chkntfs
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.022|                    /x C: before shutting down if that is not what you
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.023|                    want.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.024|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.025|  Windows default : Not applicable.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.026|
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.028|    APPLY                : shutdown /s /f /t 15 is issued: power off in 15
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.029|                           seconds, /f meaning applications are killed
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.030|                           rather than asked to close, so nothing gets to
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.031|                           prompt you to save.
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.032|    SKIP                 : OPTY returns to the menu and the machine stays
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.033|                           on. You shut it down yourself, whenever your
::T|EN|shutdown.machine.034|                           applications have actually saved.
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns, and that is the honest
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.002|                       answer: this is not a setting, it is a request, and
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.003|                       no profile can know whether you are standing there
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.004|                       with a document open. The default is SKIP
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.005|                       everywhere because the cost is unrecoverable and
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.006|                       the gain is that you do not have to click Start
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.007|                       yourself. Answer yes only for the specific run
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.008|                       where you have closed everything and are walking
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.009|                       away.
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.010|
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.011|  Target          : :shutdown label, OPTY.bat 1349-1356 - shutdown /s /f
::X|EN|shutdown.machine.012|                    /t 15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::
:: ---- reboot.machine (risky) ------------------------------------
::P|reboot.machine|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.001|REDEMARRER A LA FIN DE L'EXECUTION
::T|FR|reboot.machine.002|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.003|  Ce que c est    : Enchaine un redemarrage a la fin de l'execution, via
::T|FR|reboot.machine.004|                    shutdown /r /f /t 15 : redemarrage dans 15 secondes,
::T|FR|reboot.machine.005|                    en fermant de force toutes les applications.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.006|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.007|  Effet reel      : Planifie le redemarrage. /f tue les applications au
::T|FR|reboot.machine.008|                    lieu de leur demander de se fermer : rien ne vous
::T|FR|reboot.machine.009|                    propose d'enregistrer.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.010|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.011|  Gain            : Un redemarrage est ce qui termine reellement plusieurs
::T|FR|reboot.machine.012|                    actions de cet outil : les caches verrouilles par
::T|FR|reboot.machine.013|                    l'Explorateur sont liberes, les services relances se
::T|FR|reboot.machine.014|                    stabilisent, et tout reglage qui l'exige prend effet.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.015|                    Si vous avez touche a l'integrite de la memoire ou a
::T|FR|reboot.machine.016|                    la VBS, ou si le fichier d'echange a ete repare, le
::T|FR|reboot.machine.017|                    redemarrage n'est pas optionnel : seul son moment
::T|FR|reboot.machine.018|                    l'est.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.019|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.020|  Cout            : Le travail non enregistre est perdu sans
::T|FR|reboot.machine.021|                    avertissement, et un CHKDSK /f /r planifie demarre a
::T|FR|reboot.machine.022|                    ce redemarrage et peut monopoliser la machine des
::T|FR|reboot.machine.023|                    heures. shutdown /a dans les 15 secondes l'annule.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.024|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.025|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.026|
::T|FR|reboot.machine.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|reboot.machine.028|    APPLY                : shutdown /r /f /t 15 est lance : redemarrage
::T|FR|reboot.machine.029|                           dans 15 secondes, /f tuant les applications au
::T|FR|reboot.machine.030|                           lieu de leur demander de se fermer.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.031|    SKIP                 : OPTY revient au menu. Les reglages qui exigent
::T|FR|reboot.machine.032|                           un redemarrage - HVCI et VBS en premier lieu,
::T|FR|reboot.machine.033|                           ainsi que la reparation du fichier d'echange -
::T|FR|reboot.machine.034|                           restent en attente jusqu'a ce que vous
::T|FR|reboot.machine.035|                           redemarriez a votre convenance.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques. Le redemarrage est
::X|FR|reboot.machine.002|                         necessaire sur tout profil ayant modifie HVCI, la
::X|FR|reboot.machine.003|                         VBS ou le fichier d'echange, et inutile sur ceux
::X|FR|reboot.machine.004|                         qui n'y ont pas touche : c'est une propriete de
::X|FR|reboot.machine.005|                         l'execution, pas du role de la machine. SKIP est
::X|FR|reboot.machine.006|                         le defaut partout parce que vous pouvez
::X|FR|reboot.machine.007|                         redemarrer vous-meme en dix secondes, documents
::X|FR|reboot.machine.008|                         enregistres, et parce que la version forcee
::X|FR|reboot.machine.009|                         n'avertit personne.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.010|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.011|  Cible           : :reboot label, OPTY.bat 1358-1367 - shutdown /r /f /t
::X|FR|reboot.machine.012|                    15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::T|EN|reboot.machine.001|RESTART WHEN THE RUN FINISHES
::T|EN|reboot.machine.002|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.003|  What it is      : Chains a restart onto the end of the run, with
::T|EN|reboot.machine.004|                    shutdown /r /f /t 15 - restart in 15 seconds, forcing
::T|EN|reboot.machine.005|                    every application closed.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.006|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.007|  Actual effect   : Schedules the restart. /f kills applications rather
::T|EN|reboot.machine.008|                    than asking them to close, so nothing prompts you to
::T|EN|reboot.machine.009|                    save.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.010|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.011|  Gain            : A reboot is what actually finishes several of the
::T|EN|reboot.machine.012|                    things this tool does: caches locked by a running
::T|EN|reboot.machine.013|                    Explorer are released, restarted services settle, and
::T|EN|reboot.machine.014|                    any setting that needs a restart takes effect. If you
::T|EN|reboot.machine.015|                    touched Memory Integrity or VBS, or the pagefile was
::T|EN|reboot.machine.016|                    repaired, a reboot is not optional - only its timing
::T|EN|reboot.machine.017|                    is.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.018|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.019|  Cost            : Unsaved work is lost without a prompt, and a scheduled
::T|EN|reboot.machine.020|                    CHKDSK /f /r starts on this reboot and can hold the
::T|EN|reboot.machine.021|                    machine for hours. shutdown /a within 15 seconds
::T|EN|reboot.machine.022|                    cancels it.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.023|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.024|  Windows default : Not applicable.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.025|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.026|  Possible values:
::T|EN|reboot.machine.027|    APPLY                : shutdown /r /f /t 15 is issued: restart in 15
::T|EN|reboot.machine.028|                           seconds, /f killing applications instead of
::T|EN|reboot.machine.029|                           asking them to close.
::T|EN|reboot.machine.030|    SKIP                 : OPTY returns to the menu. Settings that need a
::T|EN|reboot.machine.031|                           restart - HVCI and VBS above all, and the
::T|EN|reboot.machine.032|                           pagefile repair - stay pending until you reboot
::T|EN|reboot.machine.033|                           on your own terms.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns. The reboot is needed on
::X|EN|reboot.machine.002|                       every profile that changed HVCI, VBS or the
::X|EN|reboot.machine.003|                       pagefile, and needed on none that did not - which
::X|EN|reboot.machine.004|                       is a property of the run, not of the machine's job.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.005|                       SKIP is the default everywhere because you can
::X|EN|reboot.machine.006|                       reboot yourself in ten seconds, with your documents
::X|EN|reboot.machine.007|                       saved, and because the forced version gives no
::X|EN|reboot.machine.008|                       warning at all.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.009|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.010|  Target          : :reboot label, OPTY.bat 1358-1367 - shutdown /r /f /t
::X|EN|reboot.machine.011|                    15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::
:: ---- mmcss.systemresponsiveness (repair) ------------------------
::P|mmcss.systemresponsiveness|20|20|20|20|20|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|MMCSS SYSTEMRESPONSIVENESS = 20
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|  Ce que c est    : SystemResponsiveness indique au service MMCSS quel
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                    pourcentage de temps CPU garder garanti pour le
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                    travail ordinaire, non multimedia, quand un thread
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                    multimedia s'est enregistre aupres de lui. Les
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|                    editions client de Windows 11 livrent 20. Les guides
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|                    d'optimisation conseillent 0 ou 10 depuis des annees.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20, la valeur que votre machine possede deja
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                    sauf si quelque chose l'a changee. Sur une
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                    installation propre, c'est volontairement une ecriture
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|                    sans effet ; sur une machine touchee par une ancienne
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|                    version d'OPTY, c'est une vraie reparation - 10 a ete
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|                    mesure ici en vrai.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|  Gain            : Aucun sur une machine saine, et cette fiche ne
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    pretendra pas le contraire. Son seul role est
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.019|                    d'annuler un tweak anterieur. Il n'y a aucun mecanisme
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.020|                    cote images par seconde : cette valeur gouverne une
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.021|                    reserve CPU pour les threads enregistres aupres de
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.022|                    MMCSS, pas le thread de rendu du jeu.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.023|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.024|  Cout            : Rien. Elle restaure ou preserve la valeur d'origine.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.025|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.026|  Defaut Windows  : 20 (0x14) sur les editions client. Windows Server est
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.027|                    livre a 100.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.028|
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.030|    20                   : La valeur d'origine des editions client. MMCSS
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.031|                           garantit 20 pour cent du temps CPU aux threads
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.032|                           ordinaires, non multimedias, et laisse les
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.033|                           threads multimedias enregistres prioritaires
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.034|                           sur les 80 pour cent restants. C'est ce
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.035|                           qu'installent Windows 11 Famille et Pro.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.036|    10                   : Moitie moins de reserve. Les threads
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.037|                           multimedias enregistres obtiennent une part
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.038|                           garantie plus grande, les threads ordinaires
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.039|                           une plus petite. C'est la valeur laissee par
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.040|                           d'anciennes versions d'OPTY, mesuree en vrai
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.041|                           sur cette machine.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.042|    0                    : Ne rien reserver du tout aux threads
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.043|                           ordinaires. C'est la valeur classique des
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.044|                           guides de bidouille. Priver les threads
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.045|                           d'arriere-plan de CPU garanti est precisement
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.046|                           ce qui produit les craquements audio en forte
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.047|                           charge, l'inverse de ce que promettent ces
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.048|                           guides.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.049|    100                  : Tout reserver aux threads ordinaires, ce qui
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.050|                           neutralise de fait la priorite multimedia.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.051|                           C'est la valeur livree par Windows Server, un
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.052|                           serveur n'ayant pas de lecture media locale a
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.053|                           proteger.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.054|    absent               : Si la valeur manque, MMCSS retombe sur son
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.055|                           comportement interne. Windows 11 client la
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.056|                           livre toujours presente a 20 : son absence
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.057|                           signifie que quelque chose l'a supprimee.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils ecrivent 20 parce que 20 EST la
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|                         valeur Windows, et que ni le jeu, ni
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|                         l'hebergement, ni la bureautique, ni la batterie
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                         ne changent ce que devrait valoir une reserve CPU
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                         pour threads multimedias. Une machine jamais
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                         bidouillee ne verra strictement aucune difference
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|                         : c'est le resultat attendu, pas un echec.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|  Non verifie (en)  : Microsoft documents the useful range as 10 to 100
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|                      and values outside it are said to be clamped, but I
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                      have not verified the clamping behaviour on this
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                      machine, so treat 0 as 'requests zero reserve'
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|                      rather than as a proven starvation setting.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    1534, in :gaming_perf)
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|MMCSS SYSTEMRESPONSIVENESS = 20
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|  What it is      : SystemResponsiveness tells the Multimedia Class
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                    Scheduler Service what percentage of CPU time to keep
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                    guaranteed for ordinary, non-multimedia work when a
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                    multimedia thread has registered with it. Windows 11
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|                    client editions ship 20. Optimizer guides have been
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|                    telling people to write 0 or 10 for years.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|  Actual effect   : Writes 20, the value your machine already has unless
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                    something changed it. On a clean install this is a
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                    deliberate no-op; on a machine an older OPTY build
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|                    touched, it is a real repair - 10 was measured live
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|                    here.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|  Gain            : None on a healthy machine, and this card will not
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|                    pretend otherwise. Its only job is to undo a previous
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    tweak. There is no frame-rate mechanism here: the
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.019|                    value governs a CPU reserve for threads that
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.020|                    registered with MMCSS, not the game's render thread.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.021|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.022|  Cost            : Nothing. It restores or preserves the shipped value.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.023|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.024|  Windows default : 20 (0x14) on client editions. Windows Server ships
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.025|                    100.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.026|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.027|  Possible values:
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.028|    20                   : The shipped client value. MMCSS guarantees 20
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.029|                           percent of CPU time to ordinary, non-multimedia
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.030|                           threads and lets registered multimedia threads
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.031|                           have priority over the remaining 80 percent.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.032|                           This is what Windows 11 Home and Pro install
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.033|                           with.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.034|    10                   : Half the reserve. Registered multimedia threads
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.035|                           get a larger guaranteed slice, ordinary threads
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.036|                           a smaller one. This is the value earlier OPTY
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.037|                           builds left behind, measured live on this
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.038|                           machine.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.039|    0                    : Ask for no reserve at all for ordinary threads.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.040|                           This is the classic tweak-guide value. Starving
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.041|                           background threads of guaranteed CPU is what
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.042|                           produces audio crackle under heavy load - the
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.043|                           opposite of what the guides promise.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.044|    100                  : Reserve everything for ordinary threads, which
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.045|                           effectively neutralises the multimedia priority
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.046|                           boost. This is what Windows Server ships,
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.047|                           because a server has no local media playback to
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.048|                           protect.
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.049|    absent               : If the value is missing, MMCSS falls back to
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.050|                           its built-in behaviour. Windows 11 client
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.051|                           always ships it present at 20, so absence means
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.052|                           something removed it.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|  Why these profiles : All five profiles write 20 because 20 IS the
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|                       Windows value and nothing about gaming, hosting,
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|                       office work or battery changes what a CPU reserve
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                       for multimedia threads should be. A machine that
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                       never had a tweak applied will notice no difference
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                       at all - that is the expected outcome, not a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|                       failure.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|  Unverified      : Microsoft documents the useful range as 10 to 100 and
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|                    values outside it are said to be clamped, but I have
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                    not verified the clamping behaviour on this machine,
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                    so treat 0 as 'requests zero reserve' rather than as a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|                    proven starvation setting.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    1534, in :gaming_perf)
::
:: ---- pagefile.system.managed (repair) ---------------------------
::P|pagefile.system.managed|1|1|1|1|1|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.001|FICHIER D'ECHANGE GERE PAR LE SYSTEME
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.002|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.003|  Ce que c est    : Le fichier d'echange est le fichier disque sur lequel
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.004|                    Windows appuie la memoire virtuelle. « Desactive ton
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.005|                    fichier d'echange, tu as 32 Go de RAM » est l'un des
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.006|                    conseils d'optimisation les plus tenaces et les plus
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.007|                    nuisibles. Cette etape ecrit
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.008|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile=1.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.009|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.010|  Effet reel      : Lisez la mecanique de pres, car le commentaire du
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.011|                    script a la ligne 1875 la dit noir sur blanc :
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.012|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile n'est que l'etat de la CASE A
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.013|                    COCHER, lue par wbem\cimwin32.dll. La valeur que le
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.014|                    noyau utilise reellement est PagingFiles, dans la meme
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.015|                    cle. Sur une machine dont le fichier d'echange a ete
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.016|                    supprime, cette ecriture seule ne repare rien :
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.017|                    l'etape qui retablit vraiment un fichier d'echange est
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.018|                    la reparation separee de PagingFiles, et elle exige un
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.019|                    redemarrage.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.020|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.021|  Gain            : Sur une machine dont le fichier d'echange fonctionne,
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.022|                    aucun : elle coche une case deja cochee. Elle compte
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.023|                    sur une machine reglee en taille fixe : la gestion
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.024|                    revient a Windows, et les applications qui reservent
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.025|                    beaucoup de memoire virtuelle (jeux recents,
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.026|                    navigateur charge d'onglets, montage video, machines
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.027|                    virtuelles) cessent de buter sur des echecs de limite
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.028|                    de validation sans rapport avec la RAM installee.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.029|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.030|  Cout            : De l'espace disque : Windows dimensionnera lui-meme le
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.031|                    fichier, en general quelques Go, davantage sur une
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.032|                    machine tres dotee en RAM. C'est tout.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.033|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.034|  Defaut Windows  : 1 - automatique, gere par le systeme.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.035|
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.036|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.037|    1                    : La case « Gestion automatique du fichier
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.038|                           d'echange pour tous les lecteurs » est cochee.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.039|                           Windows dimensionne lui-meme le fichier
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.040|                           d'echange, l'agrandissant ou le reduisant dans
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.041|                           ses propres limites. C'est ce que livre un
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.042|                           Windows 11 propre.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.043|    0                    : La case est decochee : les entrees listees dans
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.044|                           PagingFiles sont traitees comme votre choix
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.045|                           explicite - taille initiale et maximale fixes,
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.046|                           ou liste vide si vous avez supprime le fichier
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.047|                           d'echange. Ecrire 0 ne supprime rien en soi.
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.048|    absent               : Se comporte comme 0 pour l'interface de
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.049|                           gestion. Windows 11 livre la valeur presente :
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.050|                           son absence signifie generalement qu'un outil
::T|FR|pagefile.system.managed.051|                           l'a retiree.
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a 1, SERVEUR compris :
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.002|                         les charges orientees debit ont besoin de plus de
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.003|                         marge de validation, pas de moins. La vieille
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.004|                         idee qu'un serveur ou une machine de jeu voudrait
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.005|                         un fichier d'echange fixe regle a la main n'a
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.006|                         aucun mecanisme derriere elle sur une machine a
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.007|                         SSD unique et rapide. Refusez seulement si vous
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.008|                         exploitez volontairement un fichier de taille
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.009|                         fixe sur un disque dedie et savez pourquoi.
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.010|
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.011|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.012|                    Manager\Memory Management" /v
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.013|                    "AutomaticManagedPagefile" /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.014|                    (OPTY.bat 1559, in :gaming_perf). The value the kernel
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.015|                    actually reads is PagingFiles in the same key - see
::X|FR|pagefile.system.managed.016|                    rad-pagefile-repair, OPTY.bat 1884-1887.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.001|SYSTEM-MANAGED PAGEFILE
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.002|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.003|  What it is      : The pagefile is the file on disk Windows uses to back
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.004|                    virtual memory. 'Disable your pagefile, you have 32 GB
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.005|                    of RAM' is one of the most persistent and most
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.006|                    damaging pieces of optimizer advice. This step writes
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.007|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile=1.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.008|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.009|  Actual effect   : Read the mechanics carefully, because the script's own
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.010|                    comment at line 1875 spells them out:
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.011|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile is only the CHECKBOX state,
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.012|                    read by wbem\cimwin32.dll. The value the kernel
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.013|                    actually uses is PagingFiles in the same key. On a
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.014|                    machine whose pagefile was deleted, this write alone
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.015|                    repairs nothing - the step that genuinely restores a
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.016|                    pagefile is the separate PagingFiles repair, and it
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.017|                    needs a reboot.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.018|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.019|  Gain            : On a machine with a working pagefile, none - it ticks
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.020|                    a checkbox that is already ticked. Where it matters is
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.021|                    on a machine someone set to a fixed size: management
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.022|                    goes back to Windows, so applications that reserve
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.023|                    large amounts of virtual memory (modern games, a
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.024|                    browser with many tabs, video editors, VMs) stop
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.025|                    hitting commit-limit failures that have nothing to do
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.026|                    with installed RAM.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.027|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.028|  Cost            : Disk space: Windows will size a pagefile itself,
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.029|                    typically a few GB, more on a machine with a lot of
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.030|                    RAM. That is the whole cost.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.031|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.032|  Windows default : 1 - automatic, system-managed.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.033|
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.034|  Possible values:
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.035|    1                    : The 'Automatically manage paging file size for
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.036|                           all drives' checkbox is on. Windows sizes the
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.037|                           pagefile itself, growing and shrinking it
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.038|                           within its own limits. This is what a clean
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.039|                           Windows 11 ships.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.040|    0                    : The checkbox is off, meaning the entries listed
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.041|                           in PagingFiles are treated as your explicit
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.042|                           choice - a fixed initial and maximum size, or
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.043|                           an empty list if you removed the pagefile
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.044|                           entirely. Writing 0 does not itself delete
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.045|                           anything.
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.046|    absent               : Behaves like 0 for the management UI. Windows
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.047|                           11 ships the value present, so absence usually
::T|EN|pagefile.system.managed.048|                           means a tool removed it.
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns of 1, including SERVER:
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.002|                       throughput workloads need commit headroom more than
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.003|                       anyone, not less. The old idea that a server or a
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.004|                       gaming rig wants a hand-tuned fixed pagefile has no
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.005|                       mechanism behind it on a machine with a single fast
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.006|                       SSD. Say no only if you deliberately run a fixed-
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.007|                       size pagefile on a separate drive and can explain
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.008|                       why.
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.009|
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.010|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.011|                    Manager\Memory Management" /v
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.012|                    "AutomaticManagedPagefile" /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.013|                    (OPTY.bat 1559, in :gaming_perf). The value the kernel
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.014|                    actually reads is PagingFiles in the same key - see
::X|EN|pagefile.system.managed.015|                    rad-pagefile-repair, OPTY.bat 1884-1887.
::
:: ---- hvci.vbs.off (risky) --------------------------------------
::P|hvci.vbs.off|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.001|INTEGRITE DE LA MEMOIRE (HVCI) ET SECURITE BASEE SUR LA VIRTUALISATION
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.002|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.003|  Ce que c est    : L'integrite de la memoire utilise l'hyperviseur pour
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.004|                    verifier chaque pilote noyau avant de l'autoriser a
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.005|                    s'executer, ce qui bloque toute une famille d'attaques
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.006|                    consistant a charger un pilote signe malveillant ou
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.007|                    vulnerable dans le noyau. La VBS est la couche de
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.008|                    virtualisation dont elle depend. Ensemble, elles
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.009|                    forment la protection noyau la plus solide livree avec
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.010|                    Windows 11.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.011|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.012|  Effet reel      : Ecrit HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0 et
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.013|                    EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0. Rien ne change
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.014|                    avant un redemarrage. C'est la seule etape du script
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.015|                    qui s'arrete pour demander sa propre confirmation
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.016|                    explicite.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.017|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.018|  Gain            : Une baisse de surcharge CPU, surtout sur les charges
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.019|                    et jeux limites par le processeur, et tres variable -
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.020|                    voyez la note d'incertitude. C'est aussi parfois le
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.021|                    seul moyen de charger un pilote ancien, non signe ou
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.022|                    incompatible : interface audio d'epoque, vieille carte
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.023|                    d'acquisition, certains logiciels de virtualisation.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.024|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.025|  Cout            : La plus grande regression de securite de tout le
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.026|                    script. L'argument anti-triche que tout le monde
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.027|                    repete concerne un AUTRE reglage : Vanguard et FACEIT
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.028|                    exigent le demarrage securise et le TPM 2.0 sous
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.029|                    Windows 11, pas l'integrite de la memoire. Couper HVCI
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.030|                    n'est donc pas ce qui vous ferme ces jeux - ce que
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.031|                    vous perdez vraiment, c'est la protection noyau autour
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.032|                    de laquelle les anti-triches en mode noyau et la liste
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.033|                    de blocage des pilotes vulnerables sont construits. La
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.034|                    seconde ecriture desactive la securite basee sur la
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.035|                    virtualisation dans son ensemble : Credential Guard,
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.036|                    la partie materielle de la protection LSA et toute
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.037|                    strategie WDAC appliquee via HVCI disparaissent avec.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.038|                    Sur une machine professionnelle ou jointe a Entra,
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.039|                    cela peut la marquer non conforme et couper l'acces
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.040|                    aux ressources de l'entreprise. La reactivation ne
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.041|                    reussira que si aucun pilote incompatible n'est
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.042|                    installe - Securite Windows vous nommera le coupable.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.043|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.044|  Defaut Windows  : L'integrite de la memoire est ACTIVEE par defaut sur
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.045|                    les installations propres de Windows 11 et les
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.046|                    machines OEM compatibles. Elle est souvent desactivee
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.047|                    apres une mise a niveau depuis Windows 10, ou quand un
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.048|                    pilote incompatible la bloque. Les valeurs de
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.049|                    registre, elles, sont livrees ABSENTES.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.050|
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.051|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.052|    SKIP                 : Aucune des deux valeurs n'est ecrite.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.053|                           L'integrite de la memoire et la VBS restent
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.054|                           dans l'etat ou les mettent votre firmware, vos
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.055|                           pilotes et vos strategies - soit, sur une
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.056|                           machine Windows 11 compatible, actives.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.057|    0                    : HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.058|                           empeche l'hyperviseur de valider les pages de
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.059|                           code noyau : les pilotes non signes ou
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.060|                           incompatibles se chargent de nouveau.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.061|                           EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0 demonte la
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.062|                           couche de virtualisation en dessous, ce qui
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.063|                           emporte Credential Guard, la partie materielle
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.064|                           de la protection LSA et toute strategie WDAC
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.065|                           appliquee via HVCI. Effectif apres redemarrage.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.066|    1                    : Force les deux, quel que soit le materiel. Sur
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.067|                           une machine dont les pilotes ou le firmware ne
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.068|                           supportent pas HVCI, cela provoque des echecs
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.069|                           de chargement de pilotes : c'est pourquoi les
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.070|                           chemins de reparation suppriment ces valeurs au
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.071|                           lieu d'ecrire 1.
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.072|    absent               : Le vrai defaut Windows. Windows decide selon
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.073|                           les capacites materielles, la compatibilite des
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.074|                           pilotes et les strategies. C'est l'etat que
::T|FR|hvci.vbs.off.075|                           retablissent les deux fiches de reparation.
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes a SKIP, et inventer une difference
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.002|                         ici serait malhonnete. Le gain est faible et non
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.003|                         demontre sur du materiel recent, et la perte est
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.004|                         identique quel que soit l'usage : JEU perd la
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.005|                         protection que suppose l'anti-triche noyau,
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.006|                         SERVEUR expose une machine tournee vers le
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.007|                         reseau, BUREAU et PORTABLE peuvent etre joints a
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.008|                         Entra et se retrouver non conformes, et WINDOWS
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.009|                         consiste a ne pas toucher a l'etat d'origine. A
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.010|                         envisager uniquement sur une machine sacrifiable
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.011|                         ou vous avez mesure un vrai probleme de fluidite,
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.012|                         ou si un pilote dont vous avez reellement besoin
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.013|                         refuse de se charger.
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.014|
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.015|  Non verifie (en)  : The performance cost of HVCI is real but highly
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.016|                      variable - published measurements range from near
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.017|                      zero to a few percent in CPU-bound games, depending
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.018|                      on CPU generation and title. On modern hardware it
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.019|                      is usually small enough that most people would not
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.020|                      notice it in normal play.
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.021|
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.022|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 1600-1601, behind an explicit prompt in
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.023|                    :gaming_perf - reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.024|                    Control\DeviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIn
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.025|                    tegrity" /v "Enabled" /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f and reg add
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.026|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard" /v
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.027|                    "EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity" /t REG_DWORD /d 0
::X|FR|hvci.vbs.off.028|                    /f
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.001|MEMORY INTEGRITY (HVCI) AND VIRTUALIZATION-BASED SECURITY
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.002|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.003|  What it is      : Memory Integrity uses the hypervisor to verify every
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.004|                    kernel driver before it is allowed to run, which
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.005|                    blocks a whole class of attacks that load a malicious
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.006|                    or vulnerable signed driver into the kernel. VBS is
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.007|                    the underlying virtualisation layer it depends on.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.008|                    Together they are the strongest kernel-level
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.009|                    protection Windows 11 ships with.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.010|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.011|  Actual effect   : Writes HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0 and
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.012|                    EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0. Nothing changes
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.013|                    until you reboot. This is the one step in the script
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.014|                    that stops and asks for its own explicit confirmation.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.015|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.016|  Gain            : A reduction in CPU overhead, mostly in CPU-bound
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.017|                    workloads and games, and highly variable - see the
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.018|                    uncertainty note. It is also sometimes the only way to
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.019|                    load an older, unsigned or incompatible driver: a
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.020|                    legacy audio interface, an old capture card, some
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.021|                    virtualisation software.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.022|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.023|  Cost            : The single largest security regression in this script.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.024|                    The commonly repeated anti-cheat argument is about a
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.025|                    DIFFERENT setting: Vanguard and FACEIT require Secure
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.026|                    Boot and TPM 2.0 on Windows 11, not Memory Integrity,
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.027|                    so turning HVCI off is not what locks you out of those
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.028|                    games - what you actually lose is the kernel
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.029|                    protection that kernel-mode anti-cheats and the
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.030|                    vulnerable-driver blocklist are built alongside. The
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.031|                    second write switches off virtualization-based
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.032|                    security as a whole, so Credential Guard, the
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.033|                    hardware-backed part of LSA protection and any HVCI-
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.034|                    enforced WDAC policy go with it. On a work or Entra-
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.035|                    joined machine that can mark the device non-compliant
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.036|                    and cut off company resources. Re-enabling later only
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.037|                    succeeds if no incompatible driver is installed -
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.038|                    Windows Security names the one that blocks it.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.039|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.040|  Windows default : Memory Integrity is ON by default on Windows 11 clean
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.041|                    installs and compatible OEM machines. It is commonly
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.042|                    OFF after an in-place upgrade from Windows 10, or when
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.043|                    an incompatible driver blocks it. The registry values
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.044|                    themselves ship ABSENT.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.045|
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.046|  Possible values:
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.047|    SKIP                 : Neither value is written. Memory Integrity and
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.048|                           VBS stay in whatever state your firmware,
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.049|                           drivers and policy put them in - which on a
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.050|                           compatible Windows 11 machine means enabled.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.051|    0                    : HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\Enabled=0 stops
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.052|                           the hypervisor from validating kernel code
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.053|                           pages, so unsigned or incompatible drivers load
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.054|                           again. EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity=0
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.055|                           tears down the virtualization layer underneath,
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.056|                           which takes Credential Guard, the hardware-
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.057|                           backed part of LSA protection and any HVCI-
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.058|                           enforced WDAC policy with it. Effective after a
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.059|                           reboot.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.060|    1                    : Forces both on regardless of hardware. On a
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.061|                           machine whose drivers or firmware cannot
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.062|                           support HVCI this causes driver-load failures,
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.063|                           which is why the repair paths delete these
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.064|                           values instead of writing 1.
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.065|    absent               : The true Windows default. Windows decides from
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.066|                           hardware capability, driver compatibility and
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.067|                           policy. This is the state the two repair cards
::T|EN|hvci.vbs.off.068|                           restore.
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.001|  Why these profiles : Five SKIP columns, and inventing a difference here
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.002|                       would be dishonest. The gain is small and unproven
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.003|                       on modern hardware, and the loss is identical
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.004|                       whatever the machine does: GAMING loses the
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.005|                       protection kernel anti-cheat assumes, SERVER
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.006|                       exposes a box that faces the network, OFFICE and
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.007|                       LAPTOP may be Entra-joined and get flagged non-
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.008|                       compliant, and WINDOWS means leaving the shipped
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.009|                       state alone. Consider yes only on a disposable
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.010|                       machine where you measured a real frame-time
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.011|                       problem, or where a driver you genuinely need
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.012|                       refuses to load.
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.013|
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.014|  Unverified      : The performance cost of HVCI is real but highly
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.015|                    variable - published measurements range from near zero
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.016|                    to a few percent in CPU-bound games, depending on CPU
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.017|                    generation and title. On modern hardware it is usually
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.018|                    small enough that most people would not notice it in
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.019|                    normal play.
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.020|
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.021|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1600-1601, behind an explicit prompt in
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.022|                    :gaming_perf - reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.023|                    Control\DeviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIn
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.024|                    tegrity" /v "Enabled" /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f and reg add
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.025|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard" /v
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.026|                    "EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity" /t REG_DWORD /d 0
::X|EN|hvci.vbs.off.027|                    /f
::
:: ---- gr.systemresponsiveness (repair) ---------------------------
::P|gr.systemresponsiveness|20|20|20|20|20|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|SYSTEMRESPONSIVENESS (MMCSS) REMIS A 20
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|  Ce que c est    : La part de CPU que MMCSS garde garantie au travail non
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                    multimedia pendant qu'une application media est
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|                    enregistree aupres de lui. C'est la moitie « retour en
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|                    arriere » du profil jeu.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20. Les guides mettent 0 ou 10, ce qui ne
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    modifie l'ordonnancement que pour les threads
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.010|                    enregistres aupres de MMCSS - typiquement les threads
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.011|                    de rendu audio des jeux et des applications media,
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.012|                    puisque les profils de tache Games, Audio et Pro Audio
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.013|                    existent precisement pour cela, et non le thread de
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.014|                    rendu principal d'un jeu.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.015|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.016|  Gain            : Aucun si vous etiez deja a 20. Sur une machine restee
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.017|                    a 0, cela supprime la condition qui affame les threads
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.018|                    audio en charge, symptome audible que les gens
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.019|                    rapportent.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.020|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.021|  Cout            : Rien. Simple remise a la valeur d'usine.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.022|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.023|  Defaut Windows  : 20 (0x14).
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.024|
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.026|    20                   : La valeur d'origine des editions client : 20
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.027|                           pour cent du temps CPU restent garantis aux
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.028|                           threads ordinaires, le reste est disponible
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.029|                           pour les threads enregistres aupres de MMCSS.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.030|    10                   : Moitie moins de reserve pour les threads
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.031|                           ordinaires. La valeur laissee sur cette machine
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.032|                           meme par d'anciennes versions d'OPTY.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.033|    0                    : Ne demande aucune reserve. Le chouchou des
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.034|                           guides de bidouille, et l'etat dans lequel les
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.035|                           threads de rendu audio peuvent manquer de CPU
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.036|                           en forte charge.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.037|    100                  : Tout reserve aux threads ordinaires, ce qui
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.038|                           neutralise le coup de pouce multimedia. La
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.039|                           valeur de Windows Server.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.040|    absent               : MMCSS retombe sur son comportement interne.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.041|                           Windows 11 client livre la valeur presente :
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.042|                           son absence signifie qu'elle a ete supprimee.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq ecrivent 20 parce que 20 est la valeur
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|                         Windows et qu'aucun usage ne plaide pour une
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|                         reserve CPU differente. Refusez seulement si vous
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                         avez un setup audio faible latence regle sur un
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|                         chiffre que vous savez justifier.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.010|                    1621, in :gaming_restore)
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|MMCSS SYSTEMRESPONSIVENESS BACK TO 20
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|  What it is      : The share of CPU that MMCSS keeps guaranteed for non-
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                    multimedia work while a media application is
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|                    registered with it. This is the revert half of the
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|                    gaming profile.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|  Actual effect   : Writes 20. Tweak guides use 0 or 10, which changes
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    scheduling only for threads registered with MMCSS -
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.010|                    typically the audio render threads of games and media
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.011|                    applications, since the Games, Audio and Pro Audio
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.012|                    task profiles exist for exactly that, and not a game's
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.013|                    main render thread.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.014|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.015|  Gain            : None if you were already at 20. On a machine sitting
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.016|                    at 0 it removes the condition that starves audio
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.017|                    threads under load, which is the audible symptom
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.018|                    people report.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.019|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.020|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair back to the shipped
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.021|                    number.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.022|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.023|  Windows default : 20 (0x14).
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.024|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.025|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.026|    20                   : The shipped client value: 20 percent of CPU
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.027|                           time stays guaranteed to ordinary threads, the
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.028|                           rest is available to threads registered with
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.029|                           MMCSS.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.030|    10                   : Half the reserve for ordinary threads. The
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.031|                           value earlier OPTY builds left behind on this
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.032|                           very machine.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.033|    0                    : Asks for no reserve at all. The tweak-guide
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.034|                           favourite, and the state in which audio render
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.035|                           threads can be starved under heavy load.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.036|    100                  : Everything reserved for ordinary threads,
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.037|                           neutralising the multimedia boost. The Windows
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.038|                           Server value.
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.039|    absent               : MMCSS falls back to internal behaviour. Windows
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.040|                           11 client ships the value present, so absence
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.041|                           means it was removed.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|  Why these profiles : All five write 20 because 20 is the Windows value
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|                       and no use case argues for a different CPU reserve.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|                       Say no only if you run a low-latency audio rig on a
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                       number you chose yourself and can justify.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    1621, in :gaming_restore)
::
:: ---- gr.hvci.vbs.killkey (risky) -------------------------------
::P|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.001|RETIRER LE FORCAGE INTEGRITE DE LA MEMOIRE / VBS
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.002|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : Deux valeurs de registre qui desactivent HVCI et la
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.004|                    VBS de force, quel que soit le materiel. Les supprimer
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.005|                    rend la decision a Windows.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.006|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.007|  Effet reel      : Supprime au lieu d'ecrire 1, parce que le defaut
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.008|                    Windows est l'absence de la valeur et qu'ecrire 1
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.009|                    serait un troisieme etat. Redemarrage requis.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.010|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.011|  Gain            : Retablit une vraie attenuation d'exploitation du noyau
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.012|                    sur le materiel qui la supporte, et avec elle la
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.013|                    couche VBS sur laquelle reposent Credential Guard et
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.014|                    la protection LSA materielle.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.015|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.016|  Cout            : Un vieux pilote non signe peut refuser de se charger
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.017|                    une fois HVCI actif - Securite Windows vous le
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.018|                    nommera. Quelques jeux limites par le CPU perdent un
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.019|                    peu de performance, de facon variable.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.020|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.021|  Defaut Windows  : Depend de l'image, mais ce n'est jamais un forcage a
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.022|                    0, et forcer 1 n'est pas correct non plus.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.023|
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.024|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.025|    DELETE               : Les deux valeurs sont supprimees : plus rien
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.026|                           dans le registre n'impose de reponse. Windows
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.027|                           decide selon les capacites du firmware, la
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.028|                           compatibilite des pilotes et les strategies -
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.029|                           donc, sur une machine Windows 11 compatible,
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.030|                           l'integrite de la memoire revient au prochain
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.031|                           redemarrage.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.032|    0                    : Conserve le forcage qui desactive HVCI et la
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.033|                           VBS. C'est l'etat laisse par un script de
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.034|                           bidouille ou par le profil jeu.
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.035|    1                    : Force les deux. Ce n'est PAS le defaut mais un
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.036|                           troisieme etat : sur du materiel ou des pilotes
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.037|                           incapables de supporter HVCI, cela provoque des
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.038|                           echecs de chargement de pilotes, raison pour
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.039|                           laquelle la reparation supprime au lieu
::T|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.040|                           d'ecrire 1.
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a DELETE parce que le
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.002|                         bon etat est « laisser Windows decider » sur
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.003|                         toutes les machines, profil WINDOWS compris, ou
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.004|                         l'absence EST l'etat d'origine. Si un pilote dont
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.005|                         vous avez besoin bloque reellement HVCI, Windows
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.006|                         refusera de l'activer tout seul et aucun forcage
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.007|                         n'est necessaire.
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.008|
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.009|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether these values ship absent or present as 1
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.010|                      depends on the image: machines that ship with Memory
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.011|                      Integrity enabled can carry Enabled=1 placed there
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.012|                      by the OEM or by policy.
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.013|
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.014|  Cible           : call :killkey "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\D
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.015|                    eviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity"
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.016|                    "Enabled" and call :killkey
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.017|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard"
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.018|                    "EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity" (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.019|                    1681-1682, in :gaming_restore)
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.001|REMOVE THE MEMORY INTEGRITY / VBS OVERRIDE
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.002|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.003|  What it is      : Two registry values that force HVCI and VBS off
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.004|                    regardless of what the hardware could do. Deleting
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.005|                    them hands the decision back to Windows.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.006|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes rather than writing 1, because the Windows
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.008|                    default is the value being absent and writing 1 would
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.009|                    be a third state. Needs a reboot to take effect.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.010|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.011|  Gain            : Restores a real kernel exploit mitigation on hardware
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.012|                    that supports it, and with it the VBS layer that
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.013|                    Credential Guard and hardware-backed LSA protection
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.014|                    sit on.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.015|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.016|  Cost            : An old unsigned driver may refuse to load once HVCI is
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.017|                    active again - Windows Security will name it. Some
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.018|                    CPU-bound games lose a small and variable amount of
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.019|                    performance.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.020|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.021|  Windows default : Depends on the image, but forcing 0 is never it, and
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.022|                    forcing 1 is never correct either.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.023|
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.025|    DELETE               : Both values are removed, so nothing in the
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.026|                           registry forces an answer. Windows decides from
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.027|                           firmware capability, driver compatibility and
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.028|                           policy - which on a compatible Windows 11
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.029|                           machine means Memory Integrity comes back on at
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.030|                           the next reboot.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.031|    0                    : Keeps the override that forces HVCI and VBS
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.032|                           off. This is the state a tweak script or the
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.033|                           gaming profile left behind.
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.034|    1                    : Forces both on. This is NOT the default and is
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.035|                           a third state: on hardware or drivers that
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.036|                           cannot support HVCI it produces driver-load
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.037|                           failures, which is precisely why the repair
::T|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.038|                           deletes instead of writing 1.
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns because the correct
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.002|                       state is 'let Windows decide' on every machine,
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.003|                       including the WINDOWS profile where absence IS the
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.004|                       shipped state. If a driver you need genuinely
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.005|                       blocks HVCI, Windows will refuse to enable it by
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.006|                       itself and no override is required.
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.007|
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.008|  Unverified      : Whether these values ship absent or present as 1
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.009|                    depends on the image: machines that ship with Memory
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.010|                    Integrity enabled can carry Enabled=1 placed there by
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.011|                    the OEM or by policy.
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.012|
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.013|  Target          : call :killkey "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\D
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.014|                    eviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity"
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.015|                    "Enabled" and call :killkey
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.016|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard"
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.017|                    "EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity" (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|gr.hvci.vbs.killkey.018|                    1681-1682, in :gaming_restore)
::
:: ---- rad.mmcss.scheduler (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.mmcss.scheduler|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|REECRIRE LES TROIS VALEURS MMCSS ET ORDONNANCEUR
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|  Ce que c est    : SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex et
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|                    Win32PrioritySeparation, ecrites ensemble parce que
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|                    les guides de bidouille les modifient toujours
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|                    ensemble.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20, 10 et 2, les valeurs d'origine. Ce n'est pas
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|                    theorique : 10, 0xFFFFFFFF et 38 ont ete mesurees sur
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|                    cette machine meme, laissees par d'anciennes versions
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|                    de cet outil, et ont survecu a toutes les
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|                    restaurations precedentes parce que :reassert_defaults
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|                    n'y touchait pas.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.015|  Gain            : Supprime des tweaks capables de provoquer des coupures
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.016|                    audio en charge, sans le moindre gain en images par
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.017|                    seconde en echange. Sur une machine jamais bidouillee,
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.018|                    aucun changement observable : l'ecriture sert a
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.019|                    remettre une machine inconnue dans un etat connu.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.020|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.021|  Cout            : Rien. Les trois reviennent a ce que Windows installe.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.022|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.023|  Defaut Windows  : 20 / 10 / 2.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.024|
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.026|    20/10/2              : Le trio d'origine. SystemResponsiveness=20
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.027|                           garde un cinquieme du temps CPU garanti aux
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.028|                           threads ordinaires. NetworkThrottlingIndex=10
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.029|                           plafonne le traitement reseau non multimedia a
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.030|                           environ 10 paquets par milliseconde quand un
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.031|                           flux multimedia est enregistre.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.032|                           Win32PrioritySeparation=2, c'est le quantum
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.033|                           court, variable et favorable au premier plan
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.034|                           qu'utilise un Windows client.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.035|    10/0xFFFFFFFF/38     : Les reliquats exacts mesures en vrai sur cette
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.036|                           machine, ecrits par d'anciennes versions d'OPTY
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.037|                           et jamais nettoyes par aucune restauration
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.038|                           precedente. 0xFFFFFFFF desactive completement
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.039|                           la limitation reseau, et 38 (0x26) est ce
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.040|                           qu'ecrit « Ajuster pour obtenir les meilleures
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.041|                           performances des programmes » : sur une edition
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.042|                           client, cela se decode en le meme profil
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.043|                           court/variable/premier plan que 2, donc
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.044|                           n'apporte rien.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.045|    0 for SystemResponsiveness :
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.046|        Aucune reserve CPU pour les threads ordinaires. En forte charge,
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.047|        c'est la configuration ou les threads de rendu audio craquent.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.048|    0xFFFFFFFF for NetworkThrottlingIndex :
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.049|        Limitation entierement desactivee. Cela retire un plafond qui ne
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.050|        s'applique que lorsqu'un flux multimedia est enregistre, et la
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.051|        promesse habituelle de latence de jeu reduite n'a aucune mesure
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.052|        derriere elle : le trafic de jeu est en UDP et n'est pas ce que ce
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.053|        plafond gouverne.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.054|    absent               : Les trois sont livrees presentes sous Windows
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.055|                           11 : une valeur absente signifie qu'un outil
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.056|                           l'a retiree. L'ordonnanceur retombe alors sur
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.057|                           son comportement client interne.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Une seule valeur pour les cinq profils, parce que
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|                         ces trois nombres sont simplement les defauts
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|                         client et qu'aucun ne se comporte differemment
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|                         sur une machine de jeu, un serveur, un poste
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|                         bureautique ou un portable. Refusez seulement si
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|                         vous les avez reglees expres et savez justifier
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|                         chacune.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 1815-1817, in :reassert_defaults - call
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|                    :regset on SystemResponsiveness=20 and
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex=10 under
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile, and
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|                    Win32PrioritySeparation=2 under
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.015|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\PriorityControl
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|REWRITE THE THREE MMCSS AND SCHEDULER VALUES
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|  What it is      : SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex and
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|                    Win32PrioritySeparation, written together because
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|                    tweak guides always change them together.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|  Actual effect   : Writes 20, 10 and 2 - the shipped numbers. This is not
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|                    theoretical: 10, 0xFFFFFFFF and 38 were measured on
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|                    this very machine, left by older builds of this tool,
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|                    and survived every earlier restore because
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|                    :reassert_defaults never touched them.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|  Gain            : Removes tweaks that can cause audio glitching under
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|                    load, with no frame-rate gain to show for them. On a
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.015|                    machine that was never tweaked, no observable change -
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.016|                    the write exists so an unknown machine ends in a known
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.017|                    state.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.018|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.019|  Cost            : Nothing. All three are being set back to what Windows
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.020|                    installs.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.021|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.022|  Windows default : 20 / 10 / 2.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.023|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.024|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.025|    20/10/2              : The shipped trio. SystemResponsiveness=20 keeps
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.026|                           a fifth of CPU time guaranteed to ordinary
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.027|                           threads. NetworkThrottlingIndex=10 caps non-
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.028|                           multimedia network processing at roughly 10
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.029|                           packets per millisecond while a multimedia
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.030|                           stream is registered. Win32PrioritySeparation=2
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.031|                           is the short, variable, foreground-boosted
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.032|                           quantum a client Windows uses.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.033|    10/0xFFFFFFFF/38     : The exact leftovers measured live on this
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.034|                           machine, written by earlier OPTY builds and
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.035|                           never cleaned up by any previous restore.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.036|                           0xFFFFFFFF disables network throttling
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.037|                           entirely, and 38 (0x26) is what 'Adjust for
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.038|                           best performance of: Programs' writes - on a
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.039|                           client SKU it decodes to the same
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.040|                           short/variable/foreground profile as 2, so it
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.041|                           buys nothing.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.042|    0 for SystemResponsiveness :
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.043|        No CPU reserve for ordinary threads. Under heavy load this is the
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.044|        configuration in which audio render threads glitch.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.045|    0xFFFFFFFF for NetworkThrottlingIndex :
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.046|        Throttling off completely. It removes a cap that only applies
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.047|        while a multimedia stream is registered, and the usual claim of
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.048|        lower game latency has no measurement behind it - game traffic is
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.049|        UDP and is not what the cap governs.
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.050|    absent               : All three ship present on Windows 11, so an
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.051|                           absent value means a tool removed it. The
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.052|                           scheduler then falls back to its built-in
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.053|                           client behaviour.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|  Why these profiles : One literal for all five profiles, because all
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|                       three values are simply the client defaults and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|                       none of them behaves differently on a gaming rig, a
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|                       server, an office desktop or a laptop. Say no only
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|                       if you set these deliberately and can explain each
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|                       one.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1815-1817, in :reassert_defaults - call
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|                    :regset on SystemResponsiveness=20 and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex=10 under
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile, and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|                    Win32PrioritySeparation=2 under
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\PriorityControl
::
:: ---- rad.disablepagingexec (repair) -----------------------------
::P|rad.disablepagingexec|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.001|DISABLEPAGINGEXECUTIVE REMIS A 0
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.002|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.003|  Ce que c est    : Decide si le code du noyau et les pilotes peuvent etre
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.004|                    dechargés de la RAM vers le fichier d'echange.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.005|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.006|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 0, la valeur d'origine : le gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.007|                    memoire peut de nouveau les paginer quand la RAM
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.008|                    manque.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.009|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.010|  Gain            : Aucun que vous sentirez. Cela retire un tweak dont le
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.011|                    gain n'a jamais ete demontre sur une machine avec de
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.012|                    la RAM moderne et un SSD.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.013|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.014|  Cout            : Rien. Simple remise en ordre.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.015|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.016|  Defaut Windows  : 0.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.017|
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.018|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.019|    0                    : La valeur d'origine. Le code noyau et les
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.020|                           pilotes marques paginables peuvent etre ecrits
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.021|                           vers le fichier d'echange quand la memoire se
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.022|                           tend, puis relus a la demande. Le gestionnaire
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.023|                           de memoire decide page par page, selon la
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.024|                           pression reelle.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.025|    1                    : Le code noyau et les pilotes doivent rester
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.026|                           residents en RAM physique et ne sont jamais
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.027|                           pagines. La promesse des guides est d'eviter
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.028|                           une lecture disque lors d'un appel de pilote ;
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.029|                           en pratique, cela retire definitivement cette
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.030|                           RAM aux applications, ce qui, sur une machine a
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.031|                           SSD rapide, echange une certitude contre une
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.032|                           rarete. Microsoft demande 1 pendant un debogage
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.033|                           noyau en direct.
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.034|    absent               : Se comporte comme 0. Windows 11 livre la valeur
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.035|                           presente a 0 : son absence signifie
::T|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.036|                           generalement qu'un outil l'a supprimee.
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques. Le seul vrai argument pour
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.002|                         1 est le debogage noyau en direct, qui ne fait
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.003|                         partie d'aucun des cinq profils - et meme un
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.004|                         serveur ne gagne rien a epingler du code de
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.005|                         pilote que le gestionnaire de memoire n'aurait de
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.006|                         toute facon pas evince sous une pression normale.
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.007|
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.008|  Non verifie (en)  : The claim that value 0 is needed for crash dumps is
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.009|                      not something I can substantiate; Microsoft asks for
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.010|                      1 during live kernel debugging, which points the
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.011|                      other way.
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.012|
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.013|  Cible           : call :regset
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.014|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.015|                    Manager\Memory Management" "DisablePagingExecutive"
::X|FR|rad.disablepagingexec.016|                    REG_DWORD 0 (OPTY.bat 1866, in :reassert_defaults)
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.001|DISABLEPAGINGEXECUTIVE BACK TO 0
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.002|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.003|  What it is      : Decides whether kernel code and drivers may be paged
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.004|                    out of RAM to the pagefile.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.005|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.006|  Actual effect   : Writes 0, the shipped value, so the memory manager can
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.007|                    page them again when memory is tight.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.008|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.009|  Gain            : None you will feel. It removes a tweak whose
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.010|                    performance claim has never been demonstrated on a
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.011|                    machine with modern amounts of RAM and an SSD.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.012|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.013|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.014|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.015|  Windows default : 0.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.016|
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.017|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.018|    0                    : The shipped value. Kernel code and drivers that
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.019|                           are marked pageable may be written out to the
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.020|                           pagefile when memory gets tight, and read back
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.021|                           on demand. The memory manager decides, page by
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.022|                           page, based on actual pressure.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.023|    1                    : Kernel code and drivers must stay resident in
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.024|                           physical RAM and are never paged out. The
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.025|                           tweak-guide promise is that this avoids a disk
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.026|                           read on a driver call; what it actually does is
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.027|                           take that RAM away from applications
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.028|                           permanently, which on a machine with a fast SSD
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.029|                           trades a certainty for a rarity. Microsoft asks
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.030|                           for 1 during live kernel debugging.
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.031|    absent               : Behaves like 0. Windows 11 ships the value
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.032|                           present at 0, so absence usually means a tool
::T|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.033|                           deleted it.
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeros. The only real argument for 1
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.002|                       is live kernel debugging, which is not one of the
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.003|                       five profiles - and even a server gains nothing
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.004|                       from pinning driver code that the memory manager
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.005|                       was never going to evict under normal pressure.
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.006|
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.007|  Unverified      : The claim that value 0 is needed for crash dumps is
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.008|                    not something I can substantiate; Microsoft asks for 1
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.009|                    during live kernel debugging, which points the other
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.010|                    way.
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.011|
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.012|  Target          : call :regset
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.013|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.014|                    Manager\Memory Management" "DisablePagingExecutive"
::X|EN|rad.disablepagingexec.015|                    REG_DWORD 0 (OPTY.bat 1866, in :reassert_defaults)
::
:: ---- rad.clearpagefile (repair) ---------------------------------
::P|rad.clearpagefile|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.001|NE PLUS EFFACER LE FICHIER D'ECHANGE A L'ARRET
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.002|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.003|  Ce que c est    : ClearPageFileAtShutdown force Windows a reecrire tout
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.004|                    le fichier d'echange avec des zeros a chaque arret.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.005|                    Cela existe pour que les donnees sorties de la memoire
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.006|                    ne survivent pas sur le disque.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.007|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 0 : l'arret cesse de le faire. Rien n'est
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.009|                    supprime et le fichier d'echange lui-meme n'est pas
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.010|                    touche.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.011|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.012|  Gain            : La duree de l'arret cesse de dependre de la taille du
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.013|                    fichier d'echange. Sur un gros fichier et un disque
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.014|                    mecanique, c'est quelques secondes au lieu de longues
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.015|                    minutes ; sur NVMe, c'est plus modeste mais reel.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.016|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.017|  Cout            : Les donnees restees dans le fichier d'echange ne sont
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.018|                    plus effacees a l'arret. Si cela compte pour vous, le
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.019|                    chiffrement integral du disque resout le meme probleme
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.020|                    sans allonger chaque extinction.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.021|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.022|  Defaut Windows  : 0.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.023|
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.024|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.025|    0                    : La valeur d'origine. A l'arret, le fichier
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.026|                           d'echange est simplement laisse tel quel. La
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.027|                           duree de l'arret ne depend pas de sa taille.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.028|    1                    : Chaque arret reecrit tout le fichier d'echange
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.029|                           avec des zeros avant l'extinction. Le cout en
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.030|                           temps est proportionnel a la taille du fichier
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.031|                           et a la vitesse d'ecriture du disque : quelques
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.032|                           secondes sur un NVMe avec un petit fichier, de
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.033|                           longues minutes sur un disque mecanique avec un
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.034|                           gros. C'est une mesure de confidentialite, pas
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.035|                           de performance.
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.036|    absent               : Se comporte comme 0. Windows 11 la livre
::T|FR|rad.clearpagefile.037|                           presente a 0.
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques. Meme le profil SERVEUR n'y
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.002|                         gagne rien : une machine qu'on eteint rarement
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.003|                         paie le cout exactement au moment ou l'on veut la
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.004|                         voir redemarrer, et le chiffrement du disque
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.005|                         couvre correctement la menace. Refusez seulement
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.006|                         sur un poste partage traitant des donnees
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.007|                         sensibles sans aucun chiffrement.
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.008|
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.009|  Cible           : call :regset
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.010|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.011|                    Manager\Memory Management" "ClearPageFileAtShutdown"
::X|FR|rad.clearpagefile.012|                    REG_DWORD 0 (OPTY.bat 1867, in :reassert_defaults)
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.001|STOP ZEROING THE PAGEFILE AT SHUTDOWN
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.002|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.003|  What it is      : ClearPageFileAtShutdown makes Windows overwrite the
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.004|                    whole pagefile with zeros every time it shuts down. It
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.005|                    exists so that data paged out of memory does not
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.006|                    survive on disk.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.007|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.008|  Actual effect   : Writes 0, so shutdown stops doing that. Nothing is
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.009|                    deleted and the pagefile itself is untouched.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.010|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.011|  Gain            : Shutdown stops scaling with pagefile size. On a large
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.012|                    pagefile on a mechanical disk that is the difference
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.013|                    between seconds and many minutes; on an NVMe it is
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.014|                    smaller but still real.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.015|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.016|  Cost            : Data left in the pagefile is no longer wiped at
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.017|                    shutdown. If that matters to you, full-disk encryption
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.018|                    solves the same problem without adding time to every
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.019|                    shutdown.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.020|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.021|  Windows default : 0.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.022|
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.023|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.024|    0                    : The shipped value. At shutdown the pagefile is
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.025|                           simply left where it is. Shutdown time does not
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.026|                           depend on its size.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.027|    1                    : Every shutdown overwrites the entire pagefile
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.028|                           with zeros before powering off. The time cost
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.029|                           is proportional to the pagefile size and to how
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.030|                           fast the drive writes - seconds on an NVMe with
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.031|                           a small pagefile, many minutes on a mechanical
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.032|                           disk with a large one. It is a privacy measure,
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.033|                           not a performance one.
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.034|    absent               : Behaves like 0. Windows 11 ships it present at
::T|EN|rad.clearpagefile.035|                           0.
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeros. Even the SERVER profile gains
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.002|                       nothing from it: a machine you rarely shut down
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.003|                       pays the cost precisely when you most want it back
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.004|                       up, and disk encryption covers the threat properly.
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.005|                       Say no only on a shared machine handling sensitive
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.006|                       data with no disk encryption at all.
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.007|
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.008|  Target          : call :regset
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.009|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.010|                    Manager\Memory Management" "ClearPageFileAtShutdown"
::X|EN|rad.clearpagefile.011|                    REG_DWORD 0 (OPTY.bat 1867, in :reassert_defaults)
::
:: ---- rad.prefetcher (repair) ------------------------------------
::P|rad.prefetcher|3|3|3|3|3|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.001|ENABLEPREFETCHER REMIS A 3
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.002|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.003|  Ce que c est    : Le prefetch note ce qu'un programme lit au lancement
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.004|                    et le lit a l'avance la fois suivante. 3 signifie que
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.005|                    les traces applicatives et la trace de demarrage sont
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.006|                    conservees toutes les deux.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.007|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 3, reparant une machine ou un script de
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.009|                    bidouille avait mis 0 ou 1.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.010|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.011|  Gain            : Modeste et honnete : cela compte sur un disque
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.012|                    mecanique, ou une lecture anticipee en une passe
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.013|                    sequentielle bat des petites lectures eparpillees. Sur
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.014|                    NVMe l'ecart est faible et difficile a mesurer, mais
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.015|                    jamais negatif.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.016|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.017|  Cout            : Rien. Les fichiers .pf occupent quelques Mo dans
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.018|                    C:\Windows\Prefetch, plus un peu d'activite disque
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.019|                    dans les premieres secondes apres le demarrage, le
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.020|                    temps d'ecrire les traces.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.021|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.022|  Defaut Windows  : 3.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.023|
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.024|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.025|    3                    : Les deux traces actives. Windows note ce que
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.026|                           chaque programme lit pendant ses premieres
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.027|                           secondes dans un fichier .pf sous
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.028|                           C:\Windows\Prefetch, et note la sequence de
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.029|                           demarrage dans ReadyBoot, puis utilise les deux
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.030|                           pour lire a l'avance la fois suivante.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.031|    2                    : Prefetch de demarrage uniquement. Les
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.032|                           lancements d'applications ne sont plus traces,
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.033|                           leurs fichiers .pf cessent donc d'etre
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.034|                           rafraichis.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.035|    1                    : Prefetch des applications uniquement. La trace
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.036|                           de demarrage n'est plus entretenue : ReadyBoot
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.037|                           perd l'information d'ordre qui lui sert a
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.038|                           sequencer les lectures au demarrage.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.039|    0                    : Prefetch entierement desactive. C'est ce
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.040|                           qu'ecrit le vieux conseil « pas de prefetch sur
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.041|                           SSD ». Cela economise quelques Mo de fichiers
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.042|                           .pf et un peu d'activite disque dans les
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.043|                           premieres secondes apres le demarrage, et
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.044|                           aucune mesure publiee ne montre que cela
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.045|                           accelere quoi que ce soit.
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.046|    absent               : Windows 11 livre la valeur presente a 3. Si
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.047|                           elle manque, le gestionnaire de memoire retombe
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.048|                           sur son comportement interne plutot que sur un
::T|FR|rad.prefetcher.049|                           chiffre documente.
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq fois 3. Le comportement du prefetch ne
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.002|                         depend pas du role de la machine, et la colonne
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.003|                         portable est identique parce qu'une lecture
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.004|                         anticipee en une passe coute moins de temps
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.005|                         disque eveille que les lectures eparpillees
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.006|                         qu'elle remplace. Aucune bonne raison de rester a
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.007|                         0.
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.008|
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.009|  Non verifie (en)  : On NVMe the measurable benefit of prefetching is
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.010|                      small; the reason to keep 3 is that it is the state
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.011|                      Windows 11 expects, not a proven speed gain.
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.012|
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.013|  Cible           : call :regset
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.014|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.015|                    Manager\Memory Management\PrefetchParameters"
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.016|                    "EnablePrefetcher" REG_DWORD 3 (OPTY.bat 1871, in
::X|FR|rad.prefetcher.017|                    :reassert_defaults)
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.001|ENABLEPREFETCHER BACK TO 3
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.002|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.003|  What it is      : Prefetching records what a program reads at startup
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.004|                    and reads it ahead of time on the next launch. 3 means
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.005|                    both application traces and the boot trace are kept.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.006|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.007|  Actual effect   : Writes 3, repairing a machine where a tweak script set
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.008|                    0 or 1.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.009|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.010|  Gain            : Modest and honest: it matters on a mechanical disk,
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.011|                    where reading ahead in one sequential pass beats
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.012|                    scattered small reads. On NVMe the difference is small
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.013|                    and hard to measure, but it is never negative.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.014|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.015|  Cost            : Nothing. The .pf files use a few MB in
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.016|                    C:\Windows\Prefetch and a little disk activity in the
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.017|                    first seconds after boot while traces are written.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.018|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.019|  Windows default : 3.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.020|
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.021|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.022|    3                    : Both traces on. Windows records what each
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.023|                           program reads during its first seconds in a .pf
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.024|                           file under C:\Windows\Prefetch, and records the
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.025|                           boot sequence in ReadyBoot, then uses both to
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.026|                           read ahead next time.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.027|    2                    : Boot prefetching only. Application launches are
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.028|                           no longer traced, so their .pf files stop being
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.029|                           refreshed.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.030|    1                    : Application prefetching only. The boot trace is
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.031|                           no longer maintained, so ReadyBoot loses the
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.032|                           ordering information it uses to sequence the
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.033|                           startup reads.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.034|    0                    : Prefetching off entirely. This is what the old
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.035|                           'do not prefetch on an SSD' advice writes. It
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.036|                           saves a few MB of .pf files and stops a little
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.037|                           disk activity in the first seconds after boot,
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.038|                           and no published measurement shows it making
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.039|                           anything faster.
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.040|    absent               : Windows 11 ships the value present at 3. If it
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.041|                           is missing, the memory manager falls back to
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.042|                           its built-in behaviour rather than to a
::T|EN|rad.prefetcher.043|                           documented number.
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical threes. Prefetch behaviour does not
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.002|                       depend on the machine's job, and the laptop column
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.003|                       is the same because reading ahead in one pass costs
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.004|                       less drive-idle time than the scattered reads it
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.005|                       replaces. There is no good reason to run 0.
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.006|
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.007|  Unverified      : On NVMe the measurable benefit of prefetching is
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.008|                    small; the reason to keep 3 is that it is the state
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.009|                    Windows 11 expects, not a proven speed gain.
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.010|
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.011|  Target          : call :regset
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.012|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.013|                    Manager\Memory Management\PrefetchParameters"
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.014|                    "EnablePrefetcher" REG_DWORD 3 (OPTY.bat 1871, in
::X|EN|rad.prefetcher.015|                    :reassert_defaults)
::
:: ---- rad.superfetch.killkey (repair) ----------------------------
::P|rad.superfetch.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.001|SUPPRIMER LA VALEUR HERITEE ENABLESUPERFETCH
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.002|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.003|  Ce que c est    : Une valeur de l'epoque Windows 7 dans la cle
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.004|                    PrefetchParameters, que Windows 11 ne cree jamais.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.005|                    C'est SysMain qui gere ce comportement aujourd'hui.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.006|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.007|  Effet reel      : La supprime si elle existe, au lieu d'y ecrire un
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.008|                    chiffre, car ecrire un chiffre inventerait un etat que
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.009|                    le systeme n'a pas.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.010|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.011|  Gain            : Aucun effet fonctionnel, et cette fiche ne pretendra
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.012|                    pas le contraire. Cela retire une valeur trompeuse
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.013|                    pour quiconque lit la base de registre, outils
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.014|                    compris.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.015|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.016|  Cout            : Rien. Simple remise en ordre.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.017|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.018|  Defaut Windows  : Absente.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.019|
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.020|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.021|    DELETE               : La valeur est supprimee. Windows 11 ne la cree
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.022|                           jamais, et le type de demarrage de SysMain est
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.023|                           desormais le seul reglage qui gouverne
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.024|                           reellement ce comportement.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.025|    0                    : La valeur « Superfetch desactive » de l'epoque
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.026|                           Windows 7. Sous Windows 11, l'ecrire invente un
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.027|                           etat que le systeme n'a pas et laisse le
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.028|                           registre en contradiction avec la configuration
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.029|                           du service.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.030|    1, 2 or 3            : Les modes hérités - demarrage seul,
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.031|                           applications seules, les deux. Meme histoire :
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.032|                           ils appartiennent a un composant que Windows 11
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.033|                           a remplace par SysMain.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.034|    absent               : L'etat correct et celui d'un Windows 11 propre.
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.035|                           Cette cle ne contient d'origine
::T|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.036|                           qu'EnablePrefetcher, rien d'autre.
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a DELETE, WINDOWS
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.002|                         compris, parce que l'absence EST ce que livre
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.003|                         Windows. Si vous voulez couper SysMain sur une
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.004|                         machine, passez par le type de demarrage du
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.005|                         service : c'est le reglage que Windows lit a coup
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.006|                         sur.
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.007|
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.008|  Cible           : call :killkey
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.009|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.010|                    Manager\Memory Management\PrefetchParameters"
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.011|                    "EnableSuperfetch" (OPTY.bat 1874, in
::X|FR|rad.superfetch.killkey.012|                    :reassert_defaults)
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.001|DELETE THE LEGACY ENABLESUPERFETCH VALUE
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.002|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.003|  What it is      : A Windows 7 era value in the PrefetchParameters key
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.004|                    that Windows 11 never creates. SysMain owns this
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.005|                    behaviour now.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.006|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes it if present rather than writing a number,
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.008|                    because writing one would invent a state the OS does
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.009|                    not have.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.010|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.011|  Gain            : None functional, and this card will not claim
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.012|                    otherwise. It removes a value that misleads anyone
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.013|                    reading the registry, tools included.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.014|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.015|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.016|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.017|  Windows default : Absent.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.018|
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.019|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.020|    DELETE               : The value is removed. Windows 11 never creates
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.021|                           it, and SysMain's start type is the only knob
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.022|                           that actually governs this behaviour now.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.023|    0                    : The Windows 7 era 'Superfetch off' value. On
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.024|                           Windows 11 writing it invents a state the OS
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.025|                           does not have, and it leaves the registry
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.026|                           contradicting the service configuration.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.027|    1, 2 or 3            : The legacy modes - boot only, applications
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.028|                           only, both. Same story: they belong to a
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.029|                           component Windows 11 replaced with SysMain.
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.030|    absent               : The correct state and what a clean Windows 11
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.031|                           has. That key ships holding EnablePrefetcher
::T|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.032|                           and nothing else.
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, including WINDOWS,
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.002|                       because absence IS what Windows ships. If you want
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.003|                       SysMain off on a particular machine, do it through
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.004|                       the service start type - that is the knob Windows
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.005|                       definitely still reads.
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.006|
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.007|  Target          : call :killkey
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.008|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.009|                    Manager\Memory Management\PrefetchParameters"
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.010|                    "EnableSuperfetch" (OPTY.bat 1874, in
::X|EN|rad.superfetch.killkey.011|                    :reassert_defaults)
::
:: ---- rad.pagefile.repair (risky) -------------------------------
::P|rad.pagefile.repair|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.001|RETABLIR UN FICHIER D'ECHANGE S'IL A ETE SUPPRIME
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.002|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.003|  Ce que c est    : PagingFiles est la valeur que le noyau lit reellement
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.004|                    pour savoir s'il existe un fichier d'echange, et ou.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.005|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile n'est que la case a cocher
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.006|                    affichee par l'interface de gestion.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.007|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.008|  Effet reel      : Lit d'abord PagingFiles et n'agit que s'il est absent
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.009|                    ou vide. Ecrit alors un fichier gere par le systeme
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.010|                    sur le disque systeme et coche la case. Redemarrage
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.011|                    requis. Notez le detail d'analyse que le script
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.012|                    commente : tokens=2* est utilise parce qu'une entree
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.013|                    REG_MULTI_SZ compte plusieurs mots, et la forme
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.014|                    tokens=3 employee ailleurs pour les DWORD rapportait «
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.015|                    0 0 » comme etant tout le fichier d'echange.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.016|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.017|  Gain            : Corrige les plantages par manque de memoire et les
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.018|                    applications qui meurent sans raison visible sur une
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.019|                    machine ou quelqu'un a coupe la memoire virtuelle pour
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.020|                    gagner de la place. Cela retablit aussi l'ecriture de
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.021|                    MEMORY.DMP lors d'un bugcheck, qui exige un fichier
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.022|                    d'echange sur le disque systeme.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.023|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.024|  Cout            : De l'espace disque pour le fichier - quelques Go,
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.025|                    dimensionnes par Windows. Un fichier d'echange fixe
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.026|                    choisi volontairement n'est jamais ecrase : le garde-
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.027|                    fou lit la valeur avant d'ecrire quoi que ce soit.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.028|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.029|  Defaut Windows  : Un fichier d'echange gere par le systeme sur le disque
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.030|                    systeme.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.031|
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.033|    APPLY                : Si et seulement si PagingFiles est absent ou
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.034|                           vide, il est ecrit comme «
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.035|                           %SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0 0 » - le couple 0
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.036|                           0 signifiant taille initiale et maximale gerees
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.037|                           par le systeme - et la case
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.038|                           AutomaticManagedPagefile est mise a 1. Effectif
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.039|                           au prochain redemarrage. Si PagingFiles
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.040|                           contient deja quelque chose, l'etape journalise
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.041|                           ce qu'elle a trouve et n'ecrit rien.
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.042|    SKIP                 : Une machine dont la memoire virtuelle est
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.043|                           coupee le reste : les applications continuent
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.044|                           de mourir sur des echecs de limite de
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.045|                           validation qui ressemblent a des plantages
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.046|                           aleatoires, et aucun dump noyau ne peut etre
::T|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.047|                           ecrit lors d'un bugcheck.
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a APPLY, WINDOWS
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.002|                         compris, parce qu'un fichier d'echange est ce que
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.003|                         livre Windows et que l'etape ne fait rien sur une
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.004|                         machine qui en a deja un. Personne, sur aucun de
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.005|                         ces cinq profils, ne gagne a n'en avoir aucun -
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.006|                         refusez seulement si vous tournez sans en
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.007|                         connaissance de cause et acceptez la perte des
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.008|                         dumps.
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.009|
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.010|  Non verifie (en)  : The exact stock PagingFiles string varies by
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.011|                      installation; '%SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0 0' is a
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.012|                      valid system-managed equivalent, not necessarily
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.013|                      byte-identical to the one your image shipped.
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.014|
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.015|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 1884-1887, in :reassert_defaults - reads
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.016|                    PagingFiles under
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.017|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.018|                    Manager\Memory Management with tokens=2*, and only
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.019|                    when it is missing or empty writes PagingFiles as
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.020|                    REG_MULTI_SZ "%SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0 0" plus
::X|FR|rad.pagefile.repair.021|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile=1
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.001|RESTORE A PAGEFILE IF VIRTUAL MEMORY WAS TURNED OFF
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.002|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.003|  What it is      : PagingFiles is the value the kernel actually reads to
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.004|                    know whether there is a pagefile at all, and where.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.005|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile is only the checkbox the
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.006|                    management UI shows.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.007|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.008|  Actual effect   : Reads PagingFiles first and acts only when it is
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.009|                    missing or empty. Then it writes a system-managed
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.010|                    pagefile on the system drive and ticks the checkbox.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.011|                    Needs a reboot. Note the parsing detail the script
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.012|                    comments on: tokens=2* is used because a REG_MULTI_SZ
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.013|                    entry is multi-word, and the tokens=3 form used
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.014|                    elsewhere for DWORDs reported '0 0' as the whole
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.015|                    pagefile.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.016|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.017|  Gain            : Fixes out-of-memory crashes and applications dying for
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.018|                    no visible reason on a machine where someone disabled
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.019|                    virtual memory to save disk space. It also restores
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.020|                    the ability to write MEMORY.DMP on a bugcheck, which
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.021|                    requires a pagefile on the system drive.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.022|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.023|  Cost            : Disk space for the pagefile - a few GB, sized by
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.024|                    Windows. A deliberate fixed-size pagefile is never
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.025|                    touched, because the guard reads the value before
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.026|                    writing anything.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.027|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.028|  Windows default : A system-managed pagefile on the system drive.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.029|
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.030|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.031|    APPLY                : If and only if PagingFiles is absent or empty,
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.032|                           it is written as '%SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.033|                           0' - the 0 0 pair meaning system-managed
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.034|                           initial and maximum size - and the
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.035|                           AutomaticManagedPagefile checkbox is set to 1.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.036|                           Effective at the next reboot. If PagingFiles
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.037|                           already holds anything, the step logs what it
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.038|                           found and writes nothing.
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.039|    SKIP                 : A machine with virtual memory switched off
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.040|                           stays that way: applications keep dying on
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.041|                           commit-limit failures that look like random
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.042|                           crashes, and no kernel dump can be written on a
::T|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.043|                           bugcheck.
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical APPLY columns, WINDOWS included,
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.002|                       because a pagefile is what Windows ships and the
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.003|                       step is a no-op on any machine that already has
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.004|                       one. Nobody, on any of these five profiles,
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.005|                       benefits from having no pagefile at all - decline
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.006|                       only if you run without one knowingly and accept
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.007|                       the crash-dump loss.
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.008|
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.009|  Unverified      : The exact stock PagingFiles string varies by
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.010|                    installation; '%SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0 0' is a
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.011|                    valid system-managed equivalent, not necessarily byte-
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.012|                    identical to the one your image shipped.
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.013|
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.014|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1884-1887, in :reassert_defaults - reads
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.015|                    PagingFiles under
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.016|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.017|                    Manager\Memory Management with tokens=2*, and only
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.018|                    when it is missing or empty writes PagingFiles as
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.019|                    REG_MULTI_SZ "%SystemDrive%\pagefile.sys 0 0" plus
::X|EN|rad.pagefile.repair.020|                    AutomaticManagedPagefile=1
::
:: ---- rad.mm.leftover.killkeys (repair) --------------------------
::P|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.001|RETIRER DIX FORCAGES DU GESTIONNAIRE DE MEMOIRE
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.002|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.003|  Ce que c est    : Dix valeurs que Windows ne cree jamais, dont
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.004|                    MoveImages, PoolUsageMaximum, IoPageLockLimit et
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.005|                    DisablePageCombining. Chacune vient d'un vieux guide
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.006|                    de reglage.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.007|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.008|  Effet reel      : Les supprime toutes. Ecrire zero ne retablirait pas le
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.009|                    defaut : seule l'absence le fait. SessionPoolSize et
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.010|                    SessionViewSize ne sont jamais touchees, car le
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.011|                    programme d'installation de Windows y ecrit de vraies
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.012|                    valeurs.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.013|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.014|  Gain            : Une vraie reparation de securite : MoveImages=0
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.015|                    empeche la relocalisation des images et affaiblit donc
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.016|                    l'ASLR. Les neuf autres cessent simplement de brider
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.017|                    un gestionnaire de memoire qui se dimensionne mieux
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.018|                    qu'un chiffre fige.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.019|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.020|  Cout            : Rien. Aucune de ces valeurs n'a de gain demontre sur
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.021|                    un Windows 11 64 bits.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.022|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.023|  Defaut Windows  : Les dix absentes.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.024|
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.026|    DELETE               : Les dix valeurs sont supprimees, seul moyen de
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.027|                           retablir le defaut puisque Windows livre cette
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.028|                           cle sans aucune d'elles. MoveImages disparue,
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.029|                           la relocalisation des images redevient normale
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.030|                           et l'ASLR garde son entropie. Les limites de
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.031|                           pool et de cache disparues, le gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.032|                           memoire dimensionne ces pools dynamiquement,
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.033|                           comme prevu.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.034|    0                    : Ce qu'ecrivent generalement les scripts de
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.035|                           bidouille, et ce n'est PAS le defaut : c'est un
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.036|                           troisieme etat. MoveImages=0 en particulier
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.037|                           demande au chargeur de garder les images a leur
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.038|                           adresse de base preferee, ce qui annule la
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.039|                           partie relocalisation de l'ASLR : vraie
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.040|                           regression de securite. Les limites de pool a 0
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.041|                           ne signifient pas « illimite », c'est une
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.042|                           valeur explicite que le gestionnaire de memoire
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.043|                           doit desormais respecter.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.044|    any non-zero number  : Un plafond ou une preference imposes au
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.045|                           gestionnaire de memoire au lieu de le laisser
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.046|                           s'adapter. IoPageLockLimit, PoolUsageMaximum,
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.047|                           les quatre valeurs *Limit et
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.048|                           AllocationPreference etaient des reglages pour
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.049|                           des problemes d'espace d'adressage 32 bits
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.050|                           qu'un Windows 11 64 bits n'a pas.
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.051|    absent               : L'etat d'origine pour les dix, et ce que cette
::T|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.052|                           etape retablit.
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a DELETE. Ce ne sont pas
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.002|                         des reglages lies a l'usage mais des reliquats,
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.003|                         et l'absence est correcte sur toutes les
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.004|                         machines, profil WINDOWS compris, ou l'absence
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.005|                         est litteralement l'etat livre.
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.006|
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.007|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 1899-1900, in :reassert_defaults - for %%V in
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.008|                    (DisablePageCombining IoPageLockLimit PoolUsageMaximum
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.009|                    MoveImages SystemCacheDirtyPageThreshold
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.010|                    NonPagedPoolLimit PagedPoolLimit SystemCacheLimit
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.011|                    SystemPtesLimit AllocationPreference) do call :killkey
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.012|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.013|                    Manager\Memory Management" "%%V". SessionPoolSize and
::X|FR|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.014|                    SessionViewSize are deliberately excluded.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.001|REMOVE TEN MEMORY-MANAGER OVERRIDES
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.002|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.003|  What it is      : Ten values that Windows ships without, including
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.004|                    MoveImages, PoolUsageMaximum, IoPageLockLimit and
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.005|                    DisablePageCombining. Every one of them is something
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.006|                    an old tuning guide told people to add.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.007|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes all ten. Writing zero would not restore the
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.009|                    default - only absence does. SessionPoolSize and
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.010|                    SessionViewSize are never touched, because Windows
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.011|                    Setup writes real values there.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.012|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.013|  Gain            : One genuine security repair: MoveImages=0 disables
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.014|                    image relocation and therefore weakens ASLR. The other
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.015|                    nine simply stop overriding a memory manager that
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.016|                    sizes itself better than a fixed number can.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.017|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.018|  Cost            : Nothing. None of these values has a demonstrated
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.019|                    performance benefit on 64-bit Windows 11.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.020|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.021|  Windows default : All ten absent.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.022|
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.023|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.024|    DELETE               : All ten values are removed, which is the only
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.025|                           way to restore the default, because Windows
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.026|                           ships this key without any of them. MoveImages
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.027|                           gone means image relocation works normally
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.028|                           again and ASLR keeps its entropy. The pool and
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.029|                           cache limits gone means the memory manager
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.030|                           sizes those pools dynamically as it was
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.031|                           designed to.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.032|    0                    : What tweak scripts usually write, and it is NOT
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.033|                           the default - it is a third state. MoveImages=0
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.034|                           in particular tells the loader to keep images
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.035|                           at their preferred base address, which defeats
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.036|                           the relocation part of ASLR and is a real
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.037|                           security regression. The pool limits at 0 are
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.038|                           not 'unlimited', they are an explicit value the
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.039|                           memory manager now has to honour.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.040|    any non-zero number  : A hard cap or preference the memory manager
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.041|                           must obey instead of adapting. IoPageLockLimit,
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.042|                           PoolUsageMaximum, the four *Limit values and
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.043|                           AllocationPreference were tuning knobs for
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.044|                           32-bit address-space problems that a 64-bit
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.045|                           Windows 11 does not have.
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.046|    absent               : The shipped state for all ten, and what this
::T|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.047|                           step restores.
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns. These are not use-
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.002|                       case settings - they are leftovers, and absence is
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.003|                       correct on every machine including the WINDOWS
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.004|                       profile, where absence is literally the shipped
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.005|                       state.
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.006|
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.007|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1899-1900, in :reassert_defaults - for %%V in
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.008|                    (DisablePageCombining IoPageLockLimit PoolUsageMaximum
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.009|                    MoveImages SystemCacheDirtyPageThreshold
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.010|                    NonPagedPoolLimit PagedPoolLimit SystemCacheLimit
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.011|                    SystemPtesLimit AllocationPreference) do call :killkey
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.012|                    "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.013|                    Manager\Memory Management" "%%V". SessionPoolSize and
::X|EN|rad.mm.leftover.killkeys.014|                    SessionViewSize are deliberately excluded.
::
:: ---- rad.mm.zero.values (repair) --------------------------------
::P|rad.mm.zero.values|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.001|TROIS VALEURS MEMOIRE REMISES A 0
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.002|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.003|  Ce que c est    : LargeSystemCache, SecondLevelDataCache et SystemPages
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.004|                    sont bien livrees presentes sous Windows 11, toutes
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.005|                    les trois a 0. C'est ce qui les distingue des dix
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.006|                    valeurs que l'etape voisine supprime.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.007|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.008|  Effet reel      : Reecrit 0 dans chacune, annulant les retouches du type
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.009|                    LargeSystemCache=1 recommandees par les vieux guides.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.010|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.011|  Gain            : Retirer un forcage que le gestionnaire de memoire doit
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.012|                    ensuite contourner. Le cas le plus net est
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.013|                    LargeSystemCache=1, qui privilegie le cache fichier
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.014|                    sur les applications et augmente donc la pagination
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.015|                    sur un poste de travail. Sur une machine jamais
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.016|                    modifiee, aucun changement observable.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.017|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.018|  Cout            : Rien. Simple remise aux valeurs d'usine.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.019|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.020|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois presentes a 0.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.021|
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.022|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.023|    0                    : L'etat client d'origine pour les trois.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.024|                           LargeSystemCache=0 privilegie les espaces de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.025|                           travail des processus sur le cache fichier
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.026|                           systeme. SecondLevelDataCache=0 signifie
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.027|                           prendre la taille de L2 rapportee par le
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.028|                           processeur plutot qu'un forcage. SystemPages=0
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.029|                           signifie laisser le gestionnaire de memoire
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.030|                           dimensionner lui-meme le pool de PTE systeme.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.031|    LargeSystemCache = 1 : Demande au gestionnaire de memoire de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.032|                           privilegier le cache fichier systeme sur les
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.033|                           espaces de travail des applications, l'ancien
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.034|                           reglage serveur de fichiers. Sur un poste de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.035|                           travail, cela signifie que les applications
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.036|                           sont rognees plus tot, donc plus de pagination,
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.037|                           pas moins. Sur Windows 11 64 bits, l'ecart
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.038|                           pratique est bien plus faible que ne le disent
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.039|                           les guides.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.040|    SecondLevelDataCache = a size in KB :
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.041|        Force la taille de cache L2 utilisee par le gestionnaire de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.042|        memoire pour la coloration de pages. Cela existait pour de vieux
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.043|        processeurs dont Windows ne savait pas lire le cache. Sur du
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.044|        materiel moderne, le firmware la rapporte correctement et tout
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.045|        chiffre saisi ici est une supposition qui remplace un fait.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.046|    SystemPages = a fixed count or 0xFFFFFFFF :
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.047|        Fige le nombre d'entrees de table de pages systeme au lieu de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.048|        laisser le gestionnaire agrandir le pool. C'etait un contournement
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.049|        de l'espace d'adressage 32 bits ; en x64, l'espace est assez vaste
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.050|        pour que figer ce nombre ne puisse que le contraindre.
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.051|    absent               : Contrairement aux dix valeurs supprimees
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.052|                           ailleurs, ces trois-la sont bien livrees
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.053|                           presentes a 0 sous Windows 11 - raison precise
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.054|                           pour laquelle la reparation ecrit 0 au lieu de
::T|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.055|                           les supprimer.
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques, et la colonne SERVEUR
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.002|                         merite son explication : LargeSystemCache=1 etait
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.003|                         un reglage de serveur de fichiers pour Windows 32
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.004|                         bits servant des fichiers depuis des disques
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.005|                         mecaniques. Une machine moderne qui heberge Plex,
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.006|                         des torrents ou des serveurs de jeu fait tourner
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.007|                         des applications, elle ne sert pas de cache de
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.008|                         fichiers SMB : 0 est donc juste la aussi.
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.009|
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.010|  Cible           : OPTY.bat 1903-1905, in :reassert_defaults - call
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.011|                    :regset on LargeSystemCache, SecondLevelDataCache and
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.012|                    SystemPages, each REG_DWORD 0, under
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.013|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.mm.zero.values.014|                    Manager\Memory Management
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.001|THREE MEMORY-MANAGER VALUES BACK TO 0
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.002|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.003|  What it is      : LargeSystemCache, SecondLevelDataCache and SystemPages
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.004|                    do ship present on Windows 11, all three at 0. That is
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.005|                    what separates them from the ten values the
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.006|                    neighbouring step deletes.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.007|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.008|  Actual effect   : Rewrites 0 to each, undoing edits such as
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.009|                    LargeSystemCache=1 that old tuning guides recommend.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.010|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.011|  Gain            : Removing an override the memory manager then has to
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.012|                    work around. The clearest case is LargeSystemCache=1,
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.013|                    which favours the file cache over applications and
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.014|                    therefore increases paging on a desktop. On a machine
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.015|                    that was never edited, no observable change.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.016|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.017|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair to the shipped numbers.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.018|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.019|  Windows default : All three present with 0.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.020|
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.021|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.022|    0                    : The shipped client state for all three.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.023|                           LargeSystemCache=0 favours process working sets
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.024|                           over the system file cache.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.025|                           SecondLevelDataCache=0 means take the L2 size
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.026|                           the processor reports instead of an override.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.027|                           SystemPages=0 means let the memory manager size
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.028|                           the system PTE pool itself.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.029|    LargeSystemCache = 1 : Tells the memory manager to favour the system
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.030|                           file cache over application working sets, the
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.031|                           old file-server setting. On a desktop it means
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.032|                           applications get trimmed sooner, so more
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.033|                           paging, not less. On 64-bit Windows 11 the
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.034|                           practical difference is much smaller than the
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.035|                           guides imply.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.036|    SecondLevelDataCache = a size in KB :
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.037|        Overrides the L2 cache size the memory manager uses for page
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.038|        colouring. It existed for old processors whose cache size Windows
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.039|        could not read. On anything modern the firmware reports it
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.040|        correctly and any number you type here is a guess replacing a
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.041|        fact.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.042|    SystemPages = a fixed count or 0xFFFFFFFF :
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.043|        Pins the number of system page table entries instead of letting
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.044|        the manager grow the pool. It was a 32-bit address-space
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.045|        workaround; on x64 the space is large enough that fixing the
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.046|        number can only constrain it.
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.047|    absent               : Unlike the ten killkey values, these three DO
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.048|                           ship present at 0 on Windows 11 - which is
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.049|                           exactly why the repair writes 0 rather than
::T|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.050|                           deleting them.
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.001|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeros, and the SERVER column
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.002|                       deserves its explanation: LargeSystemCache=1 was a
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.003|                       file-server setting for 32-bit Windows serving
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.004|                       files from spinning disks. A modern box hosting
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.005|                       Plex, torrents or game servers is running
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.006|                       applications, not acting as an SMB file cache, so 0
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.007|                       is right there too.
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.008|
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.009|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1903-1905, in :reassert_defaults - call
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.010|                    :regset on LargeSystemCache, SecondLevelDataCache and
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.011|                    SystemPages, each REG_DWORD 0, under
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.012|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.mm.zero.values.013|                    Manager\Memory Management
