:: ---- rp.enable.system.protection (preference) -----------------------
::P|rp.enable.system.protection|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|SKIP|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.001|ACTIVER LA PROTECTION DU SYSTEME SUR C:
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.002|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.003|  Ce que c est    : La protection du systeme est la fonction Windows
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.004|                    derriere les points de restauration. Quand elle est
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.005|                    desactivee, aucun point ne peut exister : ni celui
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.006|                    qu'OPTY veut prendre, ni celui que Windows Update
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.007|                    prend avant d'installer un pilote.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.008|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.009|  Effet reel      : Lance Enable-ComputerRestore sur le disque systeme.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.010|                    Sur une machine ou elle etait coupee, c'est un
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.011|                    changement permanent, car OPTY ne propose pas de la
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.012|                    redesactiver. Cela se fait dans Proprietes systeme,
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.013|                    Protection du systeme, Configurer.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.014|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.015|  Gain            : Vous obtenez une annulation pour tout ce qu'OPTY,
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.016|                    Windows Update et les installeurs de pilotes feront
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.017|                    ensuite. Sans elle, l'etape du point de restauration
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.018|                    echoue en silence et rien de ce que fait ce script
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.019|                    n'est annulable.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.020|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.021|  Cout            : De l'espace disque occupe en permanence sur C: : le
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.022|                    quota de cliches par defaut represente quelques pour
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.023|                    cent du volume, donc typiquement plusieurs Go sur un
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.024|                    gros SSD. Aucun cout mesurable en CPU ni en vitesse.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.025|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.026|  Defaut Windows  : Une installation propre de Windows 11 livre la
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.027|                    protection du systeme DESACTIVEE pour le disque
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.028|                    systeme. Beaucoup d'images constructeur la livrent
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.029|                    activee. Sur une machine neuve, c'est donc un
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.030|                    changement et pas une reparation.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.031|
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.033|    APPLY                : Enable-ComputerRestore s'execute sur le disque
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.034|                           systeme. Windows reserve de l'espace de cliches
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.035|                           instantanes sur C: et reautorise les points de
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.036|                           restauration - celui qu'OPTY cree comme ceux
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.037|                           que Windows Update cree avant une installation
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.038|                           de pilote. OPTY ne la redesactive jamais.
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.039|    SKIP                 : La protection reste exactement telle quelle. Si
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.040|                           elle etait coupee, le point de restauration qui
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.041|                           suit echoue en silence et rien de ce que font
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.042|                           ensuite OPTY, Windows Update ou un installeur
::T|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.043|                           de pilote n'est annulable.
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre profils disent APPLY parce que la valeur
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.002|                         d'une annulation ne depend pas de l'usage de la
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.003|                         machine, et que le cout en disque est faible face
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.004|                         a celui d'un etat de registre irrattrapable. Le
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.005|                         profil WINDOWS fait exception pour une raison
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.006|                         precise et non stylistique : sur une installation
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.007|                         propre, la protection desactivee EST l'etat
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.008|                         livre, et ce profil sert a le preserver. Refusez
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.009|                         quel que soit le profil si votre disque systeme
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.010|                         est vraiment petit, moins de 128 Go environ, ou
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.011|                         si vous faites deja des images disque completes.
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.012|
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.013|  Cible           : powershell -NoProfile -Command "Enable-ComputerRestore
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.014|                    -Drive '%SystemDrive%\'" (OPTY.bat 3999, first line of
::X|FR|rp.enable.system.protection.015|                    :restore_point)
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.001|TURN ON SYSTEM PROTECTION FOR C:
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.002|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.003|  What it is      : System Protection is the Windows feature behind
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.004|                    restore points. When it is off, no restore point can
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.005|                    exist - not the one OPTY wants to take, and not the
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.006|                    one Windows Update takes before a driver install.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.007|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.008|  Actual effect   : Runs Enable-ComputerRestore on the system drive. On a
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.009|                    machine that had it off this is a permanent change,
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.010|                    because OPTY offers no way to turn it back off. That
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.011|                    lives in System Properties, System Protection,
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.012|                    Configure.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.013|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.014|  Gain            : You get an undo for everything OPTY, Windows Update
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.015|                    and driver installers do afterwards. Without it the
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.016|                    restore-point step fails silently and nothing this
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.017|                    script does can be rolled back.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.018|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.019|  Cost            : Ongoing disk space on C: - the default shadow-copy
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.020|                    quota is a few percent of the volume, so typically
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.021|                    several GB on a large SSD. No measurable CPU or speed
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.022|                    cost.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.023|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.024|  Windows default : A clean Windows 11 install ships System Protection OFF
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.025|                    for the system drive. Many OEM images ship it ON. On a
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.026|                    fresh machine this is therefore a change, not a
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.027|                    repair.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.028|
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.029|  Possible values:
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.030|    APPLY                : Enable-ComputerRestore runs on the system
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.031|                           drive. Windows allocates shadow-copy storage on
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.032|                           C: and starts allowing restore points again -
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.033|                           both the one OPTY takes and the ones Windows
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.034|                           Update takes before a driver install. OPTY
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.035|                           never turns it back off.
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.036|    SKIP                 : Protection stays exactly as it was. If it was
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.037|                           off, the restore point that follows fails
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.038|                           silently and there is no rollback for anything
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.039|                           OPTY, Windows Update or a driver installer does
::T|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.040|                           afterwards.
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.001|  Why these profiles : Four profiles say APPLY because the value of an
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.002|                       undo does not depend on what the machine is for,
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.003|                       and the disk cost is small next to the cost of an
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.004|                       unrecoverable registry state. The WINDOWS profile
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.005|                       is the exception, and for a precise reason rather
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.006|                       than a stylistic one: on a clean install,
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.007|                       protection off IS the shipped state, and that
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.008|                       profile exists to preserve it. Decline under any
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.009|                       profile if your system drive is genuinely small,
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.010|                       under about 128 GB, or if you already keep full
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.011|                       disk images.
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.012|
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.013|  Target          : powershell -NoProfile -Command "Enable-ComputerRestore
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.014|                    -Drive '%SystemDrive%\'" (OPTY.bat 3999, first line of
::X|EN|rp.enable.system.protection.015|                    :restore_point)
