:: ---- driverstore.delete (risky) --------------------------------
::P|driverstore.delete|DELETE|DELETE|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.001|SUPPRIMER LES PILOTES REMPLACES DU MAGASIN
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.002|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.003|  Ce que c est    : Windows conserve tous les paquets de pilotes tiers
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.004|                    (oemNN.inf) jamais installes, anciennes versions
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.005|                    comprises, et ne fait jamais le menage. Ceci supprime
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.006|                    toutes les versions sauf la plus recente de chaque
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.007|                    famille d'INF.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.008|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.009|  Effet reel      : Regroupe les paquets par nom d'INF d'origine, trie
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.010|                    chaque famille par date de pilote en gardant la plus
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.011|                    recente, puis appelle pnputil /delete-driver sur le
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.012|                    reste. Pas de /force : tout ce qui sert encore survit.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.013|                    L'option 1 du menu fait la meme analyse et ne supprime
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.014|                    rien.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.015|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.016|  Gain            : De l'espace disque reel et mesurable sur le disque
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.017|                    systeme - le DriverStore est l'un des plus gros gains
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.018|                    de place legitimes sur une machine qui a enchaine les
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.019|                    mises a jour de pilote GPU ou chipset. Rien ne devient
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.020|                    plus rapide.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.021|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.022|  Cout            : La restauration du pilote precedent n'est plus
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.023|                    disponible pour les versions supprimees. Si un nouveau
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.024|                    pilote se revele moins bon, le seul retour est de
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.025|                    retelecharger l'ancien chez le fabricant. Les
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.026|                    peripheriques rarement branches - station d'accueil,
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.027|                    imprimante, carte d'acquisition - peuvent exiger un
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.028|                    telechargement de pilote la prochaine fois. Rien ne se
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.029|                    regenere tout seul ici : c'est pourquoi cette etape
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.030|                    sort de la regle de nettoyage habituelle.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.031|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.032|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - l'accumulation perpetuelle des paquets
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.033|                    est le comportement livre par Windows, qui ne les
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.034|                    elague jamais lui-meme.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.035|
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.036|  Valeurs possibles :
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.037|    DELETE               : pnputil /delete-driver s'execute une fois par
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.038|                           paquet obsolete. /force n'est volontairement
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.039|                           jamais utilise : un paquet qui sert encore a un
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.040|                           peripherique present est refuse et conserve, et
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.041|                           le journal le dit. Libere typiquement de
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.042|                           quelques centaines de Mo a plusieurs Go dans
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.043|                           C:\Windows\System32\DriverStore.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.044|    SKIP                 : Rien n'est supprime. Windows n'elague jamais le
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.045|                           DriverStore lui-meme : toutes les versions de
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.046|                           tous les pilotes tiers jamais installes restent
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.047|                           sur le disque systeme.
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.048|    ANALYSE              : L'option 1 du menu liste exactement les paquets
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.049|                           qui partiraient et ne supprime rien. C'est la
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.050|                           premiere reponse honnete sur une machine dont
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.051|                           vous n'etes pas sur, et cela prend quelques
::T|FR|driverstore.delete.052|                           secondes.
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.001|  Pourquoi ces profils : Celle-ci se scinde vraiment, selon l'origine des
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.002|                         paquets. JEU et SERVEUR passent a DELETE parce
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.003|                         que ces machines empilent les revisions de
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.004|                         pilotes GPU et chipset, la ou se trouvent
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.005|                         reellement les gigaoctets, et que ces pilotes
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.006|                         sont a un telechargement public de distance.
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.007|                         BUREAU passe a SKIP parce que presque rien ne s'y
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.008|                         accumule : vous echangeriez la restauration de
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.009|                         pilote contre quelques centaines de Mo. PORTABLE
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.010|                         passe a SKIP parce que les paquets constructeur
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.011|                         d'une station d'accueil, d'un lecteur
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.012|                         d'empreintes ou d'un chipset specifique sont
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.013|                         justement ceux qu'on retrouve difficilement.
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.014|                         WINDOWS passe a SKIP parce que Windows n'elague
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.015|                         jamais. Sur n'importe quel profil, lancez d'abord
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.016|                         l'option 1 et lisez la liste.
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.017|
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.018|  Cible           : :ds_run and :ds_del, OPTY.bat 3049-3091 - a PowerShell
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.019|                    pass over pnputil /enum-drivers groups oemNN.inf
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.020|                    packages by original INF name and lists all but the
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.021|                    newest of each family into %TEMP%\opty_ds.txt, then
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.022|                    CMD calls pnputil /delete-driver "oemNN.inf" once per
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.023|                    package, with no /force, after a typed YES
::X|FR|driverstore.delete.024|                    confirmation
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.001|DELETE SUPERSEDED DRIVER PACKAGES
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.002|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.003|  What it is      : Windows keeps every third-party driver package
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.004|                    (oemNN.inf) ever installed, old versions included, and
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.005|                    never prunes them. This removes all but the newest of
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.006|                    each INF family.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.007|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.008|  Actual effect   : Groups the packages by their original INF name, sorts
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.009|                    each family by driver date and keeps the newest, then
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.010|                    calls pnputil /delete-driver on the rest. No /force,
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.011|                    so anything still in use survives. Menu option 1 does
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.012|                    the same analysis and deletes nothing.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.013|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.014|  Gain            : Real, measurable disk space on the system drive - the
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.015|                    DriverStore is one of the largest legitimate space
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.016|                    wins on a machine that has taken several GPU or
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.017|                    chipset driver updates. Nothing gets faster.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.018|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.019|  Cost            : Roll Back Driver stops being available for the removed
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.020|                    versions. If a new driver turns out to be worse, your
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.021|                    only way back is downloading the old one from the
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.022|                    vendor. Devices you rarely plug in - a dock, a
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.023|                    printer, a capture card - can end up needing a driver
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.024|                    download next time. Nothing regenerates on its own
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.025|                    here, which is why this sits outside the normal
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.026|                    cleanup rule.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.027|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.028|  Windows default : Not applicable - accumulating packages forever is the
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.029|                    shipped Windows behaviour, and Windows never prunes
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.030|                    them itself.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.031|
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.032|  Possible values:
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.033|    DELETE               : pnputil /delete-driver runs once per superseded
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.034|                           package. /force is deliberately never used, so
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.035|                           a package still backing a device that is
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.036|                           present is refused and kept, and the log says
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.037|                           so. Typically frees a few hundred MB to several
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.038|                           GB in C:\Windows\System32\DriverStore.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.039|    SKIP                 : Nothing is removed. Windows never prunes the
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.040|                           DriverStore itself, so every version of every
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.041|                           third-party driver you have ever installed
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.042|                           stays on the system drive.
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.043|    ANALYSE              : Menu option 1 lists exactly which packages
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.044|                           would go and deletes nothing. This is the
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.045|                           honest first answer on any machine you are not
::T|EN|driverstore.delete.046|                           sure about, and it costs seconds.
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.001|  Why these profiles : This one genuinely splits, on where the packages
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.002|                       come from. GAMING and SERVER get DELETE because
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.003|                       those machines stack GPU and chipset driver
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.004|                       revisions, which is where the gigabytes actually
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.005|                       are, and their drivers are a public download away.
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.006|                       OFFICE gets SKIP because almost nothing accumulates
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.007|                       there, so you would trade rollback for a few
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.008|                       hundred MB. LAPTOP gets SKIP because OEM packages
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.009|                       for a dock, a fingerprint reader or a vendor-
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.010|                       specific chipset are the ones that are hard to find
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.011|                       again. WINDOWS gets SKIP because Windows never
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.012|                       prunes. On any profile, run option 1 first and read
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.013|                       the list.
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.014|
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.015|  Target          : :ds_run and :ds_del, OPTY.bat 3049-3091 - a PowerShell
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.016|                    pass over pnputil /enum-drivers groups oemNN.inf
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.017|                    packages by original INF name and lists all but the
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.018|                    newest of each family into %TEMP%\opty_ds.txt, then
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.019|                    CMD calls pnputil /delete-driver "oemNN.inf" once per
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.020|                    package, with no /force, after a typed YES
::X|EN|driverstore.delete.021|                    confirmation
